{"parentUuid":"55b8e9ed-1ede-4251-ab4e-3b3ceeaa85ea","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeyuZDVtwsTTKDtWYXchK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll read the video note first to see what \"harness agnostic\" means there, then check how skills are laid out now."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20788,"cache_read_input_tokens":51521,"output_tokens":612,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":462},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20788,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":612,"cache_read_input_tokens":51521,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20788,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20788},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CeyuZCqTUWPPakkS1Zbcd","type":"assistant","uuid":"7a4630c9-4bce-43f2-82bf-9832ac16aab9","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:03:33.727Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7a4630c9-4bce-43f2-82bf-9832ac16aab9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeyuZDVtwsTTKDtWYXchK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CRQCr25mMwehVujCZoioqk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md","description":"Read the harness video breakdown note"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20788,"cache_read_input_tokens":51521,"output_tokens":612,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":462},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20788,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":612,"cache_read_input_tokens":51521,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20788,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":20788},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CeyuZCqTUWPPakkS1Zbcd","type":"assistant","uuid":"3693d321-0e86-4dd1-836f-e7cf68882425","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:03:34.590Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"3693d321-0e86-4dd1-836f-e7cf68882425","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01CRQCr25mMwehVujCZoioqk","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"PreToolUse\",\"additionalContext\":\"<context_guidance>\\n  <tip>\\n    When you intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate), use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries) for multiple commands or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language: \\\"javascript\\\", code: \\\"...\\\") for one — the raw output stays in the sandbox and only what you print enters your conversation. Shell stays the right surface when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation); if you use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language: \\\"shell\\\"), write syntax for the host shell.\\n  </tip>\\n</context_guidance>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/pretooluse.mjs\"","durationMs":131},"type":"attachment","uuid":"98088664-9916-4c39-ac40-3bc8e6991754","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:03:34.731Z","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"98088664-9916-4c39-ac40-3bc8e6991754","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    When you intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate), use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries) for multiple commands or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language: \"javascript\", code: \"...\") for one — the raw output stays in the sandbox and only what you print enters your conversation. Shell stays the right surface when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation); if you use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language: \"shell\"), write syntax for the host shell.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>"],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01CRQCr25mMwehVujCZoioqk","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"66635e16-c52c-494e-b806-68f6a960ecd5","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:03:34.731Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Bash hook additional context: <context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    When you intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate), use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries) for multiple commands or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language: \"javascript\", code: \"...\") for one — the raw output stays in the sandbox and only what you print enters your conversation. Shell stays the right surface when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation); if you use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language: \"shell\"), write syntax for the host shell.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"3693d321-0e86-4dd1-836f-e7cf68882425","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"06e7d3ba-222f-4650-8a25-521650a48509","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CRQCr25mMwehVujCZoioqk","type":"tool_result","content":"---\ntitle: \"Harness over model — Mark Kashef on Nate Herk (video breakdown + gap analysis)\"\ndate: 2026-09-12\ntype: video-note\nsource: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6LNlCpQPYFc\nvideo_title: \"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"\nchannel: Nate Herk | AI Automation\nguest: Mark Kashef (Prompt Advisors)\npublished: 2026-09-11\nduration: 34:08\ntags: [ai-harness, claude-code, codex, pi, skills, aios, context-engineering]\n---\n\n# Harness over model\n\nVideo: **\"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"** — Nate Herk with **Mark Kashef**, 34 min, posted 2026-09-11.\n\n## The one idea\n\n> \"The harness of a model is much more important than the model itself.\" — Kashef, 0:06\n\nThe model is a **brain in a jar**. It takes input, gives output. That is all.\nEverything that makes it useful — read, write, edit, bash, grep, the agentic loop —\nis the **harness** wrapped around it.\n\nAt 2:20 the whiteboard shows this literally: a detailed factory drawing with a glowing\nbrain in a jar at the dead centre, surrounded by machinery (books = read, press = write,\ngears = bash, robot arms = tools). Two boxes at the bottom are crossed out — the things\na bare brain cannot do.\n\n**Proof demo (7:19–8:55):** Kashef opens LM Studio with a local Qwen model (captions read\n\"Quen 27B\" — likely a 7B-class Qwen, too small on screen to confirm) — a raw brain with no\nharness — and asks it to build a landing page *and spin up a local server*.\nIt writes the HTML. It cannot start the server. No limbs.\nGive the same prompt to Claude Code or Codex and it does not blink.\n\n**The workflow prediction that follows (3:25–3:41):** open-source local models already do\n\"like 80% of the day-to-day work.\" He expects a world within a year where you run\n**70–80% locally** (given the hardware) and only \"bring in the geniuses for genius-level\ntasks or planning.\" The harness is what makes that split possible — the local brain gets\nthe same limbs as the expensive one.\n\n**The consequence for benchmarks (20:29–21:45):** after the March 2026 Claude Code harness\nleak, Kashef read the whole thing. Most of it was *crutches* — regex lists of swear words,\nbehaviour guards, scaffolding. His takeaway:\n\n> \"There is a whole factory of workers making this model look way smarter than it is.\"\n\nSo stop being impressed by benchmark scores. Ask instead how well the provider improved\n**the harness** to match the new brain. Judge empirically, on your own tasks.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter map\n\n| Time | Topic |\n|---|---|\n| 0:00 | Intro |\n| 2:05 | Models vs. harnesses (the brain-in-a-jar diagram) |\n| 7:19 | Local models in LM Studio — the no-harness proof |\n| 9:26 | Sponsor (skip) |\n| 10:29 | Choosing and switching AI agents |\n| 13:37 | Hermes, OpenClaw, and **Pi** |\n| 16:47 | Making your skills portable (`/poly-skill`) |\n| 19:17 | Understanding your AI system |\n| 21:46 | Keeping your AI OS updated (`rot.md`) |\n| 24:14 | When models outgrow your skills |\n| 30:01 | Organizing projects and global rules (**Promotion**) |\n| 33:37 | Final thoughts |\n\n---\n\n## Hermes, OpenClaw, and Pi — the same category (13:37–15:28)\n\nAll three are **bring-your-own-model harnesses**: you supply the harness, then loop in\nwhatever brain you want.\n\n- **Hermes beat OpenClaw on the harness, not the skills.** Kashef is blunt at 14:14 —\n  \"all these skill MD files, etc., they're all great, but the thing that made Hermes\n  better than OpenClaw is its harness.\"\n- **Vanilla Hermes loses to Codex.** Run the same task one-to-one, out of the box, and\n  you get a different result. But you can *massage* Hermes's harness until it performs\n  the same verification loop that makes Codex feel sharp. Monkey see, monkey do.\n- **That is why everyone's Hermes agent is a \"special snowflake\"** (14:00) — people keep\n  hacking the harness to fit how they want to use their models.\n- **Pi is the vanilla open-source version** of the same idea (6:03), where you build your\n  own harness from a minimal base.\n\nThe endgame he wants (15:34): one harness for everything, with models as a **leased brain**\nyou swap out as needed. His framing at 15:47 — the ecosystem is tribal right now\n(\"I am team Codex, I am team Claude Code, you are all Neanderthals\"). He is deliberately\nanti-tribal: build so that whichever brain is best, fastest, or cheapest today can be\nswapped in with near-zero acclimation. If Gemini \"truly wakes up\" tomorrow, he wants the\nswap to cost 24 hours, not a rebuild.\n\n---\n\n## Gap analysis — video claim vs. your actual setup\n\n| # | Technique (timestamp) | What the video says | Your status | Your equivalent |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Zero provider loyalty** (11:13) | Be loyal to your harness and assets, never the model. Swap brains freely. | **Have** | `claudex-route`, `claudex-loop` |\n| 2 | **Claude plans, Codex executes** (12:14) | Claude Code = gifted artist / wise old owl. Codex = surgeon / rottweiler. Make them fight in a loop for ~10 rounds until Claude has what Codex saw. | **Have** | `claudex-loop`, `codex-review`, `codex-build` |\n| 3 | **`/poly-skill` — dual-format skills** (16:47) | Feed Claude *and* Codex docs to an agent, have it write one skill that both pick up reliably. Codex prioritises different fields than Claude. Python is Python; the *invocation* needs massaging. | **Partial** | `~/.claude/AGENTS.md` sibling exists, `CreateSkill` exists — but your 164 skills are Claude-shaped only |\n| 4 | **Monthly skill audit on cron** (18:40) | Auto-audit the whole ecosystem monthly: cut unused skills, merge overlapping ones into compound skills, re-check model-agnosticism. | **Missing** | `doctor-plus` is manual only |\n| 5 | **`rot.md` — decay tiers** (22:41) | One file that records how fast each AIOS layer rots, so you audit on the right clock. | **Missing** | — |\n| 6 | **Delete skills aggressively** (24:14) | *Per the video,* Boris Cherny tweeted you should delete all your skills every 6 months. A skill is a crutch. If the model outgrew it, the skill now *limits* it. | **Missing** | — |\n| 7 | **Promotion rule** (31:32) | Everything is project-level until it **earns** global. Few global things. Small blast radius. | **Missing — and inverted** | You have **0** project-level skills. Everything is global. |\n| 8 | **Many small OSes, not one mega** (30:19) | ~25 isolated operating systems (tax, consulting, school, enterprise). One meta-system audits them all. Isolation = you can tell model problem from harness problem from organisation problem. | **Partial** | `~/DEV` is one workspace; 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md` |\n| 9 | **Mine your own JSONL logs** (15:04) | Every Claude/Codex conversation lives on disk with tool-call metadata. Read them to reverse-engineer what a good harness does — \"monkey see, monkey do\". | **Missing** | You have **414** `.jsonl` sessions sitting unused |\n| 10 | **Own your harness (Pi)** (14:39) | pi.dev — copy an open-source harness onto your machine, then shape it until it behaves like the bits of Claude Code and Codex you love. | **Missing** | `pi` not installed |\n| 11 | **Ambitious prompts** (29:02) | Anthropic's own release notes say *give it more ambitious tasks*. Kashef prompts \"build this, but impress me.\" | **Missing** | — |\n\n### The decay tiers (22:41–24:14) — copy this table\n\n| Layer | How often it rots | Audit every |\n|---|---|---|\n| Identity (who you are, goals) | Very slow | 1–3 months |\n| Substrate / core context | Slow | 1–3 months |\n| Skills | **Fast** | Monthly |\n| Agents | **Fast** | Monthly |\n| Rules | **Fastest** | Weekly (Kashef changes some daily) |\n| Hooks | Slow | ~6 months |\n\nHis reasoning on rules: every new task with the same AIOS exposes a new edge case,\nso rules get rewritten constantly. Skills get *deleted*, rules get *sharpened*.\n\n### Why skills rot (24:26–27:38)\n\nA skill is a shortcut telling the brain which tools to use and in what order.\nAs the brain gets smarter it needs fewer shortcuts. His own example:\n\n- **Before:** a skill explaining exactly where conversation files live and how to chunk\n  them so it would not blow the context window.\n- **Now:** one sentence — \"read all our conversations and give me 15 optimisations\" —\n  and it handles context budgeting on its own.\n\nHis analogy (25:53): teaching Nate how to make YouTube videos was a useful skill two\nyears ago. Today Nate would look at the subscriber counts and tilt his head.\n**You outgrow skills.**\n\nThe endgame he predicts (25:37): *fewer skills, stricter rules.*\nKeep only skills carrying genuine tacit knowledge the model could never know —\nyour force-plate thresholds, your GPS conventions, Baylor's reporting norms.\nDelete anything that is just step-by-step instructions.\n\n### The counter-argument, raised on air (27:50)\n\nNate pushes back: skills are also **guardrails**. Remove them and the model improvises,\npossibly badly. Kashef's answer: that is what the *rest* of the AIOS is for —\nrules, hooks, layers that babysit. Hence \"fewer skills, stricter rules.\"\n\n---\n\n## Your numbers (measured today, 2026-09-12)\n\nI counted these from your actual machine, not from the video.\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Global skills in `~/.claude/skills/` | **164** |\n| Plugin skills exposed per session | **~37** (6 plugins enabled, 18 disabled) |\n| SKILL.md files sitting in plugin cache | 606 (mostly disabled plugins + old versions — **not** the load) |\n| Skills exposed to one session's roster | **~120** (counted from this session's skill list, not from a command) |\n| Project-level skills anywhere in `~/DEV` | **0** |\n| Sessions on disk (`.jsonl`) | **414** |\n| Distinct real skills ever invoked, all time | **58** |\n| Used 3 times or more | **30** |\n| Used exactly **once** | **19** |\n| Hooks in `~/.claude/hooks/` | 61 |\n| Custom agents in `~/.claude/agents/` | 0 |\n\n**Your actual working set** (3+ uses): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`,\n`mattpocock-skills:handoff`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `superpowers:brainstorming`,\n`watch:watch`, `superpowers:writing-plans`, `backup`, `artifact-design`, `humanizer`,\n`advisor`, `doctor-plus`, `impeccable`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`,\n`codex-review`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, + ~10 more.\n\n**Used once and never again:** `Harvest`, `code-review`, `design-taste-frontend`,\n`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`, `interview-me`, `scroll-craft`,\n`sports-science-research-grounding`, `superpowers:test-driven-development`,\n`superpowers:verification-before-completion`, the four `cli-printing-press:*` skills,\n`claude-in-chrome`, `codex:adversarial-review`, `codex:setup`, `effort`,\n`mattpocock-skills:teach`.\n\n> Note: \"used once\" is not automatically \"delete\". `forecast-capture` is seasonal.\n> `ingest-book` is occasional by design. The question per skill is\n> *would the model do this fine without the skill now?* — not *how often did I run it?*\n\nThis is the exact pattern Kashef describes at 18:55: *\"they have 300 of them,\nthey load every single time, and they use five.\"* You are a more extreme case than\nhis example, and the promotion rule at 31:32 is the direct fix.\n\n---\n\n## Pi — the part you asked about\n\n### What it actually is (verified at pi.dev, 2026-09-12)\n\n- **A minimal agent harness.** Open source, MIT. pi.dev credits **Earendil Inc. and\n  contributors**. Repo: `earendil-works/pi`. Docs: `pi.dev/docs/latest`.\n  (Third-party write-ups attribute the original work to Mario Zechner — I did not\n  confirm that on pi.dev itself.)\n- **Built-in tools, and that is all:** `read`, `bash`, `edit`, `write`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`.\n  Everything heavier is layered on via extensions, skills, prompt templates, themes,\n  and Pi packages.\n- **15+ providers:** Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, Azure, Bedrock, Mistral, Groq, Cerebras,\n  xAI, Hugging Face, more. Swap mid-session with `/model` or `Ctrl+L`.\n- **Context files:** `AGENTS.md` and `SYSTEM.md`.\n- **Four modes:** interactive TUI, print/JSON, RPC, and SDK.\n- **Tree-structured session history** with branching and sharing.\n- **Minimal default system prompt**, progressive capability disclosure.\n\nInstall (either one — **do not run yet, see below**):\n\n```\ncurl -fsSL https://pi.dev/install.sh | sh\n```\n```\nnpm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n\nYour `node` is v26.6.0 and `npm` is 11.18.0 at `/opt/homebrew/bin/` — both paths work.\n\n### Kashef's on-ramp (14:39–15:28) — the actually useful bit\n\nHe does **not** read the docs himself. He:\n\n1. Feeds the `pi.dev` docs link to Claude Code or Codex.\n2. Says: go read this documentation, fan out agents, learn the whole harness.\n3. Then points it at his **JSONL conversation files** — every Claude Code and Codex\n   session on disk, complete with which tools were called, what verifications ran,\n   in what order.\n4. Asks it to **monkey-see-monkey-do**: build a version of the Pi harness that reacts\n   the same way on the same kinds of task.\n\nThat is how you reverse-engineer the parts of Claude Code and Codex you like into a\nharness you own. You have **414** of those JSONL files already.\n\n### Three things the video does not tell you\n\n1. **Pi ships no permission popups.** Straight from the docs: confirmation flows belong\n   in *your* extension, matched to *your* threat model. Claude Code's approval prompts,\n   your `dcg` guard, your backup default-deny — none of that exists in Pi until you\n   build it. For someone handling identifiable athlete health data, that is the lead fact.\n   **Run Pi in a throwaway directory, never in `~/DEV` and never anywhere near\n   `DexaProject`.**\n2. **Your skills port with one line of config — your hooks need a real but bounded port.**\n   ~~Skills port with editing; hooks would be a rewrite of weeks.~~ **Corrected after\n   reading the docs** (see `2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`): Pi implements the same\n   Agent Skills standard, already auto-discovers `~/.agents/skills/` where 45 of your\n   skills live, and loads `CLAUDE.md` directly. Hooks become TypeScript *extensions* —\n   your hooks are already TypeScript, and Pi's `tool_call` event returns\n   `{block, reason}`, which is exactly your `dcg` guard. One deliberate port per hook,\n   not a paradigm rewrite.\n3. **Running your Claude subscription through Pi costs extra.** Per Pi's docs,\n   third-party harness usage \"draws from extra usage and is billed per token, not\n   against Claude plan limits.\" The video's \"swap the brain freely\" pitch quietly\n   assumes you are paying per token anyway.\n4. **\"24 hours to swap everything\"** (16:15) is Kashef's claim about *his* assets,\n   which he deliberately built provider-agnostic from day one. Yours are closer than\n   I first thought — but the safety layer is the part that does not come free.\n\n### My read for you\n\nPi is real and worth trying. But it is a **harness you have to build**, not a harness\nyou get. The skills and context files move almost for free; the permission prompts,\nguards and approval flows are all yours to re-create.\n\n**Status 2026-09-12:** the skill audit is DONE — 99 skills archived, 160 → 61 global,\n~19.2k → ~6.4k tokens per session. Full Pi research is in\n`2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`. Next step there is a 30-minute sandbox trial in\n`~/pi-trial`, then porting one hook (`dcg`) to measure the true cost of a full move.\n\n---\n\n## Ranked actions\n\n### 1. Skill audit (biggest win, do first)\n\nKill the bloat. Target: 164 global skills → a working set of ~30, plus the seasonal ones.\n\nRe-run my count any time:\n\nThis is the exact script that produced the 58 / 30 / 19 numbers above. The `BUILTIN` set\nmatters — without it you also count `/exit`, `/clear`, `/compact` and friends, which\ninflates the total to 74 and buries the real skills.\n\n```bash\npython3 - <<'EOF'\nimport glob,re,collections\nBUILTIN={'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin','doctor',\n'help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp','hooks','memory',\n'review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir','bug','release-notes',\n'pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline','output-style','todos','export',\n'privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts','tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind',\n'sandbox','feedback','worktrees','skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes',\n'keybindings','theme'}\nc=collections.Counter()\nfor f in glob.glob('/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl',recursive=True):\n    try:\n        for line in open(f,encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore'):\n            if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line: continue\n            for m in re.finditer(r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n            for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n    except Exception: pass\nreal={k:v for k,v in c.items() if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\nprint(\"distinct real skills ever invoked:\",len(real))\nprint(\"used >=3 times:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v>=3]))\nprint(\"used exactly once:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v==1]))\nprint()\nfor k,v in sorted(real.items(),key=lambda x:-x[1]): print(f\"{v:5d}  {k}\")\nEOF\n```\n\nFor each skill below the line, ask the video's question — not \"do I use it?\" but\n**\"would Opus 5 do this fine without the skill?\"** If yes, delete it.\n\n### 2. Adopt the promotion rule\n\nYou have 0 project-level skills and 164 global ones. Invert it.\nA skill lives in `<project>/.claude/skills/` until it proves itself across 2+ projects,\n*then* it gets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/`. Small blast radius, and when something\nunderperforms you can tell whether it is the model, the harness, or your organisation.\n\n### 3. Write a `rot.md`\n\nOne file, `~/.claude/rot.md`, with the decay table above filled in for *your* layers,\nplus a \"last audited\" date per layer. Then put the monthly skill audit on the same\nlaunchd cadence as your backup. You already have the cron habit — see\n`project_automation-cadence`.\n\n### Later, not now\n\n- **Pi in a sandbox dir.** Worth a Saturday once the audit is done.\n- **`/poly-skill`.** Build it with `CreateSkill`: feed it Claude + Codex skill docs,\n  have it emit dual-format skills. Makes sense *after* you know which 30 skills survive.\n- **Ambitious prompting.** Free to try immediately: add \"and impress me\" to a build\n  prompt and see what changes.\n- **Nate's new-model ritual (25:53–26:10).** Every time a new model ships, he runs it\n  through *all* his skills to check it still picks them up and uses them the same way.\n  Cheap habit, worth copying — and Kashef's upgrade to it is the harder question:\n  not \"can it still use the skill?\" but \"does it still **need** the skill?\"\n\n---\n\n## Verbatim lines worth keeping\n\n> \"My number one goal is to never be loyal to a provider — only to be loyal to my\n> harness and my assets. I will switch the brain interchangeably.\" — 11:13\n\n> \"I see Codex as a surgeon and Claude Code as a gifted artist.\" — 12:30\n\n> \"You can outsource the thinking, but you can never outsource the understanding.\"\n> — Nate Herk, 19:26\n\n> \"This stuff isn't magic at all. There is a whole factory of workers that are making\n> this model look way smarter than it is.\" — 21:11\n\n> \"You want to make sure that your skill is actually adding value, and not holding back\n> this dragon that gets only bigger with time.\" — 24:56\n\n> \"Everything is project until it deserves to be promoted to global.\" — 31:37\n\n> \"Mastery is just understanding how the entire factory works end to end, and where each\n> piece lies. So when in doubt, audit your setup, audit your skills, audit the bloat.\"\n> — 33:41\n\n---\n\n## Unverified\n\n- The Boris Cherny \"delete all your skills every 6 months\" tweet is reported by Kashef\n  at 24:17. I did not confirm the tweet exists or its exact wording.\n- The \"March 2026 Claude Code harness leak\" is described from memory at 20:29.\n  Treat the swear-word-regex detail as his account, not established fact.\n- The local model in the LM Studio demo is read off low-resolution captions as\n  \"Quen 27B\". Almost certainly a Qwen model; the exact size is unconfirmed.\n- Pi's original authorship (Mario Zechner) comes from third-party blog posts in search\n  results, not from pi.dev. pi.dev says \"Earendil Inc. and contributors\".","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e060475e-5912-4643-a394-77c13ce50194","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:03:36.433Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"---\ntitle: \"Harness over model — Mark Kashef on Nate Herk (video breakdown + gap analysis)\"\ndate: 2026-09-12\ntype: video-note\nsource: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6LNlCpQPYFc\nvideo_title: \"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"\nchannel: Nate Herk | AI Automation\nguest: Mark Kashef (Prompt Advisors)\npublished: 2026-09-11\nduration: 34:08\ntags: [ai-harness, claude-code, codex, pi, skills, aios, context-engineering]\n---\n\n# Harness over model\n\nVideo: **\"How to Actually Choose the Right AI Agent\"** — Nate Herk with **Mark Kashef**, 34 min, posted 2026-09-11.\n\n## The one idea\n\n> \"The harness of a model is much more important than the model itself.\" — Kashef, 0:06\n\nThe model is a **brain in a jar**. It takes input, gives output. That is all.\nEverything that makes it useful — read, write, edit, bash, grep, the agentic loop —\nis the **harness** wrapped around it.\n\nAt 2:20 the whiteboard shows this literally: a detailed factory drawing with a glowing\nbrain in a jar at the dead centre, surrounded by machinery (books = read, press = write,\ngears = bash, robot arms = tools). Two boxes at the bottom are crossed out — the things\na bare brain cannot do.\n\n**Proof demo (7:19–8:55):** Kashef opens LM Studio with a local Qwen model (captions read\n\"Quen 27B\" — likely a 7B-class Qwen, too small on screen to confirm) — a raw brain with no\nharness — and asks it to build a landing page *and spin up a local server*.\nIt writes the HTML. It cannot start the server. No limbs.\nGive the same prompt to Claude Code or Codex and it does not blink.\n\n**The workflow prediction that follows (3:25–3:41):** open-source local models already do\n\"like 80% of the day-to-day work.\" He expects a world within a year where you run\n**70–80% locally** (given the hardware) and only \"bring in the geniuses for genius-level\ntasks or planning.\" The harness is what makes that split possible — the local brain gets\nthe same limbs as the expensive one.\n\n**The consequence for benchmarks (20:29–21:45):** after the March 2026 Claude Code harness\nleak, Kashef read the whole thing. Most of it was *crutches* — regex lists of swear words,\nbehaviour guards, scaffolding. His takeaway:\n\n> \"There is a whole factory of workers making this model look way smarter than it is.\"\n\nSo stop being impressed by benchmark scores. Ask instead how well the provider improved\n**the harness** to match the new brain. Judge empirically, on your own tasks.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter map\n\n| Time | Topic |\n|---|---|\n| 0:00 | Intro |\n| 2:05 | Models vs. harnesses (the brain-in-a-jar diagram) |\n| 7:19 | Local models in LM Studio — the no-harness proof |\n| 9:26 | Sponsor (skip) |\n| 10:29 | Choosing and switching AI agents |\n| 13:37 | Hermes, OpenClaw, and **Pi** |\n| 16:47 | Making your skills portable (`/poly-skill`) |\n| 19:17 | Understanding your AI system |\n| 21:46 | Keeping your AI OS updated (`rot.md`) |\n| 24:14 | When models outgrow your skills |\n| 30:01 | Organizing projects and global rules (**Promotion**) |\n| 33:37 | Final thoughts |\n\n---\n\n## Hermes, OpenClaw, and Pi — the same category (13:37–15:28)\n\nAll three are **bring-your-own-model harnesses**: you supply the harness, then loop in\nwhatever brain you want.\n\n- **Hermes beat OpenClaw on the harness, not the skills.** Kashef is blunt at 14:14 —\n  \"all these skill MD files, etc., they're all great, but the thing that made Hermes\n  better than OpenClaw is its harness.\"\n- **Vanilla Hermes loses to Codex.** Run the same task one-to-one, out of the box, and\n  you get a different result. But you can *massage* Hermes's harness until it performs\n  the same verification loop that makes Codex feel sharp. Monkey see, monkey do.\n- **That is why everyone's Hermes agent is a \"special snowflake\"** (14:00) — people keep\n  hacking the harness to fit how they want to use their models.\n- **Pi is the vanilla open-source version** of the same idea (6:03), where you build your\n  own harness from a minimal base.\n\nThe endgame he wants (15:34): one harness for everything, with models as a **leased brain**\nyou swap out as needed. His framing at 15:47 — the ecosystem is tribal right now\n(\"I am team Codex, I am team Claude Code, you are all Neanderthals\"). He is deliberately\nanti-tribal: build so that whichever brain is best, fastest, or cheapest today can be\nswapped in with near-zero acclimation. If Gemini \"truly wakes up\" tomorrow, he wants the\nswap to cost 24 hours, not a rebuild.\n\n---\n\n## Gap analysis — video claim vs. your actual setup\n\n| # | Technique (timestamp) | What the video says | Your status | Your equivalent |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Zero provider loyalty** (11:13) | Be loyal to your harness and assets, never the model. Swap brains freely. | **Have** | `claudex-route`, `claudex-loop` |\n| 2 | **Claude plans, Codex executes** (12:14) | Claude Code = gifted artist / wise old owl. Codex = surgeon / rottweiler. Make them fight in a loop for ~10 rounds until Claude has what Codex saw. | **Have** | `claudex-loop`, `codex-review`, `codex-build` |\n| 3 | **`/poly-skill` — dual-format skills** (16:47) | Feed Claude *and* Codex docs to an agent, have it write one skill that both pick up reliably. Codex prioritises different fields than Claude. Python is Python; the *invocation* needs massaging. | **Partial** | `~/.claude/AGENTS.md` sibling exists, `CreateSkill` exists — but your 164 skills are Claude-shaped only |\n| 4 | **Monthly skill audit on cron** (18:40) | Auto-audit the whole ecosystem monthly: cut unused skills, merge overlapping ones into compound skills, re-check model-agnosticism. | **Missing** | `doctor-plus` is manual only |\n| 5 | **`rot.md` — decay tiers** (22:41) | One file that records how fast each AIOS layer rots, so you audit on the right clock. | **Missing** | — |\n| 6 | **Delete skills aggressively** (24:14) | *Per the video,* Boris Cherny tweeted you should delete all your skills every 6 months. A skill is a crutch. If the model outgrew it, the skill now *limits* it. | **Missing** | — |\n| 7 | **Promotion rule** (31:32) | Everything is project-level until it **earns** global. Few global things. Small blast radius. | **Missing — and inverted** | You have **0** project-level skills. Everything is global. |\n| 8 | **Many small OSes, not one mega** (30:19) | ~25 isolated operating systems (tax, consulting, school, enterprise). One meta-system audits them all. Isolation = you can tell model problem from harness problem from organisation problem. | **Partial** | `~/DEV` is one workspace; 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md` |\n| 9 | **Mine your own JSONL logs** (15:04) | Every Claude/Codex conversation lives on disk with tool-call metadata. Read them to reverse-engineer what a good harness does — \"monkey see, monkey do\". | **Missing** | You have **414** `.jsonl` sessions sitting unused |\n| 10 | **Own your harness (Pi)** (14:39) | pi.dev — copy an open-source harness onto your machine, then shape it until it behaves like the bits of Claude Code and Codex you love. | **Missing** | `pi` not installed |\n| 11 | **Ambitious prompts** (29:02) | Anthropic's own release notes say *give it more ambitious tasks*. Kashef prompts \"build this, but impress me.\" | **Missing** | — |\n\n### The decay tiers (22:41–24:14) — copy this table\n\n| Layer | How often it rots | Audit every |\n|---|---|---|\n| Identity (who you are, goals) | Very slow | 1–3 months |\n| Substrate / core context | Slow | 1–3 months |\n| Skills | **Fast** | Monthly |\n| Agents | **Fast** | Monthly |\n| Rules | **Fastest** | Weekly (Kashef changes some daily) |\n| Hooks | Slow | ~6 months |\n\nHis reasoning on rules: every new task with the same AIOS exposes a new edge case,\nso rules get rewritten constantly. Skills get *deleted*, rules get *sharpened*.\n\n### Why skills rot (24:26–27:38)\n\nA skill is a shortcut telling the brain which tools to use and in what order.\nAs the brain gets smarter it needs fewer shortcuts. His own example:\n\n- **Before:** a skill explaining exactly where conversation files live and how to chunk\n  them so it would not blow the context window.\n- **Now:** one sentence — \"read all our conversations and give me 15 optimisations\" —\n  and it handles context budgeting on its own.\n\nHis analogy (25:53): teaching Nate how to make YouTube videos was a useful skill two\nyears ago. Today Nate would look at the subscriber counts and tilt his head.\n**You outgrow skills.**\n\nThe endgame he predicts (25:37): *fewer skills, stricter rules.*\nKeep only skills carrying genuine tacit knowledge the model could never know —\nyour force-plate thresholds, your GPS conventions, Baylor's reporting norms.\nDelete anything that is just step-by-step instructions.\n\n### The counter-argument, raised on air (27:50)\n\nNate pushes back: skills are also **guardrails**. Remove them and the model improvises,\npossibly badly. Kashef's answer: that is what the *rest* of the AIOS is for —\nrules, hooks, layers that babysit. Hence \"fewer skills, stricter rules.\"\n\n---\n\n## Your numbers (measured today, 2026-09-12)\n\nI counted these from your actual machine, not from the video.\n\n| Metric | Count |\n|---|---|\n| Global skills in `~/.claude/skills/` | **164** |\n| Plugin skills exposed per session | **~37** (6 plugins enabled, 18 disabled) |\n| SKILL.md files sitting in plugin cache | 606 (mostly disabled plugins + old versions — **not** the load) |\n| Skills exposed to one session's roster | **~120** (counted from this session's skill list, not from a command) |\n| Project-level skills anywhere in `~/DEV` | **0** |\n| Sessions on disk (`.jsonl`) | **414** |\n| Distinct real skills ever invoked, all time | **58** |\n| Used 3 times or more | **30** |\n| Used exactly **once** | **19** |\n| Hooks in `~/.claude/hooks/` | 61 |\n| Custom agents in `~/.claude/agents/` | 0 |\n\n**Your actual working set** (3+ uses): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`,\n`mattpocock-skills:handoff`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `superpowers:brainstorming`,\n`watch:watch`, `superpowers:writing-plans`, `backup`, `artifact-design`, `humanizer`,\n`advisor`, `doctor-plus`, `impeccable`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`,\n`codex-review`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, + ~10 more.\n\n**Used once and never again:** `Harvest`, `code-review`, `design-taste-frontend`,\n`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`, `interview-me`, `scroll-craft`,\n`sports-science-research-grounding`, `superpowers:test-driven-development`,\n`superpowers:verification-before-completion`, the four `cli-printing-press:*` skills,\n`claude-in-chrome`, `codex:adversarial-review`, `codex:setup`, `effort`,\n`mattpocock-skills:teach`.\n\n> Note: \"used once\" is not automatically \"delete\". `forecast-capture` is seasonal.\n> `ingest-book` is occasional by design. The question per skill is\n> *would the model do this fine without the skill now?* — not *how often did I run it?*\n\nThis is the exact pattern Kashef describes at 18:55: *\"they have 300 of them,\nthey load every single time, and they use five.\"* You are a more extreme case than\nhis example, and the promotion rule at 31:32 is the direct fix.\n\n---\n\n## Pi — the part you asked about\n\n### What it actually is (verified at pi.dev, 2026-09-12)\n\n- **A minimal agent harness.** Open source, MIT. pi.dev credits **Earendil Inc. and\n  contributors**. Repo: `earendil-works/pi`. Docs: `pi.dev/docs/latest`.\n  (Third-party write-ups attribute the original work to Mario Zechner — I did not\n  confirm that on pi.dev itself.)\n- **Built-in tools, and that is all:** `read`, `bash`, `edit`, `write`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`.\n  Everything heavier is layered on via extensions, skills, prompt templates, themes,\n  and Pi packages.\n- **15+ providers:** Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, Azure, Bedrock, Mistral, Groq, Cerebras,\n  xAI, Hugging Face, more. Swap mid-session with `/model` or `Ctrl+L`.\n- **Context files:** `AGENTS.md` and `SYSTEM.md`.\n- **Four modes:** interactive TUI, print/JSON, RPC, and SDK.\n- **Tree-structured session history** with branching and sharing.\n- **Minimal default system prompt**, progressive capability disclosure.\n\nInstall (either one — **do not run yet, see below**):\n\n```\ncurl -fsSL https://pi.dev/install.sh | sh\n```\n```\nnpm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\n```\n\nYour `node` is v26.6.0 and `npm` is 11.18.0 at `/opt/homebrew/bin/` — both paths work.\n\n### Kashef's on-ramp (14:39–15:28) — the actually useful bit\n\nHe does **not** read the docs himself. He:\n\n1. Feeds the `pi.dev` docs link to Claude Code or Codex.\n2. Says: go read this documentation, fan out agents, learn the whole harness.\n3. Then points it at his **JSONL conversation files** — every Claude Code and Codex\n   session on disk, complete with which tools were called, what verifications ran,\n   in what order.\n4. Asks it to **monkey-see-monkey-do**: build a version of the Pi harness that reacts\n   the same way on the same kinds of task.\n\nThat is how you reverse-engineer the parts of Claude Code and Codex you like into a\nharness you own. You have **414** of those JSONL files already.\n\n### Three things the video does not tell you\n\n1. **Pi ships no permission popups.** Straight from the docs: confirmation flows belong\n   in *your* extension, matched to *your* threat model. Claude Code's approval prompts,\n   your `dcg` guard, your backup default-deny — none of that exists in Pi until you\n   build it. For someone handling identifiable athlete health data, that is the lead fact.\n   **Run Pi in a throwaway directory, never in `~/DEV` and never anywhere near\n   `DexaProject`.**\n2. **Your skills port with one line of config — your hooks need a real but bounded port.**\n   ~~Skills port with editing; hooks would be a rewrite of weeks.~~ **Corrected after\n   reading the docs** (see `2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`): Pi implements the same\n   Agent Skills standard, already auto-discovers `~/.agents/skills/` where 45 of your\n   skills live, and loads `CLAUDE.md` directly. Hooks become TypeScript *extensions* —\n   your hooks are already TypeScript, and Pi's `tool_call` event returns\n   `{block, reason}`, which is exactly your `dcg` guard. One deliberate port per hook,\n   not a paradigm rewrite.\n3. **Running your Claude subscription through Pi costs extra.** Per Pi's docs,\n   third-party harness usage \"draws from extra usage and is billed per token, not\n   against Claude plan limits.\" The video's \"swap the brain freely\" pitch quietly\n   assumes you are paying per token anyway.\n4. **\"24 hours to swap everything\"** (16:15) is Kashef's claim about *his* assets,\n   which he deliberately built provider-agnostic from day one. Yours are closer than\n   I first thought — but the safety layer is the part that does not come free.\n\n### My read for you\n\nPi is real and worth trying. But it is a **harness you have to build**, not a harness\nyou get. The skills and context files move almost for free; the permission prompts,\nguards and approval flows are all yours to re-create.\n\n**Status 2026-09-12:** the skill audit is DONE — 99 skills archived, 160 → 61 global,\n~19.2k → ~6.4k tokens per session. Full Pi research is in\n`2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`. Next step there is a 30-minute sandbox trial in\n`~/pi-trial`, then porting one hook (`dcg`) to measure the true cost of a full move.\n\n---\n\n## Ranked actions\n\n### 1. Skill audit (biggest win, do first)\n\nKill the bloat. Target: 164 global skills → a working set of ~30, plus the seasonal ones.\n\nRe-run my count any time:\n\nThis is the exact script that produced the 58 / 30 / 19 numbers above. The `BUILTIN` set\nmatters — without it you also count `/exit`, `/clear`, `/compact` and friends, which\ninflates the total to 74 and buries the real skills.\n\n```bash\npython3 - <<'EOF'\nimport glob,re,collections\nBUILTIN={'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin','doctor',\n'help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp','hooks','memory',\n'review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir','bug','release-notes',\n'pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline','output-style','todos','export',\n'privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts','tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind',\n'sandbox','feedback','worktrees','skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes',\n'keybindings','theme'}\nc=collections.Counter()\nfor f in glob.glob('/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl',recursive=True):\n    try:\n        for line in open(f,encoding='utf-8',errors='ignore'):\n            if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line: continue\n            for m in re.finditer(r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n            for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n                c[m.group(1)]+=1\n    except Exception: pass\nreal={k:v for k,v in c.items() if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\nprint(\"distinct real skills ever invoked:\",len(real))\nprint(\"used >=3 times:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v>=3]))\nprint(\"used exactly once:\",len([k for k,v in real.items() if v==1]))\nprint()\nfor k,v in sorted(real.items(),key=lambda x:-x[1]): print(f\"{v:5d}  {k}\")\nEOF\n```\n\nFor each skill below the line, ask the video's question — not \"do I use it?\" but\n**\"would Opus 5 do this fine without the skill?\"** If yes, delete it.\n\n### 2. Adopt the promotion rule\n\nYou have 0 project-level skills and 164 global ones. Invert it.\nA skill lives in `<project>/.claude/skills/` until it proves itself across 2+ projects,\n*then* it gets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/`. Small blast radius, and when something\nunderperforms you can tell whether it is the model, the harness, or your organisation.\n\n### 3. Write a `rot.md`\n\nOne file, `~/.claude/rot.md`, with the decay table above filled in for *your* layers,\nplus a \"last audited\" date per layer. Then put the monthly skill audit on the same\nlaunchd cadence as your backup. You already have the cron habit — see\n`project_automation-cadence`.\n\n### Later, not now\n\n- **Pi in a sandbox dir.** Worth a Saturday once the audit is done.\n- **`/poly-skill`.** Build it with `CreateSkill`: feed it Claude + Codex skill docs,\n  have it emit dual-format skills. Makes sense *after* you know which 30 skills survive.\n- **Ambitious prompting.** Free to try immediately: add \"and impress me\" to a build\n  prompt and see what changes.\n- **Nate's new-model ritual (25:53–26:10).** Every time a new model ships, he runs it\n  through *all* his skills to check it still picks them up and uses them the same way.\n  Cheap habit, worth copying — and Kashef's upgrade to it is the harder question:\n  not \"can it still use the skill?\" but \"does it still **need** the skill?\"\n\n---\n\n## Verbatim lines worth keeping\n\n> \"My number one goal is to never be loyal to a provider — only to be loyal to my\n> harness and my assets. I will switch the brain interchangeably.\" — 11:13\n\n> \"I see Codex as a surgeon and Claude Code as a gifted artist.\" — 12:30\n\n> \"You can outsource the thinking, but you can never outsource the understanding.\"\n> — Nate Herk, 19:26\n\n> \"This stuff isn't magic at all. There is a whole factory of workers that are making\n> this model look way smarter than it is.\" — 21:11\n\n> \"You want to make sure that your skill is actually adding value, and not holding back\n> this dragon that gets only bigger with time.\" — 24:56\n\n> \"Everything is project until it deserves to be promoted to global.\" — 31:37\n\n> \"Mastery is just understanding how the entire factory works end to end, and where each\n> piece lies. So when in doubt, audit your setup, audit your skills, audit the bloat.\"\n> — 33:41\n\n---\n\n## Unverified\n\n- The Boris Cherny \"delete all your skills every 6 months\" tweet is reported by Kashef\n  at 24:17. I did not confirm the tweet exists or its exact wording.\n- The \"March 2026 Claude Code harness leak\" is described from memory at 20:29.\n  Treat the swear-word-regex detail as his account, not established fact.\n- The local model in the LM Studio demo is read off low-resolution captions as\n  \"Quen 27B\". Almost certainly a Qwen model; the exact size is unconfirmed.\n- Pi's original authorship (Mario Zechner) comes from third-party blog posts in search\n  results, not from pi.dev. pi.dev says \"Earendil Inc. and contributors\".","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3693d321-0e86-4dd1-836f-e7cf68882425","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16