{"parentUuid":"b22a0d1f-26d8-4408-9707-a6f3ce1f3c99","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"06e7d3ba-222f-4650-8a25-521650a48509","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016Po6itGp1rFFir8VbGRFxi","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"0d66b41e-d1fd-4dbe-9343-7dc03e21af22","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:09:46.332Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","content":"# Harness-Agnostic AIOS Implementation Plan\n\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n\n**Goal:** Make every skill Eric owns load identically in Claude Code, Codex, and Pi from one canonical folder, and build a recurring audit that keeps the whole AIOS from rotting.\n\n**Architecture:** Collapse three competing skill folders into one canonical pool at `~/.agents/skills/` holding only the survivors, with `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` as thin symlink views onto it. Normalize every survivor's frontmatter to a Codex-safe core (`name` + `description`) plus Claude-only extras that Codex ignores. Then add one compound audit skill (`aios-audit`) that orchestrates the five existing audit skills instead of duplicating them, driven by a `rot.md` decay table and fired monthly by launchd.\n\n**Tech Stack:** bash, python3 (stdlib only), launchd, Agent Skills standard (SKILL.md + YAML frontmatter), Claude Code, Codex CLI, Pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`).\n\n**Spec:** `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md` (gap analysis rows 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10) and `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Global Constraints\n\n- **Never point Pi at `~/DEV`.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — documented, deliberate design. Pi work happens only in `~/pi-trial`.\n- **Never let any harness near `DexaProject`** (identifiable athlete health data, lives on Google Drive, not in `~/DEV`).\n- `~/.claude/skills/` is **gitignored** inside the `dotclaude` repo. Git is not a safety net. The tarball snapshot in Task 1 is the only undo for this plan.\n- **Plugin skills are out of scope.** `superpowers` (14 skills) and `mattpocock-skills` (35 skills) are vendored and get overwritten on update. Do not rewrite their frontmatter. Scope = the 61 skills in `~/.claude/skills/` and whatever `~/.agents/skills/` keeps.\n- **Hooks exist in two copies** (`~/.claude/hooks/` and `~/.codex/hooks/`). Any hook change patches both.\n- Global `MEMORY.md` is at 2,489 / 2,500 chars. Consolidate before adding anything.\n- `timeout` is NOT installed on this Mac (no GNU coreutils). Do not use it in any script.\n- `dcg` blocks `find ... -delete`. Use an explicit `rm` on a named path instead.\n- Python is PEP 668-managed. Use `python3` with stdlib only, or `uv`. Never `pip install` globally.\n- Every task ends with a real verification command whose output is pasted into the commit or the report. No success claim without evidence.\n\n### Measured baseline (2026-09-12, verified this session)\n\n| Fact | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `~/.claude/skills/` SKILL.md count (`find -L`) | 61 |\n| Of those, symlinks into `~/.agents/skills/` | 19 |\n| `~/.agents/skills/` top-level entries | 306 |\n| Archived yesterday to `~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/` | 99 |\n| **Archived skills still live in `~/.agents/skills/`** | **98** |\n| `~/.codex/skills/` entries | 32 (27 symlinks, 5 real dirs) |\n| Real dirs in `~/.codex/skills/` diverged from Claude copy | 0 (`dev-sync`, `closeout`, `new-project`, `startup` all identical; `session-handoff` is an empty orphan) |\n| Project-level skills in `~/DEV` | 1 (`KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit`) |\n| `~/.claude/rot.md` | does not exist |\n\n**The lead finding:** yesterday's audit cut the Claude Code roster but not the shared pool. Pi auto-discovers `~/.agents/skills/`. Installing Pi today re-exposes all 306 skills, including 98 of the 99 that were supposed to be gone. Task 2 is the fix and must land before Task 6.\n\n### Codex frontmatter ground truth (read from `~/.codex/skills/.system/*/SKILL.md`)\n\nCodex's own shipped skills use only:\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: \"skill-name\"\ndescription: \"Trigger sentence. Use when <positive triggers>. Do not use when <negative triggers>.\"\nmetadata:\n  short-description: \"One short line.\"   # optional\n---\n```\n\nCodex reads **no** `argument-hint`, `allowed-tools`, `model`, or `disable-model-invocation`. It tolerates unknown keys. Codex descriptions carry an explicit **\"Do not use when…\"** negative clause; Claude Code descriptions carry **literal trigger phrases** the user might type. The portable description needs both halves. This is the entire `/poly-skill` idea from the video, reduced to a rule.\n\n---\n\n### Task 1: Snapshot and freeze the current state\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: nothing.\n- Produces: the only rollback path for Tasks 2–4, plus `pre-canonical-inventory.txt`, the baseline every later task diffs against.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the inventory**\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits\n{\n  echo \"=== generated $(date -u +%FT%TZ) ===\"\n  echo \"--- claude skills (find -L, SKILL.md) ---\"\n  find -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | sed 's|.*/skills/||;s|/SKILL.md||' | sort\n  echo \"--- claude skills that are symlinks ---\"\n  find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec basename {} \\; | sort\n  echo \"--- agents pool ---\"\n  ls ~/.agents/skills | sort\n  echo \"--- codex skills ---\"\n  ls -la ~/.codex/skills\n  echo \"--- archived 2026-09-12 ---\"\n  ls ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | sort\n} > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\nwc -l ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\n```\n\nExpected: a file of roughly 500–520 lines.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Take the tarball**\n\n```bash\ntar czf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz \\\n  -C ~ .claude/skills .codex/skills .agents/skills\nls -lh ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz\n```\n\nExpected: a file is listed, non-zero size. `tar` may print `Removing leading '/'` — harmless.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Verify the tarball actually restores**\n\n```bash\nrm -rf /tmp/tartest && mkdir -p /tmp/tartest\ntar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C /tmp/tartest\nfind -L /tmp/tartest/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\nls /tmp/tartest/.agents/skills | wc -l\n```\n\nExpected: `61` and `306`. If either number is wrong, STOP — the snapshot is not a valid undo and no later task may run.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Record the rollback command in the inventory**\n\n```bash\ncat >> ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt <<'EOF'\n\n=== ROLLBACK (undoes Tasks 2-4 completely) ===\nrm -rf ~/.claude/skills ~/.codex/skills ~/.agents/skills\ntar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C ~\nEOF\ntail -6 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\n```\n\nExpected: the rollback block prints.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md && git commit -m \"docs: pre-canonical skill inventory + harness-agnostic plan\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 2: Prune the shared pool to the survivors\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (move 98 dirs out)\n- Create: `~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt` from Task 1.\n- Produces: an `~/.agents/skills/` containing only skills Eric intends every harness to load. Task 3 symlinks onto this; Task 6 (Pi) reads it directly.\n\n**Why this task exists:** yesterday's archive script only moved things out of `~/.claude/skills/`. 98 of the 99 archived names are still present in `~/.agents/skills/`. Claude Code no longer sees them, but Pi discovers that folder natively, so the token win evaporates the moment Pi runs. This task makes the cut real everywhere.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Compute the prune list and eyeball it**\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12\ncomm -12 <(ls ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | sort) <(ls ~/.agents/skills | sort) \\\n  > /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\nwc -l < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\ncat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n```\n\nExpected: `98`, then 98 names. Read them. Any name Eric wants back gets deleted from the list file before Step 2. This is the one human gate in the task.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Guard against pruning anything a survivor symlinks to**\n\n```bash\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec readlink {} \\; \\\n  | sed 's|.*/||' | sort > /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt\ncomm -12 /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt\n```\n\nExpected: **no output.** If any name prints, a live Claude skill points at something scheduled for pruning. Remove that name from `/tmp/pool-prune-list.txt` and re-run until this prints nothing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Move, do not delete**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.agents/skills\nwhile IFS= read -r s; do\n  [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -e \"$s\" ] && mv \"$s\" ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/\ndone < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\nls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l\nls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l\n```\n\nExpected: pool drops from `306` to `208`; archive holds `98`.\n\n> Note: `306 - 98 = 208`, not 61. The pool legitimately holds more than the Claude roster — `_shared` support dirs, Codex-only skills, and skills never registered with Claude Code. Task 4's report reconciles the remainder. Do not \"fix\" 208 down to 61 here; that would delete Codex-only assets.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Verify nothing that was working broke**\n\n```bash\nfind -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print\n```\n\nExpected: `61`, then **no output** (no dangling symlinks). A dangling symlink here means Step 2's guard was skipped — roll back with the Task 1 tarball.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Write the record and commit**\n\n```bash\n{\n  echo \"pool prune 2026-09-12\"\n  echo \"before: 306  after: $(ls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l)  archived: $(ls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l)\"\n  echo \"undo: mv ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/* ~/.agents/skills/\"\n  echo \"--- pruned ---\"\n  cat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n} > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt && git commit -m \"chore: prune shared agent skill pool to survivors\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 3: Make `~/.agents/skills/` canonical, the other two views\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/` (42 real dirs become symlinks)\n- Modify: `~/.codex/skills/` (5 real dirs become symlinks)\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the pruned pool from Task 2.\n- Produces: exactly one editable copy of every skill, at `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`. Task 4 rewrites frontmatter there and all three harnesses see the change at once.\n\n**Why this task exists:** right now a skill can exist as a real dir in `~/.claude/skills/`, a real dir in `~/.codex/skills/`, and a separate dir in `~/.agents/skills/`. Three copies, no declared winner — the same shape as the hooks two-copies trap. Today they happen to be identical; that is luck, not design. One canonical home is what makes the skills harness-agnostic.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the relink script**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Make ~/.agents/skills the single canonical home for Eric's own skills.\n# ~/.claude/skills and ~/.codex/skills become symlink views onto it.\n# Idempotent: re-running reports \"already linked\" and changes nothing.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nPOOL=\"$HOME/.agents/skills\"\nDRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-1}\"\n\nrelink_view() {\n  local view=\"$1\"\n  [ -d \"$view\" ] || { echo \"skip: $view does not exist\"; return; }\n  local d name\n  for d in \"$view\"/*/; do\n    [ -d \"$d\" ] || continue\n    name=\"$(basename \"$d\")\"\n    case \"$name\" in .*) continue ;; esac          # leave .system alone\n    [ -L \"${d%/}\" ] && { echo \"ok   $view/$name (already a link)\"; continue; }\n    if [ ! -f \"$d/SKILL.md\" ]; then\n      echo \"WARN $view/$name has no SKILL.md - left alone\"; continue\n    fi\n    if [ -e \"$POOL/$name\" ]; then\n      if diff -rq \"$d\" \"$POOL/$name\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n        echo \"LINK $view/$name -> pool (identical)\"\n        [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ] && { rm -rf \"${d%/}\"; ln -s \"$POOL/$name\" \"${d%/}\"; }\n      else\n        echo \"SKIP $view/$name DIVERGED from pool - resolve by hand\"\n      fi\n    else\n      echo \"MOVE $view/$name -> pool, then link\"\n      [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ] && { mv \"${d%/}\" \"$POOL/$name\"; ln -s \"$POOL/$name\" \"${d%/}\"; }\n    fi\n  done\n}\n\nrelink_view \"$HOME/.claude/skills\"\nrelink_view \"$HOME/.codex/skills\"\necho\necho \"pool:   $(ls \"$POOL\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\necho \"claude: $(find -L \"$HOME/.claude/skills\" -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\necho \"codex:  $(find -L \"$HOME/.codex/skills\" -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\n[ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ] && echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Dry run and read every line**\n\n```bash\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh\n```\n\nExpected: ~42 `MOVE` lines under `.claude/skills`, ~5 `LINK`/`MOVE` lines under `.codex/skills`, ~24 `ok` lines, and the `(DRY RUN)` footer. Any `SKIP … DIVERGED` line must be resolved by hand before Step 3 — pick the newer file, copy it into the pool, delete the other. Any `WARN … no SKILL.md` is an orphan dir (expect `session-handoff`); `rm -rf` it by exact name.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Apply**\n\n```bash\nDRY_RUN=0 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh\n```\n\nExpected: the same lines without the DRY footer, then three counts.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Verify all three views resolve and nothing dangles**\n\n```bash\nfind -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print\nfind ~/.codex/skills  -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type d ! -path ~/.claude/skills | wc -l\n```\n\nExpected: `61`; no output; no output; `0` real dirs left under `.claude/skills`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Confirm Claude Code still loads the roster**\n\nRun `/reload-skills` in a Claude Code session, then `/skills`. Expected: the same skill count as before Task 2 (61 personal + plugin skills). If the count dropped, roll back with the Task 1 tarball.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add scripts/skills-relink.sh && git commit -m \"feat: one canonical skill pool, harness dirs become symlink views\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 4: Normalize every survivor to portable frontmatter\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`\n- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md` (frontmatter only, body untouched)\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the canonical pool from Task 3.\n- Produces: `skill-portability-check.py`, which Task 5's `aios-audit` skill calls as its harness-parity check. Its exit code is `0` when every skill passes, `1` otherwise.\n\n**The portable frontmatter contract** (superset — Codex ignores keys it does not know, Claude Code ignores `metadata`):\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: skill-name                      # REQUIRED. kebab-case, matches the dir name.\ndescription: >-                       # REQUIRED. <=1024 chars. Must contain BOTH:\n  <what it does in one sentence>.     #   (a) \"Use when ...\" + literal trigger phrases\n  Use when <triggers, quoted phrases>.#       the user would actually type\n  Do not use when <negative>.         #   (b) \"Do not use when ...\" / \"NOT FOR ...\"\nmetadata:\n  short-description: <one short line> # optional, Codex-only, ignored by Claude\nargument-hint: \"[...]\"                # optional, Claude-only, ignored by Codex\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep       # optional, Claude-only, ignored by Codex\n---\n```\n\nClaude Code triggers on literal phrases; Codex leans on the negative clause to avoid over-firing. A description with only one half fires reliably in one harness and badly in the other. That is the whole `/poly-skill` problem.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing check**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Check every skill in the canonical pool against the portable frontmatter\ncontract. Exit 0 if all pass, 1 if any fail. stdlib only (PEP 668 machine).\"\"\"\nimport os, re, sys, glob\n\nPOOL = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.agents/skills\")\nALLOWED = {\"name\", \"description\", \"metadata\", \"argument-hint\",\n           \"allowed-tools\", \"model\", \"disable-model-invocation\", \"license\",\n           \"version\", \"author\"}\n\ndef parse_frontmatter(text):\n    if not text.startswith(\"---\"):\n        return None\n    end = text.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n    if end == -1:\n        return None\n    return text[3:end]\n\ndef top_keys(fm):\n    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*):\", fm)]\n\ndef field(fm, key):\n    m = re.search(rf\"(?ms)^{re.escape(key)}:\\s*(.*?)(?=^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*:|\\Z)\", fm)\n    return m.group(1).strip().strip('\"').strip(\"'\") if m else \"\"\n\ndef check(path):\n    name = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path))\n    problems = []\n    text = open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read()\n    fm = parse_frontmatter(text)\n    if fm is None:\n        return name, [\"no YAML frontmatter\"]\n    keys = top_keys(fm)\n    if \"name\" not in keys:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'name'\")\n    elif field(fm, \"name\") != name:\n        problems.append(f\"name '{field(fm,'name')}' != dir '{name}'\")\n    desc = field(fm, \"description\")\n    if not desc:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'description'\")\n    else:\n        if len(desc) > 1024:\n            problems.append(f\"description {len(desc)} chars (>1024)\")\n        low = desc.lower()\n        if \"use when\" not in low and \"use for\" not in low:\n            problems.append(\"description has no 'Use when/for' trigger clause\")\n        if not any(p in low for p in (\"do not use\", \"not for\", \"don't use\")):\n            problems.append(\"description has no negative clause (Codex over-fires)\")\n    for k in keys:\n        if k not in ALLOWED:\n            problems.append(f\"unknown frontmatter key '{k}'\")\n    return name, problems\n\ndef main():\n    paths = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(POOL, \"*\", \"SKILL.md\")))\n    failed = 0\n    for p in paths:\n        name, probs = check(p)\n        if probs:\n            failed += 1\n            print(f\"FAIL {name}\")\n            for x in probs:\n                print(f\"       - {x}\")\n    print(f\"\\nchecked {len(paths)} skills, {failed} failing, {len(paths)-failed} portable\")\n    return 1 if failed else 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run it and confirm it fails**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: a long FAIL list and `exit=1`. Most failures will be \"no negative clause\" — the existing skills were written Claude-only. This baseline number goes in the report.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Fix frontmatter in priority order, highest-use first**\n\nDo **not** bulk-rewrite with sed. Each description is a trigger surface; a careless edit silently stops a skill firing. Work through the failures in this order, editing only the frontmatter block of `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`:\n\n1. The confirmed working set (3+ invocations): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `backup`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`, `codex-review`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, `doctor-plus`, `startup`, `new-project`, `status-report`.\n2. Everything else in the pool that Claude Code exposes.\n3. Codex-only skills last.\n\nFor each, add the missing half. Worked example — `backup` today has triggers but no negative clause:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: >-\n  Run the KeystoneOS daily backup on demand (git-push the memory repos, rsync\n  state to Google Drive and the CRASHBOX SSD, reindex memsearch), then report\n  what was saved and what was skipped. Use when the user says \"/backup\", \"run\n  the backup\", \"back up now\", \"did the backup run\", \"the backup failed\", or\n  when a session-start HOOK FAILURE names daily-backup. Do not use for\n  whole-stack health checks - use claude-stack-doctor instead.\n```\n\nRe-run the checker after each batch of five:\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py | tail -3\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Run it and confirm it passes**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: `checked N skills, 0 failing, N portable` and `exit=0`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Prove portability in both harnesses, not just the linter**\n\nPick three skills that were edited. In a fresh Claude Code session, type one trigger phrase for each and confirm the skill fires. In a fresh Codex session (`cx`), do the same. Record the six results in the report. A linter that passes while a skill stops firing is the failure mode this step exists to catch.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Write the report and commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n{\n  echo \"# Skill portability normalization - 2026-09-12\"\n  echo\n  echo \"Contract: name + description (Use when / Do not use when) are the Codex-safe\"\n  echo \"core. argument-hint and allowed-tools are Claude-only and ignored by Codex.\"\n  echo\n  echo '```'\n  python3 scripts/skill-portability-check.py\n  echo '```'\n} > docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit add scripts/skill-portability-check.py docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: portable skill frontmatter contract + checker\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 5: `rot.md` and the `aios-audit` compound skill\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/.claude/rot.md`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`\n- Modify: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md` (add a cross-reference line only)\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `skill-portability-check.py` (Task 4) and the canonical pool (Task 3).\n- Produces: `aios-metrics.py`, which prints a machine-readable metrics block that Task 6's launchd job captures into the trend table.\n\n**Placement is deliberate.** This skill is **project-level**, in `KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/`, not global — that is the promotion rule from the video (31:32), adopted yesterday. It gets promoted to `~/.agents/skills/` only after it proves useful across 2+ projects.\n\n**It must not become a sixth audit skill.** Five already exist and each owns a layer:\n\n| Existing skill | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `os-audit` (project) | project data: routing, index truth, freshness, duplication |\n| `brain-audit` | SecondBrain as a system — the four C's |\n| `improve-system` | note-level rot: stale seeds, duplicates, conflicts |\n| `claude-stack-doctor` | automation plumbing: launchd, venvs, memsearch, backup freshness |\n| `doctor-plus` | context-engineering posture of the workspace |\n\n`aios-audit` owns the one layer none of them touch — **the harness itself**: the skill roster, agents, hooks, rules, and cross-harness parity. It **delegates** the other five rather than re-implementing them. That is the video's \"merge overlapping into compound skills,\" applied honestly.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write `~/.claude/rot.md`**\n\n```markdown\n# rot.md — how fast each AIOS layer goes stale\n\nAudit each layer on its own clock, not all at once. `aios-audit` reads this\nfile: any layer past due is a RED finding.\n\n| Layer | Where it lives | Rots | Audit every | Last audited | Owner |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Identity | `global-memory/USER.md` | very slow | 3 months | 2026-09-12 | memory-write |\n| Substrate | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `~/DEV/*.md` routers | slow | 3 months | 2026-09-12 | os-audit |\n| Skills | `~/.agents/skills/` | fast | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit |\n| Agents | `~/.claude/agents/` | fast | 1 month | never (0 exist) | aios-audit |\n| Rules | `CLAUDE.md` behaviour rules, output styles | fastest | 1 week | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit |\n| Hooks | `~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/` | slow | 6 months | 2026-09-12 | claude-stack-doctor |\n| Memory | `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `global-memory/` | medium | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | improve-system |\n| Data feeds | Fireflies, GPS, CFBD, Zotero | varies | 1 week | 2026-09-12 | os-audit |\n\n## The question that governs a skill's fate\n\nNot \"how often did I run it?\" but **\"would the current model do this fine\nwithout the skill?\"** If yes, the skill is a crutch that now limits the model.\nDelete it. Keep only skills carrying tacit knowledge the model cannot know:\nforce-plate thresholds, GPS conventions, Baylor reporting norms, Eric's own\nroutines.\n\nSeasonal skills (`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`) are exempt from\nlow-use pressure. Judge them in season.\n\n## Direction of travel\n**Fewer skills, stricter rules.** Skills get deleted; rules get sharpened.\n```\n\nVerify: `cat ~/.claude/rot.md | head -20`\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Write the metrics script**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`. It must print exactly these lines, one `key=value` per line, and nothing else on stdout:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Machine-readable AIOS metrics. stdlib only. Prints key=value lines.\"\"\"\nimport os, re, glob, subprocess, collections, datetime, sys\n\nH = os.path.expanduser(\"~\")\nPOOL = f\"{H}/.agents/skills\"\nBUILTIN = {'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin',\n'doctor','help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp',\n'hooks','memory','review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir',\n'bug','release-notes','pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline',\n'output-style','todos','export','privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts',\n'tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind','sandbox','feedback','worktrees',\n'skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes','keybindings','theme'}\n\ndef count(pattern):\n    return len(glob.glob(pattern))\n\ndef usage():\n    c = collections.Counter()\n    for f in glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl\", recursive=True):\n        try:\n            for line in open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\"):\n                if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line:\n                    continue\n                for m in re.finditer(r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n                    c[m.group(1)] += 1\n                for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n                    c[m.group(1)] += 1\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return {k: v for k, v in c.items()\n            if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\n\ndef token_estimate():\n    \"\"\"Roster cost ~= sum of name+description chars / 4.\"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for p in glob.glob(f\"{POOL}/*/SKILL.md\"):\n        try:\n            t = open(p, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read()\n            end = t.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n            if t.startswith(\"---\") and end != -1:\n                total += len(t[3:end])\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return total // 4\n\nu = usage()\nprint(f\"date={datetime.date.today().isoformat()}\")\nprint(f\"pool_skills={count(POOL + '/*/SKILL.md')}\")\nprint(f\"claude_skills={len(glob.glob(H + '/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md')) + len([d for d in glob.glob(H + '/.claude/skills/*') if os.path.islink(d) and os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, 'SKILL.md'))])}\")\nprint(f\"codex_skills={len([d for d in glob.glob(H + '/.codex/skills/*') if os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, 'SKILL.md'))])}\")\nprint(f\"project_skills={count(H + '/DEV/*/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md')}\")\nprint(f\"agents={count(H + '/.claude/agents/*.md')}\")\nprint(f\"hooks_claude={count(H + '/.claude/hooks/*')}\")\nprint(f\"hooks_codex={count(H + '/.codex/hooks/*')}\")\nprint(f\"sessions={count(H + '/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl')}\")\nprint(f\"skills_ever_used={len(u)}\")\nprint(f\"skills_used_3plus={len([k for k,v in u.items() if v>=3])}\")\nprint(f\"skills_used_once={len([k for k,v in u.items() if v==1])}\")\nprint(f\"never_used={len([p for p in glob.glob(POOL + '/*/SKILL.md') if os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(p)) not in u])}\")\nprint(f\"roster_tokens_est={token_estimate()}\")\nrc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, f\"{H}/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\"],\n                    capture_output=True, text=True)\nfails = re.search(r\"(\\d+) failing\", rc.stdout)\nprint(f\"portability_failures={fails.group(1) if fails else 'unknown'}\")\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Run it and confirm the shape**\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n```\n\nExpected: 16 `key=value` lines, no stack trace. `pool_skills` should equal Task 2's post-prune count. `project_skills` should be `2` (`os-audit` + `aios-audit`). If any value reads `0` where it should not, fix the glob before continuing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Write the `aios-audit` skill**\n\nInvoke `CreateSkill` first — it owns skill authoring in this workspace. Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` with this frontmatter (note it satisfies the Task 4 contract):\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: aios-audit\ndescription: >-\n  Audit the whole AI operating system across decay tiers - skills, agents,\n  hooks, rules, memory, and cross-harness parity between Claude Code, Codex,\n  and Pi - then propose exactly two next builds. Use when the user says\n  \"/aios-audit\", \"audit my system\", \"audit my AI OS\", \"audit the bloat\",\n  \"is my setup rotting\", \"what should I cut\", \"are my skills portable\",\n  \"monthly skill audit\", or when rot.md shows a layer past its audit date.\n  Do not use for project data drift, routing, or index freshness - use\n  os-audit. Do not use for launchd, venv, or backup health - use\n  claude-stack-doctor. Do not use for SecondBrain note rot - use\n  improve-system or brain-audit.\nmetadata:\n  short-description: Decay-tier audit of the AI OS; delegates to the five layer-specific audit skills.\nargument-hint: \"[optional: a layer name from rot.md]\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Agent\n---\n```\n\nThe body must specify, in this order:\n\n1. **Read-only.** The only writes are the report and the `Last audited` column in `rot.md`. Never delete a skill during the audit — propose, then wait for approval.\n2. **Step 0 — read `~/.claude/rot.md`.** Compute days since each layer's `Last audited`. Any layer past due is RED and gets audited this run; layers inside their window are reported as green and skipped. This is what makes the audit cheap enough to run monthly.\n3. **Step 1 — run `python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`** and capture every value.\n4. **Step 2 — delegate, do not duplicate.** For each due layer, invoke the owning skill from the `rot.md` table (`os-audit`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `improve-system`, `brain-audit`) and fold its findings in under that layer's heading. Only the harness layer is audited by `aios-audit` itself.\n5. **Step 3 — the harness layer, audited directly:**\n   - **Bloat:** every skill in the pool never invoked across all sessions. List them.\n   - **Crutch test:** for each never-used and once-used skill, answer the rot.md question — *would the current model do this fine without it?* Recommend DELETE, KEEP (tacit knowledge), or SEASONAL with one line of reasoning each.\n   - **Overlap:** skills whose descriptions cover the same trigger space. Recommend merging into one compound skill.\n   - **Parity:** run `skill-portability-check.py`. Every failure is a finding.\n   - **Promotion:** global skills used in fewer than 2 projects — candidates for demotion to project-level. Project skills used in 2+ projects — candidates for promotion.\n   - **Canonical drift:** any real directory (not a symlink) under `~/.claude/skills/` or `~/.codex/skills/`. That means someone edited a view instead of the pool.\n6. **Step 4 — write the report** to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, append the metrics line to the trend table at the top of `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`, and update `Last audited` in `rot.md` for every layer audited.\n7. **Step 5 — end with exactly two proposed next builds,** ranked, each with the one command that starts it. Not a list of ten. Two.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Cross-reference `os-audit` so the two do not fight**\n\nAdd one line near the top of `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md`:\n\n```markdown\n> Scope note: this skill audits **project data** — routing, indexes, freshness,\n> duplication. The harness layer (skills, agents, hooks, rules, cross-harness\n> parity) belongs to `aios-audit`, which calls this skill for the data layer.\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Smoke-test the skill end to end**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n```\n\nIn a Claude Code session with cwd `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`, run `/reload-skills`, then type \"audit my AI OS\". Expected: `aios-audit` fires, reads `rot.md`, runs the metrics script, and produces a report file. Confirm the report exists:\n\n```bash\nls -l ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-*.md\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ngit add .claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md .claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md scripts/aios-metrics.py docs/audits/\ngit commit -m \"feat: aios-audit compound skill + rot.md decay tiers\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ncp ~/.claude/rot.md ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/rot.md 2>/dev/null && git add docs/rot.md && git commit -m \"docs: track rot.md in the repo\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 6: Put the audit on a monthly clock\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/rot.md` (add the cadence line)\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `aios-metrics.py` from Task 5.\n- Produces: a monthly trend row in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`. Nothing consumes this; it is the terminal deliverable.\n\n**Design choice:** the cron job runs the **metrics script only** and appends a trend row plus a nudge. It does not run the full agentic audit unattended — an agent that proposes deletions with nobody reading the proposal is how a system gets quietly broken. The job's job is to make the audit impossible to forget.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the cron wrapper**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Monthly: append AIOS metrics to the trend table and nudge for a real audit.\n# Does NOT run the agentic audit - that needs a human reading the output.\nset -uo pipefail\nREPO=\"$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS\"\nTREND=\"$REPO/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nLOG=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$LOG\")\" \"$(dirname \"$TREND\")\"\n\nexec >>\"$LOG\" 2>&1\necho \"=== $(date -u +%FT%TZ) aios-audit-cron ===\"\n\nM=\"$(python3 \"$REPO/scripts/aios-metrics.py\")\" || { echo \"metrics FAILED\"; exit 1; }\necho \"$M\"\n\nget() { echo \"$M\" | grep \"^$1=\" | cut -d= -f2; }\n\nif [ ! -f \"$TREND\" ]; then\n  {\n    echo \"# AIOS trend\"\n    echo\n    echo \"One row per monthly run. Written by scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh.\"\n    echo\n    echo \"| date | pool | claude | codex | project | never used | roster tok | portability fails |\"\n    echo \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"\n  } > \"$TREND\"\nfi\n\nprintf '| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |\\n' \\\n  \"$(get date)\" \"$(get pool_skills)\" \"$(get claude_skills)\" \"$(get codex_skills)\" \\\n  \"$(get project_skills)\" \"$(get never_used)\" \"$(get roster_tokens_est)\" \\\n  \"$(get portability_failures)\" >> \"$TREND\"\n\necho \"[$(date +%F)] aios-audit due - run \\\"audit my AI OS\\\" in ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\" \\\n  >> \"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\"\n\ncd \"$REPO\" && git add docs/audits/aios-trend.md \\\n  && git commit -m \"chore: monthly AIOS metrics $(get date)\" \\\n  && git push 2>&1 | tail -2\necho \"=== done ===\"\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run it by hand and verify the trend row**\n\n```bash\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh\ncat ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\ntail -20 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\n```\n\nExpected: a header plus one data row with no empty cells. Empty cells mean a `get` key does not match a metrics key — fix the key name, delete the bad row, re-run.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Write the launchd plist**\n\nCreate `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`. Day 1 at 09:30 — after the 09:00 daily backup, so it never races it:\n\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\"\n  \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n<plist version=\"1.0\">\n<dict>\n  <key>Label</key><string>com.keystone.aiosaudit</string>\n  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n  <array>\n    <string>/bin/bash</string>\n    <string>-lc</string>\n    <string>$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh</string>\n  </array>\n  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>Day</key><integer>1</integer>\n    <key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer>\n    <key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err</string>\n</dict>\n</plist>\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Load it and verify launchd accepted it**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist 2>/dev/null\nlaunchctl load  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist\nlaunchctl list | grep aiosaudit\n```\n\nExpected: one line ending `com.keystone.aiosaudit`. A non-zero middle column means the last run failed — check `/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Force a run through launchd, not just bash**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl start com.keystone.aiosaudit\ncat /tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err\ntail -5 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n```\n\nExpected: `.err` empty, a second trend row appended. A launchd run has a different PATH than an interactive shell — this step is what catches that. If `python3` is not found, use an absolute interpreter path in `aios-metrics.py`'s shebang.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Remove the duplicate test row, note the cadence, commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\npython3 - <<'EOF'\nimport re\np=\"docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nlines=open(p).read().splitlines()\nseen=set(); out=[]\nfor l in lines:\n    if l.startswith(\"| 20\"):\n        if l in seen: continue\n        seen.add(l)\n    out.append(l)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(out)+\"\\n\")\nEOF\ncat docs/audits/aios-trend.md\nprintf '\\n## Cadence\\nMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 09:30.\\nThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.\\n' >> ~/.claude/rot.md\ncp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md\ngit add scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh docs/audits/aios-trend.md docs/rot.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: monthly AIOS metrics on launchd\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 7: Pi sandbox trial and one hook port\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/pi-trial/` (throwaway, never `~/DEV`)\n- Create: `~/pi-trial/AGENTS.md`\n- Create: `~/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the canonical pool from Task 3 (Pi auto-discovers `~/.agents/skills/`), and the portable frontmatter from Task 4.\n- Produces: a measured migration cost in `2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`. Nothing consumes it; it is a decision input.\n\n**This task is last for a reason.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — that is documented, deliberate design, not an oversight. Running Pi before Task 2 prunes the pool would expose all 306 skills. Running it anywhere near `~/DEV` or `DexaProject` risks unprompted writes to real athlete data.\n\n**Stop conditions.** If Pi cannot be installed, or the guard extension does not block after two attempts, stop and write up what failed. Do not iterate past that — the deliverable here is a cost measurement, not a working Pi setup.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Install into a throwaway directory**\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/pi-trial && cd ~/pi-trial\nnpm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\nwhich -a pi && pi --version\n```\n\nExpected: a path under `/opt/homebrew/` and a version string. `--ignore-scripts` is deliberate: no postinstall scripts run.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Confirm what Pi actually discovers**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/pi-trial\ncat > AGENTS.md <<'EOF'\n# pi-trial\n\nThrowaway sandbox for evaluating the Pi harness. Nothing here is real work.\n\nHARD RULES\n- Never read or write outside ~/pi-trial.\n- Never touch ~/DEV, ~/SecondBrain, or any Google Drive path.\n- Never touch anything named DexaProject.\nEOF\npi --print \"List every skill you can see. Print only the count and the first ten names.\" 2>&1 | tail -20\n```\n\nExpected: a count close to Task 2's post-prune `pool_skills`. **If the count is near 306, Task 2 did not take — stop and re-run it.** This step is the proof that pruning the pool was the right fix.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Verify Pi has no guard, so the port is necessary**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/pi-trial\npi --print \"Run: ls ~/DEV | head -3\" 2>&1 | tail -10\n```\n\nExpected: it lists `~/DEV` with no approval prompt, despite `AGENTS.md` saying not to. That is the finding — context files are guidance, not enforcement. Record the exact output. Nothing was written, so no harm; do not escalate to a write test.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Port `dcg` to a Pi extension**\n\nRead the existing guard first:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.claude/hooks/ | grep -i -E 'dcg|guard'\n```\n\nCreate `~/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`. Pi's `tool_call` event returns `{block, reason}`, which maps directly onto the hook's deny decision:\n\n```typescript\n// Minimal port of the dcg guard. Proves the hook -> extension mapping.\nconst DENY = [\n  /\\brm\\s+-rf\\s+\\/(?!tmp)/,\n  /DexaProject/i,\n  /GoogleDrive-/,\n  /\\/DEV\\//,\n  /\\/SecondBrain\\//,\n];\n\nexport default {\n  name: \"guard\",\n  async tool_call(ev: { tool: string; args: Record<string, unknown> }) {\n    const blob = JSON.stringify(ev.args ?? {});\n    for (const re of DENY) {\n      if (re.test(blob)) {\n        return { block: true, reason: `guard: blocked by ${re}` };\n      }\n    }\n    return { block: false };\n  },\n};\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Prove the guard blocks**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/pi-trial\npi --print \"Run: ls ~/DEV | head -3\" 2>&1 | tail -10\npi --print \"Run: echo hello\" 2>&1 | tail -5\n```\n\nExpected: the first is **blocked** with the guard reason. The second **succeeds** — a guard that blocks everything is not a guard. If the first is not blocked after two attempts at the extension path or event name, stop per the stop conditions.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Write the cost measurement and commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n{\n  echo \"# Pi trial - 2026-09-12\"\n  echo\n  echo \"Sandbox: ~/pi-trial (never ~/DEV, never DexaProject).\"\n  echo\n  echo \"| Question | Answer |\"\n  echo \"|---|---|\"\n  echo \"| pi version | FILL IN |\"\n  echo \"| skills auto-discovered | FILL IN (expect pool count, not 306) |\"\n  echo \"| skills needing edits to fire in Pi | FILL IN |\"\n  echo \"| AGENTS.md honoured without an extension? | FILL IN (expect: no) |\"\n  echo \"| minutes to port the dcg guard | FILL IN |\"\n  echo \"| hooks still to port | FILL IN of 61 |\"\n  echo \"| verdict | ADOPT / WAIT / DROP |\"\n  echo\n  echo \"## What the guard proved\"\n  echo \"FILL IN: paste the blocked and allowed outputs from Step 5.\"\n} > docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md\n```\n\nFill in every `FILL IN` from the real runs above — a report with placeholders left in is a failed task. Then:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md && git commit -m \"docs: Pi harness sandbox trial + dcg guard port cost\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Decide, then clean up or keep**\n\nIf the verdict is DROP or WAIT:\n\n```bash\nnpm uninstall -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\nrm -rf ~/pi-trial\n```\n\nIf ADOPT, leave `~/pi-trial` in place and write the next plan. Do not widen Pi's reach beyond the sandbox in this plan.\n\n---\n\n## Review Gates (not optional)\n\nEric asked for Codex review of both the plan and the code. These are steps, not suggestions.\n\n- [ ] **Gate A — before Task 1.** Run `claudex-loop` in review mode on this plan file. Feed Codex the plan plus the two Inbox source notes. Fold its findings back in and re-save before any task executes.\n- [ ] **Gate B — after Task 4.** Run `codex-review` on `skills-relink.sh`, `skill-portability-check.py`, and a sample of ten rewritten SKILL.md frontmatter blocks. Codex is the right reviewer here specifically because it is the harness whose triggering the rewrite claims to fix.\n- [ ] **Gate C — after Task 6.** Run `codex-review` on `aios-metrics.py`, `aios-audit-cron.sh`, the plist, and the `aios-audit` SKILL.md body.\n- [ ] **Gate D — before declaring done.** Run `superpowers:verification-before-completion`. Every success claim needs a pasted command output.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Rewriting plugin skills (`superpowers`, `mattpocock-skills`) — vendored, overwritten on update.\n- Migrating all 61 hooks to Pi. Task 7 ports exactly one to measure cost.\n- Deleting any skill outright. Everything moves to an archive folder with a recorded undo.\n- The Windows machine. Both machines are active; this plan changes Mac paths only. Re-run Tasks 2–4 there separately after they prove out here.\n- Mining the 414 JSONL sessions to reverse-engineer harness behaviour (video gap #9). Real, but its own project.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b22a0d1f-26d8-4408-9707-a6f3ce1f3c99","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
