{"parentUuid":"7cd7d892-1758-49bd-9c4f-26f13d82b7c7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"06e7d3ba-222f-4650-8a25-521650a48509","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YALbgMYAyGJc6B8kfn8xJT","type":"tool_result","content":"status: completed exit: 0 provider: codex roles: {'host': 'claude', 'planner': 'claude', 'reviewer': 'codex', 'builder': 'claude', 'inspector': 'codex'}\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: The plan contains material migration, data-loss, guard, and audit-correctness defects. The on-disk plan matches the supplied SHA256.\n\n[HIGH] R1\n  where: ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh\n  E: Task 3 leaves this existing writer unchanged. dev-sync instructs users to run it after pulling skills. It deletes ~/.agents/skills/<name> before copying from ~/.claude/skills/<name>. After relinking, that source points to the directory just deleted; running dev-sync destroys canonical copies of shared skills. The PowerShell twin has the same delete-then-copy design.\n  F: Update both sync scripts and their callers before relinking. Make synchronization create validated views from the canonical pool without deleting source targets.\n\n[HIGH] R2\n  where: Task 3, Step 2\n  E: The plan classifies every directory without SKILL.md as an orphan and instructs deletion. ~/.claude/skills/_shared is a real support directory containing config.json and Inference assets. meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py and audit_filenames.py resolve their configuration through parents[2]/_shared/config.json; other surviving skills reference its existing absolute path.\n  F: Inventory and migrate support directories explicitly, preserve their existing view paths, and verify dependent scripts resolve the intended configuration. Never infer orphan status solely from missing SKILL.md.\n\n[HIGH] R3\n  where: ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1\n  E: Contrary to the plan's safety-net premise, ~/.claude/skills has its own .git directory. daily-backup.ps1 calls Sync-Repo on it and uses git add -A. Relinking replaces tracked content with absolute Mac symlinks that the backup can publish to the shared skills repository. The new canonical pool has no .git directory and is absent from the inspected backup manifest, so subsequent canonical edits lose the existing persistence path while Windows can receive unusable symlinks.\n  F: Define canonical versioning, backup, and restore behavior before migration. Update backup and dev-sync consumers together, prevent publication of machine-specific views, and preserve Windows-compatible repository contents.\n\n[HIGH] R4\n  where: Task 7, Steps 4–5\n  E: The supplied Pi research specifies an exported factory receiving ExtensionAPI, pi.on('tool_call', ...), and event.input. The plan instead exports an object with a tool_call method and reads ev.args. This does not implement the documented registration or payload contract. Independently, /\\/DEV\\// does not match the exact proposed probe 'ls ~/DEV | head -3', because DEV has no trailing slash.\n  F: Use the documented extension factory and event.input, explicitly establish project-extension trust for print mode, and ensure the exact blocked probe reaches and matches the handler.\n\n[HIGH] R5\n  where: Task 7\n  E: Changing cwd to ~/pi-trial provides no filesystem isolation, as the source note explicitly explains. The plan launches unrestricted Pi before installing a guard, then treats a small denylist as sufficient for an ADOPT verdict. Commands such as 'cat ../DEV/file' or shell-generated paths bypass that list. All processes retain access to the protected host data.\n  F: Run the trial under an enforced filesystem boundary with only disposable fixtures and required skill copies mounted. Measure the hook port inside that boundary; do not characterize cwd or a regex denylist as a sandbox.\n\n[MEDIUM] R6\n  where: Task 5, aios-metrics.py\n  E: claude_skills counts globbed SKILL.md files and then adds symlink directories containing the same files. Python glob already traverses those symlink directories, so the expected 61 linked skills become 122. Also, count() omits recursive=True for the sessions '**' pattern, while usage() uses recursive traversal; nested sessions therefore have inconsistent coverage. The expected pool count incorrectly equates top-level entries with SKILL.md files and ignores Task 3 additions.\n  F: Count each resolved skill once, explicitly define session traversal, and derive expected counts from the post-migration inventory rather than Task 2 directory totals.\n\n[MEDIUM] R7\n  where: Task 4, skill-portability-check.py\n  E: The checker does not parse YAML or enforce the stated kebab-case name contract. It accepts uppercase directory/name pairs such as CreateSkill, and a description containing only 'Do not use when...' also satisfies its positive-clause substring check. An absent or empty pool returns success with zero checked skills. Thus exit=0 cannot establish the claimed portable contract.\n  F: Validate an explicitly supported YAML subset or use an approved YAML parser, enforce name constraints and distinct positive/negative clauses, and fail on missing or unexpectedly empty inventory. Resolve uppercase-name compatibility and references explicitly.\n\n[MEDIUM] R8\n  where: Task 3, skills-relink.sh\n  E: The script ends with '[ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ] && echo ...'. In apply mode that expression returns 1, making a successful migration report failure. In dry mode, a final MOVE/LINK branch can also leave relink_view returning 1 under set -e and stop execution before the second view and summary.\n  F: Use explicit if statements for optional actions and explicit successful returns from relink_view and the script. Reserve nonzero exits for actual migration failures.\n\n[HIGH] R9\n  where: Task 6, aios-audit-cron.sh\n  E: The unattended wrapper commits using the shared working tree's index. If the user already staged unrelated files, git commit includes those files and git push publishes them. Git failures are then masked by the final successful echo because the script lacks failure propagation. Scheduling at 09:30 also does not exclude a backup race: data/jobs.json permits daily-backup to run for 240 minutes.\n  F: Avoid unattended commits in the user's working tree, or use an isolated checkout and scoped publication. Add locking, explicit failure propagation, and a durable completion record; do not rely on start-time spacing.\n\n[MEDIUM] R10\n  where: Task 5, audit workflow\n  E: The harness owns Agents and Rules in rot.md, but the direct audit only specifies skill checks and a directory count; it supplies no actual rules or agent audit. Nevertheless Step 4 updates Last audited for audited layers. brain-audit and doctor-plus are advertised as delegates but have no ownership row that schedules them. Initializing all layers as audited today also skips work without corresponding evidence.\n  F: Define concrete checks and delegation for each layer, initialize unknown audit dates as unknown, and advance dates only after the relevant checks complete successfully.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"7e4a6590-a58e-4910-8ec4-81f4f465433a","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:11:59.389Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"status: completed exit: 0 provider: codex roles: {'host': 'claude', 'planner': 'claude', 'reviewer': 'codex', 'builder': 'claude', 'inspector': 'codex'}\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: The plan contains material migration, data-loss, guard, and audit-correctness defects. The on-disk plan matches the supplied SHA256.\n\n[HIGH] R1\n  where: ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh\n  E: Task 3 leaves this existing writer unchanged. dev-sync instructs users to run it after pulling skills. It deletes ~/.agents/skills/<name> before copying from ~/.claude/skills/<name>. After relinking, that source points to the directory just deleted; running dev-sync destroys canonical copies of shared skills. The PowerShell twin has the same delete-then-copy design.\n  F: Update both sync scripts and their callers befor