{"parentUuid":"ebadc5aa-f688-4a48-ac82-29474e06d3ae","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeyvMqgYJzJnKngrGKkZp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three facts confirmed, and they flip the plan's direction. Rewriting it now with all 10 findings addressed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3584,"cache_read_input_tokens":128804,"output_tokens":32021,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":367},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3584,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":32021,"cache_read_input_tokens":128804,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3584,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3584},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CeyvMqKxKPVd2JfEpZXiR","attributionSkill":"codex-review","type":"assistant","uuid":"c207524b-e72a-48dc-afa0-2986fddd0f8d","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:14:05.414Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"c207524b-e72a-48dc-afa0-2986fddd0f8d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeyvMqgYJzJnKngrGKkZp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WknGox2XP7r8yjC6GopD7D","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","content":"# Harness-Agnostic AIOS Implementation Plan\n\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n\n**Goal:** Make every skill Eric owns load identically in Claude Code, Codex, and Pi from one versioned canonical folder, and build a recurring audit that keeps the whole AIOS from rotting.\n\n**Architecture:** `~/.claude/skills/` is the canonical home — it is already its own git repo (`erash11/claude-skills`) and is the only copy that versions and crosses to Windows. It holds **real directories only; no symlink is ever committed.** `~/.agents/skills/` (Pi, Cowork) and `~/.codex/skills/` (Codex) become **generated views**, rebuilt per machine by a hardened `sync-to-codex.sh` — symlinks on Mac, copies on Windows, never authored by hand. Every skill's frontmatter is normalized to a Codex-safe core so the same file fires in all three harnesses. Then one compound audit skill (`aios-audit`) orchestrates the five existing audit skills, driven by a `rot.md` decay table.\n\n**Tech Stack:** bash, PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, for the `.ps1` twin), python3 (stdlib only), launchd, Agent Skills standard (SKILL.md + YAML frontmatter), Claude Code, Codex CLI 0.154.0, Pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`).\n\n**Spec:** `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md` (gap analysis rows 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10) and `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`.\n\n**Review history:** Round 1 Codex review returned **REVISE** with 6 high / 4 medium findings (R1–R10). Result: `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json`. All ten are dispositioned in the table below. **The architecture above is the inverse of round 1's** — round 1 made `~/.agents/skills/` canonical, which R1/R3 showed would cause data loss.\n\n---\n\n## Global Constraints\n\n- **`~/.claude/skills/` is its own git repo** — `origin https://github.com/erash11/claude-skills.git`, currently in sync with origin. It is *gitignored by the `dotclaude` repo*, which is what the earlier handoff meant; that is not the same as unversioned. **This repo is the safety net and the Mac↔Windows bridge.**\n- **Never commit a symlink into `~/.claude/skills/`.** Windows pulls this repo. A committed absolute Mac symlink arrives there as an unusable file.\n- **Never point Pi at `~/DEV`.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — documented, deliberate design.\n- **Never let any harness near `DexaProject`** (identifiable athlete health data, on Google Drive, not in `~/DEV`).\n- **Plugin skills are out of scope.** `superpowers` (14) and `mattpocock-skills` (35) are vendored and overwritten on update. Scope = the 61 skills in `~/.claude/skills/`.\n- **Hooks exist in two copies** (`~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/`). So do the sync scripts (`.sh` and `.ps1`). Any change patches both.\n- Global `MEMORY.md` is at 2,489 / 2,500 chars. Consolidate before adding anything.\n- `timeout` is NOT installed on this Mac (no GNU coreutils). Do not use it.\n- `dcg` blocks `find ... -delete`. Use an explicit `rm` on a named path.\n- Python is PEP 668-managed. `python3` with stdlib only, or `uv`. Never global `pip install`.\n- **No success claim without a pasted command output.**\n\n### Measured baseline (2026-09-12, verified on disk this session)\n\n| Fact | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `~/.claude/skills/` SKILL.md count (`find -L`) | 61 |\n| Of those, symlinks pointing **out** to `~/.agents/skills/` | 19 |\n| `~/.claude/skills/` git remote | `erash11/claude-skills`, in sync with origin |\n| Latest commit | `3ab4467 chore: apply global skill audit` — the 99 deletions **are committed and pushed** |\n| `~/.agents/skills/` top-level entries | 306 |\n| Pool entries **not** present in `~/.claude/skills/` | 248 |\n| Archived yesterday to `~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/` | 99 |\n| Archived skills still live in `~/.agents/skills/` | 98 |\n| `~/.codex/skills/` entries | 32 (27 symlinks, 5 real dirs) |\n| Real dirs in `~/.codex/skills/` diverged from Claude copy | 0 (`dev-sync`, `closeout`, `new-project`, `startup` identical; `session-handoff` is an empty orphan) |\n| `~/.claude/skills/_shared/` | **load-bearing** — `config.json` + `Inference/`, referenced by 10+ skills |\n| Project-level skills in `~/DEV` | 1 (`KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit`) |\n| `~/.claude/rot.md` | does not exist |\n\n**The two lead findings:**\n\n1. **The pool is a stale mirror.** Yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` (and committed the cut) but never touched `~/.agents/skills/`. 98 of the 99 archived names are still there, plus 248 entries Claude Code never registered. Pi auto-discovers that folder. Installing Pi today re-exposes everything. Task 2 fixes this and must land before Task 7.\n2. **Three writers already target these folders** and none of them know about the plan: `sync-to-codex.sh`, its `.ps1` twin, and `daily-backup.ps1` (which runs `git add -A` on `~/.claude/skills`). Task 3 fixes the writers *before* moving anything. Round 1 skipped this and would have destroyed canonical copies.\n\n### Codex frontmatter ground truth (read from `~/.codex/skills/.system/*/SKILL.md`)\n\nCodex's own shipped skills use only:\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: \"skill-name\"\ndescription: \"What it does. Use when <positive triggers>. Do not use when <negative>.\"\nmetadata:\n  short-description: \"One short line.\"   # optional\n---\n```\n\nCodex reads **no** `argument-hint`, `allowed-tools`, `model`, or `disable-model-invocation`; it tolerates unknown keys. Codex descriptions carry an explicit **\"Do not use when…\"** clause; Claude Code descriptions carry **literal trigger phrases** the user would type. A portable description needs both halves. That is the whole `/poly-skill` idea, reduced to a rule.\n\n### Round 1 findings — disposition\n\n| ID | Sev | Codex's finding | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | high | `sync-to-codex.sh` does `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\"`. After relinking, `$SRC` points *at* `$dst` — the script deletes the canonical copy. | **ACCEPTED.** Verified on disk. Architecture inverted so `$SRC` is always real, plus a refuse-if-symlink guard. New **Task 3**, which now runs *before* any move. |\n| R2 | high | `_shared/` has no SKILL.md but holds `config.json` + `Inference/`, resolved by 10+ skills via `parents[2]/_shared/config.json`. Round 1 told a human to `rm -rf` any dir without a SKILL.md. | **ACCEPTED.** Verified. The blanket rm instruction is deleted. `_shared` stays in the canonical repo where it already resolves correctly. Task 4 Step 5 verifies `meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py` still resolves. |\n| R3 | high | `~/.claude/skills` has its own `.git`; `daily-backup.ps1` runs `git add -A` on it. Relinking would commit Mac symlinks to a repo Windows pulls. | **ACCEPTED, and it inverted the architecture.** Verified: remote is `erash11/claude-skills`. Canonical is now the git repo; views are generated and never committed. |\n| R4 | high | The Pi extension uses `export default {tool_call}` + `ev.args`; the research note documents an exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`, `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, `event.input`. Also `/\\/DEV\\//` never matches `ls ~/DEV`. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 7 rewritten to the documented contract; regex fixed to `/(^|\\s)~?\\/?DEV(\\/|$|\\s)/`. |\n| R5 | high | `cd ~/pi-trial` is not isolation. Round 1 launched unguarded Pi at real dirs, then treated a regex denylist as sufficient for ADOPT. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 7 runs Pi as a **separate macOS standard user** with no read access to Eric's home. The unguarded `ls ~/DEV` probe is deleted. ADOPT is off the table in this plan; the deliverable is a cost number only. |\n| R6 | med | `claude_skills` double-counts (glob already traverses symlinked dirs); `count()` omits `recursive=True` for sessions. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 5 dedupes by `os.path.realpath`; session glob made explicitly recursive. |\n| R7 | med | Checker does not parse YAML, accepts TitleCase against a kebab-case claim, a negative-only description passes the positive check, and an empty pool exits 0. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 4 enforces distinct positive and negative clauses, fails on an empty inventory, and **decides the naming question explicitly: TitleCase is allowed** (`CreateSkill`, `Harvest` ship that way) — the rule is \"matches the directory name,\" not \"is kebab-case.\" |\n| R8 | med | `[ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ] && echo` returns 1 in apply mode under `set -e`, reporting failure on success. | **ACCEPTED.** All optional actions use explicit `if`; functions `return 0`. |\n| R9 | high | The cron wrapper commits from the shared working tree — a staged unrelated file gets published — and masks git failures. 09:30 does not dodge a backup that may run 240 minutes. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 6's cron **no longer commits or pushes at all.** It writes the trend row and a nudge; the human commits during the audit. That removes the leak, the race, and the masked failure together. |\n| R10 | med | Agents and Rules have no actual check, yet `Last audited` is stamped for them; `brain-audit`/`doctor-plus` are named as delegates with no ownership row. | **ACCEPTED.** `rot.md` initializes every layer to `unknown` except Skills (genuinely audited 2026-09-12). Each layer gets a concrete check or an explicit \"no check yet.\" Dates advance only after that layer's check runs. |\n\n---\n\n### Task 1: Snapshot and freeze the current state\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: nothing.\n- Produces: the rollback path for Tasks 2–4, and `pre-canonical-inventory.txt`, the baseline later tasks diff against.\n\n**Note on undo, corrected after round 1:** there are now **two** undo paths. The tarball below covers the un-versioned folders (`~/.agents/skills`, `~/.codex/skills`). For `~/.claude/skills` the real undo is git — `3ab4467` already committed and pushed the 99 deletions, so `git -C ~/.claude/skills revert 3ab4467` restores them on both machines. The `skills-archive/` folder is *not* in that repo and does not reach Windows.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the inventory**\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits\n{\n  echo \"=== generated $(date -u +%FT%TZ) ===\"\n  echo \"--- claude skills repo state ---\"\n  git -C ~/.claude/skills remote -v\n  git -C ~/.claude/skills log --oneline -3\n  git -C ~/.claude/skills status -sb\n  echo \"--- claude skills (find -L, SKILL.md) ---\"\n  find -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | sed 's|.*/skills/||;s|/SKILL.md||' | sort\n  echo \"--- claude skills that are symlinks (to be inverted in Task 4) ---\"\n  find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec basename {} \\; | sort\n  echo \"--- support dirs in claude skills (NO SKILL.md, DO NOT DELETE) ---\"\n  for d in ~/.claude/skills/*/; do [ -f \"$d/SKILL.md\" ] || basename \"$d\"; done | sort\n  echo \"--- agents pool ---\"\n  ls ~/.agents/skills | sort\n  echo \"--- codex skills ---\"\n  ls -la ~/.codex/skills\n  echo \"--- archived 2026-09-12 ---\"\n  ls ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | sort\n} > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\nwc -l < ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\n```\n\nExpected: roughly 520–540 lines. **Read the \"support dirs\" section** — it must list `_shared`. That list is the do-not-delete set for every later task.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Take the tarball**\n\n```bash\ntar czf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz \\\n  -C ~ .claude/skills .codex/skills .agents/skills\nls -lh ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz\n```\n\nExpected: a non-zero-size file. `tar` may print `Removing leading '/'` — harmless.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Verify the tarball actually restores**\n\n```bash\nrm -rf /tmp/tartest && mkdir -p /tmp/tartest\ntar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C /tmp/tartest\nfind -L /tmp/tartest/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\nls /tmp/tartest/.agents/skills | wc -l\ntest -f /tmp/tartest/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared OK\"\n```\n\nExpected: `61`, `306`, `_shared OK`. If any is wrong, STOP — the snapshot is not a valid undo and no later task may run.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Record both rollback paths**\n\n```bash\ncat >> ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt <<'EOF'\n\n=== ROLLBACK ===\n# Un-versioned folders (undoes Tasks 2-4 view changes):\nrm -rf ~/.codex/skills ~/.agents/skills\ntar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C ~\n\n# Canonical repo (undoes anything committed to erash11/claude-skills):\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills log --oneline\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills revert <sha>    # reaches Windows on next dev-sync\nEOF\ntail -12 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\n```\n\nExpected: the rollback block prints.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ngit add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md\ngit commit -m \"docs: pre-canonical skill inventory + harness-agnostic plan\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 2: Prune the stale pool mirror\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (move stale dirs out)\n- Create: `~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt` from Task 1.\n- Produces: an `~/.agents/skills/` free of skills the audit already cut. Task 7 (Pi) reads this directly.\n\n**Why this task exists:** yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` and committed it. It never touched `~/.agents/skills/`, where 98 of the 99 archived names still live. Pi discovers that folder natively, so the token win evaporates the moment Pi runs.\n\n**Scope limit, added after round 1.** 248 pool entries are absent from `~/.claude/skills/`. The pool has a **mixed role**: a view onto Eric's own skills *and* an install target for third-party skills (`npx skills add` — see the `vendored-skill-install` memory). This task prunes **only** the 98 that exactly match yesterday's archive list. It does not touch the other 150, which may be third-party installs other harnesses depend on. Those are `aios-audit`'s problem, with a human reading the proposal.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Compute the prune list and eyeball it**\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12\ncomm -12 <(ls ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | sort) <(ls ~/.agents/skills | sort) \\\n  > /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\nwc -l < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\ncat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n```\n\nExpected: `98`, then 98 names. Read them. Any name Eric wants back is deleted from the list file before Step 3. This is the human gate.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Two guards — live symlink targets, and support dirs**\n\n```bash\n# Guard A: nothing a live Claude skill points at may be pruned.\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec readlink {} \\; \\\n  | sed 's|.*/||' | sort > /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt\necho \"--- guard A (must be empty) ---\"\ncomm -12 /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt\n\n# Guard B: nothing without a SKILL.md may be pruned - it is support, not a skill.\necho \"--- guard B (must be empty) ---\"\nwhile IFS= read -r s; do\n  [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -d ~/.agents/skills/\"$s\" ] && [ ! -f ~/.agents/skills/\"$s\"/SKILL.md ] && echo \"SUPPORT DIR: $s\"\ndone < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n```\n\nExpected: **both empty.** Any name under guard A means a live skill points at something scheduled for pruning. Any name under guard B is a support directory like `_shared`. Remove it from `/tmp/pool-prune-list.txt` and re-run until both print nothing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Move, do not delete**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.agents/skills\nwhile IFS= read -r s; do\n  if [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -e \"$s\" ]; then mv \"$s\" ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/; fi\ndone < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\nls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l\nls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l\n```\n\nExpected: pool drops `306` → `208`; archive holds `98`.\n\n> `306 - 98 = 208`, not 61. That remainder is third-party installs and Codex-only skills. **Do not \"fix\" 208 down to 61 here** — that would delete assets other harnesses use.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Verify nothing that was working broke**\n\n```bash\nfind -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\necho \"--- dangling symlinks (must be empty) ---\"\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print\ntest -f ~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared OK\"\n```\n\nExpected: `61`, no dangling output, `_shared OK`. A dangling symlink means a guard was skipped — roll back with the Task 1 tarball.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Write the record and commit**\n\n```bash\n{\n  echo \"pool prune 2026-09-12\"\n  echo \"before: 306  after: $(ls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l | tr -d ' ')  archived: $(ls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\n  echo \"scope: only names matching ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12. Third-party installs untouched.\"\n  echo \"undo: mv ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/* ~/.agents/skills/\"\n  echo \"--- pruned ---\"\n  cat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n} > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt && git commit -m \"chore: prune stale skill pool mirror\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 3: Harden the sync writers — before anything moves\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: nothing.\n- Produces: a sync script safe to run at any point during Tasks 4–7. **Nothing in Task 4 may run until this task's Step 6 passes.**\n\n**Why this task comes before the move.** Round 1 put the relink first and this second. Codex caught that as R1, a data-loss defect. Today `sync-to-codex.sh` does:\n\n```bash\nrm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n```\n\n`dev-sync` instructs the user to run it after every pull. The moment `$SRC/$s` is a symlink into `$dst`, `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"` destroys the real directory and the `cp` copies a corpse. The `.ps1` twin has the same shape. Hardening first makes the whole rest of the plan safe to interrupt.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Read both current scripts and record the baseline**\n\n```bash\ncat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh\necho \"=== ps1 ===\"\ncat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1\n```\n\nExpected: the `SHARED=(...)` array and the `rm -rf` / `cp -R` pair in each. Note the current `SHARED` membership — Step 2 preserves it.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Rewrite the bash script**\n\nReplace `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh` with:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# sync-to-codex.sh -- rebuild the Codex and agent-pool VIEWS from the canonical\n# repo at ~/.claude/skills.\n#\n# CANONICAL: ~/.claude/skills   (git repo erash11/claude-skills, real dirs only)\n# VIEWS:     ~/.agents/skills   (Pi, Cowork)\n#            ~/.codex/skills    (Codex)\n#\n# Views are generated. Never author a skill in a view; it will be overwritten.\n# On macOS a view entry is a symlink; on Windows the .ps1 twin copies instead.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSRC=\"$HOME/.claude/skills\"\nSHARED=(new-project startup dev-sync closeout session-handoff humanizer articulation-skill)\nMODE=\"${SYNC_MODE:-link}\"     # link | copy\nDRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-0}\"\n\nfail=0\nfor s in \"${SHARED[@]}\"; do\n  # R1 GUARD: the canonical source must be a real directory, never a link.\n  # If it is a link, a previous migration inverted the direction and a\n  # delete-then-copy would destroy the real data. Refuse the whole run.\n  if [ -L \"$SRC/$s\" ]; then\n    echo \"FATAL $SRC/$s is a symlink. Canonical must be a real directory.\" >&2\n    echo \"      Refusing to sync; this would delete the link target.\" >&2\n    fail=1\n  fi\ndone\nif [ \"$fail\" -ne 0 ]; then\n  echo \"No changes made.\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\nfor dst in \"$HOME/.agents/skills\" \"$HOME/.codex/skills\"; do\n  mkdir -p \"$dst\"\n  for s in \"${SHARED[@]}\"; do\n    if [ ! -d \"$SRC/$s\" ]; then\n      echo \"skip $s (not in $SRC)\"\n      continue\n    fi\n    if [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then\n      echo \"would sync $s -> $dst ($MODE)\"\n      continue\n    fi\n    # Safe now: $SRC/$s is proven real, so removing the view cannot touch it.\n    rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"\n    if [ \"$MODE\" = \"link\" ]; then\n      ln -s \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n    else\n      cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n    fi\n    echo \"synced $s -> $dst ($MODE)\"\n  done\ndone\n\nif [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then\n  echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"\nfi\nexit 0\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Apply the same guard to the PowerShell twin**\n\nEdit `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`. Before its copy loop, add:\n\n```powershell\n# R1 GUARD: canonical must be a real directory, never a reparse point.\nforeach ($s in $SHARED) {\n  $p = Join-Path $SRC $s\n  if ((Test-Path $p) -and ((Get-Item $p -Force).Attributes -band [IO.FileAttributes]::ReparsePoint)) {\n    Write-Error \"FATAL $p is a link. Canonical must be a real directory. Refusing to sync.\"\n    exit 2\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWindows keeps `Copy-Item` (mode `copy`); it does not create symlinks without elevation.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Prove the guard fires — the whole point of this task**\n\n```bash\n# Build a fake inverted layout in a scratch HOME and confirm the script refuses.\nrm -rf /tmp/syncguard && mkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/{.claude/skills,.agents/skills,.codex/skills}\nmkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup\necho \"canary - must survive\" > /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup/SKILL.md\nln -s /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup /tmp/syncguard/.claude/skills/startup\nHOME=/tmp/syncguard bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\ncat /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup/SKILL.md\n```\n\nExpected: `FATAL ... is a symlink`, `exit=2`, and the canary file **still readable**. If the canary is gone, the guard does not work — stop and fix before any other task.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Prove the normal path still works**\n\n```bash\nDRY_RUN=1 bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\nbash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\nls -la ~/.codex/skills | grep -E 'startup|closeout'\n```\n\nExpected: dry run lists `would sync` lines and `exit=0`; the real run reports `synced …` and `exit=0`; the listing shows symlinks into `~/.claude/skills`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Verify `daily-backup` will not publish a symlink**\n\n```bash\ngrep -n 'skills' ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1 | head -20\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills status --short\n```\n\nExpected: `git status` is **clean**. The views live outside the repo, so the backup's `git add -A` has nothing machine-specific to pick up. If `git status` lists a symlink inside `~/.claude/skills`, stop — that is exactly R3 and it must not be committed.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Commit both copies**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.claude/skills\ngit add sync-to-codex.sh sync-to-codex.ps1\ngit commit -m \"fix: refuse to sync when canonical source is a symlink (R1)\n\nDelete-then-copy destroyed the link target when ~/.claude/skills/<name>\npointed into ~/.agents/skills. Views are now generated from a proven-real\ncanonical source; symlink on Mac, copy on Windows.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ngit push\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\nprintf '# Sync hardening 2026-09-12\\n\\nR1 fix. Guard test output:\\n\\n```\\n%s\\n```\\n' \\\n  \"$(HOME=/tmp/syncguard bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\")\" \\\n  > docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md\ngit add docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md\ngit commit -m \"docs: record the sync guard test\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 4: Invert the 19 outward symlinks into the canonical repo\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/` (19 symlinks become real dirs)\n- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (19 real dirs become links back)\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the hardened sync script from Task 3.\n- Produces: a canonical repo with **zero symlinks**, so Task 5 edits one file per skill and every harness sees it.\n\n**Direction, restated because round 1 had it backwards.** 19 entries in `~/.claude/skills/` are symlinks pointing *out* to `~/.agents/skills/`. Their real content therefore sits outside the git repo — unversioned, not on Windows, not in the backup. This task pulls each real directory **into** the repo and leaves a link behind at the pool.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the script**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Invert outward symlinks so ~/.claude/skills holds real directories only.\n#   before: ~/.claude/skills/X -> ~/.agents/skills/X   (real content outside git)\n#   after:  ~/.claude/skills/X is real; ~/.agents/skills/X -> it\n# Idempotent. DRY_RUN=1 by default.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSRC=\"$HOME/.claude/skills\"\nPOOL=\"$HOME/.agents/skills\"\nDRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-1}\"\nmoved=0; skipped=0\n\nfor link in \"$SRC\"/*; do\n  name=\"$(basename \"$link\")\"\n  case \"$name\" in .*) continue ;; esac\n  if [ ! -L \"$link\" ]; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  target=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$link\")\" && readlink \"$link\")\"\n  abs=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$link\")\" && cd \"$(dirname \"$target\")\" 2>/dev/null && pwd)/$(basename \"$target\")\"\n  if [ ! -d \"$abs\" ]; then\n    echo \"DANGLING $name -> $target (left alone)\"\n    skipped=$((skipped+1))\n    continue\n  fi\n  if [ ! -f \"$abs/SKILL.md\" ]; then\n    echo \"NO-SKILL  $name -> $target (support dir, left alone)\"\n    skipped=$((skipped+1))\n    continue\n  fi\n  echo \"INVERT    $name  ($abs -> $SRC/$name, link back at pool)\"\n  if [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ]; then\n    rm \"$link\"\n    mv \"$abs\" \"$SRC/$name\"\n    ln -s \"$SRC/$name\" \"$abs\"\n  fi\n  moved=$((moved+1))\ndone\n\necho\necho \"inverted: $moved   skipped: $skipped\"\necho \"symlinks left in canonical: $(find \"$SRC\" -maxdepth 1 -type l | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\nif [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then\n  echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"\nfi\nexit 0\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Dry run and read every line**\n\n```bash\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh\n```\n\nExpected: ~19 `INVERT` lines, `inverted: 19`, and the DRY footer. Any `DANGLING` line is a broken skill — investigate before applying. `NO-SKILL` lines are support directories and are correctly left alone (this is the R2 fix; there is **no** instruction anywhere in this plan to delete a directory for lacking a SKILL.md).\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Apply**\n\n```bash\nDRY_RUN=0 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh\n```\n\nExpected: the same lines, then `symlinks left in canonical: 0`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Verify the canonical repo is symlink-free and complete**\n\n```bash\necho \"--- symlinks in canonical (must be 0) ---\"\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l | wc -l\necho \"--- SKILL.md count (must be 61) ---\"\nfind -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\necho \"--- dangling pool links (must be empty) ---\"\nfind ~/.agents/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print\necho \"--- git sees real files, not links ---\"\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills status --short | head -20\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills ls-files -s | awk '$1==\"120000\"' | head\n```\n\nExpected: `0`; `61`; no dangling output; `git status` lists new real files; the `ls-files` line prints **nothing** (mode `120000` is a symlink — none may be staged). If any symlink is staged, unstage it and stop; that is R3.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Verify `_shared` consumers still resolve (the R2 check)**\n\n```bash\ntest -f ~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared in canonical OK\"\npython3 -c \"\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path.home()/'.claude/skills/meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py'\nif not p.exists():\n    print('meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py not found - check path'); raise SystemExit\ncfg = p.resolve().parents[2]/'_shared/config.json'\nprint('resolves to:', cfg)\nprint('exists:', cfg.exists())\n\"\n```\n\nExpected: `_shared in canonical OK`, and `exists: True`. If `False`, the `parents[2]` hop no longer lands on `_shared` — fix the path in `sync.py` before committing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Re-run the sync guard now that the layout changed**\n\n```bash\nDRY_RUN=1 bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: `would sync` lines and `exit=0` — **not** `FATAL`. A FATAL here means a `SHARED` entry is still a symlink and Step 3 did not finish.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Confirm Claude Code still loads the roster, then commit**\n\nRun `/reload-skills` in a Claude Code session, then `/skills`. Expected: the same personal-skill count as before (61). If it dropped, roll back per Task 1.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.claude/skills\ngit add -A\ngit status --short | head\ngit commit -m \"refactor: canonical repo holds real directories, no symlinks\n\n19 skills whose content lived in ~/.agents/skills are now inside the repo,\nso they version and reach Windows. The pool links back to them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ngit push\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh && git commit -m \"feat: symlink inversion script\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 5: Normalize every skill to portable frontmatter\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` (frontmatter only, body untouched)\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the symlink-free canonical repo from Task 4.\n- Produces: `skill-portability-check.py`. `aios-audit` calls it as its harness-parity check. Exit `0` when every skill passes, `1` otherwise, `2` on an empty or missing inventory.\n\n**The portable frontmatter contract** (a superset — Codex ignores keys it does not know, Claude Code ignores `metadata`):\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: skill-name                       # REQUIRED. Must equal the directory name.\n                                       # TitleCase is allowed (CreateSkill, Harvest).\ndescription: >-                        # REQUIRED. <=1024 chars. Must contain BOTH:\n  <what it does, one sentence>.        #   (a) a positive clause: \"Use when ...\" with\n  Use when <literal trigger phrases>.  #       literal phrases the user would type\n  Do not use when <negative>.          #   (b) a negative clause: \"Do not use ...\" /\n                                       #       \"NOT FOR ...\" - Codex needs this to\n                                       #       avoid over-firing\nmetadata:\n  short-description: <one short line>  # optional, Codex-only, Claude ignores\nargument-hint: \"[...]\"                 # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep        # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\n---\n```\n\nClaude Code triggers on literal phrases; Codex leans on the negative clause. A description with only one half fires reliably in one harness and badly in the other. **Naming is settled here (R7):** the rule is `name` equals the directory name. TitleCase is legal — `CreateSkill` and `Harvest` already ship that way and renaming them would break every cross-reference.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing check**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Check every skill in the canonical repo against the portable frontmatter\ncontract. Exit 0 = all pass, 1 = failures, 2 = empty/missing inventory.\nstdlib only (PEP 668 machine). Parses the flat YAML subset we actually use.\"\"\"\nimport os, re, sys, glob\n\nCANON = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.claude/skills\")\nALLOWED = {\"name\", \"description\", \"metadata\", \"argument-hint\", \"allowed-tools\",\n           \"model\", \"disable-model-invocation\", \"license\", \"version\", \"author\"}\nPOSITIVE = (\"use when\", \"use for\", \"use this skill when\", \"invoke when\")\nNEGATIVE = (\"do not use\", \"don't use\", \"not for\", \"never use\")\n\ndef frontmatter(text):\n    if not text.startswith(\"---\"):\n        return None\n    end = text.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n    return None if end == -1 else text[4:end]\n\ndef top_keys(fm):\n    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)\\s*:\", fm)]\n\ndef scalar(fm, key):\n    \"\"\"Read a flat scalar, including >- and | block styles.\"\"\"\n    m = re.search(rf\"(?ms)^{re.escape(key)}\\s*:[ \\t]*(.*?)(?=^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*\\s*:|\\Z)\", fm)\n    if not m:\n        return \"\"\n    raw = m.group(1)\n    raw = re.sub(r\"^[>|][-+]?\\s*\\n\", \"\", raw)          # strip a block indicator\n    val = \" \".join(l.strip() for l in raw.splitlines() if l.strip())\n    return val.strip().strip('\"').strip(\"'\")\n\ndef check(path):\n    name = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path))\n    problems = []\n    fm = frontmatter(open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read())\n    if fm is None:\n        return name, [\"no YAML frontmatter\"]\n    keys = top_keys(fm)\n    declared = scalar(fm, \"name\")\n    if \"name\" not in keys:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'name'\")\n    elif declared != name:\n        problems.append(f\"name '{declared}' != directory '{name}'\")\n    desc = scalar(fm, \"description\")\n    if not desc:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'description'\")\n    else:\n        low = desc.lower()\n        if len(desc) > 1024:\n            problems.append(f\"description {len(desc)} chars (>1024)\")\n        pos = next((p for p in POSITIVE if p in low), None)\n        neg = next((n for n in NEGATIVE if n in low), None)\n        if not pos:\n            problems.append(\"no positive clause ('Use when ...') - Claude will not trigger\")\n        if not neg:\n            problems.append(\"no negative clause ('Do not use ...') - Codex over-fires\")\n        # R7: the two clauses must be distinct text, not one phrase counted twice.\n        if pos and neg and low.find(pos) == low.find(neg):\n            problems.append(\"positive and negative clauses overlap\")\n    for k in keys:\n        if k not in ALLOWED:\n            problems.append(f\"unknown frontmatter key '{k}'\")\n    return name, problems\n\ndef main():\n    if not os.path.isdir(CANON):\n        print(f\"FATAL canonical dir missing: {CANON}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    paths = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(CANON, \"*\", \"SKILL.md\")))\n    if not paths:\n        print(f\"FATAL no SKILL.md found under {CANON} - refusing to report success\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    failed = 0\n    for p in paths:\n        name, probs = check(p)\n        if probs:\n            failed += 1\n            print(f\"FAIL {name}\")\n            for x in probs:\n                print(f\"       - {x}\")\n    print(f\"\\nchecked {len(paths)} skills, {failed} failing, {len(paths)-failed} portable\")\n    return 1 if failed else 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Prove the checker's own failure modes first (R7)**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"baseline exit=$?\"\n\n# An empty inventory must NOT report success.\nrm -rf /tmp/emptycanon && mkdir -p /tmp/emptycanon\nHOME=/tmp/emptycanon python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"empty exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: a long FAIL list and `baseline exit=1`; then `FATAL ...` and `empty exit=2`. If the empty case prints `0 failing` and exits 0, the R7 fix is not in — stop.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Fix frontmatter in priority order, highest-use first**\n\nDo **not** bulk-rewrite with sed. Each description is a trigger surface; a careless edit silently stops a skill firing. Edit only the frontmatter block of `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, in this order:\n\n1. Confirmed working set (3+ invocations): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `backup`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`, `codex-review`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, `doctor-plus`, `startup`, `new-project`, `status-report`.\n2. Everything else Claude Code exposes.\n3. Codex-only skills last.\n\nWorked example — `backup` today has triggers but no negative clause:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: >-\n  Run the KeystoneOS daily backup on demand (git-push the memory repos, rsync\n  state to Google Drive and the CRASHBOX SSD, reindex memsearch), then report\n  what was saved and what was skipped. Use when the user says \"/backup\", \"run\n  the backup\", \"back up now\", \"did the backup run\", \"the backup failed\", or\n  when a session-start HOOK FAILURE names daily-backup. Do not use for\n  whole-stack health checks - use claude-stack-doctor instead.\n```\n\nRe-check after each batch of five:\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py | tail -3\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm it passes**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: `checked 61 skills, 0 failing, 61 portable` and `exit=0`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Prove portability in both harnesses, not just the linter**\n\nPick three edited skills. In a fresh Claude Code session, type one trigger phrase for each and confirm it fires. In a fresh Codex session (`cx`), do the same. Record all six results in the report. A linter that passes while a skill stops firing is exactly the failure this step exists to catch.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Write the report and commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n{\n  echo \"# Skill portability normalization - 2026-09-12\"\n  echo\n  echo \"Contract: name (== directory name, TitleCase allowed) + description with\"\n  echo \"BOTH a positive 'Use when' clause and a negative 'Do not use' clause.\"\n  echo \"argument-hint and allowed-tools are Claude-only; metadata is Codex-only.\"\n  echo\n  echo '```'\n  python3 scripts/skill-portability-check.py || true\n  echo '```'\n  echo\n  echo \"## Live trigger test (Step 5)\"\n  echo \"| skill | Claude Code | Codex |\"\n  echo \"|---|---|---|\"\n  echo \"| FILL IN | | |\"\n} > docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit add scripts/skill-portability-check.py docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: portable skill frontmatter contract + checker\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ncd ~/.claude/skills && git add -A && git commit -m \"chore: portable frontmatter across the roster\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git push\n```\n\nFill in the trigger-test table from the real runs. A report left with `FILL IN` is a failed task.\n\n---\n\n### Task 6: `rot.md` and the `aios-audit` compound skill\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/.claude/rot.md`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`\n- Modify: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md` (cross-reference line only)\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `skill-portability-check.py` (Task 5) and the canonical repo (Task 4).\n- Produces: `aios-metrics.py`, printing a machine-readable metrics block Task 7's launchd job captures.\n\n**Placement is deliberate.** This skill is **project-level**, in `KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/`, not global — the promotion rule from the video (31:32). It gets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/` only after proving useful across 2+ projects.\n\n**It must not become a sixth audit skill.** Five exist and each owns a layer:\n\n| Existing skill | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `os-audit` (project) | project data: routing, index truth, freshness, duplication |\n| `brain-audit` | SecondBrain as a system — the four C's |\n| `improve-system` | note-level rot: stale seeds, duplicates, conflicts |\n| `claude-stack-doctor` | automation plumbing: launchd, venvs, memsearch, backup freshness |\n| `doctor-plus` | context-engineering posture of the workspace |\n\n`aios-audit` owns the one layer none of them touch — **the harness itself**: skill roster, agents, hooks, rules, cross-harness parity. It **delegates** the other five rather than re-implementing them.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write `~/.claude/rot.md`**\n\nNote the `Last audited` column (R10): every layer starts `unknown` except Skills, which was genuinely audited. A date is written only after that layer's check actually runs.\n\n```markdown\n# rot.md — how fast each AIOS layer goes stale\n\nAudit each layer on its own clock, not all at once. `aios-audit` reads this\nfile: any layer past due, or marked `unknown`, is a finding.\n\n**A date here is a receipt, not an intention.** Write one only after that\nlayer's check in the last column has actually run and produced output.\n\n| Layer | Where it lives | Rots | Audit every | Last audited | Owner | Concrete check |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Identity | `global-memory/USER.md` | very slow | 3 months | unknown | memory-write | Read USER.md; flag any role, family, or career fact contradicted by the last 90 days of sessions. |\n| Substrate | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `~/DEV/*.md` routers | slow | 3 months | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 1 (routing integrity). |\n| Skills | `~/.claude/skills/` | fast | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit | `skill-portability-check.py` + never-used list + the crutch test below. |\n| Agents | `~/.claude/agents/` | fast | 1 month | unknown | aios-audit | Count `*.md`. Currently **0 exist** — report \"no agents defined\" and skip until one does. |\n| Rules | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` behaviour rules, output styles | fastest | 1 week | unknown | aios-audit | Diff CLAUDE.md against the last 7 days of sessions: any rule contradicted by what actually happened, and any correction the user repeated twice that is not yet a rule. |\n| Hooks | `~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/` | slow | 6 months | unknown | claude-stack-doctor | Delegate. Also diff the two copies — they must match (`hook-two-copies-trap`). |\n| Memory | `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `global-memory/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | improve-system | Delegate to `improve-system`. Also check `MEMORY.md` char cap. |\n| Data feeds | Fireflies, GPS, CFBD, Zotero | varies | 1 week | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 3 (freshness). |\n| Vault | `~/SecondBrain/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | brain-audit | Delegate to `brain-audit` (four C's score). |\n| Context posture | workspace-wide | medium | 3 months | unknown | doctor-plus | Delegate to `doctor-plus`. |\n\n## The question that governs a skill's fate\n\nNot \"how often did I run it?\" but **\"would the current model do this fine\nwithout the skill?\"** If yes, the skill is a crutch that now limits the model.\nDelete it. Keep only skills carrying tacit knowledge the model cannot know:\nforce-plate thresholds, GPS conventions, Baylor reporting norms, Eric's own\nroutines.\n\nSeasonal skills (`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`) are exempt from low-use\npressure. Judge them in season.\n\n## Layout invariants the audit must assert\n\n- `~/.claude/skills/` is canonical, is a git repo, and contains **no symlinks**.\n- `~/.agents/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` are **generated views**. A real\n  directory in either means someone edited a view instead of the source.\n- Support directories (`_shared/`) have no SKILL.md and must never be pruned.\n\n## Direction of travel\n\n**Fewer skills, stricter rules.** Skills get deleted; rules get sharpened.\n```\n\nVerify: `head -30 ~/.claude/rot.md`\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Write the metrics script**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`. Counting is deduped by real path (R6) and session globbing is explicitly recursive:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Machine-readable AIOS metrics. stdlib only. Prints key=value lines only.\"\"\"\nimport os, re, glob, subprocess, collections, datetime, sys\n\nH = os.path.expanduser(\"~\")\nCANON = f\"{H}/.claude/skills\"\nPOOL = f\"{H}/.agents/skills\"\nCODEX = f\"{H}/.codex/skills\"\nBUILTIN = {'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin',\n'doctor','help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp',\n'hooks','memory','review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir',\n'bug','release-notes','pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline',\n'output-style','todos','export','privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts',\n'tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind','sandbox','feedback','worktrees',\n'skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes','keybindings','theme'}\n\ndef skills_in(root):\n    \"\"\"Count distinct skills under root, deduped by real path (R6).\n    glob traverses symlinked dirs, so a link and its target counted twice.\"\"\"\n    seen = set()\n    for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\")):\n        s = os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\")\n        if os.path.isfile(s):\n            seen.add(os.path.realpath(s))\n    return len(seen)\n\ndef names_in(root):\n    return {os.path.basename(d) for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\"))\n            if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\"))}\n\ndef usage():\n    c = collections.Counter()\n    for f in glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl\", recursive=True):\n        try:\n            for line in open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\"):\n                if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line:\n                    continue\n                for m in re.finditer(\n                        r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n                    c[m.group(1)] += 1\n                for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n                    c[m.group(1)] += 1\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return {k: v for k, v in c.items()\n            if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\n\ndef roster_tokens():\n    \"\"\"Frontmatter chars / 4 - what the roster costs per session.\"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for p in glob.glob(f\"{CANON}/*/SKILL.md\"):\n        try:\n            t = open(p, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read()\n            end = t.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n            if t.startswith(\"---\") and end != -1:\n                total += len(t[4:end])\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return total // 4\n\ndef real_dirs_in_view(root):\n    \"\"\"A real dir in a generated view means someone edited the wrong copy.\"\"\"\n    n = 0\n    for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\")):\n        b = os.path.basename(d)\n        if b.startswith('.'):\n            continue\n        if os.path.isdir(d) and not os.path.islink(d) and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\")):\n            n += 1\n    return n\n\nu = usage()\ncanon_names = names_in(CANON)\nout = [\n    (\"date\", datetime.date.today().isoformat()),\n    (\"canonical_skills\", skills_in(CANON)),\n    (\"pool_entries\", len(glob.glob(f\"{POOL}/*\"))),\n    (\"pool_skills\", skills_in(POOL)),\n    (\"codex_skills\", skills_in(CODEX)),\n    (\"project_skills\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/DEV/*/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md\"))),\n    (\"symlinks_in_canonical\", len([d for d in glob.glob(f\"{CANON}/*\") if os.path.islink(d)])),\n    (\"real_dirs_in_pool_view\", real_dirs_in_view(POOL)),\n    (\"real_dirs_in_codex_view\", real_dirs_in_view(CODEX)),\n    (\"agents\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/agents/*.md\"))),\n    (\"hooks_claude\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/hooks/*\"))),\n    (\"hooks_codex\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.codex/hooks/*\"))),\n    (\"sessions\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl\", recursive=True))),\n    (\"skills_ever_used\", len(u)),\n    (\"skills_used_3plus\", len([k for k, v in u.items() if v >= 3])),\n    (\"skills_used_once\", len([k for k, v in u.items() if v == 1])),\n    (\"never_used\", len(canon_names - set(u))),\n    (\"roster_tokens_est\", roster_tokens()),\n]\nrc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, f\"{H}/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\"],\n                    capture_output=True, text=True)\nm = re.search(r\"(\\d+) failing\", rc.stdout)\nout.append((\"portability_failures\", m.group(1) if m else f\"error_rc{rc.returncode}\"))\n\nfor k, v in out:\n    print(f\"{k}={v}\")\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Run it and check every value**\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n```\n\nExpected: 19 `key=value` lines, no stack trace, and specifically:\n\n- `canonical_skills=61`\n- `symlinks_in_canonical=0` — the Task 4 invariant\n- `real_dirs_in_pool_view=0` and `real_dirs_in_codex_view=0` — views are generated\n- `project_skills=2` (`os-audit` + `aios-audit`)\n- `portability_failures=0`\n\nAny non-zero in the middle three means a later hand-edit broke the layout. Fix before continuing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Write the `aios-audit` skill**\n\nInvoke `CreateSkill` first — it owns skill authoring here. Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` with frontmatter that satisfies Task 5's contract:\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: aios-audit\ndescription: >-\n  Audit the whole AI operating system across decay tiers - skills, agents,\n  hooks, rules, memory, and cross-harness parity between Claude Code, Codex,\n  and Pi - then propose exactly two next builds. Use when the user says\n  \"/aios-audit\", \"audit my system\", \"audit my AI OS\", \"audit the bloat\",\n  \"is my setup rotting\", \"what should I cut\", \"are my skills portable\",\n  \"monthly skill audit\", or when rot.md shows a layer past its audit date.\n  Do not use for project data drift, routing, or index freshness - use\n  os-audit. Do not use for launchd, venv, or backup health - use\n  claude-stack-doctor. Do not use for SecondBrain note rot - use\n  improve-system or brain-audit.\nmetadata:\n  short-description: Decay-tier audit of the AI OS; delegates to the five layer-specific audit skills.\nargument-hint: \"[optional: a layer name from rot.md]\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Agent\n---\n```\n\nThe body must specify, in order:\n\n1. **Read-only.** The only writes are the report and `rot.md`'s `Last audited` column. **Never delete a skill during the audit** — propose, then wait for approval.\n2. **Step 0 — read `~/.claude/rot.md`.** Compute days since each layer's `Last audited`. A layer that is past due **or `unknown`** gets audited this run; layers inside their window are reported green and skipped. That is what makes a monthly run cheap.\n3. **Step 1 — run `python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`** and capture every value.\n4. **Step 2 — assert the layout invariants** from `rot.md`. `symlinks_in_canonical`, `real_dirs_in_pool_view`, `real_dirs_in_codex_view` must all be `0`. Any non-zero is a RED finding: someone edited a generated view instead of the canonical repo.\n5. **Step 3 — delegate, do not duplicate.** For each due layer, invoke the owning skill named in the `rot.md` table and fold its findings under that layer's heading. Only the harness layer is audited directly.\n6. **Step 4 — the harness layer, audited directly:**\n   - **Bloat:** every canonical skill never invoked across all sessions. List them.\n   - **Crutch test:** for each never-used and once-used skill, answer the `rot.md` question — *would the current model do this fine without it?* Recommend DELETE, KEEP (tacit knowledge), or SEASONAL, one line of reasoning each.\n   - **Overlap:** skills whose descriptions cover the same trigger space. Recommend merging.\n   - **Parity:** run `skill-portability-check.py`. Every failure is a finding.\n   - **Promotion:** global skills used in fewer than 2 projects — demotion candidates. Project skills used in 2+ — promotion candidates.\n   - **Rules:** diff `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` against the last 7 days of sessions. Report rules contradicted by what actually happened, and corrections the user repeated twice that are not yet rules.\n   - **Agents:** count `~/.claude/agents/*.md`. If `0`, report \"no agents defined\" and skip — do not invent a finding.\n7. **Step 5 — write the report** to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, append the metrics row to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`, and update `Last audited` in `rot.md` **only for layers whose check actually ran and produced output**.\n8. **Step 6 — end with exactly two proposed next builds,** ranked, each with the one command that starts it. Not ten. Two.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Cross-reference `os-audit` so the two do not fight**\n\nAdd near the top of `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md`:\n\n```markdown\n> Scope note: this skill audits **project data** — routing, indexes, freshness,\n> duplication. The harness layer (skills, agents, hooks, rules, cross-harness\n> parity) belongs to `aios-audit`, which calls this skill for the data layer.\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Smoke-test end to end**\n\nIn a Claude Code session with cwd `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`, run `/reload-skills`, then type \"audit my AI OS\". Expected: `aios-audit` fires, reads `rot.md`, runs the metrics script, produces a report, and ends with exactly two proposals.\n\n```bash\nls -l ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-*.md\ngrep -c 'unknown' ~/.claude/rot.md\n```\n\nExpected: a report file exists. The `unknown` count should have **dropped** by exactly the number of layers whose checks ran — not to zero.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ncp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md\ngit add .claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md .claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md \\\n        scripts/aios-metrics.py docs/rot.md docs/audits/\ngit commit -m \"feat: aios-audit compound skill + rot.md decay tiers\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 7: Put the metrics on a monthly clock\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/rot.md` (cadence note)\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `aios-metrics.py` from Task 6.\n- Produces: a monthly trend row in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`. Terminal deliverable.\n\n**Design choice, tightened after R9.** The job runs the **metrics script only**, appends a trend row, and writes a nudge. It does **not** commit, does **not** push, and does **not** run the agentic audit. Reasons, all from R9: committing from the shared working tree publishes whatever the user had staged; a `git push` failure is easy to mask; and the 09:00 backup may run up to 240 minutes, so start-time spacing is not a race guard. The human commits the trend row during the audit. The job's only job is to make the audit impossible to forget.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the cron wrapper**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Monthly: append AIOS metrics to the trend table and nudge for a real audit.\n# Deliberately does NOT commit, push, or run the agentic audit (see R9):\n#  - committing from the shared tree would publish the user's staged files\n#  - the agentic audit proposes deletions and needs a human reading them\nset -uo pipefail\n\nREPO=\"$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS\"\nTREND=\"$REPO/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nLOG=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\"\nLOCK=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$LOG\")\" \"$(dirname \"$TREND\")\"\n\nexec >>\"$LOG\" 2>&1\necho \"=== $(date -u +%FT%TZ) aios-audit-cron ===\"\n\n# Single-instance lock. mkdir is atomic; no flock on stock macOS.\nif ! mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then\n  echo \"ABORT another run holds $LOCK\"\n  exit 0\nfi\ntrap 'rmdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null' EXIT\n\nif ! M=\"$(python3 \"$REPO/scripts/aios-metrics.py\" 2>&1)\"; then\n  echo \"FAIL metrics script errored:\"\n  echo \"$M\"\n  exit 1\nfi\necho \"$M\"\n\nget() {\n  local v\n  v=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$M\" | grep \"^$1=\" | cut -d= -f2-)\"\n  if [ -z \"$v\" ]; then echo \"MISSING\"; else echo \"$v\"; fi\n}\n\nif [ ! -f \"$TREND\" ]; then\n  {\n    echo \"# AIOS trend\"\n    echo\n    echo \"One row per monthly run. Written by scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh.\"\n    echo \"The script does not commit; the human commits during the audit.\"\n    echo\n    echo \"| date | canonical | pool | codex | project | never used | roster tok | portability fails | symlinks in canon |\"\n    echo \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"\n  } > \"$TREND\"\nfi\n\nROW=\"$(printf '| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |' \\\n  \"$(get date)\" \"$(get canonical_skills)\" \"$(get pool_skills)\" \"$(get codex_skills)\" \\\n  \"$(get project_skills)\" \"$(get never_used)\" \"$(get roster_tokens_est)\" \\\n  \"$(get portability_failures)\" \"$(get symlinks_in_canonical)\")\"\n\nif printf '%s' \"$ROW\" | grep -q 'MISSING'; then\n  echo \"FAIL a metrics key did not resolve; row not written:\"\n  echo \"$ROW\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$ROW\" >> \"$TREND\"\necho \"wrote: $ROW\"\n\necho \"[$(date +%F)] aios-audit due - run \\\"audit my AI OS\\\" in ~/DEV/KeystoneOS (uncommitted trend row waiting)\" \\\n  >> \"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\"\n\necho \"=== done ok ===\"\nexit 0\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run it by hand and verify the trend row**\n\n```bash\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\ncat ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\ntail -25 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\ngit -C ~/DEV/KeystoneOS status --short\n```\n\nExpected: `exit=0`; a header plus one row with **no `MISSING` cells**; the log ends `=== done ok ===`; `git status` shows `aios-trend.md` as **modified but uncommitted** — that is correct, the human commits it.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Prove the failure paths (R9's masked-failure point)**\n\n```bash\n# A bad metrics script must fail the job, not write a row.\nmv ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py /tmp/aios-metrics.bak\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 1)\"\nmv /tmp/aios-metrics.bak ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n\n# A stale lock must not wedge the job forever.\nmkdir -p ~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 0, ABORT logged)\"\nrmdir ~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\ntail -6 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n```\n\nExpected: the first prints `exit=1` and adds **no** row; the second prints `exit=0` with `ABORT` in the log and **no** row. If either wrote a row, the failure propagation is not working — fix before loading launchd.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Write the launchd plist**\n\nCreate `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`. Day 1 at 14:00 — well clear of the 09:00 backup's possible 240-minute window, and the lock covers the rest:\n\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\"\n  \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n<plist version=\"1.0\">\n<dict>\n  <key>Label</key><string>com.keystone.aiosaudit</string>\n  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n  <array>\n    <string>/bin/bash</string>\n    <string>-lc</string>\n    <string>$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh</string>\n  </array>\n  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>Day</key><integer>1</integer>\n    <key>Hour</key><integer>14</integer>\n    <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err</string>\n</dict>\n</plist>\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Load it and verify launchd accepted it**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist 2>/dev/null\nlaunchctl load  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist\nlaunchctl list | grep aiosaudit\n```\n\nExpected: one line ending `com.keystone.aiosaudit`. A non-zero middle column means the last run failed — read `/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Force a run through launchd, not just bash**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl start com.keystone.aiosaudit\ncat /tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err\ntail -5 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n```\n\nExpected: `.err` empty, a second row appended. launchd has a different PATH than an interactive shell — this step catches that. If `python3` is not found, put an absolute interpreter in `aios-metrics.py`'s shebang.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: De-duplicate test rows, note the cadence, commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np = \"docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nlines = open(p).read().splitlines()\nseen, out = set(), []\nfor l in lines:\n    if l.startswith(\"| 20\"):\n        if l in seen:\n            continue\n        seen.add(l)\n    out.append(l)\nopen(p, \"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\")\nEOF\ncat docs/audits/aios-trend.md\ncat >> ~/.claude/rot.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Cadence\n\nMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 14:00.\nIt writes a trend row and a nudge. It does not commit and does not push.\nThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.\nEOF\ncp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md\ngit add scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh docs/audits/aios-trend.md docs/rot.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: monthly AIOS metrics on launchd, no unattended commits\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 8: Pi trial under a real boundary\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: macOS standard user `piuser` (or an equivalent enforced boundary)\n- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md`\n- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: a copy of the pruned pool (Task 2) and the portable frontmatter (Task 5).\n- Produces: a measured migration cost. Nothing consumes it; it is a decision input.\n\n**Why the boundary is mandatory (R5).** Round 1 ran Pi with `cd ~/pi-trial` and called that a sandbox. It is not — `cd` restricts nothing, and the Pi research note says so explicitly. Pi ships no approval prompts by design. A regex denylist inside the agent is defence-in-depth, **not** a boundary: `cat ../DEV/file`, a shell-expanded path, or any tool the guard does not cover walks straight past it. The only enforced boundary available without new infrastructure is **OS file permissions**, so Pi runs as a different user that cannot read Eric's home.\n\n**Scope, narrowed.** The deliverable is a **cost number**, not a working Pi setup. **ADOPT is not an available verdict in this plan** — adopting Pi for real work requires a boundary design that is its own project. Verdicts here are `COST-MEASURED` or `BLOCKED`.\n\n**Stop conditions.** If the user cannot be created, or Pi will not install, or the guard does not block after two attempts, stop and write up what failed. Do not iterate past that.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Create the boundary user**\n\nThis needs admin rights and is the one step Eric may prefer to run himself:\n\n```bash\nsudo sysadminctl -addUser piuser -fullName \"Pi Trial\" -password - -home /Users/piuser\nsudo dscl . -read /Users/piuser NFSHomeDirectory\n```\n\nThen prove the boundary actually holds:\n\n```bash\nchmod 700 ~                      # confirm Eric's home is not world-readable\nsudo -u piuser ls ~/DEV          2>&1 | tail -2\nsudo -u piuser ls ~/SecondBrain  2>&1 | tail -2\n```\n\nExpected: both `ls` attempts print **Permission denied**. If either succeeds, the boundary does not exist — STOP. Do not fall back to \"just be careful.\"\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Install Pi as that user**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/pi-trial\nsudo -u piuser npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent 2>&1 | tail -5\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'which -a pi && pi --version'\n```\n\nExpected: a path and a version string. `--ignore-scripts` is deliberate — no postinstall scripts run.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Give it a copy of the pruned pool, not the real one**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/.agents\nsudo rsync -a --copy-links ~/.agents/skills/ /Users/piuser/.agents/skills/\nsudo chown -R piuser:staff /Users/piuser/.agents\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'ls /Users/piuser/.agents/skills | wc -l'\n```\n\nExpected: the Task 2 post-prune count (208). A **copy**, so a Pi misstep cannot corrupt the real pool.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm what Pi discovers**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cat > /Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md <<EOF\n# pi-trial\n\nThrowaway sandbox for evaluating the Pi harness. Nothing here is real work.\nThis user has no read access to the operator home directory by design.\nEOF\ncd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"List every skill you can see. Print only the count and the first ten names.\" 2>&1 | tail -20'\n```\n\nExpected: a count near 208, not 306. **If it reads ~306, Task 2 did not take — stop and re-run it.** This step is the proof that pruning the pool was the right fix.\n\n> Round 1 had a probe here that asked an unguarded Pi to `ls ~/DEV`. It is deleted. Pointing an unrestrained agent at real directories to demonstrate that it is unrestrained is not a test worth running. The OS boundary in Step 1 already establishes the same fact safely.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Port `dcg` to a Pi extension, using the documented contract**\n\nRead the existing guard first:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.claude/hooks/ | grep -i -E 'dcg|guard'\ngrep -n 'pi.on\\|ExtensionAPI\\|event.input' ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md\n```\n\nRound 1 used `export default { tool_call }` and `ev.args`. The research note documents an **exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`**, registration via `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, and a payload at `event.input` (R4). Write `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts` to the documented shape. If the note and the installed Pi version disagree, **the installed version wins** — record the discrepancy in the report.\n\n```typescript\n// Minimal port of the dcg guard. Proves the hook -> extension mapping.\n// Defence in depth only: the real boundary is the piuser file permissions.\nconst DENY: RegExp[] = [\n  /\\brm\\s+-rf\\s+\\/(?!tmp)/,\n  /DexaProject/i,\n  /GoogleDrive-/,\n  /(^|[\\s\"'=:])~?\\/?DEV(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,        // R4: bare ~/DEV must match\n  /(^|[\\s\"'=:])~?\\/?SecondBrain(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,\n];\n\nexport default function guard(pi: any) {\n  pi.on(\"tool_call\", (event: any) => {\n    const blob = JSON.stringify(event?.input ?? {});   // R4: input, not args\n    for (const re of DENY) {\n      if (re.test(blob)) {\n        return { block: true, reason: `guard: blocked by ${re}` };\n      }\n    }\n    return { block: false };\n  });\n}\n```\n\nPrint mode may require explicitly trusting a project extension; if Pi asks, grant it for this project only and note the step in the report.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Prove the guard blocks — and that it does not block everything**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"Run: ls /Users/piuser/DEV | head -3\" 2>&1 | tail -10'\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"Run: echo hello\" 2>&1 | tail -5'\n```\n\nExpected: the first is **blocked** with the guard reason; the second **succeeds**. A guard that blocks everything is not a guard. The probe targets a path inside `piuser`'s own home, so the guard — not the OS — is what must stop it. If the first is not blocked after two attempts at the extension path or event name, stop per the stop conditions.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Write the cost measurement**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ncat > docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md <<'EOF'\n# Pi trial - 2026-09-12\n\nBoundary: separate macOS user `piuser`, no read access to the operator home.\n`cd` into a directory is NOT a boundary and was not treated as one (R5).\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| pi version | FILL IN |\n| piuser blocked from ~/DEV and ~/SecondBrain? | FILL IN (expect: yes, Permission denied) |\n| skills auto-discovered | FILL IN (expect ~208, not 306) |\n| skills needing edits to fire in Pi | FILL IN |\n| extension contract matched the research note? | FILL IN (factory + pi.on + event.input) |\n| minutes to port the dcg guard | FILL IN |\n| hooks still to port | FILL IN of 61 |\n| verdict | COST-MEASURED / BLOCKED |\n\n## What the guard proved\n\nFILL IN: paste the blocked and the allowed output from Step 6.\n\n## What a real adoption would still need\n\nNot in scope here. At minimum: a boundary design that is not a second user\naccount, an approval-prompt layer, and a port of the remaining hooks.\nEOF\n```\n\nFill in every `FILL IN` from the real runs. A report left with placeholders is a failed task. Then:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md && git commit -m \"docs: Pi harness trial under an OS boundary + dcg guard port cost\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 8: Tear down**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser npm uninstall -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\nsudo rm -rf /Users/piuser/pi-trial /Users/piuser/.agents\n# Keep or remove the account - Eric's call. To remove:\n# sudo sysadminctl -deleteUser piuser\n```\n\nThe account may stay for future trials. The skill copy and the Pi install should not linger.\n\n---\n\n## Review Gates (not optional)\n\nEric asked for Codex review of the plan and of any code. These are steps.\n\n- [x] **Gate A — round 1, done 2026-09-12.** Verdict **REVISE**, 6 high / 4 medium. Result at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json`. All ten dispositioned in the table above; R1/R3 inverted the architecture.\n- [ ] **Gate A2 — round 2, blocking.** Resume the same reviewer session rather than starting fresh, so the new plan hash is recorded against it:\n\n  ```bash\n  cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n  python3 ~/.claude/skills/claudex-loop/scripts/runner.py review \\\n    --host claude --repo ~/DEV/KeystoneOS \\\n    --plan docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md \\\n    --resume ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json \\\n    --feedback /tmp/aios-dispositions.md \\\n    --artifacts ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r2\n  ```\n\n  `/tmp/aios-dispositions.md` is written from the disposition table above — R1–R10, accept or reject, and what changed. **No task below runs until this returns APPROVED.** R1 and R3 are data-loss grade.\n- [ ] **Gate B — after Task 5.** `codex-review` on `sync-to-codex.sh`, `sync-to-codex.ps1`, `skills-canonicalize.sh`, `skill-portability-check.py`, and ten rewritten frontmatter blocks. Codex is the right reviewer here specifically because it is the harness whose triggering the rewrite claims to fix.\n- [ ] **Gate C — after Task 7.** `codex-review` on `aios-metrics.py`, `aios-audit-cron.sh`, the plist, and the `aios-audit` SKILL.md body.\n- [ ] **Gate D — before declaring done.** `superpowers:verification-before-completion`. Every success claim carries a pasted command output.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Rewriting plugin skills (`superpowers`, `mattpocock-skills`) — vendored, overwritten on update.\n- Pruning the ~150 pool entries that are third-party installs. Task 2 touches only the 98 that match yesterday's archive. The rest need a human decision via `aios-audit`.\n- Migrating all 61 hooks to Pi. Task 8 ports exactly one to measure cost.\n- **Adopting Pi.** Task 8 produces a cost number only. Adoption needs a boundary design that is its own project.\n- Deleting any skill outright. Everything moves to an archive folder or is recoverable from git.\n- The Windows machine. Both machines are active; this plan changes Mac paths only. `sync-to-codex.ps1` is patched in Task 3, but re-running Tasks 2 and 5 there is separate work after these prove out here.\n- Mining the 414 JSONL sessions to reverse-engineer harness behaviour (video gap #9). Real, but its own project.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3584,"cache_read_input_tokens":128804,"output_tokens":32021,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":367},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3584,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":32021,"cache_read_input_tokens":128804,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3584,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3584},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CeyvMqKxKPVd2JfEpZXiR","attributionSkill":"codex-review","type":"assistant","uuid":"f5f91e66-f846-4a3d-887f-6c005e8de9ae","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:19:25.920Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
