{"parentUuid":"f5f91e66-f846-4a3d-887f-6c005e8de9ae","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"06e7d3ba-222f-4650-8a25-521650a48509","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WknGox2XP7r8yjC6GopD7D","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"32f0e6e4-2c45-425a-9a10-d4e7aa9af7c3","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:19:27.086Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","content":"# Harness-Agnostic AIOS Implementation Plan\n\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n\n**Goal:** Make every skill Eric owns load identically in Claude Code, Codex, and Pi from one versioned canonical folder, and build a recurring audit that keeps the whole AIOS from rotting.\n\n**Architecture:** `~/.claude/skills/` is the canonical home — it is already its own git repo (`erash11/claude-skills`) and is the only copy that versions and crosses to Windows. It holds **real directories only; no symlink is ever committed.** `~/.agents/skills/` (Pi, Cowork) and `~/.codex/skills/` (Codex) become **generated views**, rebuilt per machine by a hardened `sync-to-codex.sh` — symlinks on Mac, copies on Windows, never authored by hand. Every skill's frontmatter is normalized to a Codex-safe core so the same file fires in all three harnesses. Then one compound audit skill (`aios-audit`) orchestrates the five existing audit skills, driven by a `rot.md` decay table.\n\n**Tech Stack:** bash, PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, for the `.ps1` twin), python3 (stdlib only), launchd, Agent Skills standard (SKILL.md + YAML frontmatter), Claude Code, Codex CLI 0.154.0, Pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`).\n\n**Spec:** `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md` (gap analysis rows 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10) and `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`.\n\n**Review history:** Round 1 Codex review returned **REVISE** with 6 high / 4 medium findings (R1–R10). Result: `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json`. All ten are dispositioned in the table below. **The architecture above is the inverse of round 1's** — round 1 made `~/.agents/skills/` canonical, which R1/R3 showed would cause data loss.\n\n---\n\n## Global Constraints\n\n- **`~/.claude/skills/` is its own git repo** — `origin https://github.com/erash11/claude-skills.git`, currently in sync with origin. It is *gitignored by the `dotclaude` repo*, which is what the earlier handoff meant; that is not the same as unversioned. **This repo is the safety net and the Mac↔Windows bridge.**\n- **Never commit a symlink into `~/.claude/skills/`.** Windows pulls this repo. A committed absolute Mac symlink arrives there as an unusable file.\n- **Never point Pi at `~/DEV`.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — documented, deliberate design.\n- **Never let any harness near `DexaProject`** (identifiable athlete health data, on Google Drive, not in `~/DEV`).\n- **Plugin skills are out of scope.** `superpowers` (14) and `mattpocock-skills` (35) are vendored and overwritten on update. Scope = the 61 skills in `~/.claude/skills/`.\n- **Hooks exist in two copies** (`~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/`). So do the sync scripts (`.sh` and `.ps1`). Any change patches both.\n- Global `MEMORY.md` is at 2,489 / 2,500 chars. Consolidate before adding anything.\n- `timeout` is NOT installed on this Mac (no GNU coreutils). Do not use it.\n- `dcg` blocks `find ... -delete`. Use an explicit `rm` on a named path.\n- Python is PEP 668-managed. `python3` with stdlib only, or `uv`. Never global `pip install`.\n- **No success claim without a pasted command output.**\n\n### Measured baseline (2026-09-12, verified on disk this session)\n\n| Fact | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `~/.claude/skills/` SKILL.md count (`find -L`) | 61 |\n| Of those, symlinks pointing **out** to `~/.agents/skills/` | 19 |\n| `~/.claude/skills/` git remote | `erash11/claude-skills`, in sync with origin |\n| Latest commit | `3ab4467 chore: apply global skill audit` — the 99 deletions **are committed and pushed** |\n| `~/.agents/skills/` top-level entries | 306 |\n| Pool entries **not** present in `~/.claude/skills/` | 248 |\n| Archived yesterday to `~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/` | 99 |\n| Archived skills still live in `~/.agents/skills/` | 98 |\n| `~/.codex/skills/` entries | 32 (27 symlinks, 5 real dirs) |\n| Real dirs in `~/.codex/skills/` diverged from Claude copy | 0 (`dev-sync`, `closeout`, `new-project`, `startup` identical; `session-handoff` is an empty orphan) |\n| `~/.claude/skills/_shared/` | **load-bearing** — `config.json` + `Inference/`, referenced by 10+ skills |\n| Project-level skills in `~/DEV` | 1 (`KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit`) |\n| `~/.claude/rot.md` | does not exist |\n\n**The two lead findings:**\n\n1. **The pool is a stale mirror.** Yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` (and committed the cut) but never touched `~/.agents/skills/`. 98 of the 99 archived names are still there, plus 248 entries Claude Code never registered. Pi auto-discovers that folder. Installing Pi today re-exposes everything. Task 2 fixes this and must land before Task 7.\n2. **Three writers already target these folders** and none of them know about the plan: `sync-to-codex.sh`, its `.ps1` twin, and `daily-backup.ps1` (which runs `git add -A` on `~/.claude/skills`). Task 3 fixes the writers *before* moving anything. Round 1 skipped this and would have destroyed canonical copies.\n\n### Codex frontmatter ground truth (read from `~/.codex/skills/.system/*/SKILL.md`)\n\nCodex's own shipped skills use only:\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: \"skill-name\"\ndescription: \"What it does. Use when <positive triggers>. Do not use when <negative>.\"\nmetadata:\n  short-description: \"One short line.\"   # optional\n---\n```\n\nCodex reads **no** `argument-hint`, `allowed-tools`, `model`, or `disable-model-invocation`; it tolerates unknown keys. Codex descriptions carry an explicit **\"Do not use when…\"** clause; Claude Code descriptions carry **literal trigger phrases** the user would type. A portable description needs both halves. That is the whole `/poly-skill` idea, reduced to a rule.\n\n### Round 1 findings — disposition\n\n| ID | Sev | Codex's finding | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | high | `sync-to-codex.sh` does `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\"`. After relinking, `$SRC` points *at* `$dst` — the script deletes the canonical copy. | **ACCEPTED.** Verified on disk. Architecture inverted so `$SRC` is always real, plus a refuse-if-symlink guard. New **Task 3**, which now runs *before* any move. |\n| R2 | high | `_shared/` has no SKILL.md but holds `config.json` + `Inference/`, resolved by 10+ skills via `parents[2]/_shared/config.json`. Round 1 told a human to `rm -rf` any dir without a SKILL.md. | **ACCEPTED.** Verified. The blanket rm instruction is deleted. `_shared` stays in the canonical repo where it already resolves correctly. Task 4 Step 5 verifies `meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py` still resolves. |\n| R3 | high | `~/.claude/skills` has its own `.git`; `daily-backup.ps1` runs `git add -A` on it. Relinking would commit Mac symlinks to a repo Windows pulls. | **ACCEPTED, and it inverted the architecture.** Verified: remote is `erash11/claude-skills`. Canonical is now the git repo; views are generated and never committed. |\n| R4 | high | The Pi extension uses `export default {tool_call}` + `ev.args`; the research note documents an exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`, `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, `event.input`. Also `/\\/DEV\\//` never matches `ls ~/DEV`. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 7 rewritten to the documented contract; regex fixed to `/(^|\\s)~?\\/?DEV(\\/|$|\\s)/`. |\n| R5 | high | `cd ~/pi-trial` is not isolation. Round 1 launched unguarded Pi at real dirs, then treated a regex denylist as sufficient for ADOPT. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 7 runs Pi as a **separate macOS standard user** with no read access to Eric's home. The unguarded `ls ~/DEV` probe is deleted. ADOPT is off the table in this plan; the deliverable is a cost number only. |\n| R6 | med | `claude_skills` double-counts (glob already traverses symlinked dirs); `count()` omits `recursive=True` for sessions. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 5 dedupes by `os.path.realpath`; session glob made explicitly recursive. |\n| R7 | med | Checker does not parse YAML, accepts TitleCase against a kebab-case claim, a negative-only description passes the positive check, and an empty pool exits 0. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 4 enforces distinct positive and negative clauses, fails on an empty inventory, and **decides the naming question explicitly: TitleCase is allowed** (`CreateSkill`, `Harvest` ship that way) — the rule is \"matches the directory name,\" not \"is kebab-case.\" |\n| R8 | med | `[ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ] && echo` returns 1 in apply mode under `set -e`, reporting failure on success. | **ACCEPTED.** All optional actions use explicit `if`; functions `return 0`. |\n| R9 | high | The cron wrapper commits from the shared working tree — a staged unrelated file gets published — and masks git failures. 09:30 does not dodge a backup that may run 240 minutes. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 6's cron **no longer commits or pushes at all.** It writes the trend row and a nudge; the human commits during the audit. That removes the leak, the race, and the masked failure together. |\n| R10 | med | Agents and Rules have no actual check, yet `Last audited` is stamped for them; `brain-audit`/`doctor-plus` are named as delegates with no ownership row. | **ACCEPTED.** `rot.md` initializes every layer to `unknown` except Skills (genuinely audited 2026-09-12). Each layer gets a concrete check or an explicit \"no check yet.\" Dates advance only after that layer's check runs. |\n\n---\n\n### Task 1: Snapshot and freeze the current state\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: nothing.\n- Produces: the rollback path for Tasks 2–4, and `pre-canonical-inventory.txt`, the baseline later tasks diff against.\n\n**Note on undo, corrected after round 1:** there are now **two** undo paths. The tarball below covers the un-versioned folders (`~/.agents/skills`, `~/.codex/skills`). For `~/.claude/skills` the real undo is git — `3ab4467` already committed and pushed the 99 deletions, so `git -C ~/.claude/skills revert 3ab4467` restores them on both machines. The `skills-archive/` folder is *not* in that repo and does not reach Windows.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the inventory**\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits\n{\n  echo \"=== generated $(date -u +%FT%TZ) ===\"\n  echo \"--- claude skills repo state ---\"\n  git -C ~/.claude/skills remote -v\n  git -C ~/.claude/skills log --oneline -3\n  git -C ~/.claude/skills status -sb\n  echo \"--- claude skills (find -L, SKILL.md) ---\"\n  find -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | sed 's|.*/skills/||;s|/SKILL.md||' | sort\n  echo \"--- claude skills that are symlinks (to be inverted in Task 4) ---\"\n  find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec basename {} \\; | sort\n  echo \"--- support dirs in claude skills (NO SKILL.md, DO NOT DELETE) ---\"\n  for d in ~/.claude/skills/*/; do [ -f \"$d/SKILL.md\" ] || basename \"$d\"; done | sort\n  echo \"--- agents pool ---\"\n  ls ~/.agents/skills | sort\n  echo \"--- codex skills ---\"\n  ls -la ~/.codex/skills\n  echo \"--- archived 2026-09-12 ---\"\n  ls ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | sort\n} > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\nwc -l < ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\n```\n\nExpected: roughly 520–540 lines. **Read the \"support dirs\" section** — it must list `_shared`. That list is the do-not-delete set for every later task.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Take the tarball**\n\n```bash\ntar czf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz \\\n  -C ~ .claude/skills .codex/skills .agents/skills\nls -lh ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz\n```\n\nExpected: a non-zero-size file. `tar` may print `Removing leading '/'` — harmless.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Verify the tarball actually restores**\n\n```bash\nrm -rf /tmp/tartest && mkdir -p /tmp/tartest\ntar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C /tmp/tartest\nfind -L /tmp/tartest/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\nls /tmp/tartest/.agents/skills | wc -l\ntest -f /tmp/tartest/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared OK\"\n```\n\nExpected: `61`, `306`, `_shared OK`. If any is wrong, STOP — the snapshot is not a valid undo and no later task may run.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Record both rollback paths**\n\n```bash\ncat >> ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt <<'EOF'\n\n=== ROLLBACK ===\n# Un-versioned folders (undoes Tasks 2-4 view changes):\nrm -rf ~/.codex/skills ~/.agents/skills\ntar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C ~\n\n# Canonical repo (undoes anything committed to erash11/claude-skills):\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills log --oneline\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills revert <sha>    # reaches Windows on next dev-sync\nEOF\ntail -12 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt\n```\n\nExpected: the rollback block prints.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ngit add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md\ngit commit -m \"docs: pre-canonical skill inventory + harness-agnostic plan\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 2: Prune the stale pool mirror\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (move stale dirs out)\n- Create: `~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt` from Task 1.\n- Produces: an `~/.agents/skills/` free of skills the audit already cut. Task 7 (Pi) reads this directly.\n\n**Why this task exists:** yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` and committed it. It never touched `~/.agents/skills/`, where 98 of the 99 archived names still live. Pi discovers that folder natively, so the token win evaporates the moment Pi runs.\n\n**Scope limit, added after round 1.** 248 pool entries are absent from `~/.claude/skills/`. The pool has a **mixed role**: a view onto Eric's own skills *and* an install target for third-party skills (`npx skills add` — see the `vendored-skill-install` memory). This task prunes **only** the 98 that exactly match yesterday's archive list. It does not touch the other 150, which may be third-party installs other harnesses depend on. Those are `aios-audit`'s problem, with a human reading the proposal.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Compute the prune list and eyeball it**\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12\ncomm -12 <(ls ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | sort) <(ls ~/.agents/skills | sort) \\\n  > /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\nwc -l < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\ncat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n```\n\nExpected: `98`, then 98 names. Read them. Any name Eric wants back is deleted from the list file before Step 3. This is the human gate.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Two guards — live symlink targets, and support dirs**\n\n```bash\n# Guard A: nothing a live Claude skill points at may be pruned.\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec readlink {} \\; \\\n  | sed 's|.*/||' | sort > /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt\necho \"--- guard A (must be empty) ---\"\ncomm -12 /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt\n\n# Guard B: nothing without a SKILL.md may be pruned - it is support, not a skill.\necho \"--- guard B (must be empty) ---\"\nwhile IFS= read -r s; do\n  [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -d ~/.agents/skills/\"$s\" ] && [ ! -f ~/.agents/skills/\"$s\"/SKILL.md ] && echo \"SUPPORT DIR: $s\"\ndone < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n```\n\nExpected: **both empty.** Any name under guard A means a live skill points at something scheduled for pruning. Any name under guard B is a support directory like `_shared`. Remove it from `/tmp/pool-prune-list.txt` and re-run until both print nothing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Move, do not delete**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.agents/skills\nwhile IFS= read -r s; do\n  if [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -e \"$s\" ]; then mv \"$s\" ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/; fi\ndone < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\nls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l\nls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l\n```\n\nExpected: pool drops `306` → `208`; archive holds `98`.\n\n> `306 - 98 = 208`, not 61. That remainder is third-party installs and Codex-only skills. **Do not \"fix\" 208 down to 61 here** — that would delete assets other harnesses use.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Verify nothing that was working broke**\n\n```bash\nfind -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\necho \"--- dangling symlinks (must be empty) ---\"\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print\ntest -f ~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared OK\"\n```\n\nExpected: `61`, no dangling output, `_shared OK`. A dangling symlink means a guard was skipped — roll back with the Task 1 tarball.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Write the record and commit**\n\n```bash\n{\n  echo \"pool prune 2026-09-12\"\n  echo \"before: 306  after: $(ls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l | tr -d ' ')  archived: $(ls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\n  echo \"scope: only names matching ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12. Third-party installs untouched.\"\n  echo \"undo: mv ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/* ~/.agents/skills/\"\n  echo \"--- pruned ---\"\n  cat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt\n} > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt && git commit -m \"chore: prune stale skill pool mirror\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 3: Harden the sync writers — before anything moves\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: nothing.\n- Produces: a sync script safe to run at any point during Tasks 4–7. **Nothing in Task 4 may run until this task's Step 6 passes.**\n\n**Why this task comes before the move.** Round 1 put the relink first and this second. Codex caught that as R1, a data-loss defect. Today `sync-to-codex.sh` does:\n\n```bash\nrm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n```\n\n`dev-sync` instructs the user to run it after every pull. The moment `$SRC/$s` is a symlink into `$dst`, `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"` destroys the real directory and the `cp` copies a corpse. The `.ps1` twin has the same shape. Hardening first makes the whole rest of the plan safe to interrupt.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Read both current scripts and record the baseline**\n\n```bash\ncat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh\necho \"=== ps1 ===\"\ncat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1\n```\n\nExpected: the `SHARED=(...)` array and the `rm -rf` / `cp -R` pair in each. Note the current `SHARED` membership — Step 2 preserves it.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Rewrite the bash script**\n\nReplace `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh` with:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# sync-to-codex.sh -- rebuild the Codex and agent-pool VIEWS from the canonical\n# repo at ~/.claude/skills.\n#\n# CANONICAL: ~/.claude/skills   (git repo erash11/claude-skills, real dirs only)\n# VIEWS:     ~/.agents/skills   (Pi, Cowork)\n#            ~/.codex/skills    (Codex)\n#\n# Views are generated. Never author a skill in a view; it will be overwritten.\n# On macOS a view entry is a symlink; on Windows the .ps1 twin copies instead.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSRC=\"$HOME/.claude/skills\"\nSHARED=(new-project startup dev-sync closeout session-handoff humanizer articulation-skill)\nMODE=\"${SYNC_MODE:-link}\"     # link | copy\nDRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-0}\"\n\nfail=0\nfor s in \"${SHARED[@]}\"; do\n  # R1 GUARD: the canonical source must be a real directory, never a link.\n  # If it is a link, a previous migration inverted the direction and a\n  # delete-then-copy would destroy the real data. Refuse the whole run.\n  if [ -L \"$SRC/$s\" ]; then\n    echo \"FATAL $SRC/$s is a symlink. Canonical must be a real directory.\" >&2\n    echo \"      Refusing to sync; this would delete the link target.\" >&2\n    fail=1\n  fi\ndone\nif [ \"$fail\" -ne 0 ]; then\n  echo \"No changes made.\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\nfor dst in \"$HOME/.agents/skills\" \"$HOME/.codex/skills\"; do\n  mkdir -p \"$dst\"\n  for s in \"${SHARED[@]}\"; do\n    if [ ! -d \"$SRC/$s\" ]; then\n      echo \"skip $s (not in $SRC)\"\n      continue\n    fi\n    if [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then\n      echo \"would sync $s -> $dst ($MODE)\"\n      continue\n    fi\n    # Safe now: $SRC/$s is proven real, so removing the view cannot touch it.\n    rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"\n    if [ \"$MODE\" = \"link\" ]; then\n      ln -s \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n    else\n      cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n    fi\n    echo \"synced $s -> $dst ($MODE)\"\n  done\ndone\n\nif [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then\n  echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"\nfi\nexit 0\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Apply the same guard to the PowerShell twin**\n\nEdit `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`. Before its copy loop, add:\n\n```powershell\n# R1 GUARD: canonical must be a real directory, never a reparse point.\nforeach ($s in $SHARED) {\n  $p = Join-Path $SRC $s\n  if ((Test-Path $p) -and ((Get-Item $p -Force).Attributes -band [IO.FileAttributes]::ReparsePoint)) {\n    Write-Error \"FATAL $p is a link. Canonical must be a real directory. Refusing to sync.\"\n    exit 2\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWindows keeps `Copy-Item` (mode `copy`); it does not create symlinks without elevation.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Prove the guard fires — the whole point of this task**\n\n```bash\n# Build a fake inverted layout in a scratch HOME and confirm the script refuses.\nrm -rf /tmp/syncguard && mkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/{.claude/skills,.agents/skills,.codex/skills}\nmkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup\necho \"canary - must survive\" > /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup/SKILL.md\nln -s /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup /tmp/syncguard/.claude/skills/startup\nHOME=/tmp/syncguard bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\ncat /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup/SKILL.md\n```\n\nExpected: `FATAL ... is a symlink`, `exit=2`, and the canary file **still readable**. If the canary is gone, the guard does not work — stop and fix before any other task.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Prove the normal path still works**\n\n```bash\nDRY_RUN=1 bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\nbash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\nls -la ~/.codex/skills | grep -E 'startup|closeout'\n```\n\nExpected: dry run lists `would sync` lines and `exit=0`; the real run reports `synced …` and `exit=0`; the listing shows symlinks into `~/.claude/skills`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Verify `daily-backup` will not publish a symlink**\n\n```bash\ngrep -n 'skills' ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1 | head -20\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills status --short\n```\n\nExpected: `git status` is **clean**. The views live outside the repo, so the backup's `git add -A` has nothing machine-specific to pick up. If `git status` lists a symlink inside `~/.claude/skills`, stop — that is exactly R3 and it must not be committed.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Commit both copies**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.claude/skills\ngit add sync-to-codex.sh sync-to-codex.ps1\ngit commit -m \"fix: refuse to sync when canonical source is a symlink (R1)\n\nDelete-then-copy destroyed the link target when ~/.claude/skills/<name>\npointed into ~/.agents/skills. Views are now generated from a proven-real\ncanonical source; symlink on Mac, copy on Windows.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ngit push\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\nprintf '# Sync hardening 2026-09-12\\n\\nR1 fix. Guard test output:\\n\\n```\\n%s\\n```\\n' \\\n  \"$(HOME=/tmp/syncguard bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\")\" \\\n  > docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md\ngit add docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md\ngit commit -m \"docs: record the sync guard test\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 4: Invert the 19 outward symlinks into the canonical repo\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/` (19 symlinks become real dirs)\n- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (19 real dirs become links back)\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the hardened sync script from Task 3.\n- Produces: a canonical repo with **zero symlinks**, so Task 5 edits one file per skill and every harness sees it.\n\n**Direction, restated because round 1 had it backwards.** 19 entries in `~/.claude/skills/` are symlinks pointing *out* to `~/.agents/skills/`. Their real content therefore sits outside the git repo — unversioned, not on Windows, not in the backup. This task pulls each real directory **into** the repo and leaves a link behind at the pool.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the script**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Invert outward symlinks so ~/.claude/skills holds real directories only.\n#   before: ~/.claude/skills/X -> ~/.agents/skills/X   (real content outside git)\n#   after:  ~/.claude/skills/X is real; ~/.agents/skills/X -> it\n# Idempotent. DRY_RUN=1 by default.\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSRC=\"$HOME/.claude/skills\"\nPOOL=\"$HOME/.agents/skills\"\nDRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-1}\"\nmoved=0; skipped=0\n\nfor link in \"$SRC\"/*; do\n  name=\"$(basename \"$link\")\"\n  case \"$name\" in .*) continue ;; esac\n  if [ ! -L \"$link\" ]; then\n    continue\n  fi\n  target=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$link\")\" && readlink \"$link\")\"\n  abs=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$link\")\" && cd \"$(dirname \"$target\")\" 2>/dev/null && pwd)/$(basename \"$target\")\"\n  if [ ! -d \"$abs\" ]; then\n    echo \"DANGLING $name -> $target (left alone)\"\n    skipped=$((skipped+1))\n    continue\n  fi\n  if [ ! -f \"$abs/SKILL.md\" ]; then\n    echo \"NO-SKILL  $name -> $target (support dir, left alone)\"\n    skipped=$((skipped+1))\n    continue\n  fi\n  echo \"INVERT    $name  ($abs -> $SRC/$name, link back at pool)\"\n  if [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ]; then\n    rm \"$link\"\n    mv \"$abs\" \"$SRC/$name\"\n    ln -s \"$SRC/$name\" \"$abs\"\n  fi\n  moved=$((moved+1))\ndone\n\necho\necho \"inverted: $moved   skipped: $skipped\"\necho \"symlinks left in canonical: $(find \"$SRC\" -maxdepth 1 -type l | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\nif [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then\n  echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"\nfi\nexit 0\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Dry run and read every line**\n\n```bash\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh\n```\n\nExpected: ~19 `INVERT` lines, `inverted: 19`, and the DRY footer. Any `DANGLING` line is a broken skill — investigate before applying. `NO-SKILL` lines are support directories and are correctly left alone (this is the R2 fix; there is **no** instruction anywhere in this plan to delete a directory for lacking a SKILL.md).\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Apply**\n\n```bash\nDRY_RUN=0 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh\n```\n\nExpected: the same lines, then `symlinks left in canonical: 0`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Verify the canonical repo is symlink-free and complete**\n\n```bash\necho \"--- symlinks in canonical (must be 0) ---\"\nfind ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l | wc -l\necho \"--- SKILL.md count (must be 61) ---\"\nfind -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l\necho \"--- dangling pool links (must be empty) ---\"\nfind ~/.agents/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print\necho \"--- git sees real files, not links ---\"\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills status --short | head -20\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills ls-files -s | awk '$1==\"120000\"' | head\n```\n\nExpected: `0`; `61`; no dangling output; `git status` lists new real files; the `ls-files` line prints **nothing** (mode `120000` is a symlink — none may be staged). If any symlink is staged, unstage it and stop; that is R3.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Verify `_shared` consumers still resolve (the R2 check)**\n\n```bash\ntest -f ~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared in canonical OK\"\npython3 -c \"\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path.home()/'.claude/skills/meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py'\nif not p.exists():\n    print('meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py not found - check path'); raise SystemExit\ncfg = p.resolve().parents[2]/'_shared/config.json'\nprint('resolves to:', cfg)\nprint('exists:', cfg.exists())\n\"\n```\n\nExpected: `_shared in canonical OK`, and `exists: True`. If `False`, the `parents[2]` hop no longer lands on `_shared` — fix the path in `sync.py` before committing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Re-run the sync guard now that the layout changed**\n\n```bash\nDRY_RUN=1 bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: `would sync` lines and `exit=0` — **not** `FATAL`. A FATAL here means a `SHARED` entry is still a symlink and Step 3 did not finish.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Confirm Claude Code still loads the roster, then commit**\n\nRun `/reload-skills` in a Claude Code session, then `/skills`. Expected: the same personal-skill count as before (61). If it dropped, roll back per Task 1.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/.claude/skills\ngit add -A\ngit status --short | head\ngit commit -m \"refactor: canonical repo holds real directories, no symlinks\n\n19 skills whose content lived in ~/.agents/skills are now inside the repo,\nso they version and reach Windows. The pool links back to them.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ngit push\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh && git commit -m \"feat: symlink inversion script\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 5: Normalize every skill to portable frontmatter\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` (frontmatter only, body untouched)\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the symlink-free canonical repo from Task 4.\n- Produces: `skill-portability-check.py`. `aios-audit` calls it as its harness-parity check. Exit `0` when every skill passes, `1` otherwise, `2` on an empty or missing inventory.\n\n**The portable frontmatter contract** (a superset — Codex ignores keys it does not know, Claude Code ignores `metadata`):\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: skill-name                       # REQUIRED. Must equal the directory name.\n                                       # TitleCase is allowed (CreateSkill, Harvest).\ndescription: >-                        # REQUIRED. <=1024 chars. Must contain BOTH:\n  <what it does, one sentence>.        #   (a) a positive clause: \"Use when ...\" with\n  Use when <literal trigger phrases>.  #       literal phrases the user would type\n  Do not use when <negative>.          #   (b) a negative clause: \"Do not use ...\" /\n                                       #       \"NOT FOR ...\" - Codex needs this to\n                                       #       avoid over-firing\nmetadata:\n  short-description: <one short line>  # optional, Codex-only, Claude ignores\nargument-hint: \"[...]\"                 # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep        # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\n---\n```\n\nClaude Code triggers on literal phrases; Codex leans on the negative clause. A description with only one half fires reliably in one harness and badly in the other. **Naming is settled here (R7):** the rule is `name` equals the directory name. TitleCase is legal — `CreateSkill` and `Harvest` already ship that way and renaming them would break every cross-reference.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing check**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Check every skill in the canonical repo against the portable frontmatter\ncontract. Exit 0 = all pass, 1 = failures, 2 = empty/missing inventory.\nstdlib only (PEP 668 machine). Parses the flat YAML subset we actually use.\"\"\"\nimport os, re, sys, glob\n\nCANON = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.claude/skills\")\nALLOWED = {\"name\", \"description\", \"metadata\", \"argument-hint\", \"allowed-tools\",\n           \"model\", \"disable-model-invocation\", \"license\", \"version\", \"author\"}\nPOSITIVE = (\"use when\", \"use for\", \"use this skill when\", \"invoke when\")\nNEGATIVE = (\"do not use\", \"don't use\", \"not for\", \"never use\")\n\ndef frontmatter(text):\n    if not text.startswith(\"---\"):\n        return None\n    end = text.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n    return None if end == -1 else text[4:end]\n\ndef top_keys(fm):\n    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)\\s*:\", fm)]\n\ndef scalar(fm, key):\n    \"\"\"Read a flat scalar, including >- and | block styles.\"\"\"\n    m = re.search(rf\"(?ms)^{re.escape(key)}\\s*:[ \\t]*(.*?)(?=^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*\\s*:|\\Z)\", fm)\n    if not m:\n        return \"\"\n    raw = m.group(1)\n    raw = re.sub(r\"^[>|][-+]?\\s*\\n\", \"\", raw)          # strip a block indicator\n    val = \" \".join(l.strip() for l in raw.splitlines() if l.strip())\n    return val.strip().strip('\"').strip(\"'\")\n\ndef check(path):\n    name = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path))\n    problems = []\n    fm = frontmatter(open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read())\n    if fm is None:\n        return name, [\"no YAML frontmatter\"]\n    keys = top_keys(fm)\n    declared = scalar(fm, \"name\")\n    if \"name\" not in keys:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'name'\")\n    elif declared != name:\n        problems.append(f\"name '{declared}' != directory '{name}'\")\n    desc = scalar(fm, \"description\")\n    if not desc:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'description'\")\n    else:\n        low = desc.lower()\n        if len(desc) > 1024:\n            problems.append(f\"description {len(desc)} chars (>1024)\")\n        pos = next((p for p in POSITIVE if p in low), None)\n        neg = next((n for n in NEGATIVE if n in low), None)\n        if not pos:\n            problems.append(\"no positive clause ('Use when ...') - Claude will not trigger\")\n        if not neg:\n            problems.append(\"no negative clause ('Do not use ...') - Codex over-fires\")\n        # R7: the two clauses must be distinct text, not one phrase counted twice.\n        if pos and neg and low.find(pos) == low.find(neg):\n            problems.append(\"positive and negative clauses overlap\")\n    for k in keys:\n        if k not in ALLOWED:\n            problems.append(f\"unknown frontmatter key '{k}'\")\n    return name, problems\n\ndef main():\n    if not os.path.isdir(CANON):\n        print(f\"FATAL canonical dir missing: {CANON}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    paths = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(CANON, \"*\", \"SKILL.md\")))\n    if not paths:\n        print(f\"FATAL no SKILL.md found under {CANON} - refusing to report success\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    failed = 0\n    for p in paths:\n        name, probs = check(p)\n        if probs:\n            failed += 1\n            print(f\"FAIL {name}\")\n            for x in probs:\n                print(f\"       - {x}\")\n    print(f\"\\nchecked {len(paths)} skills, {failed} failing, {len(paths)-failed} portable\")\n    return 1 if failed else 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Prove the checker's own failure modes first (R7)**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"baseline exit=$?\"\n\n# An empty inventory must NOT report success.\nrm -rf /tmp/emptycanon && mkdir -p /tmp/emptycanon\nHOME=/tmp/emptycanon python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"empty exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: a long FAIL list and `baseline exit=1`; then `FATAL ...` and `empty exit=2`. If the empty case prints `0 failing` and exits 0, the R7 fix is not in — stop.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Fix frontmatter in priority order, highest-use first**\n\nDo **not** bulk-rewrite with sed. Each description is a trigger surface; a careless edit silently stops a skill firing. Edit only the frontmatter block of `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, in this order:\n\n1. Confirmed working set (3+ invocations): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `backup`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`, `codex-review`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, `doctor-plus`, `startup`, `new-project`, `status-report`.\n2. Everything else Claude Code exposes.\n3. Codex-only skills last.\n\nWorked example — `backup` today has triggers but no negative clause:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: >-\n  Run the KeystoneOS daily backup on demand (git-push the memory repos, rsync\n  state to Google Drive and the CRASHBOX SSD, reindex memsearch), then report\n  what was saved and what was skipped. Use when the user says \"/backup\", \"run\n  the backup\", \"back up now\", \"did the backup run\", \"the backup failed\", or\n  when a session-start HOOK FAILURE names daily-backup. Do not use for\n  whole-stack health checks - use claude-stack-doctor instead.\n```\n\nRe-check after each batch of five:\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py | tail -3\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm it passes**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: `checked 61 skills, 0 failing, 61 portable` and `exit=0`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Prove portability in both harnesses, not just the linter**\n\nPick three edited skills. In a fresh Claude Code session, type one trigger phrase for each and confirm it fires. In a fresh Codex session (`cx`), do the same. Record all six results in the report. A linter that passes while a skill stops firing is exactly the failure this step exists to catch.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Write the report and commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n{\n  echo \"# Skill portability normalization - 2026-09-12\"\n  echo\n  echo \"Contract: name (== directory name, TitleCase allowed) + description with\"\n  echo \"BOTH a positive 'Use when' clause and a negative 'Do not use' clause.\"\n  echo \"argument-hint and allowed-tools are Claude-only; metadata is Codex-only.\"\n  echo\n  echo '```'\n  python3 scripts/skill-portability-check.py || true\n  echo '```'\n  echo\n  echo \"## Live trigger test (Step 5)\"\n  echo \"| skill | Claude Code | Codex |\"\n  echo \"|---|---|---|\"\n  echo \"| FILL IN | | |\"\n} > docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit add scripts/skill-portability-check.py docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: portable skill frontmatter contract + checker\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ncd ~/.claude/skills && git add -A && git commit -m \"chore: portable frontmatter across the roster\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git push\n```\n\nFill in the trigger-test table from the real runs. A report left with `FILL IN` is a failed task.\n\n---\n\n### Task 6: `rot.md` and the `aios-audit` compound skill\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/.claude/rot.md`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`\n- Modify: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md` (cross-reference line only)\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `skill-portability-check.py` (Task 5) and the canonical repo (Task 4).\n- Produces: `aios-metrics.py`, printing a machine-readable metrics block Task 7's launchd job captures.\n\n**Placement is deliberate.** This skill is **project-level**, in `KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/`, not global — the promotion rule from the video (31:32). It gets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/` only after proving useful across 2+ projects.\n\n**It must not become a sixth audit skill.** Five exist and each owns a layer:\n\n| Existing skill | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `os-audit` (project) | project data: routing, index truth, freshness, duplication |\n| `brain-audit` | SecondBrain as a system — the four C's |\n| `improve-system` | note-level rot: stale seeds, duplicates, conflicts |\n| `claude-stack-doctor` | automation plumbing: launchd, venvs, memsearch, backup freshness |\n| `doctor-plus` | context-engineering posture of the workspace |\n\n`aios-audit` owns the one layer none of them touch — **the harness itself**: skill roster, agents, hooks, rules, cross-harness parity. It **delegates** the other five rather than re-implementing them.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write `~/.claude/rot.md`**\n\nNote the `Last audited` column (R10): every layer starts `unknown` except Skills, which was genuinely audited. A date is written only after that layer's check actually runs.\n\n```markdown\n# rot.md — how fast each AIOS layer goes stale\n\nAudit each layer on its own clock, not all at once. `aios-audit` reads this\nfile: any layer past due, or marked `unknown`, is a finding.\n\n**A date here is a receipt, not an intention.** Write one only after that\nlayer's check in the last column has actually run and produced output.\n\n| Layer | Where it lives | Rots | Audit every | Last audited | Owner | Concrete check |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Identity | `global-memory/USER.md` | very slow | 3 months | unknown | memory-write | Read USER.md; flag any role, family, or career fact contradicted by the last 90 days of sessions. |\n| Substrate | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `~/DEV/*.md` routers | slow | 3 months | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 1 (routing integrity). |\n| Skills | `~/.claude/skills/` | fast | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit | `skill-portability-check.py` + never-used list + the crutch test below. |\n| Agents | `~/.claude/agents/` | fast | 1 month | unknown | aios-audit | Count `*.md`. Currently **0 exist** — report \"no agents defined\" and skip until one does. |\n| Rules | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` behaviour rules, output styles | fastest | 1 week | unknown | aios-audit | Diff CLAUDE.md against the last 7 days of sessions: any rule contradicted by what actually happened, and any correction the user repeated twice that is not yet a rule. |\n| Hooks | `~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/` | slow | 6 months | unknown | claude-stack-doctor | Delegate. Also diff the two copies — they must match (`hook-two-copies-trap`). |\n| Memory | `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `global-memory/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | improve-system | Delegate to `improve-system`. Also check `MEMORY.md` char cap. |\n| Data feeds | Fireflies, GPS, CFBD, Zotero | varies | 1 week | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 3 (freshness). |\n| Vault | `~/SecondBrain/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | brain-audit | Delegate to `brain-audit` (four C's score). |\n| Context posture | workspace-wide | medium | 3 months | unknown | doctor-plus | Delegate to `doctor-plus`. |\n\n## The question that governs a skill's fate\n\nNot \"how often did I run it?\" but **\"would the current model do this fine\nwithout the skill?\"** If yes, the skill is a crutch that now limits the model.\nDelete it. Keep only skills carrying tacit knowledge the model cannot know:\nforce-plate thresholds, GPS conventions, Baylor reporting norms, Eric's own\nroutines.\n\nSeasonal skills (`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`) are exempt from low-use\npressure. Judge them in season.\n\n## Layout invariants the audit must assert\n\n- `~/.claude/skills/` is canonical, is a git repo, and contains **no symlinks**.\n- `~/.agents/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` are **generated views**. A real\n  directory in either means someone edited a view instead of the source.\n- Support directories (`_shared/`) have no SKILL.md and must never be pruned.\n\n## Direction of travel\n\n**Fewer skills, stricter rules.** Skills get deleted; rules get sharpened.\n```\n\nVerify: `head -30 ~/.claude/rot.md`\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Write the metrics script**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`. Counting is deduped by real path (R6) and session globbing is explicitly recursive:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Machine-readable AIOS metrics. stdlib only. Prints key=value lines only.\"\"\"\nimport os, re, glob, subprocess, collections, datetime, sys\n\nH = os.path.expanduser(\"~\")\nCANON = f\"{H}/.claude/skills\"\nPOOL = f\"{H}/.agents/skills\"\nCODEX = f\"{H}/.codex/skills\"\nBUILTIN = {'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin',\n'doctor','help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp',\n'hooks','memory','review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir',\n'bug','release-notes','pr-comments','ide','permissions','statusline',\n'output-style','todos','export','privacy-settings','upgrade','fast','artifacts',\n'tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind','sandbox','feedback','worktrees',\n'skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes','keybindings','theme'}\n\ndef skills_in(root):\n    \"\"\"Count distinct skills under root, deduped by real path (R6).\n    glob traverses symlinked dirs, so a link and its target counted twice.\"\"\"\n    seen = set()\n    for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\")):\n        s = os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\")\n        if os.path.isfile(s):\n            seen.add(os.path.realpath(s))\n    return len(seen)\n\ndef names_in(root):\n    return {os.path.basename(d) for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\"))\n            if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\"))}\n\ndef usage():\n    c = collections.Counter()\n    for f in glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl\", recursive=True):\n        try:\n            for line in open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\"):\n                if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line:\n                    continue\n                for m in re.finditer(\n                        r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):\n                    c[m.group(1)] += 1\n                for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):\n                    c[m.group(1)] += 1\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return {k: v for k, v in c.items()\n            if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}\n\ndef roster_tokens():\n    \"\"\"Frontmatter chars / 4 - what the roster costs per session.\"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for p in glob.glob(f\"{CANON}/*/SKILL.md\"):\n        try:\n            t = open(p, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read()\n            end = t.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n            if t.startswith(\"---\") and end != -1:\n                total += len(t[4:end])\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n    return total // 4\n\ndef real_dirs_in_view(root):\n    \"\"\"A real dir in a generated view means someone edited the wrong copy.\"\"\"\n    n = 0\n    for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\")):\n        b = os.path.basename(d)\n        if b.startswith('.'):\n            continue\n        if os.path.isdir(d) and not os.path.islink(d) and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\")):\n            n += 1\n    return n\n\nu = usage()\ncanon_names = names_in(CANON)\nout = [\n    (\"date\", datetime.date.today().isoformat()),\n    (\"canonical_skills\", skills_in(CANON)),\n    (\"pool_entries\", len(glob.glob(f\"{POOL}/*\"))),\n    (\"pool_skills\", skills_in(POOL)),\n    (\"codex_skills\", skills_in(CODEX)),\n    (\"project_skills\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/DEV/*/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md\"))),\n    (\"symlinks_in_canonical\", len([d for d in glob.glob(f\"{CANON}/*\") if os.path.islink(d)])),\n    (\"real_dirs_in_pool_view\", real_dirs_in_view(POOL)),\n    (\"real_dirs_in_codex_view\", real_dirs_in_view(CODEX)),\n    (\"agents\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/agents/*.md\"))),\n    (\"hooks_claude\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/hooks/*\"))),\n    (\"hooks_codex\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.codex/hooks/*\"))),\n    (\"sessions\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl\", recursive=True))),\n    (\"skills_ever_used\", len(u)),\n    (\"skills_used_3plus\", len([k for k, v in u.items() if v >= 3])),\n    (\"skills_used_once\", len([k for k, v in u.items() if v == 1])),\n    (\"never_used\", len(canon_names - set(u))),\n    (\"roster_tokens_est\", roster_tokens()),\n]\nrc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, f\"{H}/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\"],\n                    capture_output=True, text=True)\nm = re.search(r\"(\\d+) failing\", rc.stdout)\nout.append((\"portability_failures\", m.group(1) if m else f\"error_rc{rc.returncode}\"))\n\nfor k, v in out:\n    print(f\"{k}={v}\")\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Run it and check every value**\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n```\n\nExpected: 19 `key=value` lines, no stack trace, and specifically:\n\n- `canonical_skills=61`\n- `symlinks_in_canonical=0` — the Task 4 invariant\n- `real_dirs_in_pool_view=0` and `real_dirs_in_codex_view=0` — views are generated\n- `project_skills=2` (`os-audit` + `aios-audit`)\n- `portability_failures=0`\n\nAny non-zero in the middle three means a later hand-edit broke the layout. Fix before continuing.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Write the `aios-audit` skill**\n\nInvoke `CreateSkill` first — it owns skill authoring here. Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` with frontmatter that satisfies Task 5's contract:\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: aios-audit\ndescription: >-\n  Audit the whole AI operating system across decay tiers - skills, agents,\n  hooks, rules, memory, and cross-harness parity between Claude Code, Codex,\n  and Pi - then propose exactly two next builds. Use when the user says\n  \"/aios-audit\", \"audit my system\", \"audit my AI OS\", \"audit the bloat\",\n  \"is my setup rotting\", \"what should I cut\", \"are my skills portable\",\n  \"monthly skill audit\", or when rot.md shows a layer past its audit date.\n  Do not use for project data drift, routing, or index freshness - use\n  os-audit. Do not use for launchd, venv, or backup health - use\n  claude-stack-doctor. Do not use for SecondBrain note rot - use\n  improve-system or brain-audit.\nmetadata:\n  short-description: Decay-tier audit of the AI OS; delegates to the five layer-specific audit skills.\nargument-hint: \"[optional: a layer name from rot.md]\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep, Glob, Agent\n---\n```\n\nThe body must specify, in order:\n\n1. **Read-only.** The only writes are the report and `rot.md`'s `Last audited` column. **Never delete a skill during the audit** — propose, then wait for approval.\n2. **Step 0 — read `~/.claude/rot.md`.** Compute days since each layer's `Last audited`. A layer that is past due **or `unknown`** gets audited this run; layers inside their window are reported green and skipped. That is what makes a monthly run cheap.\n3. **Step 1 — run `python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`** and capture every value.\n4. **Step 2 — assert the layout invariants** from `rot.md`. `symlinks_in_canonical`, `real_dirs_in_pool_view`, `real_dirs_in_codex_view` must all be `0`. Any non-zero is a RED finding: someone edited a generated view instead of the canonical repo.\n5. **Step 3 — delegate, do not duplicate.** For each due layer, invoke the owning skill named in the `rot.md` table and fold its findings under that layer's heading. Only the harness layer is audited directly.\n6. **Step 4 — the harness layer, audited directly:**\n   - **Bloat:** every canonical skill never invoked across all sessions. List them.\n   - **Crutch test:** for each never-used and once-used skill, answer the `rot.md` question — *would the current model do this fine without it?* Recommend DELETE, KEEP (tacit knowledge), or SEASONAL, one line of reasoning each.\n   - **Overlap:** skills whose descriptions cover the same trigger space. Recommend merging.\n   - **Parity:** run `skill-portability-check.py`. Every failure is a finding.\n   - **Promotion:** global skills used in fewer than 2 projects — demotion candidates. Project skills used in 2+ — promotion candidates.\n   - **Rules:** diff `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` against the last 7 days of sessions. Report rules contradicted by what actually happened, and corrections the user repeated twice that are not yet rules.\n   - **Agents:** count `~/.claude/agents/*.md`. If `0`, report \"no agents defined\" and skip — do not invent a finding.\n7. **Step 5 — write the report** to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, append the metrics row to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`, and update `Last audited` in `rot.md` **only for layers whose check actually ran and produced output**.\n8. **Step 6 — end with exactly two proposed next builds,** ranked, each with the one command that starts it. Not ten. Two.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Cross-reference `os-audit` so the two do not fight**\n\nAdd near the top of `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md`:\n\n```markdown\n> Scope note: this skill audits **project data** — routing, indexes, freshness,\n> duplication. The harness layer (skills, agents, hooks, rules, cross-harness\n> parity) belongs to `aios-audit`, which calls this skill for the data layer.\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Smoke-test end to end**\n\nIn a Claude Code session with cwd `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`, run `/reload-skills`, then type \"audit my AI OS\". Expected: `aios-audit` fires, reads `rot.md`, runs the metrics script, produces a report, and ends with exactly two proposals.\n\n```bash\nls -l ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-*.md\ngrep -c 'unknown' ~/.claude/rot.md\n```\n\nExpected: a report file exists. The `unknown` count should have **dropped** by exactly the number of layers whose checks ran — not to zero.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ncp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md\ngit add .claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md .claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md \\\n        scripts/aios-metrics.py docs/rot.md docs/audits/\ngit commit -m \"feat: aios-audit compound skill + rot.md decay tiers\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 7: Put the metrics on a monthly clock\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/rot.md` (cadence note)\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `aios-metrics.py` from Task 6.\n- Produces: a monthly trend row in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`. Terminal deliverable.\n\n**Design choice, tightened after R9.** The job runs the **metrics script only**, appends a trend row, and writes a nudge. It does **not** commit, does **not** push, and does **not** run the agentic audit. Reasons, all from R9: committing from the shared working tree publishes whatever the user had staged; a `git push` failure is easy to mask; and the 09:00 backup may run up to 240 minutes, so start-time spacing is not a race guard. The human commits the trend row during the audit. The job's only job is to make the audit impossible to forget.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the cron wrapper**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Monthly: append AIOS metrics to the trend table and nudge for a real audit.\n# Deliberately does NOT commit, push, or run the agentic audit (see R9):\n#  - committing from the shared tree would publish the user's staged files\n#  - the agentic audit proposes deletions and needs a human reading them\nset -uo pipefail\n\nREPO=\"$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS\"\nTREND=\"$REPO/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nLOG=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\"\nLOCK=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$LOG\")\" \"$(dirname \"$TREND\")\"\n\nexec >>\"$LOG\" 2>&1\necho \"=== $(date -u +%FT%TZ) aios-audit-cron ===\"\n\n# Single-instance lock. mkdir is atomic; no flock on stock macOS.\nif ! mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then\n  echo \"ABORT another run holds $LOCK\"\n  exit 0\nfi\ntrap 'rmdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null' EXIT\n\nif ! M=\"$(python3 \"$REPO/scripts/aios-metrics.py\" 2>&1)\"; then\n  echo \"FAIL metrics script errored:\"\n  echo \"$M\"\n  exit 1\nfi\necho \"$M\"\n\nget() {\n  local v\n  v=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$M\" | grep \"^$1=\" | cut -d= -f2-)\"\n  if [ -z \"$v\" ]; then echo \"MISSING\"; else echo \"$v\"; fi\n}\n\nif [ ! -f \"$TREND\" ]; then\n  {\n    echo \"# AIOS trend\"\n    echo\n    echo \"One row per monthly run. Written by scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh.\"\n    echo \"The script does not commit; the human commits during the audit.\"\n    echo\n    echo \"| date | canonical | pool | codex | project | never used | roster tok | portability fails | symlinks in canon |\"\n    echo \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"\n  } > \"$TREND\"\nfi\n\nROW=\"$(printf '| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |' \\\n  \"$(get date)\" \"$(get canonical_skills)\" \"$(get pool_skills)\" \"$(get codex_skills)\" \\\n  \"$(get project_skills)\" \"$(get never_used)\" \"$(get roster_tokens_est)\" \\\n  \"$(get portability_failures)\" \"$(get symlinks_in_canonical)\")\"\n\nif printf '%s' \"$ROW\" | grep -q 'MISSING'; then\n  echo \"FAIL a metrics key did not resolve; row not written:\"\n  echo \"$ROW\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nprintf '%s\\n' \"$ROW\" >> \"$TREND\"\necho \"wrote: $ROW\"\n\necho \"[$(date +%F)] aios-audit due - run \\\"audit my AI OS\\\" in ~/DEV/KeystoneOS (uncommitted trend row waiting)\" \\\n  >> \"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\"\n\necho \"=== done ok ===\"\nexit 0\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run it by hand and verify the trend row**\n\n```bash\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\ncat ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\ntail -25 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\ngit -C ~/DEV/KeystoneOS status --short\n```\n\nExpected: `exit=0`; a header plus one row with **no `MISSING` cells**; the log ends `=== done ok ===`; `git status` shows `aios-trend.md` as **modified but uncommitted** — that is correct, the human commits it.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Prove the failure paths (R9's masked-failure point)**\n\n```bash\n# A bad metrics script must fail the job, not write a row.\nmv ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py /tmp/aios-metrics.bak\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 1)\"\nmv /tmp/aios-metrics.bak ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n\n# A stale lock must not wedge the job forever.\nmkdir -p ~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 0, ABORT logged)\"\nrmdir ~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\ntail -6 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n```\n\nExpected: the first prints `exit=1` and adds **no** row; the second prints `exit=0` with `ABORT` in the log and **no** row. If either wrote a row, the failure propagation is not working — fix before loading launchd.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Write the launchd plist**\n\nCreate `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`. Day 1 at 14:00 — well clear of the 09:00 backup's possible 240-minute window, and the lock covers the rest:\n\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\"\n  \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n<plist version=\"1.0\">\n<dict>\n  <key>Label</key><string>com.keystone.aiosaudit</string>\n  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n  <array>\n    <string>/bin/bash</string>\n    <string>-lc</string>\n    <string>$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh</string>\n  </array>\n  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>Day</key><integer>1</integer>\n    <key>Hour</key><integer>14</integer>\n    <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err</string>\n</dict>\n</plist>\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Load it and verify launchd accepted it**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist 2>/dev/null\nlaunchctl load  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist\nlaunchctl list | grep aiosaudit\n```\n\nExpected: one line ending `com.keystone.aiosaudit`. A non-zero middle column means the last run failed — read `/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Force a run through launchd, not just bash**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl start com.keystone.aiosaudit\ncat /tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err\ntail -5 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n```\n\nExpected: `.err` empty, a second row appended. launchd has a different PATH than an interactive shell — this step catches that. If `python3` is not found, put an absolute interpreter in `aios-metrics.py`'s shebang.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: De-duplicate test rows, note the cadence, commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np = \"docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nlines = open(p).read().splitlines()\nseen, out = set(), []\nfor l in lines:\n    if l.startswith(\"| 20\"):\n        if l in seen:\n            continue\n        seen.add(l)\n    out.append(l)\nopen(p, \"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\")\nEOF\ncat docs/audits/aios-trend.md\ncat >> ~/.claude/rot.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Cadence\n\nMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 14:00.\nIt writes a trend row and a nudge. It does not commit and does not push.\nThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.\nEOF\ncp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md\ngit add scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh docs/audits/aios-trend.md docs/rot.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: monthly AIOS metrics on launchd, no unattended commits\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 8: Pi trial under a real boundary\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: macOS standard user `piuser` (or an equivalent enforced boundary)\n- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md`\n- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: a copy of the pruned pool (Task 2) and the portable frontmatter (Task 5).\n- Produces: a measured migration cost. Nothing consumes it; it is a decision input.\n\n**Why the boundary is mandatory (R5).** Round 1 ran Pi with `cd ~/pi-trial` and called that a sandbox. It is not — `cd` restricts nothing, and the Pi research note says so explicitly. Pi ships no approval prompts by design. A regex denylist inside the agent is defence-in-depth, **not** a boundary: `cat ../DEV/file`, a shell-expanded path, or any tool the guard does not cover walks straight past it. The only enforced boundary available without new infrastructure is **OS file permissions**, so Pi runs as a different user that cannot read Eric's home.\n\n**Scope, narrowed.** The deliverable is a **cost number**, not a working Pi setup. **ADOPT is not an available verdict in this plan** — adopting Pi for real work requires a boundary design that is its own project. Verdicts here are `COST-MEASURED` or `BLOCKED`.\n\n**Stop conditions.** If the user cannot be created, or Pi will not install, or the guard does not block after two attempts, stop and write up what failed. Do not iterate past that.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Create the boundary user**\n\nThis needs admin rights and is the one step Eric may prefer to run himself:\n\n```bash\nsudo sysadminctl -addUser piuser -fullName \"Pi Trial\" -password - -home /Users/piuser\nsudo dscl . -read /Users/piuser NFSHomeDirectory\n```\n\nThen prove the boundary actually holds:\n\n```bash\nchmod 700 ~                      # confirm Eric's home is not world-readable\nsudo -u piuser ls ~/DEV          2>&1 | tail -2\nsudo -u piuser ls ~/SecondBrain  2>&1 | tail -2\n```\n\nExpected: both `ls` attempts print **Permission denied**. If either succeeds, the boundary does not exist — STOP. Do not fall back to \"just be careful.\"\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Install Pi as that user**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/pi-trial\nsudo -u piuser npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent 2>&1 | tail -5\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'which -a pi && pi --version'\n```\n\nExpected: a path and a version string. `--ignore-scripts` is deliberate — no postinstall scripts run.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Give it a copy of the pruned pool, not the real one**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/.agents\nsudo rsync -a --copy-links ~/.agents/skills/ /Users/piuser/.agents/skills/\nsudo chown -R piuser:staff /Users/piuser/.agents\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'ls /Users/piuser/.agents/skills | wc -l'\n```\n\nExpected: the Task 2 post-prune count (208). A **copy**, so a Pi misstep cannot corrupt the real pool.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm what Pi discovers**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cat > /Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md <<EOF\n# pi-trial\n\nThrowaway sandbox for evaluating the Pi harness. Nothing here is real work.\nThis user has no read access to the operator home directory by design.\nEOF\ncd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"List every skill you can see. Print only the count and the first ten names.\" 2>&1 | tail -20'\n```\n\nExpected: a count near 208, not 306. **If it reads ~306, Task 2 did not take — stop and re-run it.** This step is the proof that pruning the pool was the right fix.\n\n> Round 1 had a probe here that asked an unguarded Pi to `ls ~/DEV`. It is deleted. Pointing an unrestrained agent at real directories to demonstrate that it is unrestrained is not a test worth running. The OS boundary in Step 1 already establishes the same fact safely.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Port `dcg` to a Pi extension, using the documented contract**\n\nRead the existing guard first:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.claude/hooks/ | grep -i -E 'dcg|guard'\ngrep -n 'pi.on\\|ExtensionAPI\\|event.input' ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md\n```\n\nRound 1 used `export default { tool_call }` and `ev.args`. The research note documents an **exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`**, registration via `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, and a payload at `event.input` (R4). Write `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts` to the documented shape. If the note and the installed Pi version disagree, **the installed version wins** — record the discrepancy in the report.\n\n```typescript\n// Minimal port of the dcg guard. Proves the hook -> extension mapping.\n// Defence in depth only: the real boundary is the piuser file permissions.\nconst DENY: RegExp[] = [\n  /\\brm\\s+-rf\\s+\\/(?!tmp)/,\n  /DexaProject/i,\n  /GoogleDrive-/,\n  /(^|[\\s\"'=:])~?\\/?DEV(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,        // R4: bare ~/DEV must match\n  /(^|[\\s\"'=:])~?\\/?SecondBrain(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,\n];\n\nexport default function guard(pi: any) {\n  pi.on(\"tool_call\", (event: any) => {\n    const blob = JSON.stringify(event?.input ?? {});   // R4: input, not args\n    for (const re of DENY) {\n      if (re.test(blob)) {\n        return { block: true, reason: `guard: blocked by ${re}` };\n      }\n    }\n    return { block: false };\n  });\n}\n```\n\nPrint mode may require explicitly trusting a project extension; if Pi asks, grant it for this project only and note the step in the report.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Prove the guard blocks — and that it does not block everything**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"Run: ls /Users/piuser/DEV | head -3\" 2>&1 | tail -10'\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"Run: echo hello\" 2>&1 | tail -5'\n```\n\nExpected: the first is **blocked** with the guard reason; the second **succeeds**. A guard that blocks everything is not a guard. The probe targets a path inside `piuser`'s own home, so the guard — not the OS — is what must stop it. If the first is not blocked after two attempts at the extension path or event name, stop per the stop conditions.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Write the cost measurement**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ncat > docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md <<'EOF'\n# Pi trial - 2026-09-12\n\nBoundary: separate macOS user `piuser`, no read access to the operator home.\n`cd` into a directory is NOT a boundary and was not treated as one (R5).\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| pi version | FILL IN |\n| piuser blocked from ~/DEV and ~/SecondBrain? | FILL IN (expect: yes, Permission denied) |\n| skills auto-discovered | FILL IN (expect ~208, not 306) |\n| skills needing edits to fire in Pi | FILL IN |\n| extension contract matched the research note? | FILL IN (factory + pi.on + event.input) |\n| minutes to port the dcg guard | FILL IN |\n| hooks still to port | FILL IN of 61 |\n| verdict | COST-MEASURED / BLOCKED |\n\n## What the guard proved\n\nFILL IN: paste the blocked and the allowed output from Step 6.\n\n## What a real adoption would still need\n\nNot in scope here. At minimum: a boundary design that is not a second user\naccount, an approval-prompt layer, and a port of the remaining hooks.\nEOF\n```\n\nFill in every `FILL IN` from the real runs. A report left with placeholders is a failed task. Then:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md && git commit -m \"docs: Pi harness trial under an OS boundary + dcg guard port cost\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 8: Tear down**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser npm uninstall -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent\nsudo rm -rf /Users/piuser/pi-trial /Users/piuser/.agents\n# Keep or remove the account - Eric's call. To remove:\n# sudo sysadminctl -deleteUser piuser\n```\n\nThe account may stay for future trials. The skill copy and the Pi install should not linger.\n\n---\n\n## Review Gates (not optional)\n\nEric asked for Codex review of the plan and of any code. These are steps.\n\n- [x] **Gate A — round 1, done 2026-09-12.** Verdict **REVISE**, 6 high / 4 medium. Result at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json`. All ten dispositioned in the table above; R1/R3 inverted the architecture.\n- [ ] **Gate A2 — round 2, blocking.** Resume the same reviewer session rather than starting fresh, so the new plan hash is recorded against it:\n\n  ```bash\n  cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n  python3 ~/.claude/skills/claudex-loop/scripts/runner.py review \\\n    --host claude --repo ~/DEV/KeystoneOS \\\n    --plan docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md \\\n    --resume ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json \\\n    --feedback /tmp/aios-dispositions.md \\\n    --artifacts ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r2\n  ```\n\n  `/tmp/aios-dispositions.md` is written from the disposition table above — R1–R10, accept or reject, and what changed. **No task below runs until this returns APPROVED.** R1 and R3 are data-loss grade.\n- [ ] **Gate B — after Task 5.** `codex-review` on `sync-to-codex.sh`, `sync-to-codex.ps1`, `skills-canonicalize.sh`, `skill-portability-check.py`, and ten rewritten frontmatter blocks. Codex is the right reviewer here specifically because it is the harness whose triggering the rewrite claims to fix.\n- [ ] **Gate C — after Task 7.** `codex-review` on `aios-metrics.py`, `aios-audit-cron.sh`, the plist, and the `aios-audit` SKILL.md body.\n- [ ] **Gate D — before declaring done.** `superpowers:verification-before-completion`. Every success claim carries a pasted command output.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Rewriting plugin skills (`superpowers`, `mattpocock-skills`) — vendored, overwritten on update.\n- Pruning the ~150 pool entries that are third-party installs. Task 2 touches only the 98 that match yesterday's archive. The rest need a human decision via `aios-audit`.\n- Migrating all 61 hooks to Pi. Task 8 ports exactly one to measure cost.\n- **Adopting Pi.** Task 8 produces a cost number only. Adoption needs a boundary design that is its own project.\n- Deleting any skill outright. Everything moves to an archive folder or is recoverable from git.\n- The Windows machine. Both machines are active; this plan changes Mac paths only. `sync-to-codex.ps1` is patched in Task 3, but re-running Tasks 2 and 5 there is separate work after these prove out here.\n- Mining the 414 JSONL sessions to reverse-engineer harness behaviour (video gap #9). Real, but its own project.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":2,"oldLines":45,"newStart":2,"newLines":55,"lines":[" "," > **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking."," ","-**Goal:** Make every skill Eric owns load identically in Claude Code, Codex, and Pi from one canonical folder, and build a recurring audit that keeps the whole AIOS from rotting.","+**Goal:** Make every skill Eric owns load identically in Claude Code, Codex, and Pi from one versioned canonical folder, and build a recurring audit that keeps the whole AIOS from rotting."," ","-**Architecture:** Collapse three competing skill folders into one canonical pool at `~/.agents/skills/` holding only the survivors, with `~/.claude/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` as thin symlink views onto it. Normalize every survivor's frontmatter to a Codex-safe core (`name` + `description`) plus Claude-only extras that Codex ignores. Then add one compound audit skill (`aios-audit`) that orchestrates the five existing audit skills instead of duplicating them, driven by a `rot.md` decay table and fired monthly by launchd.","+**Architecture:** `~/.claude/skills/` is the canonical home — it is already its own git repo (`erash11/claude-skills`) and is the only copy that versions and crosses to Windows. It holds **real directories only; no symlink is ever committed.** `~/.agents/skills/` (Pi, Cowork) and `~/.codex/skills/` (Codex) become **generated views**, rebuilt per machine by a hardened `sync-to-codex.sh` — symlinks on Mac, copies on Windows, never authored by hand. Every skill's frontmatter is normalized to a Codex-safe core so the same file fires in all three harnesses. Then one compound audit skill (`aios-audit`) orchestrates the five existing audit skills, driven by a `rot.md` decay table."," ","-**Tech Stack:** bash, python3 (stdlib only), launchd, Agent Skills standard (SKILL.md + YAML frontmatter), Claude Code, Codex CLI, Pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`).","+**Tech Stack:** bash, PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, for the `.ps1` twin), python3 (stdlib only), launchd, Agent Skills standard (SKILL.md + YAML frontmatter), Claude Code, Codex CLI 0.154.0, Pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`)."," "," **Spec:** `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md` (gap analysis rows 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10) and `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`."," ","+**Review history:** Round 1 Codex review returned **REVISE** with 6 high / 4 medium findings (R1–R10). Result: `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json`. All ten are dispositioned in the table below. **The architecture above is the inverse of round 1's** — round 1 made `~/.agents/skills/` canonical, which R1/R3 showed would cause data loss.","+"," ---"," "," ## Global Constraints"," ","-- **Never point Pi at `~/DEV`.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — documented, deliberate design. Pi work happens only in `~/pi-trial`.","-- **Never let any harness near `DexaProject`** (identifiable athlete health data, lives on Google Drive, not in `~/DEV`).","-- `~/.claude/skills/` is **gitignored** inside the `dotclaude` repo. Git is not a safety net. The tarball snapshot in Task 1 is the only undo for this plan.","-- **Plugin skills are out of scope.** `superpowers` (14 skills) and `mattpocock-skills` (35 skills) are vendored and get overwritten on update. Do not rewrite their frontmatter. Scope = the 61 skills in `~/.claude/skills/` and whatever `~/.agents/skills/` keeps.","-- **Hooks exist in two copies** (`~/.claude/hooks/` and `~/.codex/hooks/`). Any hook change patches both.","+- **`~/.claude/skills/` is its own git repo** — `origin https://github.com/erash11/claude-skills.git`, currently in sync with origin. It is *gitignored by the `dotclaude` repo*, which is what the earlier handoff meant; that is not the same as unversioned. **This repo is the safety net and the Mac↔Windows bridge.**","+- **Never commit a symlink into `~/.claude/skills/`.** Windows pulls this repo. A committed absolute Mac symlink arrives there as an unusable file.","+- **Never point Pi at `~/DEV`.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — documented, deliberate design.","+- **Never let any harness near `DexaProject`** (identifiable athlete health data, on Google Drive, not in `~/DEV`).","+- **Plugin skills are out of scope.** `superpowers` (14) and `mattpocock-skills` (35) are vendored and overwritten on update. Scope = the 61 skills in `~/.claude/skills/`.","+- **Hooks exist in two copies** (`~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/`). So do the sync scripts (`.sh` and `.ps1`). Any change patches both."," - Global `MEMORY.md` is at 2,489 / 2,500 chars. Consolidate before adding anything.","-- `timeout` is NOT installed on this Mac (no GNU coreutils). Do not use it in any script.","-- `dcg` blocks `find ... -delete`. Use an explicit `rm` on a named path instead.","-- Python is PEP 668-managed. Use `python3` with stdlib only, or `uv`. Never `pip install` globally.","-- Every task ends with a real verification command whose output is pasted into the commit or the report. No success claim without evidence.","+- `timeout` is NOT installed on this Mac (no GNU coreutils). Do not use it.","+- `dcg` blocks `find ... -delete`. Use an explicit `rm` on a named path.","+- Python is PEP 668-managed. `python3` with stdlib only, or `uv`. Never global `pip install`.","+- **No success claim without a pasted command output.**"," ","-### Measured baseline (2026-09-12, verified this session)","+### Measured baseline (2026-09-12, verified on disk this session)"," "," | Fact | Value |"," |---|---|"," | `~/.claude/skills/` SKILL.md count (`find -L`) | 61 |","-| Of those, symlinks into `~/.agents/skills/` | 19 |","+| Of those, symlinks pointing **out** to `~/.agents/skills/` | 19 |","+| `~/.claude/skills/` git remote | `erash11/claude-skills`, in sync with origin |","+| Latest commit | `3ab4467 chore: apply global skill audit` — the 99 deletions **are committed and pushed** |"," | `~/.agents/skills/` top-level entries | 306 |","+| Pool entries **not** present in `~/.claude/skills/` | 248 |"," | Archived yesterday to `~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/` | 99 |","-| **Archived skills still live in `~/.agents/skills/`** | **98** |","+| Archived skills still live in `~/.agents/skills/` | 98 |"," | `~/.codex/skills/` entries | 32 (27 symlinks, 5 real dirs) |","-| Real dirs in `~/.codex/skills/` diverged from Claude copy | 0 (`dev-sync`, `closeout`, `new-project`, `startup` all identical; `session-handoff` is an empty orphan) |","+| Real dirs in `~/.codex/skills/` diverged from Claude copy | 0 (`dev-sync`, `closeout`, `new-project`, `startup` identical; `session-handoff` is an empty orphan) |","+| `~/.claude/skills/_shared/` | **load-bearing** — `config.json` + `Inference/`, referenced by 10+ skills |"," | Project-level skills in `~/DEV` | 1 (`KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit`) |"," | `~/.claude/rot.md` | does not exist |"," ","-**The lead finding:** yesterday's audit cut the Claude Code roster but not the shared pool. Pi auto-discovers `~/.agents/skills/`. Installing Pi today re-exposes all 306 skills, including 98 of the 99 that were supposed to be gone. Task 2 is the fix and must land before Task 6.","+**The two lead findings:**"," ","+1. **The pool is a stale mirror.** Yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` (and committed the cut) but never touched `~/.agents/skills/`. 98 of the 99 archived names are still there, plus 248 entries Claude Code never registered. Pi auto-discovers that folder. Installing Pi today re-exposes everything. Task 2 fixes this and must land before Task 7.","+2. **Three writers already target these folders** and none of them know about the plan: `sync-to-codex.sh`, its `.ps1` twin, and `daily-backup.ps1` (which runs `git add -A` on `~/.claude/skills`). Task 3 fixes the writers *before* moving anything. Round 1 skipped this and would have destroyed canonical copies.","+"," ### Codex frontmatter ground truth (read from `~/.codex/skills/.system/*/SKILL.md`)"," "," Codex's own shipped skills use only:"]},{"oldStart":48,"oldLines":14,"newStart":58,"newLines":29,"lines":[" ```yaml"," ---"," name: \"skill-name\"","-description: \"Trigger sentence. Use when <positive triggers>. Do not use when <negative triggers>.\"","+description: \"What it does. Use when <positive triggers>. Do not use when <negative>.\""," metadata:","   short-description: \"One short line.\"   # optional"," ---"," ```"," ","-Codex reads **no** `argument-hint`, `allowed-tools`, `model`, or `disable-model-invocation`. It tolerates unknown keys. Codex descriptions carry an explicit **\"Do not use when…\"** negative clause; Claude Code descriptions carry **literal trigger phrases** the user might type. The portable description needs both halves. This is the entire `/poly-skill` idea from the video, reduced to a rule.","+Codex reads **no** `argument-hint`, `allowed-tools`, `model`, or `disable-model-invocation`; it tolerates unknown keys. Codex descriptions carry an explicit **\"Do not use when…\"** clause; Claude Code descriptions carry **literal trigger phrases** the user would type. A portable description needs both halves. That is the whole `/poly-skill` idea, reduced to a rule."," ","+### Round 1 findings — disposition","+","+| ID | Sev | Codex's finding | Disposition |","+|---|---|---|---|","+| R1 | high | `sync-to-codex.sh` does `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\"`. After relinking, `$SRC` points *at* `$dst` — the script deletes the canonical copy. | **ACCEPTED.** Verified on disk. Architecture inverted so `$SRC` is always real, plus a refuse-if-symlink guard. New **Task 3**, which now runs *before* any move. |","+| R2 | high | `_shared/` has no SKILL.md but holds `config.json` + `Inference/`, resolved by 10+ skills via `parents[2]/_shared/config.json`. Round 1 told a human to `rm -rf` any dir without a SKILL.md. | **ACCEPTED.** Verified. The blanket rm instruction is deleted. `_shared` stays in the canonical repo where it already resolves correctly. Task 4 Step 5 verifies `meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py` still resolves. |","+| R3 | high | `~/.claude/skills` has its own `.git`; `daily-backup.ps1` runs `git add -A` on it. Relinking would commit Mac symlinks to a repo Windows pulls. | **ACCEPTED, and it inverted the architecture.** Verified: remote is `erash11/claude-skills`. Canonical is now the git repo; views are generated and never committed. |","+| R4 | high | The Pi extension uses `export default {tool_call}` + `ev.args`; the research note documents an exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`, `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, `event.input`. Also `/\\/DEV\\//` never matches `ls ~/DEV`. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 7 rewritten to the documented contract; regex fixed to `/(^|\\s)~?\\/?DEV(\\/|$|\\s)/`. |","+| R5 | high | `cd ~/pi-trial` is not isolation. Round 1 launched unguarded Pi at real dirs, then treated a regex denylist as sufficient for ADOPT. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 7 runs Pi as a **separate macOS standard user** with no read access to Eric's home. The unguarded `ls ~/DEV` probe is deleted. ADOPT is off the table in this plan; the deliverable is a cost number only. |","+| R6 | med | `claude_skills` double-counts (glob already traverses symlinked dirs); `count()` omits `recursive=True` for sessions. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 5 dedupes by `os.path.realpath`; session glob made explicitly recursive. |","+| R7 | med | Checker does not parse YAML, accepts TitleCase against a kebab-case claim, a negative-only description passes the positive check, and an empty pool exits 0. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 4 enforces distinct positive and negative clauses, fails on an empty inventory, and **decides the naming question explicitly: TitleCase is allowed** (`CreateSkill`, `Harvest` ship that way) — the rule is \"matches the directory name,\" not \"is kebab-case.\" |","+| R8 | med | `[ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ] && echo` returns 1 in apply mode under `set -e`, reporting failure on success. | **ACCEPTED.** All optional actions use explicit `if`; functions `return 0`. |","+| R9 | high | The cron wrapper commits from the shared working tree — a staged unrelated file gets published — and masks git failures. 09:30 does not dodge a backup that may run 240 minutes. | **ACCEPTED.** Task 6's cron **no longer commits or pushes at all.** It writes the trend row and a nudge; the human commits during the audit. That removes the leak, the race, and the masked failure together. |","+| R10 | med | Agents and Rules have no actual check, yet `Last audited` is stamped for them; `brain-audit`/`doctor-plus` are named as delegates with no ownership row. | **ACCEPTED.** `rot.md` initializes every layer to `unknown` except Skills (genuinely audited 2026-09-12). Each layer gets a concrete check or an explicit \"no check yet.\" Dates advance only after that layer's check runs. |","+"," ---"," "," ### Task 1: Snapshot and freeze the current state"]},{"oldStart":66,"oldLines":18,"newStart":91,"newLines":26,"lines":[" "," **Interfaces:**"," - Consumes: nothing.","-- Produces: the only rollback path for Tasks 2–4, plus `pre-canonical-inventory.txt`, the baseline every later task diffs against.","+- Produces: the rollback path for Tasks 2–4, and `pre-canonical-inventory.txt`, the baseline later tasks diff against."," ","+**Note on undo, corrected after round 1:** there are now **two** undo paths. The tarball below covers the un-versioned folders (`~/.agents/skills`, `~/.codex/skills`). For `~/.claude/skills` the real undo is git — `3ab4467` already committed and pushed the 99 deletions, so `git -C ~/.claude/skills revert 3ab4467` restores them on both machines. The `skills-archive/` folder is *not* in that repo and does not reach Windows.","+"," - [ ] **Step 1: Write the inventory**"," "," ```bash"," mkdir -p ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits"," {","   echo \"=== generated $(date -u +%FT%TZ) ===\"","+  echo \"--- claude skills repo state ---\"","+  git -C ~/.claude/skills remote -v","+  git -C ~/.claude/skills log --oneline -3","+  git -C ~/.claude/skills status -sb","   echo \"--- claude skills (find -L, SKILL.md) ---\"","   find -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | sed 's|.*/skills/||;s|/SKILL.md||' | sort","-  echo \"--- claude skills that are symlinks ---\"","+  echo \"--- claude skills that are symlinks (to be inverted in Task 4) ---\"","   find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec basename {} \\; | sort","+  echo \"--- support dirs in claude skills (NO SKILL.md, DO NOT DELETE) ---\"","+  for d in ~/.claude/skills/*/; do [ -f \"$d/SKILL.md\" ] || basename \"$d\"; done | sort","   echo \"--- agents pool ---\"","   ls ~/.agents/skills | sort","   echo \"--- codex skills ---\""]},{"oldStart":85,"oldLines":10,"newStart":118,"newLines":10,"lines":["   echo \"--- archived 2026-09-12 ---\"","   ls ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | sort"," } > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt","-wc -l ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt","+wc -l < ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt"," ```"," ","-Expected: a file of roughly 500–520 lines.","+Expected: roughly 520–540 lines. **Read the \"support dirs\" section** — it must list `_shared`. That list is the do-not-delete set for every later task."," "," - [ ] **Step 2: Take the tarball**"," "]},{"oldStart":98,"oldLines":7,"newStart":131,"newLines":7,"lines":[" ls -lh ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz"," ```"," ","-Expected: a file is listed, non-zero size. `tar` may print `Removing leading '/'` — harmless.","+Expected: a non-zero-size file. `tar` may print `Removing leading '/'` — harmless."," "," - [ ] **Step 3: Verify the tarball actually restores**"," "]},{"oldStart":107,"oldLines":20,"newStart":140,"newLines":26,"lines":[" tar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C /tmp/tartest"," find -L /tmp/tartest/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l"," ls /tmp/tartest/.agents/skills | wc -l","+test -f /tmp/tartest/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared OK\""," ```"," ","-Expected: `61` and `306`. If either number is wrong, STOP — the snapshot is not a valid undo and no later task may run.","+Expected: `61`, `306`, `_shared OK`. If any is wrong, STOP — the snapshot is not a valid undo and no later task may run."," ","-- [ ] **Step 4: Record the rollback command in the inventory**","+- [ ] **Step 4: Record both rollback paths**"," "," ```bash"," cat >> ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt <<'EOF'"," ","-=== ROLLBACK (undoes Tasks 2-4 completely) ===","-rm -rf ~/.claude/skills ~/.codex/skills ~/.agents/skills","+=== ROLLBACK ===","+# Un-versioned folders (undoes Tasks 2-4 view changes):","+rm -rf ~/.codex/skills ~/.agents/skills"," tar xzf ~/.claude/skills-archive/pre-canonical-2026-09-12.tgz -C ~","+","+# Canonical repo (undoes anything committed to erash11/claude-skills):","+git -C ~/.claude/skills log --oneline","+git -C ~/.claude/skills revert <sha>    # reaches Windows on next dev-sync"," EOF","-tail -6 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt","+tail -12 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt"," ```"," "," Expected: the rollback block prints."]},{"oldStart":128,"oldLines":26,"newStart":167,"newLines":30,"lines":[" - [ ] **Step 5: Commit**"," "," ```bash","-cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md && git commit -m \"docs: pre-canonical skill inventory + harness-agnostic plan","+cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS","+git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","+git commit -m \"docs: pre-canonical skill inventory + harness-agnostic plan"," "," Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\""," ```"," "," ---"," ","-### Task 2: Prune the shared pool to the survivors","+### Task 2: Prune the stale pool mirror"," "," **Files:**","-- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (move 98 dirs out)","+- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (move stale dirs out)"," - Create: `~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/`"," - Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt`"," "," **Interfaces:**"," - Consumes: `2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt` from Task 1.","-- Produces: an `~/.agents/skills/` containing only skills Eric intends every harness to load. Task 3 symlinks onto this; Task 6 (Pi) reads it directly.","+- Produces: an `~/.agents/skills/` free of skills the audit already cut. Task 7 (Pi) reads this directly."," ","-**Why this task exists:** yesterday's archive script only moved things out of `~/.claude/skills/`. 98 of the 99 archived names are still present in `~/.agents/skills/`. Claude Code no longer sees them, but Pi discovers that folder natively, so the token win evaporates the moment Pi runs. This task makes the cut real everywhere.","+**Why this task exists:** yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` and committed it. It never touched `~/.agents/skills/`, where 98 of the 99 archived names still live. Pi discovers that folder natively, so the token win evaporates the moment Pi runs."," ","+**Scope limit, added after round 1.** 248 pool entries are absent from `~/.claude/skills/`. The pool has a **mixed role**: a view onto Eric's own skills *and* an install target for third-party skills (`npx skills add` — see the `vendored-skill-install` memory). This task prunes **only** the 98 that exactly match yesterday's archive list. It does not touch the other 150, which may be third-party installs other harnesses depend on. Those are `aios-audit`'s problem, with a human reading the proposal.","+"," - [ ] **Step 1: Compute the prune list and eyeball it**"," "," ```bash"]},{"oldStart":158,"oldLines":194,"newStart":201,"newLines":420,"lines":[" cat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt"," ```"," ","-Expected: `98`, then 98 names. Read them. Any name Eric wants back gets deleted from the list file before Step 2. This is the one human gate in the task.","+Expected: `98`, then 98 names. Read them. Any name Eric wants back is deleted from the list file before Step 3. This is the human gate."," ","-- [ ] **Step 2: Guard against pruning anything a survivor symlinks to**","+- [ ] **Step 2: Two guards — live symlink targets, and support dirs**"," "," ```bash","+# Guard A: nothing a live Claude skill points at may be pruned."," find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l -exec readlink {} \\; \\","   | sed 's|.*/||' | sort > /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt","+echo \"--- guard A (must be empty) ---\""," comm -12 /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt /tmp/live-symlink-targets.txt","+","+# Guard B: nothing without a SKILL.md may be pruned - it is support, not a skill.","+echo \"--- guard B (must be empty) ---\"","+while IFS= read -r s; do","+  [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -d ~/.agents/skills/\"$s\" ] && [ ! -f ~/.agents/skills/\"$s\"/SKILL.md ] && echo \"SUPPORT DIR: $s\"","+done < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt"," ```"," ","-Expected: **no output.** If any name prints, a live Claude skill points at something scheduled for pruning. Remove that name from `/tmp/pool-prune-list.txt` and re-run until this prints nothing.","+Expected: **both empty.** Any name under guard A means a live skill points at something scheduled for pruning. Any name under guard B is a support directory like `_shared`. Remove it from `/tmp/pool-prune-list.txt` and re-run until both print nothing."," "," - [ ] **Step 3: Move, do not delete**"," "," ```bash"," cd ~/.agents/skills"," while IFS= read -r s; do","-  [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -e \"$s\" ] && mv \"$s\" ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/","+  if [ -n \"$s\" ] && [ -e \"$s\" ]; then mv \"$s\" ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/; fi"," done < /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt"," ls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l"," ls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l"," ```"," ","-Expected: pool drops from `306` to `208`; archive holds `98`.","+Expected: pool drops `306` → `208`; archive holds `98`."," ","-> Note: `306 - 98 = 208`, not 61. The pool legitimately holds more than the Claude roster — `_shared` support dirs, Codex-only skills, and skills never registered with Claude Code. Task 4's report reconciles the remainder. Do not \"fix\" 208 down to 61 here; that would delete Codex-only assets.","+> `306 - 98 = 208`, not 61. That remainder is third-party installs and Codex-only skills. **Do not \"fix\" 208 down to 61 here** — that would delete assets other harnesses use."," "," - [ ] **Step 4: Verify nothing that was working broke**"," "," ```bash"," find -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l","+echo \"--- dangling symlinks (must be empty) ---\""," find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print","+test -f ~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared OK\""," ```"," ","-Expected: `61`, then **no output** (no dangling symlinks). A dangling symlink here means Step 2's guard was skipped — roll back with the Task 1 tarball.","+Expected: `61`, no dangling output, `_shared OK`. A dangling symlink means a guard was skipped — roll back with the Task 1 tarball."," "," - [ ] **Step 5: Write the record and commit**"," "," ```bash"," {","   echo \"pool prune 2026-09-12\"","-  echo \"before: 306  after: $(ls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l)  archived: $(ls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l)\"","+  echo \"before: 306  after: $(ls ~/.agents/skills | wc -l | tr -d ' ')  archived: $(ls ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12 | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"","+  echo \"scope: only names matching ~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12. Third-party installs untouched.\"","   echo \"undo: mv ~/.agents/skills-archive/2026-09-12/* ~/.agents/skills/\"","   echo \"--- pruned ---\"","   cat /tmp/pool-prune-list.txt"," } > ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt","-cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt && git commit -m \"chore: prune shared agent skill pool to survivors","+cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt && git commit -m \"chore: prune stale skill pool mirror"," "," Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\""," ```"," "," ---"," ","-### Task 3: Make `~/.agents/skills/` canonical, the other two views","+### Task 3: Harden the sync writers — before anything moves"," "," **Files:**","-- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/` (42 real dirs become symlinks)","-- Modify: `~/.codex/skills/` (5 real dirs become symlinks)","-- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh`","+- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh`","+- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`","+- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md`"," "," **Interfaces:**","-- Consumes: the pruned pool from Task 2.","-- Produces: exactly one editable copy of every skill, at `~/.agents/skills/<name>/`. Task 4 rewrites frontmatter there and all three harnesses see the change at once.","+- Consumes: nothing.","+- Produces: a sync script safe to run at any point during Tasks 4–7. **Nothing in Task 4 may run until this task's Step 6 passes.**"," ","-**Why this task exists:** right now a skill can exist as a real dir in `~/.claude/skills/`, a real dir in `~/.codex/skills/`, and a separate dir in `~/.agents/skills/`. Three copies, no declared winner — the same shape as the hooks two-copies trap. Today they happen to be identical; that is luck, not design. One canonical home is what makes the skills harness-agnostic.","+**Why this task comes before the move.** Round 1 put the relink first and this second. Codex caught that as R1, a data-loss defect. Today `sync-to-codex.sh` does:"," ","-- [ ] **Step 1: Write the relink script**","+```bash","+rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"","+```"," ","-Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh`:","+`dev-sync` instructs the user to run it after every pull. The moment `$SRC/$s` is a symlink into `$dst`, `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"` destroys the real directory and the `cp` copies a corpse. The `.ps1` twin has the same shape. Hardening first makes the whole rest of the plan safe to interrupt."," ","+- [ ] **Step 1: Read both current scripts and record the baseline**","+"," ```bash","+cat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh","+echo \"=== ps1 ===\"","+cat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1","+```","+","+Expected: the `SHARED=(...)` array and the `rm -rf` / `cp -R` pair in each. Note the current `SHARED` membership — Step 2 preserves it.","+","+- [ ] **Step 2: Rewrite the bash script**","+","+Replace `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh` with:","+","+```bash"," #!/usr/bin/env bash","-# Make ~/.agents/skills the single canonical home for Eric's own skills.","-# ~/.claude/skills and ~/.codex/skills become symlink views onto it.","-# Idempotent: re-running reports \"already linked\" and changes nothing.","+# sync-to-codex.sh -- rebuild the Codex and agent-pool VIEWS from the canonical","+# repo at ~/.claude/skills.","+#","+# CANONICAL: ~/.claude/skills   (git repo erash11/claude-skills, real dirs only)","+# VIEWS:     ~/.agents/skills   (Pi, Cowork)","+#            ~/.codex/skills    (Codex)","+#","+# Views are generated. Never author a skill in a view; it will be overwritten.","+# On macOS a view entry is a symlink; on Windows the .ps1 twin copies instead."," set -euo pipefail"," ","-POOL=\"$HOME/.agents/skills\"","-DRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-1}\"","+SRC=\"$HOME/.claude/skills\"","+SHARED=(new-project startup dev-sync closeout session-handoff humanizer articulation-skill)","+MODE=\"${SYNC_MODE:-link}\"     # link | copy","+DRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-0}\""," ","-relink_view() {","-  local view=\"$1\"","-  [ -d \"$view\" ] || { echo \"skip: $view does not exist\"; return; }","-  local d name","-  for d in \"$view\"/*/; do","-    [ -d \"$d\" ] || continue","-    name=\"$(basename \"$d\")\"","-    case \"$name\" in .*) continue ;; esac          # leave .system alone","-    [ -L \"${d%/}\" ] && { echo \"ok   $view/$name (already a link)\"; continue; }","-    if [ ! -f \"$d/SKILL.md\" ]; then","-      echo \"WARN $view/$name has no SKILL.md - left alone\"; continue","+fail=0","+for s in \"${SHARED[@]}\"; do","+  # R1 GUARD: the canonical source must be a real directory, never a link.","+  # If it is a link, a previous migration inverted the direction and a","+  # delete-then-copy would destroy the real data. Refuse the whole run.","+  if [ -L \"$SRC/$s\" ]; then","+    echo \"FATAL $SRC/$s is a symlink. Canonical must be a real directory.\" >&2","+    echo \"      Refusing to sync; this would delete the link target.\" >&2","+    fail=1","+  fi","+done","+if [ \"$fail\" -ne 0 ]; then","+  echo \"No changes made.\" >&2","+  exit 2","+fi","+","+for dst in \"$HOME/.agents/skills\" \"$HOME/.codex/skills\"; do","+  mkdir -p \"$dst\"","+  for s in \"${SHARED[@]}\"; do","+    if [ ! -d \"$SRC/$s\" ]; then","+      echo \"skip $s (not in $SRC)\"","+      continue","     fi","-    if [ -e \"$POOL/$name\" ]; then","-      if diff -rq \"$d\" \"$POOL/$name\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then","-        echo \"LINK $view/$name -> pool (identical)\"","-        [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ] && { rm -rf \"${d%/}\"; ln -s \"$POOL/$name\" \"${d%/}\"; }","-      else","-        echo \"SKIP $view/$name DIVERGED from pool - resolve by hand\"","-      fi","+    if [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then","+      echo \"would sync $s -> $dst ($MODE)\"","+      continue","+    fi","+    # Safe now: $SRC/$s is proven real, so removing the view cannot touch it.","+    rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"","+    if [ \"$MODE\" = \"link\" ]; then","+      ln -s \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"","     else","-      echo \"MOVE $view/$name -> pool, then link\"","-      [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ] && { mv \"${d%/}\" \"$POOL/$name\"; ln -s \"$POOL/$name\" \"${d%/}\"; }","+      cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"","     fi","+    echo \"synced $s -> $dst ($MODE)\"","   done","+done","+","+if [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then","+  echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"","+fi","+exit 0","+```","+","+- [ ] **Step 3: Apply the same guard to the PowerShell twin**","+","+Edit `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`. Before its copy loop, add:","+","+```powershell","+# R1 GUARD: canonical must be a real directory, never a reparse point.","+foreach ($s in $SHARED) {","+  $p = Join-Path $SRC $s","+  if ((Test-Path $p) -and ((Get-Item $p -Force).Attributes -band [IO.FileAttributes]::ReparsePoint)) {","+    Write-Error \"FATAL $p is a link. Canonical must be a real directory. Refusing to sync.\"","+    exit 2","+  }"," }","+```"," ","-relink_view \"$HOME/.claude/skills\"","-relink_view \"$HOME/.codex/skills\"","+Windows keeps `Copy-Item` (mode `copy`); it does not create symlinks without elevation.","+","+- [ ] **Step 4: Prove the guard fires — the whole point of this task**","+","+```bash","+# Build a fake inverted layout in a scratch HOME and confirm the script refuses.","+rm -rf /tmp/syncguard && mkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/{.claude/skills,.agents/skills,.codex/skills}","+mkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup","+echo \"canary - must survive\" > /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup/SKILL.md","+ln -s /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup /tmp/syncguard/.claude/skills/startup","+HOME=/tmp/syncguard bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"","+cat /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills/startup/SKILL.md","+```","+","+Expected: `FATAL ... is a symlink`, `exit=2`, and the canary file **still readable**. If the canary is gone, the guard does not work — stop and fix before any other task.","+","+- [ ] **Step 5: Prove the normal path still works**","+","+```bash","+DRY_RUN=1 bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"","+bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"","+ls -la ~/.codex/skills | grep -E 'startup|closeout'","+```","+","+Expected: dry run lists `would sync` lines and `exit=0`; the real run reports `synced …` and `exit=0`; the listing shows symlinks into `~/.claude/skills`.","+","+- [ ] **Step 6: Verify `daily-backup` will not publish a symlink**","+","+```bash","+grep -n 'skills' ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1 | head -20","+git -C ~/.claude/skills status --short","+```","+","+Expected: `git status` is **clean**. The views live outside the repo, so the backup's `git add -A` has nothing machine-specific to pick up. If `git status` lists a symlink inside `~/.claude/skills`, stop — that is exactly R3 and it must not be committed.","+","+- [ ] **Step 7: Commit both copies**","+","+```bash","+cd ~/.claude/skills","+git add sync-to-codex.sh sync-to-codex.ps1","+git commit -m \"fix: refuse to sync when canonical source is a symlink (R1)","+","+Delete-then-copy destroyed the link target when ~/.claude/skills/<name>","+pointed into ~/.agents/skills. Views are now generated from a proven-real","+canonical source; symlink on Mac, copy on Windows.","+","+Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"","+git push","+cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS","+printf '# Sync hardening 2026-09-12\\n\\nR1 fix. Guard test output:\\n\\n```\\n%s\\n```\\n' \\","+  \"$(HOME=/tmp/syncguard bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\")\" \\","+  > docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md","+git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md","+git commit -m \"docs: record the sync guard test","+","+Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"","+```","+","+---","+","+### Task 4: Invert the 19 outward symlinks into the canonical repo","+","+**Files:**","+- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/` (19 symlinks become real dirs)","+- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/` (19 real dirs become links back)","+- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh`","+","+**Interfaces:**","+- Consumes: the hardened sync script from Task 3.","+- Produces: a canonical repo with **zero symlinks**, so Task 5 edits one file per skill and every harness sees it.","+","+**Direction, restated because round 1 had it backwards.** 19 entries in `~/.claude/skills/` are symlinks pointing *out* to `~/.agents/skills/`. Their real content therefore sits outside the git repo — unversioned, not on Windows, not in the backup. This task pulls each real directory **into** the repo and leaves a link behind at the pool.","+","+- [ ] **Step 1: Write the script**","+","+Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh`:","+","+```bash","+#!/usr/bin/env bash","+# Invert outward symlinks so ~/.claude/skills holds real directories only.","+#   before: ~/.claude/skills/X -> ~/.agents/skills/X   (real content outside git)","+#   after:  ~/.claude/skills/X is real; ~/.agents/skills/X -> it","+# Idempotent. DRY_RUN=1 by default.","+set -euo pipefail","+","+SRC=\"$HOME/.claude/skills\"","+POOL=\"$HOME/.agents/skills\"","+DRY=\"${DRY_RUN:-1}\"","+moved=0; skipped=0","+","+for link in \"$SRC\"/*; do","+  name=\"$(basename \"$link\")\"","+  case \"$name\" in .*) continue ;; esac","+  if [ ! -L \"$link\" ]; then","+    continue","+  fi","+  target=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$link\")\" && readlink \"$link\")\"","+  abs=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$link\")\" && cd \"$(dirname \"$target\")\" 2>/dev/null && pwd)/$(basename \"$target\")\"","+  if [ ! -d \"$abs\" ]; then","+    echo \"DANGLING $name -> $target (left alone)\"","+    skipped=$((skipped+1))","+    continue","+  fi","+  if [ ! -f \"$abs/SKILL.md\" ]; then","+    echo \"NO-SKILL  $name -> $target (support dir, left alone)\"","+    skipped=$((skipped+1))","+    continue","+  fi","+  echo \"INVERT    $name  ($abs -> $SRC/$name, link back at pool)\"","+  if [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ]; then","+    rm \"$link\"","+    mv \"$abs\" \"$SRC/$name\"","+    ln -s \"$SRC/$name\" \"$abs\"","+  fi","+  moved=$((moved+1))","+done","+"," echo","-echo \"pool:   $(ls \"$POOL\" | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"","-echo \"claude: $(find -L \"$HOME/.claude/skills\" -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"","-echo \"codex:  $(find -L \"$HOME/.codex/skills\" -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"","-[ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ] && echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"","+echo \"inverted: $moved   skipped: $skipped\"","+echo \"symlinks left in canonical: $(find \"$SRC\" -maxdepth 1 -type l | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"","+if [ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]; then","+  echo \"(DRY RUN - set DRY_RUN=0 to apply)\"","+fi","+exit 0"," ```"," "," ```bash","-chmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh","+chmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh"," ```"," "," - [ ] **Step 2: Dry run and read every line**"," "," ```bash","-~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh","+~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh"," ```"," ","-Expected: ~42 `MOVE` lines under `.claude/skills`, ~5 `LINK`/`MOVE` lines under `.codex/skills`, ~24 `ok` lines, and the `(DRY RUN)` footer. Any `SKIP … DIVERGED` line must be resolved by hand before Step 3 — pick the newer file, copy it into the pool, delete the other. Any `WARN … no SKILL.md` is an orphan dir (expect `session-handoff`); `rm -rf` it by exact name.","+Expected: ~19 `INVERT` lines, `inverted: 19`, and the DRY footer. Any `DANGLING` line is a broken skill — investigate before applying. `NO-SKILL` lines are support directories and are correctly left alone (this is the R2 fix; there is **no** instruction anywhere in this plan to delete a directory for lacking a SKILL.md)."," "," - [ ] **Step 3: Apply**"," "," ```bash","-DRY_RUN=0 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-relink.sh","+DRY_RUN=0 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh"," ```"," ","-Expected: the same lines without the DRY footer, then three counts.","+Expected: the same lines, then `symlinks left in canonical: 0`."," ","-- [ ] **Step 4: Verify all three views resolve and nothing dangles**","+- [ ] **Step 4: Verify the canonical repo is symlink-free and complete**"," "," ```bash","+echo \"--- symlinks in canonical (must be 0) ---\"","+find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l | wc -l","+echo \"--- SKILL.md count (must be 61) ---\""," find -L ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 2 -name SKILL.md | wc -l","-find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print","-find ~/.codex/skills  -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print","-find ~/.claude/skills -maxdepth 1 -type d ! -path ~/.claude/skills | wc -l","+echo \"--- dangling pool links (must be empty) ---\"","+find ~/.agents/skills -maxdepth 1 -type l ! -exec test -e {} \\; -print","+echo \"--- git sees real files, not links ---\"","+git -C ~/.claude/skills status --short | head -20","+git -C ~/.claude/skills ls-files -s | awk '$1==\"120000\"' | head"," ```"," ","-Expected: `61`; no output; no output; `0` real dirs left under `.claude/skills`.","+Expected: `0`; `61`; no dangling output; `git status` lists new real files; the `ls-files` line prints **nothing** (mode `120000` is a symlink — none may be staged). If any symlink is staged, unstage it and stop; that is R3."," ","-- [ ] **Step 5: Confirm Claude Code still loads the roster**","+- [ ] **Step 5: Verify `_shared` consumers still resolve (the R2 check)**"," ","-Run `/reload-skills` in a Claude Code session, then `/skills`. Expected: the same skill count as before Task 2 (61 personal + plugin skills). If the count dropped, roll back with the Task 1 tarball.","+```bash","+test -f ~/.claude/skills/_shared/config.json && echo \"_shared in canonical OK\"","+python3 -c \"","+import pathlib","+p = pathlib.Path.home()/'.claude/skills/meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py'","+if not p.exists():","+    print('meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py not found - check path'); raise SystemExit","+cfg = p.resolve().parents[2]/'_shared/config.json'","+print('resolves to:', cfg)","+print('exists:', cfg.exists())","+\"","+```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 6: Commit**","+Expected: `_shared in canonical OK`, and `exists: True`. If `False`, the `parents[2]` hop no longer lands on `_shared` — fix the path in `sync.py` before committing."," ","+- [ ] **Step 6: Re-run the sync guard now that the layout changed**","+"," ```bash","-cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add scripts/skills-relink.sh && git commit -m \"feat: one canonical skill pool, harness dirs become symlink views","+DRY_RUN=1 bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"","+```"," ","+Expected: `would sync` lines and `exit=0` — **not** `FATAL`. A FATAL here means a `SHARED` entry is still a symlink and Step 3 did not finish.","+","+- [ ] **Step 7: Confirm Claude Code still loads the roster, then commit**","+","+Run `/reload-skills` in a Claude Code session, then `/skills`. Expected: the same personal-skill count as before (61). If it dropped, roll back per Task 1.","+","+```bash","+cd ~/.claude/skills","+git add -A","+git status --short | head","+git commit -m \"refactor: canonical repo holds real directories, no symlinks","+","+19 skills whose content lived in ~/.agents/skills are now inside the repo,","+so they version and reach Windows. The pool links back to them.","+"," Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"","+git push","+cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add scripts/skills-canonicalize.sh && git commit -m \"feat: symlink inversion script","+","+Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\""," ```"," "," ---"," ","-### Task 4: Normalize every survivor to portable frontmatter","+### Task 5: Normalize every skill to portable frontmatter"," "," **Files:**"," - Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`","-- Modify: `~/.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md` (frontmatter only, body untouched)","+- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` (frontmatter only, body untouched)"," - Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md`"," "," **Interfaces:**","-- Consumes: the canonical pool from Task 3.","-- Produces: `skill-portability-check.py`, which Task 5's `aios-audit` skill calls as its harness-parity check. Its exit code is `0` when every skill passes, `1` otherwise.","+- Consumes: the symlink-free canonical repo from Task 4.","+- Produces: `skill-portability-check.py`. `aios-audit` calls it as its harness-parity check. Exit `0` when every skill passes, `1` otherwise, `2` on an empty or missing inventory."," ","-**The portable frontmatter contract** (superset — Codex ignores keys it does not know, Claude Code ignores `metadata`):","+**The portable frontmatter contract** (a superset — Codex ignores keys it does not know, Claude Code ignores `metadata`):"," "," ```yaml"," ---","-name: skill-name                      # REQUIRED. kebab-case, matches the dir name.","-description: >-                       # REQUIRED. <=1024 chars. Must contain BOTH:","-  <what it does in one sentence>.     #   (a) \"Use when ...\" + literal trigger phrases","-  Use when <triggers, quoted phrases>.#       the user would actually type","-  Do not use when <negative>.         #   (b) \"Do not use when ...\" / \"NOT FOR ...\"","+name: skill-name                       # REQUIRED. Must equal the directory name.","+                                       # TitleCase is allowed (CreateSkill, Harvest).","+description: >-                        # REQUIRED. <=1024 chars. Must contain BOTH:","+  <what it does, one sentence>.        #   (a) a positive clause: \"Use when ...\" with","+  Use when <literal trigger phrases>.  #       literal phrases the user would type","+  Do not use when <negative>.          #   (b) a negative clause: \"Do not use ...\" /","+                                       #       \"NOT FOR ...\" - Codex needs this to","+                                       #       avoid over-firing"," metadata:","-  short-description: <one short line> # optional, Codex-only, ignored by Claude","-argument-hint: \"[...]\"                # optional, Claude-only, ignored by Codex","-allowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep       # optional, Claude-only, ignored by Codex","+  short-description: <one short line>  # optional, Codex-only, Claude ignores","+argument-hint: \"[...]\"                 # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores","+allowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep        # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores"," ---"," ```"," ","-Claude Code triggers on literal phrases; Codex leans on the negative clause to avoid over-firing. A description with only one half fires reliably in one harness and badly in the other. That is the whole `/poly-skill` problem.","+Claude Code triggers on literal phrases; Codex leans on the negative clause. A description with only one half fires reliably in one harness and badly in the other. **Naming is settled here (R7):** the rule is `name` equals the directory name. TitleCase is legal — `CreateSkill` and `Harvest` already ship that way and renaming them would break every cross-reference."," "," - [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing check**"," "]},{"oldStart":353,"oldLines":60,"newStart":622,"newLines":78,"lines":[" "," ```python"," #!/usr/bin/env python3","-\"\"\"Check every skill in the canonical pool against the portable frontmatter","-contract. Exit 0 if all pass, 1 if any fail. stdlib only (PEP 668 machine).\"\"\"","+\"\"\"Check every skill in the canonical repo against the portable frontmatter","+contract. Exit 0 = all pass, 1 = failures, 2 = empty/missing inventory.","+stdlib only (PEP 668 machine). Parses the flat YAML subset we actually use.\"\"\""," import os, re, sys, glob"," ","-POOL = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.agents/skills\")","-ALLOWED = {\"name\", \"description\", \"metadata\", \"argument-hint\",","-           \"allowed-tools\", \"model\", \"disable-model-invocation\", \"license\",","-           \"version\", \"author\"}","+CANON = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.claude/skills\")","+ALLOWED = {\"name\", \"description\", \"metadata\", \"argument-hint\", \"allowed-tools\",","+           \"model\", \"disable-model-invocation\", \"license\", \"version\", \"author\"}","+POSITIVE = (\"use when\", \"use for\", \"use this skill when\", \"invoke when\")","+NEGATIVE = (\"do not use\", \"don't use\", \"not for\", \"never use\")"," ","-def parse_frontmatter(text):","+def frontmatter(text):","     if not text.startswith(\"---\"):","         return None","     end = text.find(\"\\n---\", 3)","-    if end == -1:","-        return None","-    return text[3:end]","+    return None if end == -1 else text[4:end]"," "," def top_keys(fm):","-    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*):\", fm)]","+    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)\\s*:\", fm)]"," ","-def field(fm, key):","-    m = re.search(rf\"(?ms)^{re.escape(key)}:\\s*(.*?)(?=^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*:|\\Z)\", fm)","-    return m.group(1).strip().strip('\"').strip(\"'\") if m else \"\"","+def scalar(fm, key):","+    \"\"\"Read a flat scalar, including >- and | block styles.\"\"\"","+    m = re.search(rf\"(?ms)^{re.escape(key)}\\s*:[ \\t]*(.*?)(?=^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*\\s*:|\\Z)\", fm)","+    if not m:","+        return \"\"","+    raw = m.group(1)","+    raw = re.sub(r\"^[>|][-+]?\\s*\\n\", \"\", raw)          # strip a block indicator","+    val = \" \".join(l.strip() for l in raw.splitlines() if l.strip())","+    return val.strip().strip('\"').strip(\"'\")"," "," def check(path):","     name = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path))","     problems = []","-    text = open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read()","-    fm = parse_frontmatter(text)","+    fm = frontmatter(open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read())","     if fm is None:","         return name, [\"no YAML frontmatter\"]","     keys = top_keys(fm)","+    declared = scalar(fm, \"name\")","     if \"name\" not in keys:","         problems.append(\"missing 'name'\")","-    elif field(fm, \"name\") != name:","-        problems.append(f\"name '{field(fm,'name')}' != dir '{name}'\")","-    desc = field(fm, \"description\")","+    elif declared != name:","+        problems.append(f\"name '{declared}' != directory '{name}'\")","+    desc = scalar(fm, \"description\")","     if not desc:","         problems.append(\"missing 'description'\")","     else:","+        low = desc.lower()","         if len(desc) > 1024:","             problems.append(f\"description {len(desc)} chars (>1024)\")","-        low = desc.lower()","-        if \"use when\" not in low and \"use for\" not in low:","-            problems.append(\"description has no 'Use when/for' trigger clause\")","-        if not any(p in low for p in (\"do not use\", \"not for\", \"don't use\")):","-            problems.append(\"description has no negative clause (Codex over-fires)\")","+        pos = next((p for p in POSITIVE if p in low), None)","+        neg = next((n for n in NEGATIVE if n in low), None)","+        if not pos:","+            problems.append(\"no positive clause ('Use when ...') - Claude will not trigger\")","+        if not neg:","+            problems.append(\"no negative clause ('Do not use ...') - Codex over-fires\")","+        # R7: the two clauses must be distinct text, not one phrase counted twice.","+        if pos and neg and low.find(pos) == low.find(neg):","+            problems.append(\"positive and negative clauses overlap\")","     for k in keys:","         if k not in ALLOWED:","             problems.append(f\"unknown frontmatter key '{k}'\")","     return name, problems"," "," def main():","-    paths = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(POOL, \"*\", \"SKILL.md\")))","+    if not os.path.isdir(CANON):","+        print(f\"FATAL canonical dir missing: {CANON}\", file=sys.stderr)","+        return 2","+    paths = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(CANON, \"*\", \"SKILL.md\")))","+    if not paths:","+        print(f\"FATAL no SKILL.md found under {CANON} - refusing to report success\",","+              file=sys.stderr)","+        return 2","     failed = 0","     for p in paths:","         name, probs = check(p)"]},{"oldStart":426,"oldLines":23,"newStart":713,"newLines":27,"lines":[" chmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py"," ```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 2: Run it and confirm it fails**","+- [ ] **Step 2: Prove the checker's own failure modes first (R7)**"," "," ```bash","-python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\"","+python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"baseline exit=$?\"","+","+# An empty inventory must NOT report success.","+rm -rf /tmp/emptycanon && mkdir -p /tmp/emptycanon","+HOME=/tmp/emptycanon python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"empty exit=$?\""," ```"," ","-Expected: a long FAIL list and `exit=1`. Most failures will be \"no negative clause\" — the existing skills were written Claude-only. This baseline number goes in the report.","+Expected: a long FAIL list and `baseline exit=1`; then `FATAL ...` and `empty exit=2`. If the empty case prints `0 failing` and exits 0, the R7 fix is not in — stop."," "," - [ ] **Step 3: Fix frontmatter in priority order, highest-use first**"," ","-Do **not** bulk-rewrite with sed. Each description is a trigger surface; a careless edit silently stops a skill firing. Work through the failures in this order, editing only the frontmatter block of `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`:","+Do **not** bulk-rewrite with sed. Each description is a trigger surface; a careless edit silently stops a skill firing. Edit only the frontmatter block of `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, in this order:"," ","-1. The confirmed working set (3+ invocations): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `backup`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`, `codex-review`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, `doctor-plus`, `startup`, `new-project`, `status-report`.","-2. Everything else in the pool that Claude Code exposes.","+1. Confirmed working set (3+ invocations): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `backup`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`, `codex-review`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, `doctor-plus`, `startup`, `new-project`, `status-report`.","+2. Everything else Claude Code exposes."," 3. Codex-only skills last."," ","-For each, add the missing half. Worked example — `backup` today has triggers but no negative clause:","+Worked example — `backup` today has triggers but no negative clause:"," "," ```yaml"," description: >-"]},{"oldStart":454,"oldLines":23,"newStart":745,"newLines":23,"lines":["   whole-stack health checks - use claude-stack-doctor instead."," ```"," ","-Re-run the checker after each batch of five:","+Re-check after each batch of five:"," "," ```bash"," python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py | tail -3"," ```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 4: Run it and confirm it passes**","+- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm it passes**"," "," ```bash"," python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\""," ```"," ","-Expected: `checked N skills, 0 failing, N portable` and `exit=0`.","+Expected: `checked 61 skills, 0 failing, 61 portable` and `exit=0`."," "," - [ ] **Step 5: Prove portability in both harnesses, not just the linter**"," ","-Pick three skills that were edited. In a fresh Claude Code session, type one trigger phrase for each and confirm the skill fires. In a fresh Codex session (`cx`), do the same. Record the six results in the report. A linter that passes while a skill stops firing is the failure mode this step exists to catch.","+Pick three edited skills. In a fresh Claude Code session, type one trigger phrase for each and confirm it fires. In a fresh Codex session (`cx`), do the same. Record all six results in the report. A linter that passes while a skill stops firing is exactly the failure this step exists to catch."," "," - [ ] **Step 6: Write the report and commit**"," "]},{"oldStart":479,"oldLines":36,"newStart":770,"newLines":47,"lines":[" {","   echo \"# Skill portability normalization - 2026-09-12\"","   echo","-  echo \"Contract: name + description (Use when / Do not use when) are the Codex-safe\"","-  echo \"core. argument-hint and allowed-tools are Claude-only and ignored by Codex.\"","+  echo \"Contract: name (== directory name, TitleCase allowed) + description with\"","+  echo \"BOTH a positive 'Use when' clause and a negative 'Do not use' clause.\"","+  echo \"argument-hint and allowed-tools are Claude-only; metadata is Codex-only.\"","   echo","   echo '```'","-  python3 scripts/skill-portability-check.py","+  python3 scripts/skill-portability-check.py || true","   echo '```'","+  echo","+  echo \"## Live trigger test (Step 5)\"","+  echo \"| skill | Claude Code | Codex |\"","+  echo \"|---|---|---|\"","+  echo \"| FILL IN | | |\""," } > docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md"," git add scripts/skill-portability-check.py docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md"," git commit -m \"feat: portable skill frontmatter contract + checker"," "," Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"","+cd ~/.claude/skills && git add -A && git commit -m \"chore: portable frontmatter across the roster","+","+Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git push"," ```"," ","+Fill in the trigger-test table from the real runs. A report left with `FILL IN` is a failed task.","+"," ---"," ","-### Task 5: `rot.md` and the `aios-audit` compound skill","+### Task 6: `rot.md` and the `aios-audit` compound skill"," "," **Files:**"," - Create: `~/.claude/rot.md`"," - Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md`"," - Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`","-- Modify: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md` (add a cross-reference line only)","+- Modify: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md` (cross-reference line only)"," "," **Interfaces:**","-- Consumes: `skill-portability-check.py` (Task 4) and the canonical pool (Task 3).","-- Produces: `aios-metrics.py`, which prints a machine-readable metrics block that Task 6's launchd job captures into the trend table.","+- Consumes: `skill-portability-check.py` (Task 5) and the canonical repo (Task 4).","+- Produces: `aios-metrics.py`, printing a machine-readable metrics block Task 7's launchd job captures."," ","-**Placement is deliberate.** This skill is **project-level**, in `KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/`, not global — that is the promotion rule from the video (31:32), adopted yesterday. It gets promoted to `~/.agents/skills/` only after it proves useful across 2+ projects.","+**Placement is deliberate.** This skill is **project-level**, in `KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/`, not global — the promotion rule from the video (31:32). It gets promoted to `~/.claude/skills/` only after proving useful across 2+ projects."," ","-**It must not become a sixth audit skill.** Five already exist and each owns a layer:","+**It must not become a sixth audit skill.** Five exist and each owns a layer:"," "," | Existing skill | Owns |"," |---|---|"]},{"oldStart":518,"oldLines":27,"newStart":820,"newLines":34,"lines":[" | `claude-stack-doctor` | automation plumbing: launchd, venvs, memsearch, backup freshness |"," | `doctor-plus` | context-engineering posture of the workspace |"," ","-`aios-audit` owns the one layer none of them touch — **the harness itself**: the skill roster, agents, hooks, rules, and cross-harness parity. It **delegates** the other five rather than re-implementing them. That is the video's \"merge overlapping into compound skills,\" applied honestly.","+`aios-audit` owns the one layer none of them touch — **the harness itself**: skill roster, agents, hooks, rules, cross-harness parity. It **delegates** the other five rather than re-implementing them."," "," - [ ] **Step 1: Write `~/.claude/rot.md`**"," ","+Note the `Last audited` column (R10): every layer starts `unknown` except Skills, which was genuinely audited. A date is written only after that layer's check actually runs.","+"," ```markdown"," # rot.md — how fast each AIOS layer goes stale"," "," Audit each layer on its own clock, not all at once. `aios-audit` reads this","-file: any layer past due is a RED finding.","+file: any layer past due, or marked `unknown`, is a finding."," ","-| Layer | Where it lives | Rots | Audit every | Last audited | Owner |","-|---|---|---|---|---|---|","-| Identity | `global-memory/USER.md` | very slow | 3 months | 2026-09-12 | memory-write |","-| Substrate | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `~/DEV/*.md` routers | slow | 3 months | 2026-09-12 | os-audit |","-| Skills | `~/.agents/skills/` | fast | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit |","-| Agents | `~/.claude/agents/` | fast | 1 month | never (0 exist) | aios-audit |","-| Rules | `CLAUDE.md` behaviour rules, output styles | fastest | 1 week | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit |","-| Hooks | `~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/` | slow | 6 months | 2026-09-12 | claude-stack-doctor |","-| Memory | `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `global-memory/` | medium | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | improve-system |","-| Data feeds | Fireflies, GPS, CFBD, Zotero | varies | 1 week | 2026-09-12 | os-audit |","+**A date here is a receipt, not an intention.** Write one only after that","+layer's check in the last column has actually run and produced output."," ","+| Layer | Where it lives | Rots | Audit every | Last audited | Owner | Concrete check |","+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|","+| Identity | `global-memory/USER.md` | very slow | 3 months | unknown | memory-write | Read USER.md; flag any role, family, or career fact contradicted by the last 90 days of sessions. |","+| Substrate | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `~/DEV/*.md` routers | slow | 3 months | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 1 (routing integrity). |","+| Skills | `~/.claude/skills/` | fast | 1 month | 2026-09-12 | aios-audit | `skill-portability-check.py` + never-used list + the crutch test below. |","+| Agents | `~/.claude/agents/` | fast | 1 month | unknown | aios-audit | Count `*.md`. Currently **0 exist** — report \"no agents defined\" and skip until one does. |","+| Rules | `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` behaviour rules, output styles | fastest | 1 week | unknown | aios-audit | Diff CLAUDE.md against the last 7 days of sessions: any rule contradicted by what actually happened, and any correction the user repeated twice that is not yet a rule. |","+| Hooks | `~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/` | slow | 6 months | unknown | claude-stack-doctor | Delegate. Also diff the two copies — they must match (`hook-two-copies-trap`). |","+| Memory | `~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `global-memory/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | improve-system | Delegate to `improve-system`. Also check `MEMORY.md` char cap. |","+| Data feeds | Fireflies, GPS, CFBD, Zotero | varies | 1 week | unknown | os-audit | Delegate to `os-audit` Check 3 (freshness). |","+| Vault | `~/SecondBrain/` | medium | 1 month | unknown | brain-audit | Delegate to `brain-audit` (four C's score). |","+| Context posture | workspace-wide | medium | 3 months | unknown | doctor-plus | Delegate to `doctor-plus`. |","+"," ## The question that governs a skill's fate"," "," Not \"how often did I run it?\" but **\"would the current model do this fine"]},{"oldStart":547,"oldLines":26,"newStart":856,"newLines":36,"lines":[" force-plate thresholds, GPS conventions, Baylor reporting norms, Eric's own"," routines."," ","-Seasonal skills (`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`) are exempt from","-low-use pressure. Judge them in season.","+Seasonal skills (`forecast-capture`, `ingest-book`) are exempt from low-use","+pressure. Judge them in season."," ","+## Layout invariants the audit must assert","+","+- `~/.claude/skills/` is canonical, is a git repo, and contains **no symlinks**.","+- `~/.agents/skills/` and `~/.codex/skills/` are **generated views**. A real","+  directory in either means someone edited a view instead of the source.","+- Support directories (`_shared/`) have no SKILL.md and must never be pruned.","+"," ## Direction of travel","+"," **Fewer skills, stricter rules.** Skills get deleted; rules get sharpened."," ```"," ","-Verify: `cat ~/.claude/rot.md | head -20`","+Verify: `head -30 ~/.claude/rot.md`"," "," - [ ] **Step 2: Write the metrics script**"," ","-Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`. It must print exactly these lines, one `key=value` per line, and nothing else on stdout:","+Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`. Counting is deduped by real path (R6) and session globbing is explicitly recursive:"," "," ```python"," #!/usr/bin/env python3","-\"\"\"Machine-readable AIOS metrics. stdlib only. Prints key=value lines.\"\"\"","+\"\"\"Machine-readable AIOS metrics. stdlib only. Prints key=value lines only.\"\"\""," import os, re, glob, subprocess, collections, datetime, sys"," "," H = os.path.expanduser(\"~\")","+CANON = f\"{H}/.claude/skills\""," POOL = f\"{H}/.agents/skills\"","+CODEX = f\"{H}/.codex/skills\""," BUILTIN = {'exit','clear','compact','rename','color','login','logout','plugin',"," 'doctor','help','model','config','resume','cost','status','init','agents','mcp',"," 'hooks','memory','review','vim','terminal-setup','install-github-app','add-dir',"]},{"oldStart":575,"oldLines":9,"newStart":894,"newLines":20,"lines":[" 'tasks','loop','context','usage','rewind','sandbox','feedback','worktrees',"," 'skills','reload-skills','reload-plugins','bashes','keybindings','theme'}"," ","-def count(pattern):","-    return len(glob.glob(pattern))","+def skills_in(root):","+    \"\"\"Count distinct skills under root, deduped by real path (R6).","+    glob traverses symlinked dirs, so a link and its target counted twice.\"\"\"","+    seen = set()","+    for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\")):","+        s = os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\")","+        if os.path.isfile(s):","+            seen.add(os.path.realpath(s))","+    return len(seen)"," ","+def names_in(root):","+    return {os.path.basename(d) for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\"))","+            if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\"))}","+"," def usage():","     c = collections.Counter()","     for f in glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl\", recursive=True):"]},{"oldStart":585,"oldLines":7,"newStart":915,"newLines":8,"lines":["             for line in open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\"):","                 if 'Skill' not in line and 'command-name' not in line:","                     continue","-                for m in re.finditer(r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):","+                for m in re.finditer(","+                        r'\"name\"\\s*:\\s*\"Skill\"\\s*,\\s*\"input\"\\s*:\\s*\\{[^}]*?\"skill\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\"', line):","                     c[m.group(1)] += 1","                 for m in re.finditer(r'<command-name>/([a-zA-Z0-9:_-]+)</command-name>', line):","                     c[m.group(1)] += 1"]},{"oldStart":594,"oldLines":52,"newStart":925,"newLines":81,"lines":["     return {k: v for k, v in c.items()","             if k.split(':')[-1] not in BUILTIN and k not in BUILTIN}"," ","-def token_estimate():","-    \"\"\"Roster cost ~= sum of name+description chars / 4.\"\"\"","+def roster_tokens():","+    \"\"\"Frontmatter chars / 4 - what the roster costs per session.\"\"\"","     total = 0","-    for p in glob.glob(f\"{POOL}/*/SKILL.md\"):","+    for p in glob.glob(f\"{CANON}/*/SKILL.md\"):","         try:","             t = open(p, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read()","             end = t.find(\"\\n---\", 3)","             if t.startswith(\"---\") and end != -1:","-                total += len(t[3:end])","+                total += len(t[4:end])","         except Exception:","             pass","     return total // 4"," ","+def real_dirs_in_view(root):","+    \"\"\"A real dir in a generated view means someone edited the wrong copy.\"\"\"","+    n = 0","+    for d in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, \"*\")):","+        b = os.path.basename(d)","+        if b.startswith('.'):","+            continue","+        if os.path.isdir(d) and not os.path.islink(d) and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, \"SKILL.md\")):","+            n += 1","+    return n","+"," u = usage()","-print(f\"date={datetime.date.today().isoformat()}\")","-print(f\"pool_skills={count(POOL + '/*/SKILL.md')}\")","-print(f\"claude_skills={len(glob.glob(H + '/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md')) + len([d for d in glob.glob(H + '/.claude/skills/*') if os.path.islink(d) and os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, 'SKILL.md'))])}\")","-print(f\"codex_skills={len([d for d in glob.glob(H + '/.codex/skills/*') if os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, 'SKILL.md'))])}\")","-print(f\"project_skills={count(H + '/DEV/*/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md')}\")","-print(f\"agents={count(H + '/.claude/agents/*.md')}\")","-print(f\"hooks_claude={count(H + '/.claude/hooks/*')}\")","-print(f\"hooks_codex={count(H + '/.codex/hooks/*')}\")","-print(f\"sessions={count(H + '/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl')}\")","-print(f\"skills_ever_used={len(u)}\")","-print(f\"skills_used_3plus={len([k for k,v in u.items() if v>=3])}\")","-print(f\"skills_used_once={len([k for k,v in u.items() if v==1])}\")","-print(f\"never_used={len([p for p in glob.glob(POOL + '/*/SKILL.md') if os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(p)) not in u])}\")","-print(f\"roster_tokens_est={token_estimate()}\")","+canon_names = names_in(CANON)","+out = [","+    (\"date\", datetime.date.today().isoformat()),","+    (\"canonical_skills\", skills_in(CANON)),","+    (\"pool_entries\", len(glob.glob(f\"{POOL}/*\"))),","+    (\"pool_skills\", skills_in(POOL)),","+    (\"codex_skills\", skills_in(CODEX)),","+    (\"project_skills\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/DEV/*/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md\"))),","+    (\"symlinks_in_canonical\", len([d for d in glob.glob(f\"{CANON}/*\") if os.path.islink(d)])),","+    (\"real_dirs_in_pool_view\", real_dirs_in_view(POOL)),","+    (\"real_dirs_in_codex_view\", real_dirs_in_view(CODEX)),","+    (\"agents\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/agents/*.md\"))),","+    (\"hooks_claude\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/hooks/*\"))),","+    (\"hooks_codex\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.codex/hooks/*\"))),","+    (\"sessions\", len(glob.glob(f\"{H}/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl\", recursive=True))),","+    (\"skills_ever_used\", len(u)),","+    (\"skills_used_3plus\", len([k for k, v in u.items() if v >= 3])),","+    (\"skills_used_once\", len([k for k, v in u.items() if v == 1])),","+    (\"never_used\", len(canon_names - set(u))),","+    (\"roster_tokens_est\", roster_tokens()),","+]"," rc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, f\"{H}/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\"],","                     capture_output=True, text=True)","-fails = re.search(r\"(\\d+) failing\", rc.stdout)","-print(f\"portability_failures={fails.group(1) if fails else 'unknown'}\")","+m = re.search(r\"(\\d+) failing\", rc.stdout)","+out.append((\"portability_failures\", m.group(1) if m else f\"error_rc{rc.returncode}\"))","+","+for k, v in out:","+    print(f\"{k}={v}\")"," ```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 3: Run it and confirm the shape**","+- [ ] **Step 3: Run it and check every value**"," "," ```bash"," chmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py"," python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py"," ```"," ","-Expected: 16 `key=value` lines, no stack trace. `pool_skills` should equal Task 2's post-prune count. `project_skills` should be `2` (`os-audit` + `aios-audit`). If any value reads `0` where it should not, fix the glob before continuing.","+Expected: 19 `key=value` lines, no stack trace, and specifically:"," ","+- `canonical_skills=61`","+- `symlinks_in_canonical=0` — the Task 4 invariant","+- `real_dirs_in_pool_view=0` and `real_dirs_in_codex_view=0` — views are generated","+- `project_skills=2` (`os-audit` + `aios-audit`)","+- `portability_failures=0`","+","+Any non-zero in the middle three means a later hand-edit broke the layout. Fix before continuing.","+"," - [ ] **Step 4: Write the `aios-audit` skill**"," ","-Invoke `CreateSkill` first — it owns skill authoring in this workspace. Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` with this frontmatter (note it satisfies the Task 4 contract):","+Invoke `CreateSkill` first — it owns skill authoring here. Create `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md` with frontmatter that satisfies Task 5's contract:"," "," ```yaml"," ---"]},{"oldStart":662,"oldLines":25,"newStart":1022,"newLines":27,"lines":[" ---"," ```"," ","-The body must specify, in this order:","+The body must specify, in order:"," ","-1. **Read-only.** The only writes are the report and the `Last audited` column in `rot.md`. Never delete a skill during the audit — propose, then wait for approval.","-2. **Step 0 — read `~/.claude/rot.md`.** Compute days since each layer's `Last audited`. Any layer past due is RED and gets audited this run; layers inside their window are reported as green and skipped. This is what makes the audit cheap enough to run monthly.","+1. **Read-only.** The only writes are the report and `rot.md`'s `Last audited` column. **Never delete a skill during the audit** — propose, then wait for approval.","+2. **Step 0 — read `~/.claude/rot.md`.** Compute days since each layer's `Last audited`. A layer that is past due **or `unknown`** gets audited this run; layers inside their window are reported green and skipped. That is what makes a monthly run cheap."," 3. **Step 1 — run `python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py`** and capture every value.","-4. **Step 2 — delegate, do not duplicate.** For each due layer, invoke the owning skill from the `rot.md` table (`os-audit`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `improve-system`, `brain-audit`) and fold its findings in under that layer's heading. Only the harness layer is audited by `aios-audit` itself.","-5. **Step 3 — the harness layer, audited directly:**","-   - **Bloat:** every skill in the pool never invoked across all sessions. List them.","-   - **Crutch test:** for each never-used and once-used skill, answer the rot.md question — *would the current model do this fine without it?* Recommend DELETE, KEEP (tacit knowledge), or SEASONAL with one line of reasoning each.","-   - **Overlap:** skills whose descriptions cover the same trigger space. Recommend merging into one compound skill.","+4. **Step 2 — assert the layout invariants** from `rot.md`. `symlinks_in_canonical`, `real_dirs_in_pool_view`, `real_dirs_in_codex_view` must all be `0`. Any non-zero is a RED finding: someone edited a generated view instead of the canonical repo.","+5. **Step 3 — delegate, do not duplicate.** For each due layer, invoke the owning skill named in the `rot.md` table and fold its findings under that layer's heading. Only the harness layer is audited directly.","+6. **Step 4 — the harness layer, audited directly:**","+   - **Bloat:** every canonical skill never invoked across all sessions. List them.","+   - **Crutch test:** for each never-used and once-used skill, answer the `rot.md` question — *would the current model do this fine without it?* Recommend DELETE, KEEP (tacit knowledge), or SEASONAL, one line of reasoning each.","+   - **Overlap:** skills whose descriptions cover the same trigger space. Recommend merging.","    - **Parity:** run `skill-portability-check.py`. Every failure is a finding.","-   - **Promotion:** global skills used in fewer than 2 projects — candidates for demotion to project-level. Project skills used in 2+ projects — candidates for promotion.","-   - **Canonical drift:** any real directory (not a symlink) under `~/.claude/skills/` or `~/.codex/skills/`. That means someone edited a view instead of the pool.","-6. **Step 4 — write the report** to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, append the metrics line to the trend table at the top of `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`, and update `Last audited` in `rot.md` for every layer audited.","-7. **Step 5 — end with exactly two proposed next builds,** ranked, each with the one command that starts it. Not a list of ten. Two.","+   - **Promotion:** global skills used in fewer than 2 projects — demotion candidates. Project skills used in 2+ — promotion candidates.","+   - **Rules:** diff `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` against the last 7 days of sessions. Report rules contradicted by what actually happened, and corrections the user repeated twice that are not yet rules.","+   - **Agents:** count `~/.claude/agents/*.md`. If `0`, report \"no agents defined\" and skip — do not invent a finding.","+7. **Step 5 — write the report** to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, append the metrics row to `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`, and update `Last audited` in `rot.md` **only for layers whose check actually ran and produced output**.","+8. **Step 6 — end with exactly two proposed next builds,** ranked, each with the one command that starts it. Not ten. Two."," "," - [ ] **Step 5: Cross-reference `os-audit` so the two do not fight**"," ","-Add one line near the top of `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md`:","+Add near the top of `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md`:"," "," ```markdown"," > Scope note: this skill audits **project data** — routing, indexes, freshness,"]},{"oldStart":688,"oldLines":45,"newStart":1050,"newLines":43,"lines":[" > parity) belongs to `aios-audit`, which calls this skill for the data layer."," ```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 6: Smoke-test the skill end to end**","+- [ ] **Step 6: Smoke-test end to end**"," ","-```bash","-cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS","-```","+In a Claude Code session with cwd `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`, run `/reload-skills`, then type \"audit my AI OS\". Expected: `aios-audit` fires, reads `rot.md`, runs the metrics script, produces a report, and ends with exactly two proposals."," ","-In a Claude Code session with cwd `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`, run `/reload-skills`, then type \"audit my AI OS\". Expected: `aios-audit` fires, reads `rot.md`, runs the metrics script, and produces a report file. Confirm the report exists:","-"," ```bash"," ls -l ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-audit-*.md","+grep -c 'unknown' ~/.claude/rot.md"," ```"," ","+Expected: a report file exists. The `unknown` count should have **dropped** by exactly the number of layers whose checks ran — not to zero.","+"," - [ ] **Step 7: Commit**"," "," ```bash"," cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS","-git add .claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md .claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md scripts/aios-metrics.py docs/audits/","+cp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md","+git add .claude/skills/aios-audit/SKILL.md .claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md \\","+        scripts/aios-metrics.py docs/rot.md docs/audits/"," git commit -m \"feat: aios-audit compound skill + rot.md decay tiers"," "," Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"","-cp ~/.claude/rot.md ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/rot.md 2>/dev/null && git add docs/rot.md && git commit -m \"docs: track rot.md in the repo","-","-Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\""," ```"," "," ---"," ","-### Task 6: Put the audit on a monthly clock","+### Task 7: Put the metrics on a monthly clock"," "," **Files:**"," - Create: `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`"," - Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`","-- Modify: `~/.claude/rot.md` (add the cadence line)","+- Modify: `~/.claude/rot.md` (cadence note)"," "," **Interfaces:**","-- Consumes: `aios-metrics.py` from Task 5.","-- Produces: a monthly trend row in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`. Nothing consumes this; it is the terminal deliverable.","+- Consumes: `aios-metrics.py` from Task 6.","+- Produces: a monthly trend row in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`. Terminal deliverable."," ","-**Design choice:** the cron job runs the **metrics script only** and appends a trend row plus a nudge. It does not run the full agentic audit unattended — an agent that proposes deletions with nobody reading the proposal is how a system gets quietly broken. The job's job is to make the audit impossible to forget.","+**Design choice, tightened after R9.** The job runs the **metrics script only**, appends a trend row, and writes a nudge. It does **not** commit, does **not** push, and does **not** run the agentic audit. Reasons, all from R9: committing from the shared working tree publishes whatever the user had staged; a `git push` failure is easy to mask; and the 09:00 backup may run up to 240 minutes, so start-time spacing is not a race guard. The human commits the trend row during the audit. The job's only job is to make the audit impossible to forget."," "," - [ ] **Step 1: Write the cron wrapper**"," "]},{"oldStart":735,"oldLines":44,"newStart":1095,"newLines":71,"lines":[" ```bash"," #!/usr/bin/env bash"," # Monthly: append AIOS metrics to the trend table and nudge for a real audit.","-# Does NOT run the agentic audit - that needs a human reading the output.","+# Deliberately does NOT commit, push, or run the agentic audit (see R9):","+#  - committing from the shared tree would publish the user's staged files","+#  - the agentic audit proposes deletions and needs a human reading them"," set -uo pipefail","+"," REPO=\"$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS\""," TREND=\"$REPO/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\""," LOG=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\"","+LOCK=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\""," mkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$LOG\")\" \"$(dirname \"$TREND\")\""," "," exec >>\"$LOG\" 2>&1"," echo \"=== $(date -u +%FT%TZ) aios-audit-cron ===\""," ","-M=\"$(python3 \"$REPO/scripts/aios-metrics.py\")\" || { echo \"metrics FAILED\"; exit 1; }","+# Single-instance lock. mkdir is atomic; no flock on stock macOS.","+if ! mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then","+  echo \"ABORT another run holds $LOCK\"","+  exit 0","+fi","+trap 'rmdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null' EXIT","+","+if ! M=\"$(python3 \"$REPO/scripts/aios-metrics.py\" 2>&1)\"; then","+  echo \"FAIL metrics script errored:\"","+  echo \"$M\"","+  exit 1","+fi"," echo \"$M\""," ","-get() { echo \"$M\" | grep \"^$1=\" | cut -d= -f2; }","+get() {","+  local v","+  v=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$M\" | grep \"^$1=\" | cut -d= -f2-)\"","+  if [ -z \"$v\" ]; then echo \"MISSING\"; else echo \"$v\"; fi","+}"," "," if [ ! -f \"$TREND\" ]; then","   {","     echo \"# AIOS trend\"","     echo","     echo \"One row per monthly run. Written by scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh.\"","+    echo \"The script does not commit; the human commits during the audit.\"","     echo","-    echo \"| date | pool | claude | codex | project | never used | roster tok | portability fails |\"","-    echo \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"","+    echo \"| date | canonical | pool | codex | project | never used | roster tok | portability fails | symlinks in canon |\"","+    echo \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"","   } > \"$TREND\""," fi"," ","-printf '| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |\\n' \\","-  \"$(get date)\" \"$(get pool_skills)\" \"$(get claude_skills)\" \"$(get codex_skills)\" \\","+ROW=\"$(printf '| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |' \\","+  \"$(get date)\" \"$(get canonical_skills)\" \"$(get pool_skills)\" \"$(get codex_skills)\" \\","   \"$(get project_skills)\" \"$(get never_used)\" \"$(get roster_tokens_est)\" \\","-  \"$(get portability_failures)\" >> \"$TREND\"","+  \"$(get portability_failures)\" \"$(get symlinks_in_canonical)\")\""," ","-echo \"[$(date +%F)] aios-audit due - run \\\"audit my AI OS\\\" in ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\" \\","+if printf '%s' \"$ROW\" | grep -q 'MISSING'; then","+  echo \"FAIL a metrics key did not resolve; row not written:\"","+  echo \"$ROW\"","+  exit 1","+fi","+","+printf '%s\\n' \"$ROW\" >> \"$TREND\"","+echo \"wrote: $ROW\"","+","+echo \"[$(date +%F)] aios-audit due - run \\\"audit my AI OS\\\" in ~/DEV/KeystoneOS (uncommitted trend row waiting)\" \\","   >> \"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\""," ","-cd \"$REPO\" && git add docs/audits/aios-trend.md \\","-  && git commit -m \"chore: monthly AIOS metrics $(get date)\" \\","-  && git push 2>&1 | tail -2","-echo \"=== done ===\"","+echo \"=== done ok ===\"","+exit 0"," ```"," "," ```bash"]},{"oldStart":782,"oldLines":17,"newStart":1169,"newLines":35,"lines":[" - [ ] **Step 2: Run it by hand and verify the trend row**"," "," ```bash","-~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh","+~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$?\""," cat ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md","-tail -20 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log","+tail -25 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log","+git -C ~/DEV/KeystoneOS status --short"," ```"," ","-Expected: a header plus one data row with no empty cells. Empty cells mean a `get` key does not match a metrics key — fix the key name, delete the bad row, re-run.","+Expected: `exit=0`; a header plus one row with **no `MISSING` cells**; the log ends `=== done ok ===`; `git status` shows `aios-trend.md` as **modified but uncommitted** — that is correct, the human commits it."," ","-- [ ] **Step 3: Write the launchd plist**","+- [ ] **Step 3: Prove the failure paths (R9's masked-failure point)**"," ","-Create `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`. Day 1 at 09:30 — after the 09:00 daily backup, so it never races it:","+```bash","+# A bad metrics script must fail the job, not write a row.","+mv ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py /tmp/aios-metrics.bak","+~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 1)\"","+mv /tmp/aios-metrics.bak ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py"," ","+# A stale lock must not wedge the job forever.","+mkdir -p ~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock","+~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 0, ABORT logged)\"","+rmdir ~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock","+tail -6 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md","+```","+","+Expected: the first prints `exit=1` and adds **no** row; the second prints `exit=0` with `ABORT` in the log and **no** row. If either wrote a row, the failure propagation is not working — fix before loading launchd.","+","+- [ ] **Step 4: Write the launchd plist**","+","+Create `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`. Day 1 at 14:00 — well clear of the 09:00 backup's possible 240-minute window, and the lock covers the rest:","+"," ```xml"," <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>"," <!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\""]},{"oldStart":809,"oldLines":8,"newStart":1214,"newLines":8,"lines":["   <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>","   <dict>","     <key>Day</key><integer>1</integer>","-    <key>Hour</key><integer>9</integer>","-    <key>Minute</key><integer>30</integer>","+    <key>Hour</key><integer>14</integer>","+    <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>","   </dict>","   <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>","   <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.out</string>"]},{"oldStart":819,"oldLines":7,"newStart":1224,"newLines":7,"lines":[" </plist>"," ```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 4: Load it and verify launchd accepted it**","+- [ ] **Step 5: Load it and verify launchd accepted it**"," "," ```bash"," launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist 2>/dev/null"]},{"oldStart":827,"oldLines":9,"newStart":1232,"newLines":9,"lines":[" launchctl list | grep aiosaudit"," ```"," ","-Expected: one line ending `com.keystone.aiosaudit`. A non-zero middle column means the last run failed — check `/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err`.","+Expected: one line ending `com.keystone.aiosaudit`. A non-zero middle column means the last run failed — read `/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err`."," ","-- [ ] **Step 5: Force a run through launchd, not just bash**","+- [ ] **Step 6: Force a run through launchd, not just bash**"," "," ```bash"," launchctl start com.keystone.aiosaudit"]},{"oldStart":837,"oldLines":191,"newStart":1242,"newLines":243,"lines":[" tail -5 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md"," ```"," ","-Expected: `.err` empty, a second trend row appended. A launchd run has a different PATH than an interactive shell — this step is what catches that. If `python3` is not found, use an absolute interpreter path in `aios-metrics.py`'s shebang.","+Expected: `.err` empty, a second row appended. launchd has a different PATH than an interactive shell — this step catches that. If `python3` is not found, put an absolute interpreter in `aios-metrics.py`'s shebang."," ","-- [ ] **Step 6: Remove the duplicate test row, note the cadence, commit**","+- [ ] **Step 7: De-duplicate test rows, note the cadence, commit**"," "," ```bash"," cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS"," python3 - <<'EOF'","-import re","-p=\"docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"","-lines=open(p).read().splitlines()","-seen=set(); out=[]","+p = \"docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"","+lines = open(p).read().splitlines()","+seen, out = set(), []"," for l in lines:","     if l.startswith(\"| 20\"):","-        if l in seen: continue","+        if l in seen:","+            continue","         seen.add(l)","     out.append(l)","-open(p,\"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(out)+\"\\n\")","+open(p, \"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\")"," EOF"," cat docs/audits/aios-trend.md","-printf '\\n## Cadence\\nMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 09:30.\\nThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.\\n' >> ~/.claude/rot.md","+cat >> ~/.claude/rot.md <<'EOF'","+","+## Cadence","+","+Monthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 14:00.","+It writes a trend row and a nudge. It does not commit and does not push.","+The agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.","+EOF"," cp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md"," git add scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh docs/audits/aios-trend.md docs/rot.md","-git commit -m \"feat: monthly AIOS metrics on launchd","+git commit -m \"feat: monthly AIOS metrics on launchd, no unattended commits"," "," Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\""," ```"," "," ---"," ","-### Task 7: Pi sandbox trial and one hook port","+### Task 8: Pi trial under a real boundary"," "," **Files:**","-- Create: `~/pi-trial/` (throwaway, never `~/DEV`)","-- Create: `~/pi-trial/AGENTS.md`","-- Create: `~/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`","+- Create: macOS standard user `piuser` (or an equivalent enforced boundary)","+- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md`","+- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`"," - Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`"," "," **Interfaces:**","-- Consumes: the canonical pool from Task 3 (Pi auto-discovers `~/.agents/skills/`), and the portable frontmatter from Task 4.","-- Produces: a measured migration cost in `2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`. Nothing consumes it; it is a decision input.","+- Consumes: a copy of the pruned pool (Task 2) and the portable frontmatter (Task 5).","+- Produces: a measured migration cost. Nothing consumes it; it is a decision input."," ","-**This task is last for a reason.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — that is documented, deliberate design, not an oversight. Running Pi before Task 2 prunes the pool would expose all 306 skills. Running it anywhere near `~/DEV` or `DexaProject` risks unprompted writes to real athlete data.","+**Why the boundary is mandatory (R5).** Round 1 ran Pi with `cd ~/pi-trial` and called that a sandbox. It is not — `cd` restricts nothing, and the Pi research note says so explicitly. Pi ships no approval prompts by design. A regex denylist inside the agent is defence-in-depth, **not** a boundary: `cat ../DEV/file`, a shell-expanded path, or any tool the guard does not cover walks straight past it. The only enforced boundary available without new infrastructure is **OS file permissions**, so Pi runs as a different user that cannot read Eric's home."," ","-**Stop conditions.** If Pi cannot be installed, or the guard extension does not block after two attempts, stop and write up what failed. Do not iterate past that — the deliverable here is a cost measurement, not a working Pi setup.","+**Scope, narrowed.** The deliverable is a **cost number**, not a working Pi setup. **ADOPT is not an available verdict in this plan** — adopting Pi for real work requires a boundary design that is its own project. Verdicts here are `COST-MEASURED` or `BLOCKED`."," ","-- [ ] **Step 1: Install into a throwaway directory**","+**Stop conditions.** If the user cannot be created, or Pi will not install, or the guard does not block after two attempts, stop and write up what failed. Do not iterate past that."," ","+- [ ] **Step 1: Create the boundary user**","+","+This needs admin rights and is the one step Eric may prefer to run himself:","+"," ```bash","-mkdir -p ~/pi-trial && cd ~/pi-trial","-npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent","-which -a pi && pi --version","+sudo sysadminctl -addUser piuser -fullName \"Pi Trial\" -password - -home /Users/piuser","+sudo dscl . -read /Users/piuser NFSHomeDirectory"," ```"," ","-Expected: a path under `/opt/homebrew/` and a version string. `--ignore-scripts` is deliberate: no postinstall scripts run.","+Then prove the boundary actually holds:"," ","-- [ ] **Step 2: Confirm what Pi actually discovers**","+```bash","+chmod 700 ~                      # confirm Eric's home is not world-readable","+sudo -u piuser ls ~/DEV          2>&1 | tail -2","+sudo -u piuser ls ~/SecondBrain  2>&1 | tail -2","+```"," ","+Expected: both `ls` attempts print **Permission denied**. If either succeeds, the boundary does not exist — STOP. Do not fall back to \"just be careful.\"","+","+- [ ] **Step 2: Install Pi as that user**","+"," ```bash","-cd ~/pi-trial","-cat > AGENTS.md <<'EOF'","-# pi-trial","+sudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/pi-trial","+sudo -u piuser npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent 2>&1 | tail -5","+sudo -u piuser bash -lc 'which -a pi && pi --version'","+```"," ","-Throwaway sandbox for evaluating the Pi harness. Nothing here is real work.","+Expected: a path and a version string. `--ignore-scripts` is deliberate — no postinstall scripts run."," ","-HARD RULES","-- Never read or write outside ~/pi-trial.","-- Never touch ~/DEV, ~/SecondBrain, or any Google Drive path.","-- Never touch anything named DexaProject.","-EOF","-pi --print \"List every skill you can see. Print only the count and the first ten names.\" 2>&1 | tail -20","+- [ ] **Step 3: Give it a copy of the pruned pool, not the real one**","+","+```bash","+sudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/.agents","+sudo rsync -a --copy-links ~/.agents/skills/ /Users/piuser/.agents/skills/","+sudo chown -R piuser:staff /Users/piuser/.agents","+sudo -u piuser bash -lc 'ls /Users/piuser/.agents/skills | wc -l'"," ```"," ","-Expected: a count close to Task 2's post-prune `pool_skills`. **If the count is near 306, Task 2 did not take — stop and re-run it.** This step is the proof that pruning the pool was the right fix.","+Expected: the Task 2 post-prune count (208). A **copy**, so a Pi misstep cannot corrupt the real pool."," ","-- [ ] **Step 3: Verify Pi has no guard, so the port is necessary**","+- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm what Pi discovers**"," "," ```bash","-cd ~/pi-trial","-pi --print \"Run: ls ~/DEV | head -3\" 2>&1 | tail -10","+sudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cat > /Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md <<EOF","+# pi-trial","+","+Throwaway sandbox for evaluating the Pi harness. Nothing here is real work.","+This user has no read access to the operator home directory by design.","+EOF","+cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"List every skill you can see. Print only the count and the first ten names.\" 2>&1 | tail -20'"," ```"," ","-Expected: it lists `~/DEV` with no approval prompt, despite `AGENTS.md` saying not to. That is the finding — context files are guidance, not enforcement. Record the exact output. Nothing was written, so no harm; do not escalate to a write test.","+Expected: a count near 208, not 306. **If it reads ~306, Task 2 did not take — stop and re-run it.** This step is the proof that pruning the pool was the right fix."," ","-- [ ] **Step 4: Port `dcg` to a Pi extension**","+> Round 1 had a probe here that asked an unguarded Pi to `ls ~/DEV`. It is deleted. Pointing an unrestrained agent at real directories to demonstrate that it is unrestrained is not a test worth running. The OS boundary in Step 1 already establishes the same fact safely."," ","+- [ ] **Step 5: Port `dcg` to a Pi extension, using the documented contract**","+"," Read the existing guard first:"," "," ```bash"," ls ~/.claude/hooks/ | grep -i -E 'dcg|guard'","+grep -n 'pi.on\\|ExtensionAPI\\|event.input' ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md"," ```"," ","-Create `~/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`. Pi's `tool_call` event returns `{block, reason}`, which maps directly onto the hook's deny decision:","+Round 1 used `export default { tool_call }` and `ev.args`. The research note documents an **exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`**, registration via `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, and a payload at `event.input` (R4). Write `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts` to the documented shape. If the note and the installed Pi version disagree, **the installed version wins** — record the discrepancy in the report."," "," ```typescript"," // Minimal port of the dcg guard. Proves the hook -> extension mapping.","-const DENY = [","+// Defence in depth only: the real boundary is the piuser file permissions.","+const DENY: RegExp[] = [","   /\\brm\\s+-rf\\s+\\/(?!tmp)/,","   /DexaProject/i,","   /GoogleDrive-/,","-  /\\/DEV\\//,","-  /\\/SecondBrain\\//,","+  /(^|[\\s\"'=:])~?\\/?DEV(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,        // R4: bare ~/DEV must match","+  /(^|[\\s\"'=:])~?\\/?SecondBrain(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,"," ];"," ","-export default {","-  name: \"guard\",","-  async tool_call(ev: { tool: string; args: Record<string, unknown> }) {","-    const blob = JSON.stringify(ev.args ?? {});","+export default function guard(pi: any) {","+  pi.on(\"tool_call\", (event: any) => {","+    const blob = JSON.stringify(event?.input ?? {});   // R4: input, not args","     for (const re of DENY) {","       if (re.test(blob)) {","         return { block: true, reason: `guard: blocked by ${re}` };","       }","     }","     return { block: false };","-  },","-};","+  });","+}"," ```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 5: Prove the guard blocks**","+Print mode may require explicitly trusting a project extension; if Pi asks, grant it for this project only and note the step in the report."," ","+- [ ] **Step 6: Prove the guard blocks — and that it does not block everything**","+"," ```bash","-cd ~/pi-trial","-pi --print \"Run: ls ~/DEV | head -3\" 2>&1 | tail -10","-pi --print \"Run: echo hello\" 2>&1 | tail -5","+sudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"Run: ls /Users/piuser/DEV | head -3\" 2>&1 | tail -10'","+sudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && pi --print \"Run: echo hello\" 2>&1 | tail -5'"," ```"," ","-Expected: the first is **blocked** with the guard reason. The second **succeeds** — a guard that blocks everything is not a guard. If the first is not blocked after two attempts at the extension path or event name, stop per the stop conditions.","+Expected: the first is **blocked** with the guard reason; the second **succeeds**. A guard that blocks everything is not a guard. The probe targets a path inside `piuser`'s own home, so the guard — not the OS — is what must stop it. If the first is not blocked after two attempts at the extension path or event name, stop per the stop conditions."," ","-- [ ] **Step 6: Write the cost measurement and commit**","+- [ ] **Step 7: Write the cost measurement**"," "," ```bash"," cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS","-{","-  echo \"# Pi trial - 2026-09-12\"","-  echo","-  echo \"Sandbox: ~/pi-trial (never ~/DEV, never DexaProject).\"","-  echo","-  echo \"| Question | Answer |\"","-  echo \"|---|---|\"","-  echo \"| pi version | FILL IN |\"","-  echo \"| skills auto-discovered | FILL IN (expect pool count, not 306) |\"","-  echo \"| skills needing edits to fire in Pi | FILL IN |\"","-  echo \"| AGENTS.md honoured without an extension? | FILL IN (expect: no) |\"","-  echo \"| minutes to port the dcg guard | FILL IN |\"","-  echo \"| hooks still to port | FILL IN of 61 |\"","-  echo \"| verdict | ADOPT / WAIT / DROP |\"","-  echo","-  echo \"## What the guard proved\"","-  echo \"FILL IN: paste the blocked and allowed outputs from Step 5.\"","-} > docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md","+cat > docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md <<'EOF'","+# Pi trial - 2026-09-12","+","+Boundary: separate macOS user `piuser`, no read access to the operator home.","+`cd` into a directory is NOT a boundary and was not treated as one (R5).","+","+| Question | Answer |","+|---|---|","+| pi version | FILL IN |","+| piuser blocked from ~/DEV and ~/SecondBrain? | FILL IN (expect: yes, Permission denied) |","+| skills auto-discovered | FILL IN (expect ~208, not 306) |","+| skills needing edits to fire in Pi | FILL IN |","+| extension contract matched the research note? | FILL IN (factory + pi.on + event.input) |","+| minutes to port the dcg guard | FILL IN |","+| hooks still to port | FILL IN of 61 |","+| verdict | COST-MEASURED / BLOCKED |","+","+## What the guard proved","+","+FILL IN: paste the blocked and the allowed output from Step 6.","+","+## What a real adoption would still need","+","+Not in scope here. At minimum: a boundary design that is not a second user","+account, an approval-prompt layer, and a port of the remaining hooks.","+EOF"," ```"," ","-Fill in every `FILL IN` from the real runs above — a report with placeholders left in is a failed task. Then:","+Fill in every `FILL IN` from the real runs. A report left with placeholders is a failed task. Then:"," "," ```bash","-cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md && git commit -m \"docs: Pi harness sandbox trial + dcg guard port cost","+cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md && git commit -m \"docs: Pi harness trial under an OS boundary + dcg guard port cost"," "," Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\""," ```"," ","-- [ ] **Step 7: Decide, then clean up or keep**","+- [ ] **Step 8: Tear down**"," ","-If the verdict is DROP or WAIT:","-"," ```bash","-npm uninstall -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent","-rm -rf ~/pi-trial","+sudo -u piuser npm uninstall -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent","+sudo rm -rf /Users/piuser/pi-trial /Users/piuser/.agents","+# Keep or remove the account - Eric's call. To remove:","+# sudo sysadminctl -deleteUser piuser"," ```"," ","-If ADOPT, leave `~/pi-trial` in place and write the next plan. Do not widen Pi's reach beyond the sandbox in this plan.","+The account may stay for future trials. The skill copy and the Pi install should not linger."," "," ---"," "," ## Review Gates (not optional)"," ","-Eric asked for Codex review of both the plan and the code. These are steps, not suggestions.","+Eric asked for Codex review of the plan and of any code. These are steps."," ","-- [ ] **Gate A — before Task 1.** Run `claudex-loop` in review mode on this plan file. Feed Codex the plan plus the two Inbox source notes. Fold its findings back in and re-save before any task executes.","-- [ ] **Gate B — after Task 4.** Run `codex-review` on `skills-relink.sh`, `skill-portability-check.py`, and a sample of ten rewritten SKILL.md frontmatter blocks. Codex is the right reviewer here specifically because it is the harness whose triggering the rewrite claims to fix.","-- [ ] **Gate C — after Task 6.** Run `codex-review` on `aios-metrics.py`, `aios-audit-cron.sh`, the plist, and the `aios-audit` SKILL.md body.","-- [ ] **Gate D — before declaring done.** Run `superpowers:verification-before-completion`. Every success claim needs a pasted command output.","+- [x] **Gate A — round 1, done 2026-09-12.** Verdict **REVISE**, 6 high / 4 medium. Result at `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json`. All ten dispositioned in the table above; R1/R3 inverted the architecture.","+- [ ] **Gate A2 — round 2, blocking.** Resume the same reviewer session rather than starting fresh, so the new plan hash is recorded against it:"," ","+  ```bash","+  cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS","+  python3 ~/.claude/skills/claudex-loop/scripts/runner.py review \\","+    --host claude --repo ~/DEV/KeystoneOS \\","+    --plan docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md \\","+    --resume ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r1/claudex-q_k9_laf/result.json \\","+    --feedback /tmp/aios-dispositions.md \\","+    --artifacts ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r2","+  ```","+","+  `/tmp/aios-dispositions.md` is written from the disposition table above — R1–R10, accept or reject, and what changed. **No task below runs until this returns APPROVED.** R1 and R3 are data-loss grade.","+- [ ] **Gate B — after Task 5.** `codex-review` on `sync-to-codex.sh`, `sync-to-codex.ps1`, `skills-canonicalize.sh`, `skill-portability-check.py`, and ten rewritten frontmatter blocks. Codex is the right reviewer here specifically because it is the harness whose triggering the rewrite claims to fix.","+- [ ] **Gate C — after Task 7.** `codex-review` on `aios-metrics.py`, `aios-audit-cron.sh`, the plist, and the `aios-audit` SKILL.md body.","+- [ ] **Gate D — before declaring done.** `superpowers:verification-before-completion`. Every success claim carries a pasted command output.","+"," ## Out of Scope"," "," - Rewriting plugin skills (`superpowers`, `mattpocock-skills`) — vendored, overwritten on update.","-- Migrating all 61 hooks to Pi. Task 7 ports exactly one to measure cost.","-- Deleting any skill outright. Everything moves to an archive folder with a recorded undo.","-- The Windows machine. Both machines are active; this plan changes Mac paths only. Re-run Tasks 2–4 there separately after they prove out here.","+- Pruning the ~150 pool entries that are third-party installs. Task 2 touches only the 98 that match yesterday's archive. The rest need a human decision via `aios-audit`.","+- Migrating all 61 hooks to Pi. Task 8 ports exactly one to measure cost.","+- **Adopting Pi.** Task 8 produces a cost number only. Adoption needs a boundary design that is its own project.","+- Deleting any skill outright. Everything moves to an archive folder or is recoverable from git.","+- The Windows machine. Both machines are active; this plan changes Mac paths only. `sync-to-codex.ps1` is patched in Task 3, but re-running Tasks 2 and 5 there is separate work after these prove out here."," - Mining the 414 JSONL sessions to reverse-engineer harness behaviour (video gap #9). Real, but its own project."]}],"o