{"parentUuid":"cd87b031-b629-466f-b757-3d248ffc85f9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ceyvp8ATCtvo7YHeUbnZp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`dcg` blocked the dynamic path. Using the Write tool with a literal path instead."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4413,"cache_read_input_tokens":164545,"output_tokens":2465,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":33},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4413,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2465,"cache_read_input_tokens":164545,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4413,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4413},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ceyvp7UXX5hpwaSv6wVjv","attributionSkill":"codex-review","type":"assistant","uuid":"bb5a804c-dd36-4e2a-8158-205c34891e9a","timestamp":"2026-09-12T17:19:56.539Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"bb5a804c-dd36-4e2a-8158-205c34891e9a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ceyvp8ATCtvo7YHeUbnZp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0186de9MHQGKREnADMViLPWc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions.md","content":"# Round 1 dispositions — all ten ACCEPTED\n\nThree findings were verified directly on disk before rewriting. Two of them\ninverted the plan's architecture.\n\n## Verified on disk\n\n- **R3:** `git -C ~/.claude/skills remote -v` → `erash11/claude-skills`.\n  `git log --oneline -5` → `3ab4467 chore: apply global skill audit`, in sync\n  with origin. The earlier \"skills are gitignored\" note was about the `dotclaude`\n  repo and hid that this directory is its own versioned repo. This is the finding\n  that inverted the architecture.\n- **R1:** `sync-to-codex.sh` confirmed to contain `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\"`,\n  with `SHARED=(new-project startup dev-sync closeout session-handoff humanizer\n  articulation-skill)`. A `.ps1` twin exists with the same shape.\n- **R2:** `~/.claude/skills/_shared/` confirmed to hold `config.json` + `Inference/`\n  and to be referenced by 10+ skills including `meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py`.\n\n## Disposition table\n\n| ID | Disposition | What changed in the plan |\n|---|---|---|\n| R1 | ACCEPTED | New **Task 3** hardens both sync scripts BEFORE anything moves. A refuse-if-source-is-a-symlink guard exits 2 with no writes. Task 3 Step 4 proves it with a canary file that must survive a run against a deliberately inverted layout. The PowerShell twin gets an equivalent ReparsePoint guard. |\n| R2 | ACCEPTED | Every \"rm -rf any directory without a SKILL.md\" instruction is deleted from the plan. Task 2 adds guard B, which refuses to prune any directory lacking a SKILL.md. Task 4's script reports `NO-SKILL ... left alone` instead of deleting. Task 4 Step 5 asserts `meeting-sync/scripts/sync.py` still resolves `parents[2]/_shared/config.json` after the move. |\n| R3 | ACCEPTED — **architecture inverted** | Canonical is now `~/.claude/skills/` (the git repo), not `~/.agents/skills/`. The pool and the Codex dir become generated views. Canonical holds real directories only. Task 4 Step 4 asserts `git ls-files -s` stages no mode-120000 entry, so no absolute Mac symlink can reach Windows. Task 1 records `git revert` as the real undo for this repo and notes that `skills-archive/` is not in it. |\n| R4 | ACCEPTED | Task 8 Step 5 uses the documented exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`, registration via `pi.on('tool_call', ...)`, and the payload at `event.input`. The regex is fixed so a bare `~/DEV` with no trailing slash matches. A note says the installed Pi version wins over the research note if the two disagree, and the discrepancy gets recorded. |\n| R5 | ACCEPTED | Task 8 runs Pi as a separate macOS user `piuser` with no read access to the operator home. Step 1 proves the boundary with two Permission-denied checks and stops the task if either succeeds. Pi receives a COPY of the pool, not the real one. The unguarded `ls ~/DEV` probe is deleted entirely. The regex guard is explicitly labelled defence-in-depth, not a boundary. **ADOPT is removed as a possible verdict** — only COST-MEASURED or BLOCKED. |\n| R6 | ACCEPTED | `aios-metrics.py` dedupes by `os.path.realpath`, so a symlink and its target count once. The session glob is explicitly `recursive=True`. Expected counts are derived from post-migration invariants rather than Task 2 directory totals. Three new layout assertions added: `symlinks_in_canonical`, `real_dirs_in_pool_view`, `real_dirs_in_codex_view`, all of which must read 0. |\n| R7 | ACCEPTED | The checker parses the flat YAML subset actually in use, including `>-` and `\\|` block scalars. It requires a positive clause AND a distinct negative clause — overlapping clauses are a failure. It **exits 2 on a missing or empty inventory**, and Task 5 Step 2 proves the empty case cannot report success. Naming is decided explicitly rather than left implicit: the rule is `name` equals the directory name, and TitleCase is legal (`CreateSkill`, `Harvest` already ship that way; renaming would break cross-references). |\n| R8 | ACCEPTED | All optional actions use explicit `if ...; fi` blocks. Functions end with an explicit `return 0` and scripts with `exit 0`. The same pattern is applied to the cron wrapper's final echo. |\n| R9 | ACCEPTED | The cron **no longer commits or pushes at all** — that removes the staged-file leak, the push-failure masking, and the need to win a race in one cut. It writes a trend row and a nudge; the human commits during the audit. Added an atomic `mkdir` lock, explicit failure propagation (a metrics failure exits 1 and writes no row), and a `MISSING` sentinel that blocks a partial row. Schedule moved to 14:00, well clear of the backup's 240-minute window, with the lock covering the remainder. Task 7 Step 3 proves both failure paths write no row. |\n| R10 | ACCEPTED | `rot.md` initializes every layer's `Last audited` to `unknown` except Skills (2026-09-12, genuinely audited). Each layer gains a concrete check in a new column; Agents reads \"0 exist, report and skip\" rather than inventing a finding. `brain-audit` and `doctor-plus` now have their own ownership rows. Dates advance only for layers whose check actually ran and produced output, and Task 6 Step 6 verifies the `unknown` count drops by exactly that many rather than to zero. A stated rule was added: \"a date here is a receipt, not an intention.\" |\n\n## Rejected\n\nNone. All ten findings were accepted.\n\n## Structural changes since round 1\n\n- Task count went 7 → 8. The new Task 3 (harden the sync writers) is inserted\n  ahead of any file movement, because R1 is a data-loss defect that could fire\n  at any point during the migration if `dev-sync` were run.\n- Task ordering now is: snapshot → prune stale mirror → 