{"parentUuid":"966a9a8e-6a31-4d85-baf0-025667bbcee8","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ceyxd1ERAsidAyHYzKKNx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Saved — will be active from next session.\n\n---\n\n## What I did\n\nI read the handoff, then checked the real state of your machine. Two things were not what the handoff said.\n\n**Bad news first.** Yesterday's skill cut did not fully take. 98 of the 99 \"archived\" skills are still sitting in `~/.agents/skills/`. Claude Code can't see them. **Pi reads that exact folder.** If you had installed Pi, all 306 would have come back.\n\n**Second thing.** `~/.claude/skills/` is its own git repo (`erash11/claude-skills`). The handoff said \"gitignored, no safety net.\" That was true of a *different* repo. Git is your safety net, and it's how cuts reach the Windows laptop.\n\nThat second fact flipped the whole design. I wrote a plan, then Codex reviewed it **six times**.\n\n| Round | Result | Findings |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | REVISE | 10 (6 serious) |\n| 2 | REVISE | 9 (2 serious) |\n| 3 | REVISE | 7 (2 serious) |\n| 4 | REVISE | 4 (2 serious) |\n| 5 | REVISE | 1 (0 serious) |\n| 6 | **APPROVED** | **0** |\n\nCodex caught real damage before it happened. One example: your `sync-to-codex.sh` deletes a folder then copies into it. Under my first design, running `/dev-sync` would have wiped your real skills. Twice, Codex caught a bug that *my own fix* had just introduced.\n\n## What you get\n\n**One home for every skill.** `~/.claude/skills/` is the master. Codex and Pi get auto-built copies. Edit once, all three harnesses see it. Each skill's description gets both halves — trigger words (Claude needs these) and a \"do not use when\" line (Codex needs this, or it fires too often).\n\n**A new audit skill, `aios-audit`.** It does **not** replace your five existing audit skills — it calls them. It owns the one thing none of them check: your skills, hooks, and rules going stale. It reads a new `rot.md` that says how fast each layer rots, and it ends with exactly **two** suggestions, not ten. It lives inside KeystoneOS, not global — that's the \"earn your way to global\" rule from the video.\n\n**Pi gets a real fence.** Not a folder. A separate Mac user account that cannot read your home directory. Codex flagged that `cd ~/pi-trial` protects nothing — it was right.\n\n## Where it is\n\n```\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md\n```\n\n8 tasks, 56 steps, pushed to GitHub. Every step has a command and what you should see.\n\nI ran what I could test without changing anything: all scripts parse, the manifest guard blocks `*` and `../../.claude/skills`, and the skill-deletion flow leaves zero broken links.\n\n**Nothing on your machine has changed yet.** The plan is approved but not run.\n\n## Your call\n\nPick one:\n\n- **Run it now** — start Task 1 (snapshot and freeze). Safe, reversible, ~10 minutes.\n- **Read it first** — open the file, then tell me to go.\n\nI'd run it. Task 1 only takes backups; nothing is touched until Task 2, and every step has an undo.\n\nresult: Codex-approved 8-task plan at `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md` (pushed) makes all 61 skills harness-agnostic from one git-backed home and adds an `aios-audit` skill — 6 review rounds, 10 findings → 0; found that 98 supposedly-archived skills are still live in the folder Pi reads."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":467,"cache_read_input_tokens":296150,"output_tokens":1260,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":467,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1260,"cache_read_input