{"parentUuid":"453b12b2-68c0-433b-864f-9006d142b28a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d90457fc-55a4-4c91-beb9-bfaecc9c0aa3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014WcnTF7RzKsKSN3ken8Pvr","type":"tool_result","content":"provider: claude roles: {'host': 'codex', 'planner': 'codex', 'reviewer': 'claude', 'builder': 'codex', 'inspector': 'claude'}\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: The plan's guards are careful, but Task 4 treats the wrong set of skills as the user's own. Of the 15 \"ordinary outward links\" it will copy into the canonical repo, 14 are third-party installs recorded in ~/.agents/.skill-lock.json. Five of those come from mattpocock-skills, which the plan itself puts out of scope. The documented install procedure (the vendored-skill-install memory, plus the skills CLI with claude-code selected) also creates exactly the outward symlink that the hardened sync refuses and that daily-backup's `git add -A` would commit. So the architecture breaks on the next skill install or update. Other problems: the Task 5 checker's allowed-key list rejects `effort` and `user-invocable`, which the current roster uses. The PowerShell twin's vendor check and link removal don't match the documented Windows link layout. Gate A is stale by the plan's own rule, because R7 and AMEND-01 changes landed after round 6 approved it. There are also several stale counts and ordering gaps.\nfindings: 11\n\n[HIGH] R8-01  docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (Task 4 Ste\n  E: ~/.agents/.skill-lock.json lists 14 of the 15 links Task 4 inverts as skills-CLI installs: anti-sleep, before-building, decisions, next-decision (davidondrej/skills); cli-hub-meta-skill (HKUDS); design-taste-frontend and -v1 (Leonxlnx); scroll-craft (nateherkai); watch (bradautomates); and code-review, grilling, handoff, prototype, teach with pluginName \"mattpocock-skills\". Only persistent-localho\n  F: Build the vendor exemption from the install ledger (keys in .skill-lock.json, plus links into vendor-review, plus a declared list of known hand-installs such as persistent-localhost) instead of the hard-coded four names. Use that same inventory in al\n\n[HIGH] R8-02  ~/.claude/scripts/daily-backup.ps1; ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh \n  E: The documented install procedure (memory lines 23, 36-37: `npx skills add ... -a claude-code -a codex`, or by hand `ln -s ../../.agents/skills/NAME ~/.claude/skills/NAME`) and the ledger's lastSelectedAgents (includes claude-code, codex, pi) both create a new outward symlink in canonical on every third-party install. Nothing in the plan changes that procedure. After Task 3, the next such install m\n  F: Update the vendored-skill-install procedure (and tell the skills CLI which agents to target) so installs never create links in ~/.claude/skills, for example by installing to the pool only and letting a ledger-aware sync publish the views. Or have a c\n\n[MEDIUM] R8-03  ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py (Task 5 Step 1 ALL\n  E: The canonical roster already uses frontmatter keys that are not in ALLOWED. `effort:` appears in Science/SKILL.md:4, ArXiv/SKILL.md:4, CreateSkill/SKILL.md:4 and ISA/SKILL.md:4, and `user-invocable:` appears in impeccable/SKILL.md:5. check() adds 'unknown frontmatter key' for each one, so Task 5 Step 4's 'checked 57 skills, 0 failing' can only be reached by deleting working Claude Code keys, which\n  F: Enumerate the keys in actual use across the roster before fixing the contract. Add `effort` and `user-invocable` (plus any other supported Claude Code keys) to ALLOWED, or turn unknown keys into warnings. Say explicitly that normalization must not re\n\n[MEDIUM] R8-04  ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1 (Task 3 Step 3)\n  E: Vendor verification: the bash twin resolves the whole chain with `readlink -f`. The PowerShell twin uses `(Get-Item).Target`, which returns only the first hop. Under the documented layout (inventory AMEND-01 lines 540-542: canonical -> ~/.agents/skills/X -> ~/.agents/vendor-review/...), the first hop is the pool path, which does not start with $VENDORROOT. So if the package is installed on Windows\n  F: Resolve targets fully (follow .Target repeatedly, or use [IO.Path]/ResolveLinkTarget($true) in pwsh 7) and treat an unresolvable target as FATAL. Before any recursive removal, check Test-IsLink and delete only the link itself (for example `(Get-Item \n\n[MEDIUM] R8-05  ~/.agents/.skill-lock.json; docs/audits/2026-09-12-pool-prune.txt (Tas\n  E: About 30 of the 98 names Task 2 archived still have ledger entries: ask-matt, codebase-design, diagnosing-bugs, domain-modeling, grill-me, grill-with-docs, improve-codebase-architecture, setup-matt-pocock-skills, tdd, to-prd, to-spec, to-tickets, triage, writing-great-skills, find-skills, implement, research, wayfinder, brandkit, full-output-enforcement, gpt-taste, high-end-visual-design, image-to\n  F: Remove or update the pruned names in .skill-lock.json through the CLI (for example `skills remove`, run by the user) and record that in the prune report. Add a ledger-vs-pool consistency metric to aios-metrics.py.\n\n[MEDIUM] R8-06  docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (Review his\n  E: The plan says 'Gate A is satisfied' after round 6 APPROVED and adds 'If this plan is edited before execution, Gate A must be re-run — an approval binds only the hash it was issued against.' The plan now contains R7-01..R7-05 and AMEND-01 edits (commits d2e4a3b and bb19c06): the vendored allowlist in both sync scripts, the rewritten gitignore instructions, and changed counts in Tasks 4-7. None of t\n  F: Record round 7 and this review in the review history with their dispositions, mark Gate A as re-opened, and stop Task 3 from starting until the current plan hash is approved.\n\n[LOW] R8-07  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.dailybackup.plist; Tasks 3-5\n  E: daily-backup runs every day at 09:00 through pwsh, and Sync-Repo on ~/.claude/skills automatical