{"parentUuid":"0ccee55b-7f5e-47c1-ae81-49885ff15b00","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d90457fc-55a4-4c91-beb9-bfaecc9c0aa3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WNCjajqKfgbcFcg6rm4ELZ","type":"tool_result","content":"410\t### Task 3: Harden the sync writers — before anything moves\n411\t\n412\t**Files:**\n413\t- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh`\n414\t- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`\n415\t- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md`\n416\t\n417\t**Interfaces:**\n418\t- Consumes: nothing.\n419\t- Produces: a sync script safe to run at any point during Tasks 4–7. **Nothing in Task 4 may run until this task's Step 6 passes.**\n420\t\n421\t**Why this task comes before the move.** Round 1 put the relink first and this second. Codex caught that as R1, a data-loss defect. Today `sync-to-codex.sh` does:\n422\t\n423\t```bash\n424\trm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n425\t```\n426\t\n427\t`dev-sync` instructs the user to run it after every pull. The moment `$SRC/$s` is a symlink into `$dst`, `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"` destroys the real directory and the `cp` copies a corpse. The `.ps1` twin has the same shape. Hardening first makes the whole rest of the plan safe to interrupt.\n428\t\n429\t- [ ] **Step 1: Read both current scripts and record the baseline**\n430\t\n431\t```bash\n432\tcat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh\n433\techo \"=== ps1 ===\"\n434\tcat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1\n435\t```\n436\t\n437\tExpected: the `SHARED=(...)` array and the `rm -rf` / `cp -R` pair in each. Note the current `SHARED` membership — Step 2 preserves it.\n438\t\n439\t- [ ] **Step 2: Rewrite the bash script**"}]},"uuid":"c30abc16-6327-4ba7-9a15-d244b2d88ab0","timestamp":"2026-09-12T19:18:09.720Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","content":"### Task 3: Harden the sync writers — before anything moves\n\n**Files:**\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-sync-hardening.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: nothing.\n- Produces: a sync script safe to run at any point during Tasks 4–7. **Nothing in Task 4 may run until this task's Step 6 passes.**\n\n**Why this task comes before the move.** Round 1 put the relink first and this second. Codex caught that as R1, a data-loss defect. Today `sync-to-codex.sh` does:\n\n```bash\nrm -rf \"$dst/$s\"; cp -R \"$SRC/$s\" \"$dst/$s\"\n```\n\n`dev-sync` instructs the user to run it after every pull. The moment `$SRC/$s` is a symlink into `$dst`, `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"` destroys the real directory and the `cp` copies a corpse. The `.ps1` twin has the same shape. Hardening first makes the whole rest of the plan safe to interrupt.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Read both current scripts and record the baseline**\n\n```bash\ncat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh\necho \"=== ps1 ===\"\ncat ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1\n```\n\nExpected: the `SHARED=(...)` array and the `rm -rf` / `cp -R` pair in each. Note the current `SHARED` membership — Step 2 preserves it.\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Rewrite the bash script**","numLines":30,"startLine":410,"totalLines":2404}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0ccee55b-7f5e-47c1-ae81-49885ff15b00","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
