{"parentUuid":"22c11d93-1368-4adf-b7d7-5081c891a864","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cez6pXan2HVdRd18RKdct","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A9irdm8S98XozqQKQJBJ4c","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport json,glob\nf=sorted(glob.glob('/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/claude-review-r9/**/result.json',recursive=True))\nd=json.load(open(f[0])); r=d['response']\nprint('status:',d['status'],'provider:',d['provider'])\nprint('VERDICT:',r.get('verdict'))\nprint('SUMMARY:',r.get('summary'))\nprint('findings:',len(r.get('findings',[])))\nprint()\nfor x in r.get('findings',[]):\n    print('['+x['severity'].upper()+'] '+x['id']+'  '+x['path'][:80])\n    print('  E:',x['evidence'][:500])\n    print('  F:',x['fix'][:300])\n    print()\nprint('LIMITATIONS:'); [print(' -',l[:200]) for l in r.get('limitations',[])]\n\"","description":"Read the round 9 review verdict"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1109,"cache_read_input_tokens":455415,"output_tokens":1056,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":288},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1109,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":32,"output_tokens":1056,"cache_read_input_tokens":455415,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1109,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1109},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cez6pW7ig9Xkm5EuLp3MP","type":"assistant","uuid":"00aa1fd6-14ee-4a3b-83ce-80786ea197d3","timestamp":"2026-09-12T19:31:16.446Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"00aa1fd6-14ee-4a3b-83ce-80786ea197d3","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d90457fc-55a4-4c91-beb9-bfaecc9c0aa3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A9irdm8S98XozqQKQJBJ4c","type":"tool_result","content":"status: completed provider: claude\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: Most round-8 fixes hold up in this revision. Treating every symlink as third-party removes both the risk of deleting through a link and the 4-name list. The key allowlist, word-boundary matching, the `Stop-Fatal` exit handling, `KEEP_OWNED` in the PowerShell twin, link-only deletion, the single trend writer, and pausing and resuming the backup are all present and look right. Two new high-severity defects come from the redesign itself. First, Task 4 Step 2 now runs a real sync before Step 5 reconciles the drifted pool copies. That sync replaces those copies with links, so the pool-only edits are lost without any warning and Step 5 finds nothing to back up. Second, the generated ignore block goes into the tracked, shared `.gitignore`. Each machine's sync rewrites that block with its own set of links, and daily-backup commits and pushes the result. So when Windows runs the sync, the Mac's 19 links stop being ignored, and the Mac backup then commits them as mode-120000 entries again. That is the R3 breakage coming back. Also still open: the install-to-backup gap is the normal case rather than an edge case, the rollback guidance still describes the deleted inversion design, and one command fails on macOS.\nfindings: 7\n\n[HIGH] R9-01  docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (Task 4 Step 2 vs Ste\n  E: Task 4 Step 2 runs a real sync (`bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh`, not a dry run) to write the ignore block. In its publish loop the sync runs `rm -rf \"${dst:?}/${s:?}\"` and then `ln -s` for every owned skill, including `memory-write` and `meeting-sync`. The plan's own R2-01 disposition says those two pool copies differ from canonical and hold edits that exist nowhere else. Step 5 comes after that and only then looks for divergence. Its loop skips links (`[ ! -L \"$b\" ]`), so it prints no \n  F: Move the DIVERGED detection, the diff review and the backup into a step that runs before the first non-dry sync: at the end of Task 3, before the commit and push, or as Task 4 Step 0. Have Step 2 write the ignore block without publishing, either through a separate mode or by running the publish only\n\n[HIGH] R9-02  ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh and sync-to-codex.ps1 (generated .gitignore bl\n  E: The block lists the symlinks present on whichever machine runs the sync, but it is written into `~/.claude/skills/.gitignore`, a tracked file shared through erash11/claude-skills. After Task 4 the untracked links no longer exist on Windows, so the PowerShell twin, which dev-sync runs after every Windows pull, rewrites the block with Windows' own reparse points, probably none. The twin always calls `Set-Content`, even when nothing changed, and on Windows that writes CRLF. The tree is dirty, so th\n  F: Write the generated patterns to `~/.claude/skills/.git/info/exclude`, which is per-machine and never committed, instead of the tracked `.gitignore`. Do the same in both sync scripts, and have the twin write only when the content has changed. Keep the tracked `.gitignore` free of machine-specific sta\n\n[MEDIUM] R9-03  docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (Out of Scope, 'insta\n  E: The plan calls this residual a narrow window: an install made after the last sync but before a 09:00 backup. On this Mac, though, the sync runs only inside dev-sync (grep: nothing in ~/.claude/scripts or hooks calls sync-to-codex), while daily-backup commits a dirty tree every morning. `.skill-lock.json` shows install batches on 2026-06-19, 07-09 (twice), 08-15 (twice), 09-02 and 09-04, and the vendored-skill-install procedure creates `ln -s ../../.agents/skills/NAME ~/.claude/skills/NAME` every\n  F: Install the pre-commit hook that rejects staged mode-120000 entries now, as part of Task 3 or 4, on both machines. It is a few lines and daily-backup does not pass `--no-verify`. Alternatively, have daily-backup's Sync-Repo for .claude/skills refresh the exclude list (or run the sync) before `git ad\n\n[MEDIUM] R9-04  docs/audits/2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt (rollback block); plan Task 4\n  E: The committed rollback text still describes the design that was deleted. PHASE B says 'Task 4 inverted the links' and warns about symlink cycles. The 'AFTER A ROLLBACK' section says 'The hardened sync REFUSES outward canonical symlinks', which the rewritten script no longer does. Task 4 Step 7 says a dropped skill count should be rolled back 'per Task 1, PHASE A'. PHASE A deletes both views and extracts the VIEWS tarball, which was taken before Task 2's prune. That puts the 98 pruned skills back\n  F: Add a rollback entry for the new Task 4: revert the untrack commit, remove the generated exclude or ignore block, and rerun the sync; no tarball needed. Mark the inversion-era PHASE B/C and 'after a rollback' text in the inventory as superseded. Point Task 4 Step 7 at the new entry. Change the scope\n\n[LOW] R9-05  docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (Task 4 Step 1)\n  E: `xargs -a /tmp/tracked-links.txt git rm --cached --` uses `-a`, which exists only in GNU xargs. macOS ships BSD xargs, which rejects it with 'illegal option -- a', and the plan notes there are no GNU coreutils on this Mac. The untrack step fails, and the 19 expected `D` lines never appear.\n  F: Use `git rm --cached --pathspec-from-file=/tmp/tracked-links.txt`, or `tr '\\n' '\\0' < /tmp/tracked-links.txt | xargs -0 git rm --cached --`.\n\n[LOW] R9-06  ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh (enumeration loop)\n  E: The script builds THIRD_PARTY from `for d in \"$SRC\"/*/`, and a glob with a trailing slash matches only links that resolve to directories. A dangling third-party link, such as one left when the vendor-review package is removed or a pool install is pruned, is silently left out. It therefore never appears in the generated ignore block, and the next `git add -A` commits it as mode 120000. aios-metrics counts `third_party_links_in_canonical` with `os.path.islink`, which does include dangling links, s\n  F: Enumerate with `for d in \"$SRC\"/*` and test `[ -L \"$d\" ]` before any directory test, so dangling links are ignored too. Optionally report them as a warning.\n\n[LOW] R9-07  docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (Task 8 Step 1)\n  E: The new check `ls -ld ~ # must NOT be world-readable` looks at the wrong permission bits. `sysadminctl -addUser` gives standard users primary group staff (20), and a macOS home directory is normally owned by user:staff with group read and execute. A home that is not world-readable therefore still lets piuser in. The check passes, the `sudo -u piuser ls ~/DEV` probes then succeed, and the task stops. The stop is safe, but the check gives a false sense of isolation and leaves Task 8 without any pa\n  F: Check the group and other bits against piuser's group membership, and let the user decide beforehand whether to create piuser with a separate primary group or tighten the home permissions. Record that decision in the report.\n\nLIMITATIONS:\n - Static review only. I ran no commands, and I did not rerun the host's reported sync and checker results; I neither confirm nor dispute them.\n - I did not recompute the plan's SHA256.\n - I could not inspect the Windows machine: its git core.autocrlf and core.symlinks settings, its reparse points, and whether dev-sync or daily-backup runs there on a schedule. The R9-02 sequence follows\n - I did not recheck this revision against the filesystem. I did not look at the current pool representation of memory-write and meeting-sync, or at home directory permissions; R9-01 relies on the plan's\n - I did not read the skills CLI source, so I have not verified whether it rewrites .gitignore or the links on update.\n - I did not read the Pi research note or the spec notes.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5099d811-3990-4d09-855f-e1d14941974c","timestamp":"2026-09-12T19:31:17.759Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"status: completed provider: claude\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: Most round-8 fixes hold up in this revision. Treating every symlink as third-party removes both the risk of deleting through a link and the 4-name list. The key allowlist, word-boundary matching, the `Stop-Fatal` exit handling, `KEEP_OWNED` in the PowerShell twin, link-only deletion, the single trend writer, and pausing and resuming the backup are all present and look right. Two new high-severity defects come from the redesign itself. First, Task 4 Step 2 now runs a real sync before Step 5 reconciles the drifted pool copies. That sync replaces those copies with links, so the pool-only edits are lost without any warning and Step 5 finds nothing to back up. Second, the generated ignore block goes into the tracked, shared `.gitignore`. Each machine's sync rewrites that block with its own set of links, and daily-backup commits and pushes the result. So when Windows runs the sync, the Mac's 19 links stop being ignored, and the Mac backup then commits them as mode-120000 entries again. That is the R3 breakage coming back. Also still open: the install-to-backup gap is the normal case rather than an edge case, the rollback guidance still describes the deleted inversion design, and one command fails on macOS.\nfindings: 7\n\n[HIGH] R9-01  docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md (Task 4 Step 2 vs Ste\n  E: Task 4 Step 2 runs a real sync (`bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh`, not a dry run) to write the ignore block. In its publish loop the sync runs `rm -rf \"${dst:?}/${s:?}\"` and then `ln -s` for every owned skill, including `memory-write` and `meeting-sync`. The plan's own R2-01 disposition says those two pool copies differ from canonical and hold edits