{"parentUuid":"c26d9f40-6c8e-4f36-af56-7e2d40fd874a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J3EVjq5W7tEw1X6XB8VmFT","type":"tool_result","content":"runner exit=0\nstatus: completed provider: claude\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: All three round-10 fixes hold. Task 3 Step 5c now checks the backup's commit exit code, records `repo-commit`, and returns before the OK result at daily-backup.ps1:87, so a refused commit is no longer reported as success. Both sync twins now drop third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, after the guard. Stale `.gitignore` wording is fixed, and Task 8 has the `dseditgroup` check. The Mac-side R9-01 route through dev-sync is covered by the new Step 0 warning. One material gap is left, on Windows. Task 3 pushes a PowerShell twin that now overwrites all 43 published entries instead of 7. dev-sync, which that machine runs first thing on sit-down, runs it right after pulling, and nothing snapshots or reconciles the Windows pool first. There is also one low-severity robustness issue in how the twin probes the index.\nfindings: 2\n\n[MEDIUM] R11-01  ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1 (Task 3 Step 3, pushed in Step 7); ~/.claude/skills/dev\n  E: dev-sync is 'the first thing to run when sitting down at a machine' (SKILL.md:8). Step 2 of it (line 24) says to `git pull` the skills repo and then run `sync-to-codex.ps1` on Windows. Task 3 Step 7 pushes the new twin, so the next Windows sit-down pulls it and runs it at once. The old twin only overwrote 7 SHARED names. The new one does `Remove-Item -Recurse` followed by `Copy-Item` for every owned skill (42 plus `_shared`) in both `~/.agents/skills` and `~/.codex/skills`. On the Mac the plan measured that two pool copies had diverged and held edits found nowhere else, then added a VIEWS tarball (Task 1) and a reconcile step before the first real sync (Task 4 Step 2, R9-01). None of that ex\n  F: Keep the Windows twin from making its first destructive run unprepared. Options: (a) have the twin exit with a clear message unless a per-machine marker exists (for example `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready`), created only after a Windows step that archives `~/.agents/skills` and `~/.codex/skills` and runs the same DIVERGED/diff reconcile as Task 4 Step 2;\n\n[LOW] R11-02  ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1 (R10-01 index-cleanup block)\n  E: The twin sets `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` and then probes each third-party name with `git -C $SRC ls-files --error-unmatch -- $name 2>$null`. For any link that is not tracked, which covers every junction installed on Windows, git writes 'error: pathspec ... did not match' to stderr. In Windows PowerShell 5.1, redirected native stderr becomes an error record, and under EAP=Stop that can end the script before the exclude block or the views are written. dev-sync doesn't say which PowerShell to use (line 24), the twin being replaced said `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File` (Windows PowerShell 5.1), and the new twin declares no required version. The Mac-only `pwsh` 7 parse check cann\n  F: Don't generate stderr at all: run `$tracked = git -C $SRC ls-files -- $name` and test for non-empty output, with no `--error-unmatch`. Alternatively, set `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Continue'` just around the native git calls. Add `#Requires -Version 7` if pwsh 7 is intended, and state the invocation in dev-sync.\n\nLIMITATIONS:\n - Static review only. I ran no commands, and I did not rerun the host's reported harness, sync or hook results; I neither confirm nor dispute them.\n - I did not recompute the plan's SHA256.\n - I could not inspect the Windows machine: whether its pool copies diverge from canonical, which PowerShell version dev-sync uses there, its git core.symlinks setting, or its junction installs. R11-01's loss depends on a d\n - I did not read dev-sync SKILL.md line 25 (the tool truncated it) or the rest of Sync-Repo beyond the grepped lines.\n - I did not read the Pi research note or the spec notes.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"82e850fe-7dfc-4cc6-90c9-27d9542ad070","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:14:16.207Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"runner exit=0\nstatus: completed provider: claude\nVERDICT: REVISE\nSUMMARY: All three round-10 fixes hold. Task 3 Step 5c now checks the backup's commit exit code, records `repo-commit`, and returns before the OK result at daily-backup.ps1:87, so a refused commit is no longer reported as success. Both sync twins now drop third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, after the guard. Stale `.gitignore` wording is fixed, and Task 8 has the `dseditgroup` check. The Mac-side R9-01 route through dev-sync is covered by the new Step 0 warning. One material gap is left, on Windows. Task 3 pushes a PowerShell twin that now overwrites all 43 published entries instead of 7. dev-sync, which that machine runs first thing on sit-down, runs it right after pulling, and nothing snapshots or reconciles the Windows pool first. There is also one low-severity robustness issue in how the twin probes the index.\nfindings: 2\n\n[MEDIUM] R11-01  ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.ps1 (Task 3 Step 3, pushed in Step 7); ~/.claude/skills/dev\n  E: dev-sync is 'the first thing to run when sitting down at a machine' (SKILL.md:8). Step 2 of it (line 24) says to `git pull` the skills repo and then run `sync-to-codex.ps1` on Windows. Task 3 Step 7 pushes the new twin, so the next Windows sit-down pulls it and runs it at once. The old twin only overwrote 7 SHARED names. The new one does `Remove-Item -Recurse` followed by `Copy-Item` for every owned skill (42 plus `_shared`) in both `~/.agents/skills` and `~/.codex/skills`. On the Mac the plan measured that two pool copies had diverged and held edits found nowhere else, then added a VIEWS tarball (Task 1) and a reconcile step before the first real sync (Task 4 Step 2, R9-01). None of that ex\n  F: Keep the Windows twin from making its fi