{"parentUuid":"e0a77bff-79c3-41c4-b49d-fd5d50ea749a","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0114pzANikXr7cHEGrGPLLvW","type":"tool_result","content":"runner exit=0\nstatus: completed provider: claude\nVERDICT: APPROVED\nSUMMARY: Both round-11 fixes hold, and I found no material defects left in this revision. The first-run gate runs before enumeration, the guard, the index cleanup, the exclude write and the publish in both scripts. A refused run therefore changes nothing, and dry runs still go through. The only thing that creates the Mac marker is the end of Task 4 Step 2, after reconciliation. That closes the R9-01 route through dev-sync with code instead of an instruction. On Windows, the next dev-sync pull stops with a message and leaves the views alone, which Out of Scope now describes. The PowerShell twin's index probe no longer writes to stderr and no longer relies on `--error-unmatch` or `2>$null`, so Windows PowerShell 5.1 under EAP=Stop should not abort on it. Earlier fixes (R8 through R10) are unchanged. One low-severity edge case remains: in the bash script, a `DRY_RUN` value other than `0` or `1` skips the gate but still runs the destructive publish loop.\nfindings: 1\n\n[LOW] R12-01  ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh (Task 3 Step 2: first-run gate vs publish loop)\n  E: The bash script checks DRY two ways. The gate, the index cleanup, the exclude write, the retirement delete and the manifest write run only when `[ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ]`. The publish loop skips its destructive part only when `[ \"$DRY\" = \"1\" ]`. So `DRY_RUN=true`, `DRY_RUN=yes` or `DRY_RUN=2` skips the R11-01 gate (DRY is not \"0\"), and the publish loop then runs `rm -rf \"$dst/$s\"` and `ln -s` for every owned skill (DRY is not \"1\"). Scenario: on an unprepared machine, someone who guesses the flag's spelling runs `DRY_RUN=true bash sync-to-codex.sh` expecting a preview. The drifted real-directory pool copies (memory-write, meeting-sync) are replaced by links, no manifest is written, and no gate messag\n  F: Validate DRY once near the top of the script, before the gate: `case \"$DRY\" in 0|1) ;; *) echo \"FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got '$DRY'\" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac`. Alternatively, derive a single boolean and use it in every branch. Add a CASE 5 variant that runs `DRY_RUN=true` with no marker and expects exit 2.\n\nLIMITATIONS:\n - Static review only. I ran no commands, and I did not rerun the host's reported CASE 5, regression or parse results; I neither confirm nor dispute them.\n - I did not recompute the plan's SHA256.\n - I could not inspect the Windows machine: Windows PowerShell 5.1 behaviour of the twin, git's safe.directory or other stderr output there, or the state of its pool.\n - I did not read the Pi research note or the spec notes, so Task 8's extension contract was not checked against them this round.\n - An APPROVED verdict covers the plan text; Gates B–D still have to verify the implementation.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"4f5050cf-e3c4-4158-8d42-b67e63d35336","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:19:04.889Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"runner exit=0\nstatus: completed provider: claude\nVERDICT: APPROVED\nSUMMARY: Both round-11 fixes hold, and I found no material defects left in this revision. The first-run gate runs before enumeration, the guard, the index cleanup, the exclude write and the publish in both scripts. A refused run therefore changes nothing, and dry runs still go through. The only thing that creates the Mac marker is the end of Task 4 Step 2, after reconciliation. That closes the R9-01 route through dev-sync with code instead of an instruction. On Windows, the next dev-sync pull stops with a message and leaves the views alone, which Out of Scope now describes. The PowerShell twin's index probe no longer writes to stderr and no longer relies on `--error-unmatch` or `2>$null`, so Windows PowerShell 5.1 under EAP=Stop should n