skills whose pool copy is a real\n65\tdirectory that differs from canonical. The two named in the plan were read in\n66\tfull, and in both the pool copy is an **older version**, not unique work:\n67\t\n68\t- `memory-write` — pool `SKILL.md` dated 2026-08-07, canonical 2026-09-02. The pool copy lacks the `name:`-slug rule and the retrieval-ladder section that canonical has.\n69\t- `meeting-sync` — pool dated 2026-08-07, canonical 2026-08-15. The pool copy carries the pre-migration path `/Users/kristinarash/Desktop/SecondBrain/...`.\n70\t\n71\tThe scan also had a gap the plan did not cover: it considered only\n72\tdirectories with a `SKILL.md`. The publish also replaces the pool's `_shared`\n73\tsupport directory and any real directories in `~/.codex/skills` for managed\n74\tnames. Both are now included in the triage and the backup.\n75\t\n76\tThe plan's step logic already handles any number of DIVERGED names, so this is\n77\trecorded here rather than edited into the approved plan, which keeps its hash\n78\tvalid.\n79\t\n80\t### Full triage (read-only, content-compared, `__pycache__`/`.pyc`/`.DS_Store` ignored)\n81\t\n82\t- **Pool:** 23 entries differ — 22 owned skills plus `_shared`.\n83\t  - **22 are \"canonical newer — safe\":** no file exists only in the pool, and no differing file is newer in the pool. They are stale copies.\n84\t  - **1 needs review: `graphify`**, detailed below.\n85\t- **Codex view:** 0 real directories differ from canonical.\n86\t\n87\t**`graphify`.** Two files differ, `SKILL.md` and `references/hooks.md`. The pool\n88\t`SKILL.md` is dated 2026-08-29 14:34, **one minute after** canonical's 14:33. The\n89\tdiff is not independent work. It is graphify's installer writing a\n90\t**per-harness variant**:\n91\t\n92\t| | Canonical (Claude Code) | Pool (Codex / agents) |\n93\t|---|---|---|\n94\t| Subagent dispatch | \"Call the **Agent** tool … `subagent_type=\"general-purpose\"`\" | \"Uses the **`Task`** tool … `Task(description=…)`\" |\n95\t| Integration section | \"native **CLAUDE.md** integration\" | \"native **AGENTS.md** integration\" |\n96\t\n97\tThe 4,795 files that exist only in canonical are `graphify/.venv` (its runtime).\n98\t\n99\t### Backup taken before any publish\n100\t\n101\tAll 23 diverged pool copies were copied to\n102\t`~/.agents/skills-archive/pre-sync-2026-09-12/`, including `graphify` (29 files)\n103\tand `_shared`. File counts matched on every copy, and none were skipped. The codex\n104\tbackup root `pre-sync-2026-09-12-codex/` is empty because nothing there diverged.\n105\tThe VIEWS tarball from Task 1 remains a second, independent copy.\n106\t\n107\t### Task 4 Step 1 — done\n108\t\n109\tRun through Python `subprocess`: `dcg` blocks `git` inside shell command\n110\tsubstitution, so the plan's shell form could not be used. Results:\n111\t\n112\t- 19 tracked mode-120000 entries; every one is a symlink on disk.\n113\t- `git rm --cached --pathspec-from-file` exited 0.\n114\t- **19** staged deletions; all 19 still on disk as links; **0** tracked links left in the index.\n115\t- The 19 now show as `??` until Step 3 writes `.git/info/exclude`.\n116\t- No `git add -A` may run before Step 3. The daily backup is still unloaded.\n117\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"1a103c7a-6d89-422e-89d8-7ed85dc3d393","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r12.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r12.md","content":"# Round 12 disposition — ACCEPTED (after an APPROVED verdict)\n\nRound 12 returned **APPROVED** with one low finding. It is fixed anyway, because\non an unprepared machine it is a route to exactly the data loss the R11-01 gate\nexists to prevent. Per the plan's own rule, an approval binds only the hash it\nwas issued against, so this round-13 request asks for re-confirmation of a\nsingle, narrow change.\n\n| ID | Disposition | What changed |\n|---|---|---|\n| R12-01 | ACCEPTED | `sync-to-codex.sh` validates `DRY` once, **before** the first-run gate: `case \"$DRY\" in 0\\|1) ;; *) FATAL ... exit 2 ;; esac`. Every later branch keeps testing `= \"0\"` or `= \"1\"`. After validation those are the only two possible values, so no value can fall between them. A mistyped flag now refuses with `FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got '<value>'. No changes made.` New **CASE 5b** in Task 3 Step 4 runs `DRY_RUN=true` on the unprepared `/tmp/syncready` fixture and expects exit 2, the pool-only edit intact, and the pool copy still a real directory. **The PowerShell twin is not changed:** it computes `$DRY = $env:DRY_RUN -eq '1'`, a single boolean, so any other value is a real run and is stopped by the gate; there is no between-state. |\n\n## Rejected\n\nNone.\n\n## Executed verification (run, not claimed)\n\nThe fixed script was extracted from the plan after applying the change (3\nreplacements, each matching exactly once) and run under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57.\nExit codes were captured directly, not through a pipe. All embedded python\ncompiles, all 3 shell scripts pass `bash -n`, and the `case \"$DRY\"` validation\nsits **before** the first-run gate in the script text.\n\nBefore the change, the script tested `DRY` in seven places: lines 32, 111, 133,\n203 and 246 test `= \"0\"`, lines 233 and 261 test `= \"1\"`. That confirms the\nbetween-state the reviewer described.\n\n**Fixed script, unprepared machine** (no marker; pool copy is a real directory\nholding a pool-only edit):\n\n| `DRY_RUN` | exit | pool copy | manifest | message |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `true` | **2** | real dir | no | `FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got 'true'` |\n| `yes` | **2** | real dir | no | same, `got 'yes'` |\n| `2` | **2** | real dir | no | same, `got '2'` |\n| `1` | 0 | real dir | no | dry run |\n| unset (real run) | **2** | real dir | no | `FATAL this machine is not prepared` |\n\nThe pool-only edit still read `pool-only edit - must survive` afterwards.\n\n**Fixed script, prepared machine** (marker present): `DRY_RUN=1` → exit 0, no\nwrites; `DRY_RUN=0` → exit 0, pool copy replaced by a link and manifest written.\nThe normal path is intact.\n\n**Control — the pre-fix script**, extracted with `git show 5779c56:` from the\nlast committed plan (asserted to lack the `case` block), on a fresh unprepared\nfixture with `DRY_RUN=true`: **exit 0**, **no** gate message, pool copy\n**replaced by a link**, the pool-only edit gone (the path now resolves to the\ncanonical file), and no manifest written. The finding reproduced exactly and\nsilently, so the fix and CASE 5b both test something real.\n\n## Scope of this round\n\nOnly the R12-01 lines changed: the `case` block, CASE 5b, and one review-history\nbullet. No other script logic changed.\n","numLines":57,"startLine":1,"totalLines":57}},"displayPath":"../.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r12.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"49e48c49-cceb-4ec1-8ef1-7f8cf781b977","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:26.785Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r12.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Round 12 disposition — ACCEPTED (after an APPROVED verdict)\n2\t\n3\tRound 12 returned **APPROVED** with one low finding. It is fixed anyway, because\n4\ton an unprepared machine it is a route to exactly the data loss the R11-01 gate\n5\texists to prevent. Per the plan's own rule, an approval binds only the hash it\n6\twas issued against, so this round-13 request asks for re-confirmation of a\n7\tsingle, narrow change.\n8\t\n9\t| ID | Disposition | What changed |\n10\t|---|---|---|\n11\t| R12-01 | ACCEPTED | `sync-to-codex.sh` validates `DRY` once, **before** the first-run gate: `case \"$DRY\" in 0\\|1) ;; *) FATAL ... exit 2 ;; esac`. Every later branch keeps testing `= \"0\"` or `= \"1\"`. After validation those are the only two possible values, so no value can fall between them. A mistyped flag now refuses with `FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got '<value>'. No changes made.` New **CASE 5b** in Task 3 Step 4 runs `DRY_RUN=true` on the unprepared `/tmp/syncready` fixture and expects exit 2, the pool-only edit intact, and the pool copy still a real directory. **The PowerShell twin is not changed:** it computes `$DRY = $env:DRY_RUN -eq '1'`, a single boolean, so any other value is a real run and is stopped by the gate; there is no between-state. |\n12\t\n13\t## Rejected\n14\t\n15\tNone.\n16\t\n17\t## Executed verification (run, not claimed)\n18\t\n19\tThe fixed script was extracted from the plan after applying the change (3\n20\treplacements, each matching exactly once) and run under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57.\n21\tExit codes were captured directly, not through a pipe. All embedded python\n22\tcompiles, all 3 shell scripts pass `bash -n`, and the `case \"$DRY\"` validation\n23\tsits **before** the first-run gate in the script text.\n24\t\n25\tBefore the change, the script tested `DRY` in seven places: lines 32, 111, 133,\n26\t203 and 246 test `= \"0\"`, lines 233 and 261 test `= \"1\"`. That confirms the\n27\tbetween-state the reviewer described.\n28\t\n29\t**Fixed script, unprepared machine** (no marker; pool copy is a real directory\n30\tholding a pool-only edit):\n31\t\n32\t| `DRY_RUN` | exit | pool copy | manifest | message |\n33\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n34\t| `true` | **2** | real dir | no | `FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got 'true'` |\n35\t| `yes` | **2** | real dir | no | same, `got 'yes'` |\n36\t| `2` | **2** | real dir | no | same, `got '2'` |\n37\t| `1` | 0 | real dir | no | dry run |\n38\t| unset (real run) | **2** | real dir | no | `FATAL this machine is not prepared` |\n39\t\n40\tThe pool-only edit still read `pool-only edit - must survive` afterwards.\n41\t\n42\t**Fixed script, prepared machine** (marker present): `DRY_RUN=1` → exit 0, no\n43\twrites; `DRY_RUN=0` → exit 0, pool copy replaced by a link and manifest written.\n44\tThe normal path is intact.\n45\t\n46\t**Control — the pre-fix script**, extracted with `git show 5779c56:` from the\n47\tlast committed plan (asserted to lack the `case` block), on a fresh unprepared\n48\tfixture with `DRY_RUN=true`: **exit 0**, **no** gate message, pool copy\n49\t**replaced by a link**, the pool-only edit gone (the path now resolves to the\n50\tcanonical file), and no manifest written. The finding reproduced exactly and\n51\tsilently, so the fix and CASE 5b both test something real.\n52\t\n53\t## Scope of this round\n54\t\n55\tOnly the R12-01 lines changed: the `case` block, CASE 5b, and one review-history\n56\tbullet. No other script logic changed.\n57\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"49e48c49-cceb-4ec1-8ef1-7f8cf781b977","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r12-fixes.txt","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r12-fixes.txt","content":"%%%%OLD%%%%\n# R11-01: FIRST-RUN GATE. A real run replaces every owned skill's pool and codex\n%%%%NEW%%%%\n# R12-01: accept only 0 or 1. Every destructive branch below tests DRY with\n# either = \"0\" or = \"1\", so any other value (DRY_RUN=true, yes, 2) would skip the\n# first-run gate AND still run the publish loop's rm -rf. Refuse it up front.\ncase \"$DRY\" in\n  0|1) ;;\n  *) echo \"FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got '$DRY'. No changes made.\" >&2\n     exit 2 ;;\nesac\n\n# R11-01: FIRST-RUN GATE. A real run replaces every owned skill's pool and codex\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\nThis is exactly the Windows situation after its next `dev-sync` pull.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\nThis is exactly the Windows situation after its next `dev-sync` pull.\n\n**CASE 5b — a mistyped dry-run flag (R12-01).** Every destructive branch tests `DRY` against `0` or `1`, so any other value must be refused outright rather than fall between the two:\n\n```bash\nHOME=/tmp/syncready DRY_RUN=true /bin/bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"DRY_RUN=true: exit=$? (expect 2)\"\ncat /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned/SKILL.md\ntest -L /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned && echo \"pool copy REPLACED - wrong\" || echo \"pool copy untouched\"\n```\n\nExpected: `FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got 'true'`, `exit=2`, the pool-only edit still readable, and `pool copy untouched`. Without the check, this exact command skipped the gate and replaced the pool copy with a link. The PowerShell twin is not affected: it computes `$DRY = $env:DRY_RUN -eq '1'`, so any other value is a real run and meets the gate.\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\nR11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\nR11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n- **Round 12** — Claude (resumed): **APPROVED**, 1 low (R12-01): in the bash script a `DRY_RUN` value other than `0`/`1` skipped the first-run gate yet still ran the destructive publish loop. Fixed anyway, because on an unprepared machine it is a data-loss route; the fix changes the plan hash, so round 13 re-confirms before execution.\n%%%%END%%%%\n","numLines":36,"startLine":1,"totalLines":36}},"displayPath":"../.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r12-fixes.txt"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"486dfcc5-66b8-4896-9a5c-fde3c3e4223f","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:26.785Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r12-fixes.txt\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n2\t# R11-01: FIRST-RUN GATE. A real run replaces every owned skill's pool and codex\n3\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n4\t# R12-01: accept only 0 or 1. Every destructive branch below tests DRY with\n5\t# either = \"0\" or = \"1\", so any other value (DRY_RUN=true, yes, 2) would skip the\n6\t# first-run gate AND still run the publish loop's rm -rf. Refuse it up front.\n7\tcase \"$DRY\" in\n8\t  0|1) ;;\n9\t  *) echo \"FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got '$DRY'. No changes made.\" >&2\n10\t     exit 2 ;;\n11\tesac\n12\t\n13\t# R11-01: FIRST-RUN GATE. A real run replaces every owned skill's pool and codex\n14\t%%%%END%%%%\n15\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n16\tThis is exactly the Windows situation after its next `dev-sync` pull.\n17\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n18\tThis is exactly the Windows situation after its next `dev-sync` pull.\n19\t\n20\t**CASE 5b — a mistyped dry-run flag (R12-01).** Every destructive branch tests `DRY` against `0` or `1`, so any other value must be refused outright rather than fall between the two:\n21\t\n22\t```bash\n23\tHOME=/tmp/syncready DRY_RUN=true /bin/bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"DRY_RUN=true: exit=$? (expect 2)\"\n24\tcat /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned/SKILL.md\n25\ttest -L /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned && echo \"pool copy REPLACED - wrong\" || echo \"pool copy untouched\"\n26\t```\n27\t\n28\tExpected: `FATAL DRY_RUN must be 0 or 1, got 'true'`, `exit=2`, the pool-only edit still readable, and `pool copy untouched`. Without the check, this exact command skipped the gate and replaced the pool copy with a link. The PowerShell twin is not affected: it computes `$DRY = $env:DRY_RUN -eq '1'`, so any other value is a real run and meets the gate.\n29\t%%%%END%%%%\n30\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n31\tR11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n32\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n33\tR11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n34\t- **Round 12** — Claude (resumed): **APPROVED**, 1 low (R12-01): in the bash script a `DRY_RUN` value other than `0`/`1` skipped the first-run gate yet still ran the destructive publish loop. Fixed anyway, because on an unprepared machine it is a data-loss route; the fix changes the plan hash, so round 13 re-confirms before execution.\n35\t%%%%END%%%%\n36\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"486dfcc5-66b8-4896-9a5c-fde3c3e4223f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r11.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r11.md","content":"# Round 11 dispositions — both ACCEPTED\n\n| ID | Disposition | What changed |\n|---|---|---|\n| R11-01 | ACCEPTED — option (a), applied to **both** scripts | A first-run gate: a real (non-dry) run refuses with exit 2 and changes nothing unless the per-machine marker `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` exists. Dry runs are always allowed. The gate sits **before** ownership enumeration, the guard, the index cleanup, the exclude write and the publish, so a refused run touches nothing. In bash it is a plain file test; in the PowerShell twin it uses `Stop-Fatal`, which is defined above it. **Mac:** the marker is created by the new last paragraph of Task 4 Step 2, only after every DIVERGED pool copy has been decided, so Step 3 is the first real run that can succeed. Task 3 Step 0's prose warning (\"do not run dev-sync between Task 3 and Task 4\") is replaced by a description of the enforced behaviour — the R9-01 route via `dev-sync` is now closed by code rather than by instruction. **Windows:** the next `dev-sync` pull gets a refusal instead of an overwrite. The Out of Scope bullet states the consequence plainly — Windows views freeze, and the seven skills the old twin copied stop refreshing there, until a dedicated Windows session archives both views, runs the Task 4 Step 2 reconcile, installs the hook and creates the marker. **Tests:** every scratch-`HOME` fixture in Task 3 Step 4 and Step 4b now creates the marker, and a new **CASE 5** proves an unprepared machine allows a dry run (exit 0) but refuses a real run (exit 2), leaves a pool-only edit intact, and writes no manifest. |\n| R11-02 | ACCEPTED | The twin's index probe is now `$tracked = git -C $SRC ls-files -- $name` and tests for non-empty output. There is no `--error-unmatch` and no `2>$null`. The `git rm --cached` inside the branch runs only for a tracked path, so it writes no stderr, and its `2>$null` is removed too. `#Requires -Version 7` was deliberately **not** added: the twin being replaced ran under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (`powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File`), so requiring 7 would turn every Windows `dev-sync` into a hard failure. Making the script 5.1-safe is the smaller change. The Out of Scope bullet records that 5.1 behaviour is verified only on Windows, since this Mac has `pwsh` 7 only. |\n\n## Executed verification (run, not claimed)\n\nThe sync script and the PowerShell twin were extracted from the plan after the\nfixes were applied (11 replacements, each matching exactly once). The bash\nscript ran under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57. Exit codes were captured directly, with\noutput redirected to files, so no pipe masked them.\n\n**R11-01 — CASE 5, the first-run gate** (no marker; canonical `owned` skill; a\nreal-directory pool copy holding a pool-only edit):\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `DRY_RUN=1`, no marker | **exit 0** |\n| Real run, no marker | **exit 2**, `FATAL this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync (.../.agents/.sync-v2-ready missing).` |\n| Pool-only edit | still readable: `pool-only edit - must survive` |\n| Manifest | **not written** |\n| Pool copy | still a **real directory**, not replaced by a link |\n\n**Regressions, marker present:**\n\n| Case | Result |\n|---|---|\n| CASE 1 | exit 0, `canonical manifest: 2 entries; third-party links: 2`, dangling link warned and excluded, `/.claude/RESUME.md` kept, tracked `.gitignore` unchanged, `theirs`/`dangling` ignored, `mine` not ignored, canary intact |\n| Idempotence | second run exit 0, **0** `updated` lines |\n| Guard | owned `mine` turned into a link → **exit 2**, owned-became-link FATAL present |\n| CASE 3 + 4 | exit 0, 2 `removed third-party link` lines, index holds only `owned/SKILL.md`, `git status` shows `D  old-link` only |\n| Refused run leaves the index alone | re-staged link `120000` → refused run **exit 2**, **0** `removed` lines, link still `120000` |\n| Deletion workflow, no `.git` | both runs exit 0, 0 dangling links |\n\n**R11-02 / PowerShell twin:** parses under `pwsh` 7 (exit 0). 0 `Write-Error \"` calls, 0 `VENDORED`, 6 `Stop-Fatal` calls (one is the new gate), 3 `KEEP_OWNED`. The only\n`--error-unmatch` and the only `2>$null` in the twin sit on a single **comment** line (twin line 75, the R11-02 explanation), not in code. Windows PowerShell 5.1 behaviour cannot be exercised on this Mac and is recorded as such in Out of Scope.\n\n**Plan-wide:** all embedded python compiles; all 3 shell scripts pass `bash -n`; both scripts contain the `sync-v2-ready` gate; the replaced \"Also do not run `dev-sync`\" warning appears 0 times.\n\n## Rejected\n\nNone.\n","numLines":45,"startLine":1,"totalLines":45}},"displayPath":"../.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r11.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"33b4aed1-d501-40e6-87a5-a84d9b904a8b","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:26.785Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r11.md\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t# Round 11 dispositions — both ACCEPTED\n2\t\n3\t| ID | Disposition | What changed |\n4\t|---|---|---|\n5\t| R11-01 | ACCEPTED — option (a), applied to **both** scripts | A first-run gate: a real (non-dry) run refuses with exit 2 and changes nothing unless the per-machine marker `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` exists. Dry runs are always allowed. The gate sits **before** ownership enumeration, the guard, the index cleanup, the exclude write and the publish, so a refused run touches nothing. In bash it is a plain file test; in the PowerShell twin it uses `Stop-Fatal`, which is defined above it. **Mac:** the marker is created by the new last paragraph of Task 4 Step 2, only after every DIVERGED pool copy has been decided, so Step 3 is the first real run that can succeed. Task 3 Step 0's prose warning (\"do not run dev-sync between Task 3 and Task 4\") is replaced by a description of the enforced behaviour — the R9-01 route via `dev-sync` is now closed by code rather than by instruction. **Windows:** the next `dev-sync` pull gets a refusal instead of an overwrite. The Out of Scope bullet states the consequence plainly — Windows views freeze, and the seven skills the old twin copied stop refreshing there, until a dedicated Windows session archives both views, runs the Task 4 Step 2 reconcile, installs the hook and creates the marker. **Tests:** every scratch-`HOME` fixture in Task 3 Step 4 and Step 4b now creates the marker, and a new **CASE 5** proves an unprepared machine allows a dry run (exit 0) but refuses a real run (exit 2), leaves a pool-only edit intact, and writes no manifest. |\n6\t| R11-02 | ACCEPTED | The twin's index probe is now `$tracked = git -C $SRC ls-files -- $name` and tests for non-empty output. There is no `--error-unmatch` and no `2>$null`. The `git rm --cached` inside the branch runs only for a tracked path, so it writes no stderr, and its `2>$null` is removed too. `#Requires -Version 7` was deliberately **not** added: the twin being replaced ran under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (`powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File`), so requiring 7 would turn every Windows `dev-sync` into a hard failure. Making the script 5.1-safe is the smaller change. The Out of Scope bullet records that 5.1 behaviour is verified only on Windows, since this Mac has `pwsh` 7 only. |\n7\t\n8\t## Executed verification (run, not claimed)\n9\t\n10\tThe sync script and the PowerShell twin were extracted from the plan after the\n11\tfixes were applied (11 replacements, each matching exactly once). The bash\n12\tscript ran under `/bin/bash` 3.2.57. Exit codes were captured directly, with\n13\toutput redirected to files, so no pipe masked them.\n14\t\n15\t**R11-01 — CASE 5, the first-run gate** (no marker; canonical `owned` skill; a\n16\treal-directory pool copy holding a pool-only edit):\n17\t\n18\t| Check | Result |\n19\t|---|---|\n20\t| `DRY_RUN=1`, no marker | **exit 0** |\n21\t| Real run, no marker | **exit 2**, `FATAL this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync (.../.agents/.sync-v2-ready missing).` |\n22\t| Pool-only edit | still readable: `pool-only edit - must survive` |\n23\t| Manifest | **not written** |\n24\t| Pool copy | still a **real directory**, not replaced by a link |\n25\t\n26\t**Regressions, marker present:**\n27\t\n28\t| Case | Result |\n29\t|---|---|\n30\t| CASE 1 | exit 0, `canonical manifest: 2 entries; third-party links: 2`, dangling link warned and excluded, `/.claude/RESUME.md` kept, tracked `.gitignore` unchanged, `theirs`/`dangling` ignored, `mine` not ignored, canary intact |\n31\t| Idempotence | second run exit 0, **0** `updated` lines |\n32\t| Guard | owned `mine` turned into a link → **exit 2**, owned-became-link FATAL present |\n33\t| CASE 3 + 4 | exit 0, 2 `removed third-party link` lines, index holds only `owned/SKILL.md`, `git status` shows `D  old-link` only |\n34\t| Refused run leaves the index alone | re-staged link `120000` → refused run **exit 2**, **0** `removed` lines, link still `120000` |\n35\t| Deletion workflow, no `.git` | both runs exit 0, 0 dangling links |\n36\t\n37\t**R11-02 / PowerShell twin:** parses under `pwsh` 7 (exit 0). 0 `Write-Error \"` calls, 0 `VENDORED`, 6 `Stop-Fatal` calls (one is the new gate), 3 `KEEP_OWNED`. The only\n38\t`--error-unmatch` and the only `2>$null` in the twin sit on a single **comment** line (twin line 75, the R11-02 explanation), not in code. Windows PowerShell 5.1 behaviour cannot be exercised on this Mac and is recorded as such in Out of Scope.\n39\t\n40\t**Plan-wide:** all embedded python compiles; all 3 shell scripts pass `bash -n`; both scripts contain the `sync-v2-ready` gate; the replaced \"Also do not run `dev-sync`\" warning appears 0 times.\n41\t\n42\t## Rejected\n43\t\n44\tNone.\n45\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"33b4aed1-d501-40e6-87a5-a84d9b904a8b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r11-fixes.txt","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r11-fixes.txt","content":"%%%%OLD%%%%\n# R8-01: OWNERSHIP IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A LIST.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\n# R11-01: FIRST-RUN GATE. A real run replaces every owned skill's pool and codex\n# entry with a link (a copy on Windows). A machine must be prepared first - its\n# views archived and its drifted pool copies reconciled (plan Task 4 Step 2 on\n# the Mac; the same steps on Windows) - and marks that with this per-machine\n# file. dev-sync runs this script right after a pull, so without the gate the\n# first run on an unprepared machine overwrites those copies unannounced.\n# Dry runs never write, so they are always allowed.\nREADY=\"$HOME/.agents/.sync-v2-ready\"\nif [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ] && [ ! -f \"$READY\" ]; then\n  echo \"FATAL this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync ($READY missing).\" >&2\n  echo \"      Archive ~/.agents/skills and ~/.codex/skills and reconcile drifted\" >&2\n  echo \"      pool copies first (plan Task 4 Step 2), then: touch $READY\" >&2\n  echo \"      No changes made. DRY_RUN=1 is always allowed.\" >&2\n  exit 2\nfi\n\n# R8-01: OWNERSHIP IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A LIST.\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\n# R8-01: ownership is structural. A reparse point in canonical was installed\n%%%%NEW%%%%\n# R11-01: first-run gate - see the bash twin. dev-sync runs this right after a\n# pull, so without it the first Windows run would overwrite every owned skill's\n# pool and codex copy with no snapshot and no reconcile.\n$READY = Join-Path $HOME '.agents\\.sync-v2-ready'\nif ((-not $DRY) -and -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $READY)) {\n  Stop-Fatal \"this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync ($READY missing). Archive ~\\.agents\\skills and ~\\.codex\\skills, reconcile drifted pool copies (plan Task 4 Step 2), then create $READY. No changes made. DRY_RUN=1 is always allowed.\" 2\n}\n\n# R8-01: ownership is structural. A reparse point in canonical was installed\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\n  foreach ($name in $THIRD_PARTY) {\n    git -C $SRC ls-files --error-unmatch -- $name 2>$null | Out-Null\n    if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) {\n      git -C $SRC rm --cached --quiet -- $name 2>$null | Out-Null\n%%%%NEW%%%%\n  foreach ($name in $THIRD_PARTY) {\n    # R11-02: no --error-unmatch and no 2>$null. For an untracked path that form\n    # writes to stderr, and in Windows PowerShell 5.1 redirected native stderr\n    # becomes an error record that ends the script under EAP=Stop - before the\n    # exclude block or the views are written. Plain ls-files prints the path\n    # when it is tracked and nothing otherwise, and writes no stderr.\n    $tracked = git -C $SRC ls-files -- $name\n    if ($tracked) {\n      git -C $SRC rm --cached --quiet -- $name | Out-Null\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\nIf the pool copy holds edits worth keeping, copy them **into canonical** (`cp -R ~/.agents/skills/<s>/. ~/.claude/skills/<s>/`) — they are then committed with this task. If canonical wins, do nothing. **Do not skip reading the diffs**: Step 3 discards whatever is left only in the pool. The backup is the recovery path if a call turns out wrong.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\nIf the pool copy holds edits worth keeping, copy them **into canonical** (`cp -R ~/.agents/skills/<s>/. ~/.claude/skills/<s>/`) — they are then committed with this task. If canonical wins, do nothing. **Do not skip reading the diffs**: Step 3 discards whatever is left only in the pool. The backup is the recovery path if a call turns out wrong.\n\nOnly when every DIVERGED name has been decided, mark this machine prepared. The sync refuses every real run until this file exists (R11-01), so this is the single switch that allows Step 3:\n\n```bash\ntouch ~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready && ls -l ~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready\n```\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\n**Also do not run `dev-sync` — or `sync-to-codex.sh` by hand — between Task 3's commit and Task 4 Step 3.** Task 3 pushes the new sync script. Its first real run publishes every owned skill's pool entry as a link, which overwrites the two drifted pool copies before Task 4 Step 2 has reconciled them (R9-01). They stay recoverable from `pre-canonical-VIEWS-2026-09-12.tgz`, but the safe path is to run Tasks 3 and 4 in one sitting.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\n**Running `dev-sync` between Task 3 and Task 4 is safe, but its sync step will report an error.** Task 3 pushes a sync script whose first real run would overwrite every owned skill's pool entry — including the two drifted copies — before Task 4 Step 2 reconciles them (R9-01). So the script refuses any real run until the per-machine marker `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` exists, and that marker is created only at the end of Task 4 Step 2 (R11-01). Until then the sync fails with `FATAL this machine is not prepared` and changes nothing. The same holds on Windows: its first pull of the new script is refused until its own pool is archived and reconciled.\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\nmkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/.claude/skills /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills /tmp/syncguard/.codex/skills\n%%%%NEW%%%%\nmkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/.claude/skills /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills /tmp/syncguard/.codex/skills\ntouch /tmp/syncguard/.agents/.sync-v2-ready   # R11-01: this scratch HOME counts as prepared\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\nmkdir -p /tmp/syncstage/.claude/skills/owned /tmp/syncstage/.agents/skills/pkg /tmp/syncstage/.codex/skills\n%%%%NEW%%%%\nmkdir -p /tmp/syncstage/.claude/skills/owned /tmp/syncstage/.agents/skills/pkg /tmp/syncstage/.codex/skills\ntouch /tmp/syncstage/.agents/.sync-v2-ready   # R11-01: this scratch HOME counts as prepared\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\nrm -rf /tmp/delsync && mkdir -p /tmp/delsync/{.claude/skills,.agents/skills,.codex/skills}\n%%%%NEW%%%%\nrm -rf /tmp/delsync && mkdir -p /tmp/delsync/{.claude/skills,.agents/skills,.codex/skills}\ntouch /tmp/delsync/.agents/.sync-v2-ready   # R11-01: this scratch HOME counts as prepared\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\n**CASE 3** is the newly staged link being dropped; **CASE 4** is the tracked link being untracked — exactly Task 4's intent — with nothing re-added.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\n**CASE 3** is the newly staged link being dropped; **CASE 4** is the tracked link being untracked — exactly Task 4's intent — with nothing re-added.\n\n**CASE 5 — the first-run gate (R11-01).** An unprepared machine must refuse a real run and change nothing, while a dry run is always allowed:\n\n```bash\nrm -rf /tmp/syncready\nmkdir -p /tmp/syncready/.claude/skills/owned /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned /tmp/syncready/.codex/skills\nprintf -- '---\\nname: owned\\ndescription: Use when testing. Do not use otherwise.\\n---\\n' > /tmp/syncready/.claude/skills/owned/SKILL.md\necho \"pool-only edit - must survive\" > /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned/SKILL.md\nHOME=/tmp/syncready DRY_RUN=1 /bin/bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh >/dev/null; echo \"dry, no marker: exit=$? (expect 0)\"\nHOME=/tmp/syncready /bin/bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"real, no marker: exit=$? (expect 2)\"\ncat /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned/SKILL.md\nls /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/.generated-by-sync-to-codex 2>/dev/null || echo \"no manifest written\"\n```\n\nExpected: `dry, no marker: exit=0`; `FATAL this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync` and `real, no marker: exit=2`; the pool-only edit **still readable**; `no manifest written`. This is exactly the Windows situation after its next `dev-sync` pull.\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\n- The Windows machine. Both machines are active; this plan changes Mac paths only. `sync-to-codex.ps1` is patched in Task 3, but re-running Tasks 2 and 5 there is separate work after these prove out here.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\n- **The Windows machine** (R11-01). Both machines are active; this plan executes on the Mac only. Task 3 pushes the rewritten `sync-to-codex.ps1`, and Windows `dev-sync` runs it right after its next pull. It **refuses any real run** there until `~\\.agents\\.sync-v2-ready` exists, so that pull changes nothing and `dev-sync` reports `FATAL this machine is not prepared`. Until Windows is prepared its views stay exactly as they are, and the seven skills the old twin used to copy stop being refreshed there — a visible, deliberate pause, not a silent overwrite. Preparing Windows is its own session: archive `~\\.agents\\skills` and `~\\.codex\\skills`; run Task 4 Step 2's DIVERGED/diff reconcile against that machine's pool; install the Task 3 Step 5b pre-commit hook in its clone; create the marker; then run the sync. The twin avoids native stderr under `ErrorActionPreference=Stop` so it also runs under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (R11-02), but its behaviour there is verified only when that session happens — this Mac has `pwsh` 7 only.\n%%%%END%%%%\n%%%%OLD%%%%\nFixes: the backup checks its commit (Task 3 Step 5c); the sync removes third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, never a reset to HEAD.\n%%%%NEW%%%%\nFixes: the backup checks its commit (Task 3 Step 5c); the sync removes third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, never a reset to HEAD.\n- **Round 11** — Claude (resumed): **REVISE**, 0 high / 1 medium / 1 low (R11-01, R11-02). Reviewer: all three round-10 fixes hold. R11-01: Windows `dev-sync` would run the rewritten twin straight after its next pull and overwrite all 43 published entries with no snapshot or reconcile. Fix: both scripts refuse any real run until a per-machine `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` marker exists, created only after reconciling — which also turns Task 3's \"don't run dev-sync\" warning into an enforced rule on the Mac. R11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n%%%%END%%%%\n","numLines":116,"startLine":1,"totalLines":116}},"displayPath":"../.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r11-fixes.txt"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"a435c155-a023-46ba-b24e-46c742c236fd","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:26.785Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/r11-fixes.txt\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n2\t# R8-01: OWNERSHIP IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A LIST.\n3\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n4\t# R11-01: FIRST-RUN GATE. A real run replaces every owned skill's pool and codex\n5\t# entry with a link (a copy on Windows). A machine must be prepared first - its\n6\t# views archived and its drifted pool copies reconciled (plan Task 4 Step 2 on\n7\t# the Mac; the same steps on Windows) - and marks that with this per-machine\n8\t# file. dev-sync runs this script right after a pull, so without the gate the\n9\t# first run on an unprepared machine overwrites those copies unannounced.\n10\t# Dry runs never write, so they are always allowed.\n11\tREADY=\"$HOME/.agents/.sync-v2-ready\"\n12\tif [ \"$DRY\" = \"0\" ] && [ ! -f \"$READY\" ]; then\n13\t  echo \"FATAL this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync ($READY missing).\" >&2\n14\t  echo \"      Archive ~/.agents/skills and ~/.codex/skills and reconcile drifted\" >&2\n15\t  echo \"      pool copies first (plan Task 4 Step 2), then: touch $READY\" >&2\n16\t  echo \"      No changes made. DRY_RUN=1 is always allowed.\" >&2\n17\t  exit 2\n18\tfi\n19\t\n20\t# R8-01: OWNERSHIP IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A LIST.\n21\t%%%%END%%%%\n22\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n23\t# R8-01: ownership is structural. A reparse point in canonical was installed\n24\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n25\t# R11-01: first-run gate - see the bash twin. dev-sync runs this right after a\n26\t# pull, so without it the first Windows run would overwrite every owned skill's\n27\t# pool and codex copy with no snapshot and no reconcile.\n28\t$READY = Join-Path $HOME '.agents\\.sync-v2-ready'\n29\tif ((-not $DRY) -and -not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $READY)) {\n30\t  Stop-Fatal \"this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync ($READY missing). Archive ~\\.agents\\skills and ~\\.codex\\skills, reconcile drifted pool copies (plan Task 4 Step 2), then create $READY. No changes made. DRY_RUN=1 is always allowed.\" 2\n31\t}\n32\t\n33\t# R8-01: ownership is structural. A reparse point in canonical was installed\n34\t%%%%END%%%%\n35\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n36\t  foreach ($name in $THIRD_PARTY) {\n37\t    git -C $SRC ls-files --error-unmatch -- $name 2>$null | Out-Null\n38\t    if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) {\n39\t      git -C $SRC rm --cached --quiet -- $name 2>$null | Out-Null\n40\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n41\t  foreach ($name in $THIRD_PARTY) {\n42\t    # R11-02: no --error-unmatch and no 2>$null. For an untracked path that form\n43\t    # writes to stderr, and in Windows PowerShell 5.1 redirected native stderr\n44\t    # becomes an error record that ends the script under EAP=Stop - before the\n45\t    # exclude block or the views are written. Plain ls-files prints the path\n46\t    # when it is tracked and nothing otherwise, and writes no stderr.\n47\t    $tracked = git -C $SRC ls-files -- $name\n48\t    if ($tracked) {\n49\t      git -C $SRC rm --cached --quiet -- $name | Out-Null\n50\t%%%%END%%%%\n51\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n52\tIf the pool copy holds edits worth keeping, copy them **into canonical** (`cp -R ~/.agents/skills/<s>/. ~/.claude/skills/<s>/`) — they are then committed with this task. If canonical wins, do nothing. **Do not skip reading the diffs**: Step 3 discards whatever is left only in the pool. The backup is the recovery path if a call turns out wrong.\n53\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n54\tIf the pool copy holds edits worth keeping, copy them **into canonical** (`cp -R ~/.agents/skills/<s>/. ~/.claude/skills/<s>/`) — they are then committed with this task. If canonical wins, do nothing. **Do not skip reading the diffs**: Step 3 discards whatever is left only in the pool. The backup is the recovery path if a call turns out wrong.\n55\t\n56\tOnly when every DIVERGED name has been decided, mark this machine prepared. The sync refuses every real run until this file exists (R11-01), so this is the single switch that allows Step 3:\n57\t\n58\t```bash\n59\ttouch ~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready && ls -l ~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready\n60\t```\n61\t%%%%END%%%%\n62\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n63\t**Also do not run `dev-sync` — or `sync-to-codex.sh` by hand — between Task 3's commit and Task 4 Step 3.** Task 3 pushes the new sync script. Its first real run publishes every owned skill's pool entry as a link, which overwrites the two drifted pool copies before Task 4 Step 2 has reconciled them (R9-01). They stay recoverable from `pre-canonical-VIEWS-2026-09-12.tgz`, but the safe path is to run Tasks 3 and 4 in one sitting.\n64\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n65\t**Running `dev-sync` between Task 3 and Task 4 is safe, but its sync step will report an error.** Task 3 pushes a sync script whose first real run would overwrite every owned skill's pool entry — including the two drifted copies — before Task 4 Step 2 reconciles them (R9-01). So the script refuses any real run until the per-machine marker `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` exists, and that marker is created only at the end of Task 4 Step 2 (R11-01). Until then the sync fails with `FATAL this machine is not prepared` and changes nothing. The same holds on Windows: its first pull of the new script is refused until its own pool is archived and reconciled.\n66\t%%%%END%%%%\n67\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n68\tmkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/.claude/skills /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills /tmp/syncguard/.codex/skills\n69\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n70\tmkdir -p /tmp/syncguard/.claude/skills /tmp/syncguard/.agents/skills /tmp/syncguard/.codex/skills\n71\ttouch /tmp/syncguard/.agents/.sync-v2-ready   # R11-01: this scratch HOME counts as prepared\n72\t%%%%END%%%%\n73\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n74\tmkdir -p /tmp/syncstage/.claude/skills/owned /tmp/syncstage/.agents/skills/pkg /tmp/syncstage/.codex/skills\n75\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n76\tmkdir -p /tmp/syncstage/.claude/skills/owned /tmp/syncstage/.agents/skills/pkg /tmp/syncstage/.codex/skills\n77\ttouch /tmp/syncstage/.agents/.sync-v2-ready   # R11-01: this scratch HOME counts as prepared\n78\t%%%%END%%%%\n79\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n80\trm -rf /tmp/delsync && mkdir -p /tmp/delsync/{.claude/skills,.agents/skills,.codex/skills}\n81\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n82\trm -rf /tmp/delsync && mkdir -p /tmp/delsync/{.claude/skills,.agents/skills,.codex/skills}\n83\ttouch /tmp/delsync/.agents/.sync-v2-ready   # R11-01: this scratch HOME counts as prepared\n84\t%%%%END%%%%\n85\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n86\t**CASE 3** is the newly staged link being dropped; **CASE 4** is the tracked link being untracked — exactly Task 4's intent — with nothing re-added.\n87\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n88\t**CASE 3** is the newly staged link being dropped; **CASE 4** is the tracked link being untracked — exactly Task 4's intent — with nothing re-added.\n89\t\n90\t**CASE 5 — the first-run gate (R11-01).** An unprepared machine must refuse a real run and change nothing, while a dry run is always allowed:\n91\t\n92\t```bash\n93\trm -rf /tmp/syncready\n94\tmkdir -p /tmp/syncready/.claude/skills/owned /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned /tmp/syncready/.codex/skills\n95\tprintf -- '---\\nname: owned\\ndescription: Use when testing. Do not use otherwise.\\n---\\n' > /tmp/syncready/.claude/skills/owned/SKILL.md\n96\techo \"pool-only edit - must survive\" > /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned/SKILL.md\n97\tHOME=/tmp/syncready DRY_RUN=1 /bin/bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh >/dev/null; echo \"dry, no marker: exit=$? (expect 0)\"\n98\tHOME=/tmp/syncready /bin/bash ~/.claude/skills/sync-to-codex.sh; echo \"real, no marker: exit=$? (expect 2)\"\n99\tcat /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/owned/SKILL.md\n100\tls /tmp/syncready/.agents/skills/.generated-by-sync-to-codex 2>/dev/null || echo \"no manifest written\"\n101\t```\n102\t\n103\tExpected: `dry, no marker: exit=0`; `FATAL this machine is not prepared for the v2 sync` and `real, no marker: exit=2`; the pool-only edit **still readable**; `no manifest written`. This is exactly the Windows situation after its next `dev-sync` pull.\n104\t%%%%END%%%%\n105\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n106\t- The Windows machine. Both machines are active; this plan changes Mac paths only. `sync-to-codex.ps1` is patched in Task 3, but re-running Tasks 2 and 5 there is separate work after these prove out here.\n107\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n108\t- **The Windows machine** (R11-01). Both machines are active; this plan executes on the Mac only. Task 3 pushes the rewritten `sync-to-codex.ps1`, and Windows `dev-sync` runs it right after its next pull. It **refuses any real run** there until `~\\.agents\\.sync-v2-ready` exists, so that pull changes nothing and `dev-sync` reports `FATAL this machine is not prepared`. Until Windows is prepared its views stay exactly as they are, and the seven skills the old twin used to copy stop being refreshed there — a visible, deliberate pause, not a silent overwrite. Preparing Windows is its own session: archive `~\\.agents\\skills` and `~\\.codex\\skills`; run Task 4 Step 2's DIVERGED/diff reconcile against that machine's pool; install the Task 3 Step 5b pre-commit hook in its clone; create the marker; then run the sync. The twin avoids native stderr under `ErrorActionPreference=Stop` so it also runs under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (R11-02), but its behaviour there is verified only when that session happens — this Mac has `pwsh` 7 only.\n109\t%%%%END%%%%\n110\t%%%%OLD%%%%\n111\tFixes: the backup checks its commit (Task 3 Step 5c); the sync removes third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, never a reset to HEAD.\n112\t%%%%NEW%%%%\n113\tFixes: the backup checks its commit (Task 3 Step 5c); the sync removes third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, never a reset to HEAD.\n114\t- **Round 11** — Claude (resumed): **REVISE**, 0 high / 1 medium / 1 low (R11-01, R11-02). Reviewer: all three round-10 fixes hold. R11-01: Windows `dev-sync` would run the rewritten twin straight after its next pull and overwrite all 43 published entries with no snapshot or reconcile. Fix: both scripts refuse any real run until a per-machine `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` marker exists, created only after reconciling — which also turns Task 3's \"don't run dev-sync\" warning into an enforced rule on the Mac. R11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n115\t%%%%END%%%%\n116\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"a435c155-a023-46ba-b24e-46c742c236fd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"memory-write","path":"userSettings:memory-write","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/memory-write\n\n# Memory Write\n\n## Outcome\n- Fact added to, updated in, or removed from the correct memory tier\n- Tier-B caps enforced (2,500 chars for MEMORY.md, 1,375 for USER.md)\n- Confirmation: \"Saved — will be active from next session.\"\n\n## Pick the tier first\n\nAsk: \"Will this fact still be true and useful in 3 months, across multiple projects?\"\n\n**Tier A — `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`** (one file per fact, indexed by MEMORY.md)\n- Durable user facts → `user_*.md` (metadata.type: user)\n- Stable project conventions / gotchas → `project_*.md` (metadata.type: project)\n- Behavioral feedback rules → `feedback_*.md` (metadata.type: feedback)\n- External-system pointers → `reference_*.md` (metadata.type: reference)\n\n**Tier B — `~/.claude/global-memory/`** (single capped file)\n- Active threads (in-progress work) → `MEMORY.md` `## Active Threads`\n- Current env notes (URLs, tool versions, project structure in play) → `MEMORY.md` `## Environment Notes`\n- Pending decisions → `MEMORY.md` `## Pending Decisions`\n- Durable user profile updates → `USER.md`\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Decide tier** using the rule above.\n2. **Read** the target file in full.\n3. **Dedup check** — substring scan; if the fact already exists, skip or update in place.\n4. **Cap check** for Tier B — `wc -c < ~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` (or USER.md). If over cap, consolidate (merge similar entries, prune stale) before adding.\n5. **Write** the change.\n   - Tier A: create a new file with proper frontmatter (name, description, metadata.type) and add a one-line pointer to that slug's `MEMORY.md` index. `name:` follows the rule below.\n   - Tier B: append under the correct `##` section.\n6. **Confirm** in one line: `Saved — will be active from next session.`\n\n## The `name:` field — bare kebab slug\n\n**`name:` = the filename stem minus its leading type token, kebab-cased.** The type\nalready lives in `metadata.type` and the filename already carries the prefix; repeating\nit in `name:` is the third copy.\n\n| File | `name:` |\n|---|---|\n| `project_automation-cadence.md` | `automation-cadence` |\n| `user_sports-teams.md` | `sports-teams` |\n| `reference_baylor-house-style.md` | `baylor-house-style` |\n| `feedback_project_idea_capture.md` | `project-idea-capture` |\n\nNote the last row: strip only the **leading** token. `feedback_project_idea_capture.md`\nis a feedback memory *about* project-idea capture — its name keeps the word `project`.\n\n`[[links]]` target this bare name, never the filename and never a prefixed variant.\n\nThis rule exists because it was previously unwritten: an audit on 2026-09-02 found the\ncorpus split 59 bare / 58 prefixed, which broke 37 links. Both forms look right in\nisolation, so without a stated rule the split regrows on every write.\n\n## Actions\n\n- **add** — append (default)\n- **replace** — substring match + swap. Preferred over add when updating an existing fact.\n- **remove** — confirm with the user before deleting.\n\n## Retrieval — the tiered ladder\n\nWhen the user asks about past context, decisions, or \"did we already…?\", climb in order and stop as soon as the question is answered. Do not run memsearch when the answer is already in the injected snapshot.\n\n1. **Tier 0** — scan what is already in context (USER.md, MEMORY.md, today's compiled log). Zero cost.\n2. **L1** — `memsearch search \"query\" --top-k 5`. Hybrid vector + keyword across all indexed paths: Tier A files (including the retired Windows slugs), daily logs, transcripts.\n3. **L2** — `memsearch expand <chunk_hash>`. Full markdown section around a hit.\n4. **L3** — `memsearch transcript <session_id>`. Raw dialogue. Last resort.\n5. **Fallback** — say \"I don't have a record of that.\"\n\n## Daily compiled logs — read-only\n\n`~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/{YYYY-MM-DD}.md` is produced by `claude-memory-compiler` through its own SessionEnd/PreCompact hooks, and injected by its own SessionStart hook.\n\n- **Never hand-edit these files.** The compiler owns them.\n- **Never read them eagerly.** Treat the injected snapshot as the source of truth.\n\n## What is loaded at session start\n\n`~/.claude/global-memory/USER.md` and `MEMORY.md` are read once at session start and are a **frozen snapshot**. Mid-session writes persist to disk but only take effect next session — this is deliberate, to preserve the prefix cache. Never load more than the startup set proactively.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Never exceed Tier B caps. Consolidate first.\n- Always dedup before adding.\n- For Tier A, the slug is the current cwd with `/` and `.` replaced by `-` (so `~` → `-Users-kristinarash`, `~/DEV` → `-Users-kristinarash-DEV`, `~/.claude/skills` → `-Users-kristinarash--claude-skills`). The harness injects the correct slug each session — use that, don't reconstruct it.\n- **Never write to a `C--Users-eric-rash*` slug.** Those are pre-migration Windows keys from the box retired 2026-08-14. They are still indexed by memsearch, so old facts stay recallable, but nothing written there will ever be auto-injected again.\n- Never announce the write mid-session beyond the one-line confirmation. The injected snapshot is frozen — the new fact appears next session, not this one.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Correct project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md: ~/.claude/skills/ is its own git repo (erash11/claude-skills), not merely \"gitignored with no safety net\". Also record the canonical/view architecture decision."},{"name":"codex-review","path":"userSettings:codex-review","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/codex-review\n\n# Codex Review\n\nCompatibility entry point: explicitly use Codex as the plan reviewer. The shared workflow and executable live in the sibling `claudex-loop` skill; install that skill alongside this one.\n\nIn Claude Code, load [claudex-loop](../claudex-loop/SKILL.md) and start at `mode=review`, `host=claude`, reviewer Codex. Preserve `PLAN_FILE`, `LOG_FILE`, `MAX_ROUNDS` / `rounds`, and explicit model/effort arguments. The invocation authorizes reviewing, not implementation. Do not restart an interview when the supplied plan is already adequate.\n\nIn Codex, explain that this explicit command would use the same provider for planning and review. Use the shared `claudex-loop mode=review` workflow with Claude as the independent reviewer unless the user specifically requested same-provider review. If they did, perform and label that as ordinary Codex review; do not claim cross-provider approval or use the cross-provider approval runner to certify it.\n\nRead the shared [runtime reference](../claudex-loop/references/runtime.md). Resolve the runner from the installed sibling skill, not the target repository. Use the actual resolved plan path on every round and build handoff. A valid completed result and APPROVED verdict are both required; failed, empty, blocked or malformed results are not approval. The host logs findings and dispositions, and stops at the round cap.\n\nIf implementation follows, use the shared build/inspection phase. An independent plan review does not replace independent review of the final code.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Review the implementation plan at ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md"},{"name":"superpowers:writing-plans","path":"plugin:superpowers:writing-plans","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.3.0/skills/writing-plans\n\n# Writing Plans\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite comprehensive implementation plans assuming the engineer has zero context for our codebase and questionable taste. Document everything they need to know: which files to touch for each task, code, testing, docs they might need to check, how to test it. Give them the whole plan as bite-sized tasks. DRY. YAGNI. TDD. Frequent commits.\n\nAssume they are a skilled developer, but know almost nothing about our toolset or problem domain. Assume they don't know good test design very well.\n\n**Announce at start:** \"I'm using the writing-plans skill to create the implementation plan.\"\n\n**Context:** If working in an isolated worktree, it should have been created via the `superpowers:using-git-worktrees` skill at execution time.\n\n**Save plans to:** `docs/superpowers/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<feature-name>.md`\n- (User preferences for plan location override this default)\n\n## Scope Check\n\nIf the spec covers multiple independent subsystems, it should have been broken into sub-project specs during brainstorming. If it wasn't, suggest breaking this into separate plans — one per subsystem. Each plan should produce working, testable software on its own.\n\n## File Structure\n\nBefore defining tasks, map out which files will be created or modified and what each one is responsible for. This is where decomposition decisions get locked in.\n\n- Design units with clear boundaries and well-defined interfaces. Each file should have one clear responsibility.\n- You reason best about code you can hold in context at once, and your edits are more reliable when files are focused. Prefer smaller, focused files over large ones that do too much.\n- Files that change together should live together. Split by responsibility, not by technical layer.\n- In existing codebases, follow established patterns. If the codebase uses large files, don't unilaterally restructure - but if a file you're modifying has grown unwieldy, including a split in the plan is reasonable.\n\nThis structure informs the task decomposition. Each task should produce self-contained changes that make sense independently.\n\n## Task Right-Sizing\n\nA task is the smallest unit that carries its own test cycle and is worth a\nfresh reviewer's gate. When drawing task boundaries: fold setup,\nconfiguration, scaffolding, and documentation steps into the task whose\ndeliverable needs them; split only where a reviewer could meaningfully\nreject one task while approving its neighbor. Each task ends with an\nindependently testable deliverable.\n\n## Bite-Sized Task Granularity\n\n**Each step is one action (2-5 minutes):**\n- \"Write the failing test\" - step\n- \"Run it to make sure it fails\" - step\n- \"Implement the minimal code to make the test pass\" - step\n- \"Run the tests and make sure they pass\" - step\n- \"Commit\" - step\n\n## Plan Document Header\n\n**Every plan MUST start with this header:**\n\n```markdown\n# [Feature Name] Implementation Plan\n\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n\n**Goal:** [One sentence describing what this builds]\n\n**Architecture:** [2-3 sentences about approach]\n\n**Tech Stack:** [Key technologies/libraries]\n\n**Spec:** [path to the spec/design doc this plan implements — the plan\nargues from the spec, so the spec travels with it; executors read both]\n\n## Global Constraints\n\n[The spec's project-wide requirements — version floors, dependency limits,\nnaming and copy rules, platform requirements — one line each, with exact\nvalues copied verbatim from the spec. Every task's requirements implicitly\ninclude this section.]\n\n---\n```\n\n## Task Structure\n\n````markdown\n### Task N: [Component Name]\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `exact/path/to/file.py`\n- Modify: `exact/path/to/existing.py:123-145`\n- Test: `tests/exact/path/to/test.py`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: [what this task uses from earlier tasks — exact signatures]\n- Produces: [what later tasks rely on — exact function names, parameter\n  and return types. A task's implementer sees only their own task; this\n  block is how they learn the names and types neighboring tasks use.]\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**\n\n```python\ndef test_specific_behavior():\n    result = function(input)\n    assert result == expected\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run test to verify it fails**\n\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\nExpected: FAIL with \"function not defined\"\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Write minimal implementation**\n\n```python\ndef function(input):\n    return expected\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Run test to verify it passes**\n\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\nExpected: PASS\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\n\n```bash\ngit add tests/path/test.py src/path/file.py\ngit commit -m \"feat: add specific feature\"\n```\n````\n\n## No Placeholders\n\nEvery step must contain the actual content an engineer needs. These are **plan failures** — never write them:\n- \"TBD\", \"TODO\", \"implement later\", \"fill in details\"\n- \"Add appropriate error handling\" / \"add validation\" / \"handle edge cases\"\n- \"Write tests for the above\" (without actual test code)\n- \"Similar to Task N\" (repeat the code — the engineer may be reading tasks out of order)\n- Steps that describe what to do without showing how (code blocks required for code steps)\n- References to types, functions, or methods not defined in any task\n\n## Self-Review\n\nAfter writing the complete plan, look at the spec with fresh eyes and check the plan against it. This is a checklist you run yourself — not a subagent dispatch.\n\n**1. Spec coverage:** Skim each section/requirement in the spec. Can you point to a task that implements it? List any gaps.\n\n**2. Placeholder scan:** Search your plan for red flags — any of the patterns from the \"No Placeholders\" section above. Fix them.\n\n**3. Type consistency:** Do the types, method signatures, and property names you used in later tasks match what you defined in earlier tasks? A function called `clearLayers()` in Task 3 but `clearFullLayers()` in Task 7 is a bug.\n\nIf you find issues, fix them inline. No need to re-review — just fix and move on. If you find a spec requirement with no task, add the task.\n\n## Execution Handoff\n\nAfter saving the plan, offer execution choice:\n\n**\"Plan complete and saved to `docs/superpowers/plans/<filename>.md`. Two execution options:**\n\n**1. Subagent-Driven (recommended)** - I dispatch a fresh subagent per task, review between tasks, fast iteration\n\n**2. Inline Execution** - Execute tasks in this session using executing-plans, batch execution with checkpoints\n\n**Which approach?\"**\n\n**If Subagent-Driven chosen:**\n- **REQUIRED SUB-SKILL:** Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development\n- Fresh subagent per task + two-stage review\n\n**If Inline Execution chosen:**\n- **REQUIRED SUB-SKILL:** Use superpowers:executing-plans\n- Batch execution with checkpoints for review\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Harness-agnostic skill layout + system audit skill + rot.md + Pi trial"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"00b8f024-a6e2-4cb0-ae33-dd59bed31b2f","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:26.787Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: memory-write\nPath: userSettings:memory-write\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/memory-write\n\n# Memory Write\n\n## Outcome\n- Fact added to, updated in, or removed from the correct memory tier\n- Tier-B caps enforced (2,500 chars for MEMORY.md, 1,375 for USER.md)\n- Confirmation: \"Saved — will be active from next session.\"\n\n## Pick the tier first\n\nAsk: \"Will this fact still be true and useful in 3 months, across multiple projects?\"\n\n**Tier A — `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`** (one file per fact, indexed by MEMORY.md)\n- Durable user facts → `user_*.md` (metadata.type: user)\n- Stable project conventions / gotchas → `project_*.md` (metadata.type: project)\n- Behavioral feedback rules → `feedback_*.md` (metadata.type: feedback)\n- External-system pointers → `reference_*.md` (metadata.type: reference)\n\n**Tier B — `~/.claude/global-memory/`** (single capped file)\n- Active threads (in-progress work) → `MEMORY.md` `## Active Threads`\n- Current env notes (URLs, tool versions, project structure in play) → `MEMORY.md` `## Environment Notes`\n- Pending decisions → `MEMORY.md` `## Pending Decisions`\n- Durable user profile updates → `USER.md`\n\n## Steps\n\n1. **Decide tier** using the rule above.\n2. **Read** the target file in full.\n3. **Dedup check** — substring scan; if the fact already exists, skip or update in place.\n4. **Cap check** for Tier B — `wc -c < ~/.claude/global-memory/MEMORY.md` (or USER.md). If over cap, consolidate (merge similar entries, prune stale) before adding.\n5. **Write** the change.\n   - Tier A: create a new file with proper frontmatter (name, description, metadata.type) and add a one-line pointer to that slug's `MEMORY.md` index. `name:` follows the rule below.\n   - Tier B: append under the correct `##` section.\n6. **Confirm** in one line: `Saved — will be active from next session.`\n\n## The `name:` field — bare kebab slug\n\n**`name:` = the filename stem minus its leading type token, kebab-cased.** The type\nalready lives in `metadata.type` and the filename already carries the prefix; repeating\nit in `name:` is the third copy.\n\n| File | `name:` |\n|---|---|\n| `project_automation-cadence.md` | `automation-cadence` |\n| `user_sports-teams.md` | `sports-teams` |\n| `reference_baylor-house-style.md` | `baylor-house-style` |\n| `feedback_project_idea_capture.md` | `project-idea-capture` |\n\nNote the last row: strip only the **leading** token. `feedback_project_idea_capture.md`\nis a feedback memory *about* project-idea capture — its name keeps the word `project`.\n\n`[[links]]` target this bare name, never the filename and never a prefixed variant.\n\nThis rule exists because it was previously unwritten: an audit on 2026-09-02 found the\ncorpus split 59 bare / 58 prefixed, which broke 37 links. Both forms look right in\nisolation, so without a stated rule the split regrows on every write.\n\n## Actions\n\n- **add** — append (default)\n- **replace** — substring match + swap. Preferred over add when updating an existing fact.\n- **remove** — confirm with the user before deleting.\n\n## Retrieval — the tiered ladder\n\nWhen the user asks about past context, decisions, or \"did we already…?\", climb in order and stop as soon as the question is answered. Do not run memsearch when the answer is already in the injected snapshot.\n\n1. **Tier 0** — scan what is already in context (USER.md, MEMORY.md, today's compiled log). Zero cost.\n2. **L1** — `memsearch search \"query\" --top-k 5`. Hybrid vector + keyword across all indexed paths: Tier A files (including the retired Windows slugs), daily logs, transcripts.\n3. **L2** — `memsearch expand <chunk_hash>`. Full markdown section around a hit.\n4. **L3** — `memsearch transcript <session_id>`. Raw dialogue. Last resort.\n5. **Fallback** — say \"I don't have a record of that.\"\n\n## Daily compiled logs — read-only\n\n`~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/{YYYY-MM-DD}.md` is produced by `claude-memory-compiler` through its own SessionEnd/PreCompact hooks, and injected by its own SessionStart hook.\n\n- **Never hand-edit these files.** The compiler owns them.\n- **Never read them eagerly.** Treat the injected snapshot as the source of truth.\n\n## What is loaded at session start\n\n`~/.claude/global-memory/USER.md` and `MEMORY.md` are read once at session start and are a **frozen snapshot**. Mid-session writes persist to disk but only take effect next session — this is deliberate, to preserve the prefix cache. Never load more than the startup set proactively.\n\n## Rules\n\n- Never exceed Tier B caps. Consolidate first.\n- Always dedup before adding.\n- For Tier A, the slug is the current cwd with `/` and `.` replaced by `-` (so `~` → `-Users-kristinarash`, `~/DEV` → `-Users-kristinarash-DEV`, `~/.claude/skills` → `-Users-kristinarash--claude-skills`). The harness injects the correct slug each session — use that, don't reconstruct it.\n- **Never write to a `C--Users-eric-rash*` slug.** Those are pre-migration Windows keys from the box retired 2026-08-14. They are still indexed by memsearch, so old facts stay recallable, but nothing written there will ever be auto-injected again.\n- Never announce the write mid-session beyond the one-line confirmation. The injected snapshot is frozen — the new fact appears next session, not this one.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Correct project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md: ~/.claude/skills/ is its own git repo (erash11/claude-skills), not merely \"gitignored with no safety net\". Also record the canonical/view architecture decision.\n\n---\n\n### Skill: codex-review\nPath: userSettings:codex-review\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills/codex-review\n\n# Codex Review\n\nCompatibility entry point: explicitly use Codex as the plan reviewer. The shared workflow and executable live in the sibling `claudex-loop` skill; install that skill alongside this one.\n\nIn Claude Code, load [claudex-loop](../claudex-loop/SKILL.md) and start at `mode=review`, `host=claude`, reviewer Codex. Preserve `PLAN_FILE`, `LOG_FILE`, `MAX_ROUNDS` / `rounds`, and explicit model/effort arguments. The invocation authorizes reviewing, not implementation. Do not restart an interview when the supplied plan is already adequate.\n\nIn Codex, explain that this explicit command would use the same provider for planning and review. Use the shared `claudex-loop mode=review` workflow with Claude as the independent reviewer unless the user specifically requested same-provider review. If they did, perform and label that as ordinary Codex review; do not claim cross-provider approval or use the cross-provider approval runner to certify it.\n\nRead the shared [runtime reference](../claudex-loop/references/runtime.md). Resolve the runner from the installed sibling skill, not the target repository. Use the actual resolved plan path on every round and build handoff. A valid completed result and APPROVED verdict are both required; failed, empty, blocked or malformed results are not approval. The host logs findings and dispositions, and stops at the round cap.\n\nIf implementation follows, use the shared build/inspection phase. An independent plan review does not replace independent review of the final code.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Review the implementation plan at ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md\n\n---\n\n### Skill: superpowers:writing-plans\nPath: plugin:superpowers:writing-plans\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/superpowers/6.3.0/skills/writing-plans\n\n# Writing Plans\n\n## Overview\n\nWrite comprehensive implementation plans assuming the engineer has zero context for our codebase and questionable taste. Document everything they need to know: which files to touch for each task, code, testing, docs they might need to check, how to test it. Give them the whole plan as bite-sized tasks. DRY. YAGNI. TDD. Frequent commits.\n\nAssume they are a skilled developer, but know almost nothing about our toolset or problem domain. Assume they don't know good test design very well.\n\n**Announce at start:** \"I'm using the writing-plans skill to create the implementation plan.\"\n\n**Context:** If working in an isolated worktree, it should have been created via the `superpowers:using-git-worktrees` skill at execution time.\n\n**Save plans to:** `docs/superpowers/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-<feature-name>.md`\n- (User preferences for plan location override this default)\n\n## Scope Check\n\nIf the spec covers multiple independent subsystems, it should have been broken into sub-project specs during brainstorming. If it wasn't, suggest breaking this into separate plans — one per subsystem. Each plan should produce working, testable software on its own.\n\n## File Structure\n\nBefore defining tasks, map out which files will be created or modified and what each one is responsible for. This is where decomposition decisions get locked in.\n\n- Design units with clear boundaries and well-defined interfaces. Each file should have one clear responsibility.\n- You reason best about code you can hold in context at once, and your edits are more reliable when files are focused. Prefer smaller, focused files over large ones that do too much.\n- Files that change together should live together. Split by responsibility, not by technical layer.\n- In existing codebases, follow established patterns. If the codebase uses large files, don't unilaterally restructure - but if a file you're modifying has grown unwieldy, including a split in the plan is reasonable.\n\nThis structure informs the task decomposition. Each task should produce self-contained changes that make sense independently.\n\n## Task Right-Sizing\n\nA task is the smallest unit that carries its own test cycle and is worth a\nfresh reviewer's gate. When drawing task boundaries: fold setup,\nconfiguration, scaffolding, and documentation steps into the task whose\ndeliverable needs them; split only where a reviewer could meaningfully\nreject one task while approving its neighbor. Each task ends with an\nindependently testable deliverable.\n\n## Bite-Sized Task Granularity\n\n**Each step is one action (2-5 minutes):**\n- \"Write the failing test\" - step\n- \"Run it to make sure it fails\" - step\n- \"Implement the minimal code to make the test pass\" - step\n- \"Run the tests and make sure they pass\" - step\n- \"Commit\" - step\n\n## Plan Document Header\n\n**Every plan MUST start with this header:**\n\n```markdown\n# [Feature Name] Implementation Plan\n\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n\n**Goal:** [One sentence describing what this builds]\n\n**Architecture:** [2-3 sentences about approach]\n\n**Tech Stack:** [Key technologies/libraries]\n\n**Spec:** [path to the spec/design doc this plan implements — the plan\nargues from the spec, so the spec travels with it; executors read both]\n\n## Global Constraints\n\n[The spec's project-wide requirements — version floors, dependency limits,\nnaming and copy rules, platform requirements — one line each, with exact\nvalues copied verbatim from the spec. Every task's requirements implicitly\ninclude this section.]\n\n---\n```\n\n## Task Structure\n\n````markdown\n### Task N: [Component Name]\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `exact/path/to/file.py`\n- Modify: `exact/path/to/existing.py:123-145`\n- Test: `tests/exact/path/to/test.py`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: [what this task uses from earlier tasks — exact signatures]\n- Produces: [what later tasks rely on — exact function names, parameter\n  and return types. A task's implementer sees only their own task; this\n  block is how they learn the names and types neighboring tasks use.]\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing test**\n\n```python\ndef test_specific_behavior():\n    result = function(input)\n    assert result == expected\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run test to verify it fails**\n\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\nExpected: FAIL with \"function not defined\"\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Write minimal implementation**\n\n```python\ndef function(input):\n    return expected\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Run test to verify it passes**\n\nRun: `pytest tests/path/test.py::test_name -v`\nExpected: PASS\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Commit**\n\n```bash\ngit add tests/path/test.py src/path/file.py\ngit commit -m \"feat: add specific feature\"\n```\n````\n\n## No Placeholders\n\nEvery step must contain the actual content an engineer needs. These are **plan failures** — never write them:\n- \"TBD\", \"TODO\", \"implement later\", \"fill in details\"\n- \"Add appropriate error handling\" / \"add validation\" / \"handle edge cases\"\n- \"Write tests for the above\" (without actual test code)\n- \"Similar to Task N\" (repeat the code — the engineer may be reading tasks out of order)\n- Steps that describe what to do without showing how (code blocks required for code steps)\n- References to types, functions, or methods not defined in any task\n\n## Self-Review\n\nAfter writing the complete plan, look at the spec with fresh eyes and check the plan against it. This is a checklist you run yourself — not a subagent dispatch.\n\n**1. Spec coverage:** Skim each section/requirement in the spec. Can you point to a task that implements it? List any gaps.\n\n**2. Placeholder scan:** Search your plan for red flags — any of the patterns from the \"No Placeholders\" section above. Fix them.\n\n**3. Type consistency:** Do the types, method signatures, and property names you used in later tasks match what you defined in earlier tasks? A function called `clearLayers()` in Task 3 but `clearFullLayers()` in Task 7 is a bug.\n\nIf you find issues, fix them inline. No need to re-review — just fix and move on. If you find a spec requirement with no task, add the task.\n\n## Execution Handoff\n\nAfter saving the plan, offer execution choice:\n\n**\"Plan complete and saved to `docs/superpowers/plans/<filename>.md`. Two execution options:**\n\n**1. Subagent-Driven (recommended)** - I dispatch a fresh subagent per task, review between tasks, fast iteration\n\n**2. Inline Execution** - Execute tasks in this session using executing-plans, batch execution with checkpoints\n\n**Which approach?\"**\n\n**If Subagent-Driven chosen:**\n- **REQUIRED SUB-SKILL:** Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development\n- Fresh subagent per task + two-stage review\n\n**If Inline Execution chosen:**\n- **REQUIRED SUB-SKILL:** Use superpowers:executing-plans\n- Batch execution with checkpoints for review\n\n\nARGUMENTS: Harness-agnostic skill layout + system audit skill + rot.md + Pi trial\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
