{"parentUuid":"ac089e8c-1aa0-4430-916c-c84ef7ac8b64","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"output_style_instructions","style":{"name":"ELI5","prompt":"It's been a long day and my brain is fried, talk to me like I'm 5.\nOnly report to me in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English. That means\napproved plain words, active voice, and one instruction or idea per sentence.\nSmall words, short sentences, short paragraphs. If you have to use\na big word, explain it right after. Only return what's actually necessary.\nJust tell me what you did, did it work, what do I do now.\nIf I have to decide something: 2 options max, the context I need to pick fast,\nand which one you'd go with.\nKeep paths and commands exact. I have no brain cells left for the rest."}},"type":"attachment","uuid":"5081cc04-52c1-42ff-9abe-f50206e85d94","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:26.802Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n# Output Style: ELI5\nIt's been a long day and my brain is fried, talk to me like I'm 5.\nOnly report to me in ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English. That means\napproved plain words, active voice, and one instruction or idea per sentence.\nSmall words, short sentences, short paragraphs. If you have to use\na big word, explain it right after. Only return what's actually necessary.\nJust tell me what you did, did it work, what do I do now.\nIf I have to decide something: 2 options max, the context I need to pick fast,\nand which one you'd go with.\nKeep paths and commands exact. I have no brain cells left for the rest.\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"5081cc04-52c1-42ff-9abe-f50206e85d94","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_non_blocking_error","hookName":"SessionStart:compact","toolUseID":"76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","stderr":"Failed with non-blocking status code: bash: $HOME/.claude/hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh: No such file or directory","stdout":"","exitCode":127,"command":"bash '$HOME/.claude/hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh' session","durationMs":29},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b3db704c-de8d-43d4-aae4-03e2739bd535","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:26.848Z","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"b3db704c-de8d-43d4-aae4-03e2739bd535","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:compact","toolUseID":"76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\n  \"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n    \"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\",\n    \"additionalContext\": \"<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\\nYou have superpowers.\\n\\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\\n\\n---\\nname: using-superpowers\\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\\n---\\n\\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\\n\\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\\n\\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\\n\\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\\n\\n## The Rule\\n\\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\\n\\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\\n\\nThen announce \\\"Using [skill] to [purpose]\\\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\\n\\n## Skill Priority\\n\\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\\n\\n- \\\"Let's build X\\\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\\n- \\\"Fix this bug\\\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\\n\\n## Red Flags\\n\\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\\n\\n| Thought | Reality |\\n|---------|---------|\\n| \\\"This is just a simple question\\\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"I need more context first\\\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\\n| \\\"Let me explore the codebase first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\\n| \\\"I can check git/files quickly\\\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"Let me gather information first\\\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't need a formal skill\\\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\\n| \\\"I remember this skill\\\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\\n| \\\"This doesn't count as a task\\\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\\n| \\\"The skill is overkill\\\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\\n| \\\"I'll just do this one thing first\\\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\\n| \\\"This feels productive\\\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\\n| \\\"I know what that means\\\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\\n\\n## Platform Adaptation\\n\\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\\n\\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\\n\\n## User Instructions\\n\\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\"\n  }\n}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"\"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/run-hook.cmd\" session-start","durationMs":688},"type":"attachment","uuid":"09ad25cb-3d3b-452e-b83b-a7e9c966dfce","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:27.509Z","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"09ad25cb-3d3b-452e-b83b-a7e9c966dfce","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:compact","toolUseID":"76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\\n# User Profile\\n\\n## About\\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\\n\\n## Preferences\\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\\n\\n## Working Style\\n- Direct; says \\\"stop\\\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\\\"sharpened, not soothed\\\").\\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\\n\\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\\n# Working Memory\\n\\n## Active Threads\\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \\\"pass builder\\\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\\n\\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\\n\\n## Environment Notes\\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\\n\\n## Pending Decisions\\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\\n\\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)\"}}","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading unified cross-harness snapshot...","durationMs":692},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b600d78d-6bf1-4ab0-8537-23e78a3fe107","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:27.511Z","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"b600d78d-6bf1-4ab0-8537-23e78a3fe107","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:compact","toolUseID":"76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).","stdout":"/last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"bash \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/scripts/check-config.sh\"","durationMs":727},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ff42349c-de36-435d-8798-20bbda3030a9","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:27.548Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart:compact hook success: /last30days: Ready to use. Run /last30days to get started — setup takes 30 seconds.\n  Research any topic across Reddit, HN, X, YouTube, Polymarket (last 30 days).\n\nReddit, Hacker News, Polymarket, and YouTube (yt-dlp detected) work out of the box.\nThe setup wizard can unlock X/Twitter and more.\n  Detected: yt-dlp is installed (YouTube transcripts ready, no setup needed).\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"ff42349c-de36-435d-8798-20bbda3030a9","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:compact","toolUseID":"76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"SessionStart\", \"additionalContext\": \"## Today\\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Knowledge Base\\n\\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here \\u2014 read it only when a question actually needs it:\\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \\\"<query>\\\" --top-k 5`\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Recent Daily Log\\n\\n### Session (17:31)\\n\\n**Context:** Continuing the multi-task \\\"harness-agnostic AIOS\\\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) \\u2014 making the skills/backup stack work on both Mac and Windows. Tasks 1\\u20132 complete; paused, then resumed at round 10 of plan review.\\n\\n**Key Exchanges:**\\n- Codex hit its usage limit mid-gate (reset 4:46 PM). User said \\\"use claude, that fine\\\" \\u2192 reviewer switched to Claude via `--host codex`, which starts a fresh review session (resume can't cross providers).\\n- The Claude reviewer found what Codex missed: **14 of 19** outward symlinks in the skills repo are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). Task 4 would have copied all of them into Eric's personal repo.\\n- Reframed the inventory: **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links.**\\n- Round-10 review findings (plan not yet approved): (1) serious \\u2014 the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup: a refused commit is ignored but still logged as \\\"committed\\\", and the blocked link stays staged so every later backup fails silently; (2) minor \\u2014 text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`; (3) minor \\u2014 the Pi test user may inherit the `staff` group, stalling that boundary step.\\n- User said \\\"pause\\\" (during an API ENOTFOUND error), then \\\"resume\\\".\\n\\n**Decisions Made:**\\n- Third-party vendored symlinks (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay **out** of the `claude-skills` repo \\u2014 same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. They were already committed and pushed as dead links on Windows; now explicit rather than silent.\\n- Task 4's inversion script deleted entirely \\u2014 leave the 19 third-party links alone rather than copy them in.\\n- Task 2 prune executed as a **move, not delete** (reversible).\\n\\n**Lessons Learned:**\\n- **`.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` match directories but NOT symlinks** \\u2014 the trailing slash would have silently re-added all four dead links on the next backup run.\\n- Backups must be verified by **actually restoring them** \\u2014 the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X \\u2192 ~/.agents/skills/X \\u2192 ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan \\u2014 the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error \\u2014 the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\\n\\n**Action Items:**\\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` \\u2192 `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3\\u20138.\\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 \\u2192 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\\n\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"Loading knowledge base...","durationMs":738},"type":"attachment","uuid":"2e12371d-e480-4520-b546-4411215b1f3a","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:27.554Z","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"2e12371d-e480-4520-b546-4411215b1f3a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"SessionStart:compact","toolUseID":"76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d","hookEvent":"SessionStart","content":"","stdout":"{\"hookSpecificOutput\":{\"hookEventName\":\"SessionStart\",\"additionalContext\":\"\\n<context_window_protection>\\n  <priority_instructions>\\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\\n  </priority_instructions>\\n\\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \\\"timeline\\\")\\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\\n       - Each command: {label: \\\"section header\\\", command: \\\"shell command\\\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\\\"q1\\\", \\\"q2\\\", ...])\\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\\n\\n  <when_not_to_use>\\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\\n  </when_not_to_use>\\n\\n  <file_writing_policy>\\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\\n  </file_writing_policy>\\n\\n  <output_constraints>\\n    <artifact_policy>\\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\\n    </artifact_policy>\\n  </output_constraints>\\n  <session_continuity>\\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\\n  </session_continuity>\\n\\n  <ctx_commands>\\n    \\\"ctx stats\\\" | \\\"ctx-stats\\\" | \\\"/ctx-stats\\\" | context savings question\\n    → Call stats MCP tool, display full output verbatim.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx doctor\\\" | \\\"ctx-doctor\\\" | \\\"/ctx-doctor\\\" | diagnose context-mode\\n    → Call doctor MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx upgrade\\\" | \\\"ctx-upgrade\\\" | \\\"/ctx-upgrade\\\" | update context-mode\\n    → Call upgrade MCP tool, run returned shell command, display as checklist.\\n\\n    \\\"ctx purge\\\" | \\\"ctx-purge\\\" | \\\"/ctx-purge\\\" | wipe/reset knowledge base\\n    → Call purge MCP tool with confirm: true. Warn: irreversible.\\n\\n    After /clear or /compact: knowledge base preserved. Tell user: \\\"context-mode knowledge base preserved. Use `ctx purge` to start fresh.\\\"\\n  </ctx_commands>\\n\\n</context_window_protection>\\n<session_knowledge source=\\\"compact\\\">\\n<session_guide>\\n## Key Decisions\\n- use claude, that fine\\n\\n## Files Modified\\nhandoff-2026-09-12-harness-skill-audit.md, 2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md, aios-dispositions.md, aios-dispositions-r2.md, aios-dispositions-r3.md, aios-dispositions-r4.md, aios-dispositions-r5.md, project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md, MEMORY.md, aios-dispositions-r6.md, aios-dispositions-r7.md, task4-new.md, r8-fixes.txt, apply-fixes.py, aios-dispositions-r8.md, parse-check.ps1, task4-v3.md, r9-fixes.txt, 2026-09-12-pre-canonical-inventory.txt, aios-dispositions-r9.md, r10-fixes.txt, aios-dispositions-r10.md, r11-fixes.txt, aios-dispositions-r11.md, r12-fixes.txt, aios-dispositions-r12.md, 2026-09-12-gate-a-approval.md\\n\\n## Unresolved Errors\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"=== os-audit SKILL.md ===\\\\n---\\\\nname: os-audit\\\\ndescription: Use when someone asks to run an OS audit, check their AIOS for stale or out...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"# CLI runtime\\\\n\\\\nUse Python 3.10+ and the runner installed at `../scripts/runner.py` relative to this reference. Resolve it from the ski...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"      },\\\\n      {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"R9\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"severity\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"high\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harne...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"status: completed exit: 0 provider: codex roles: {'host': 'claude', 'planner': 'claude', 'reviewer': 'codex', 'builder': 'claude', 'insp...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"status: completed exit: 0\\\\nVERDICT: REVISE\\\\nSUMMARY: The revised architecture resolves the original migration direction and several prio...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"VERDICT: REVISE\\\\nSUMMARY: The supplied SHA256 matches the on-disk plan. Several prior fixes are resolved, but the revision introduces an...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"ok\\\\n--- Task 4 step order now ---\\\\n318:### Task 3: Harden the sync writers — before anything moves\\\\n337:- [ ] **Step 1: Read both curren...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"VERDICT: REVISE\\\\nSUMMARY: The plan hash matches. Task ordering, negative-clause detection, and Mac manifest-target checks are corrected....\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"  OPTION 1 (default) - roll back the sync script too:\\\\n    git -C ~/.claude/skills revert <sync-hardening-sha> && git push\\\\n    # dev-sy...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"VERDICT: REVISE\\\\nSUMMARY: AMEND-01 is incomplete: it bypasses the source guard, fails to ignore the vendored symlinks, and leaves incomp...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"1082:> **15 inverted, not 19.** Of the 19 outward links, four (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) are the v...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"checker updated\\\",\\\"stderr\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"interrupted\\\":false,\\\"isImage\\\":false,\\\"noOutputExpected\\\":false,\\\"bashEditDiff\\\":{\\\"files\\\":[{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/Users/k...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"--- stdout tail ---\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"thread.started\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"thread_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"01a09699-01a4-7581-a91b-b0dd3fc236fe\\\\\\\"}\\\\n{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\",...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"provider: claude roles: {'host': 'codex', 'planner': 'codex', 'reviewer': 'claude', 'builder': 'codex', 'inspector': 'claude'}\\\\nVERDICT:...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"replaced Task 4: old 279 lines -> new 185 lines\\\\n135:### Task 1: Snapshot and freeze the current state\\\\n321:### Task 2: Prune the stale ...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"=== checker ALLOWED / VENDORED / POSITIVE ===\\\\n1159:ALLOWED = {\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"metadata\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"argument-hint\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"allowed-too...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"checker + fixtures ready\\\\n=== fixtures: expect negonly, dupekey, badquote, fragments FAIL; goodone + theirs absent; exit 1 ===\\\\nFAIL bad...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"status: completed provider: claude\\\\nVERDICT: REVISE\\\\nSUMMARY: Most round-8 fixes hold up in this revision. Treating every symlink as thi...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"=== xargs -a occurrences ===\\\\n1121:`--pathspec-from-file` needs git ≥ 2.26 (this Mac has 2.55). It replaces the earlier `xargs -a`, whic...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"runner exit=0\\\\nstatus: completed provider: claude\\\\nVERDICT: REVISE\\\\nSUMMARY: All seven round-9 fixes are present and look correct. The r...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"=== Sync-Repo function ===\\\\n     1\\\\tfunction Sync-Repo($path, $branch) {\\\\n     2\\\\t    $name = Split-Path $path -Leaf\\\\n     3\\\\t    if ($p...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"applied 14 replacements\\\\nextracted: sync-test.sh, test-pre-commit, backup-commit-test.ps1\\\\nOLD backup block matched the real file exactl...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"######## PATCHED daily-backup (expect ALL PASSED, exit 0) ########\\\\n=== T1: a fresh third-party link lands; the backup must REPORT the r...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"######## control exit code, no pipe ########\\\\ncontrol exit=1 (expect 1)\\\\nFAIL lines: 4\\\\n\\\\n######## guard exit code + refused run leaves ...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"runner exit=0\\\\nstatus: completed provider: claude\\\\nVERDICT: REVISE\\\\nSUMMARY: All three round-10 fixes hold. Task 3 Step 5c now checks th...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"applied 11 replacements\\\\nplan parse: all OK (3 shell scripts)\\\\nbash script has gate: True | twin has gate: True\\\\ntwin --error-unmatch le...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"applied 3 replacements\\\\nplan parse: all OK (3 shell scripts)\\\\nvalidation precedes gate: True\\\\nfixtures ready\\\\n######## unprepared machin...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"runner exit=0\\\\nstatus: completed provider: claude plan_sha256: 71b5242ea6dadac667878bc6d4617facbfaa1958a75975762e2d2da40928c432\\\\nVERDICT...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"harness exit=0 (expect 0) - REAL patched daily-backup.ps1 + INSTALLED hook (script defaults)\\\\nLOG: REPO work : COMMIT FAILED\\\\nLOG: REPO ...\\n- {\\\"stdout\\\":\\\"=== graphify skill files (excluding .venv) that name the pool or codex skill paths ===\\\\n(none)\\\\n\\\\n=== installer code inside .venv that w...\\n\\n## Git\\ndocs: harness-agnostic AIOS plan, rounds 1-2 of Codex review folded in\\n\\nRound 1 (REVISE, 6 high) inverted the architecture: ~/.claude/skills is its\\nown git repo and must be canonical; the pool and codex dirs are generated views.\\nRound 2 (REVISE, 2 high) caught that views published only 7 of 61 skills and\\nthat a single rollback tarball would corrupt the canonical repo.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>, docs: fold round 3 Codex review into the AIOS plan\\n\\nRound 3 (REVISE, 2 high) caught two defects introduced by round 2's own\\nfixes: a task-ordering deadlock between the sync guard and the symlink\\ninversion, and a path-traversal rm -rf fed from the writable view manifest.\\nAlso fixes the negative-only frontmatter check (overlap, not containment),\\ngives the PowerShell twin full manifest semantics, and rewrites rollback\\nby migration phase.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>, docs: fold round 4 Codex review into the AIOS plan\\n\\nR4-01 wildcard metacharacters in a manifest line reached PowerShell's\\nwildcard-aware -Path; all manifest-derived ops now use -LiteralPath and\\nglob chars are refused. R4-02 archive failures were suppressed before a\\ndelete and both views shared one archive path; archival must now succeed\\ninto a per-view directory or the run aborts. R4-03 rollback no longer\\nruns a sync that rejects the layout it just restored. R4-04 copy\\nfreshness now hashes contents instead of comparing sizes.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>, docs: fold round 5 Codex review into the AIOS plan\\n\\nR5-01: deleting a canonical skill left its generated view symlink dangling\\n(-e is false for a broken link), while the name was dropped from the\\nownership manifest - permanent untracked debris reporting zero drift.\\nRetirement now tests -e OR -L, archives with cp -RP so a dangling link\\narchives without its target, retains ownership of refused entries, and the\\naudit gains pool_dangling/codex_dangling. Archive dirs are no longer hidden.\\nVerified end to end against the plan's own script.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>, docs: Codex APPROVED the AIOS plan after 6 rounds\\n\\nFindings by round: 10 -> 9 -> 7 -> 4 -> 1 -> 0.\\nHigh severity: 6 -> 2 -> 2 -> 2 -> 0 -> 0.\\nGate A satisfied; Task 1 may begin. Gates B, C, D still apply.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\ngit, push, Task 1 complete: snapshot + AMEND-01 (two-hop vendored chain)\\n\\nVerifying the snapshot found four canonical entries are a two-hop symlink\\nchain into ~/.agents/vendor-review/claudex-loop/, which was outside the\\nVIEWS tarball - the canonical count restored as 57, not 61. Tarball now\\nincludes vendor-review.\\n\\nThose four are also ALREADY committed to erash11/claude-skills as\\nmode-120000 symlinks and pushed, i.e. the R3 breakage the plan exists to\\nprevent, already live and dead on Windows. Plan amended in 6 places to\\nuntrack + gitignore them rather than vendor them into the repo, matching\\nthe existing rule for plugin skills.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\ngit, fix: R7-01..R7-05, one declared vendor inventory everywhere\\n\\nCodex round 7 found AMEND-01 was incomplete:\\n- R7-01 blanket symlink-skip also waved the 15 ordinary outward links past\\n  the R1 guard; now an explicit VENDORED allowlist with target verification.\\n- R7-02 gitignore patterns ended in '/', which matches directories, not\\n  symlinks - git add -A (here and in daily-backup.ps1) would have re-added\\n  all four. Anchored patterns + a check-ignore verification step.\\n- R7-03 PowerShell twin gets the same vendor policy.\\n- R7-04 cron wrapper still read the renamed metric key; every monthly run\\n  would have aborted before writing a row. Added a key-agreement check.\\n- R7-05 counts reconciled: 57 owned / 4 vendored / 58 published. codex-review\\n  removed from the frontmatter edit list.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>, diff, Task 2 complete: prune the stale pool mirror (306 -> 208)\\n\\n98 skills archived yesterday from ~/.claude/skills were still live in\\n~/.agents/skills, the folder Pi auto-discovers. Both guards clean; the\\n~150 third-party installs and _shared untouched. Reversible.\\n\\nAlso fixes three inversion-count expectations Codex surfaced before its\\nusage limit: the guard now FATALs on 15 links not 19, the inverter\\nreports inverted:15 skipped:4, and 4 vendored links legitimately remain.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\ngit, commit, add, fetch, pull, baseline: one file, one tracked link (pre-hook), fix: R9-01..R9-07, reconcile before sync, per-machine exclude, pre-commit hook\\n\\nClaude review round 9:\\n- R9-01 a real sync ran before reconciling memory-write/meeting-sync, whose\\n  pool copies hold edits found nowhere else. Task 4 now reconciles first.\\n- R9-02 the generated ignore list lived in the tracked, shared .gitignore;\\n  each machine would erase the other's list. Now .git/info/exclude.\\n- R9-03 pre-commit hook refuses adding/changing symlinks, allows removal.\\n- R9-04 inventory marks the inversion-era rollback superseded; adds\\n  rollback by task.\\n- R9-05 xargs -a (GNU-only) -> git rm --pathspec-from-file.\\n- R9-06 dangling third-party links now enumerated and excluded.\\n- R9-07 piuser gets its own primary group; not-world-readable was the\\n  wrong test.\\n\\nVerified by running on /bin/bash 3.2: exclude block, RESUME.md kept,\\n.gitignore unchanged, dangling link excluded, idempotent rerun, guard,\\nno-repo path; hook T1-T4; pwsh parse.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\ngit, baseline\\nln, reset, baseline\\nHOME=/tmp/sg5, fix: R10-01..R10-03, backup reports refused commits, sync unstages links\\n\\nClaude review round 10:\\n- R10-01 the pre-commit hook's refusal was invisible: daily-backup.ps1\\n  discarded git commit's exit code and logged 'committed', and a link\\n  already staged by git add -A stayed staged. New Task 3 Step 5c patches\\n  Sync-Repo to check the commit (nothing-stageable is not an error), and\\n  both sync scripts remove third-party links from the index with\\n  git rm --cached, never a reset to HEAD.\\n- R10-02 remaining .gitignore wording now says .git/info/exclude.\\n- R10-03 dseditgroup check added for piuser; staff has no nested groups\\n  on this Mac (premise checked, did not hold).\\n- Task 3 Step 0 warns not to run dev-sync between Task 3 and Task 4 Step 3.\\n\\nVerified by running: harness loads the real Sync-Repo via AST, patched\\nversion reports the refusal and still commits normal changes, unpatched\\ncontrol fails all four T1 checks; sync CASE 3/4 plus prior regressions on\\n/bin/bash 3.2; all embedded scripts parse.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\ngit, base\\nHOME=/tmp/t11/guard, fix: R11-01..R11-02, first-run gate on both sync scripts\\n\\nClaude review round 11 (0 high):\\n- R11-01 Windows dev-sync would run the rewritten twin right after its next\\n  pull and overwrite all 43 published entries with no snapshot or reconcile.\\n  Both scripts now refuse any real run until the per-machine marker\\n  ~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready exists; dry runs are always allowed. On the Mac\\n  the marker is created only at the end of Task 4 Step 2, so Task 3's\\n  'do not run dev-sync' warning is now enforced by code. Windows views\\n  pause visibly until a dedicated prep session.\\n- R11-02 the twin's index probe no longer writes native stderr, which\\n  Windows PowerShell 5.1 treats as fatal under ErrorActionPreference=Stop.\\n\\nVerified by running on /bin/bash 3.2: CASE 5 gate (dry 0, real 2, pool-only\\nedit survives, no manifest), CASE 1-4, guard, refused-run index unchanged,\\ndeletion workflow; pwsh parse of the twin.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\ngit, fix: R12-01, refuse any DRY_RUN value other than 0 or 1\\n\\nClaude review round 12 APPROVED with one low finding, fixed because it is a\\ndata-loss route: the bash sync tested DRY against \\\"0\\\" in five places and\\n\\\"1\\\" in two, so DRY_RUN=true skipped the first-run gate yet still ran the\\npublish loop's rm -rf. DRY is now validated once, before the gate.\\n\\nVerified: true/yes/2 refuse with exit 2 and leave the drifted pool copy\\nintact; 1 stays a dry run; 0 still publishes on a prepared machine.\\nControl run of the pre-fix script (5779c56) with DRY_RUN=true exited 0,\\nprinted no gate message and replaced the pool copy with a link.\\n\\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\\ngit, baseline\\ncp\\n\\n## Project Rules\\n.claude/CLAUDE.md, DEV/CLAUDE.md, memory/project_skill-bloat-and-promotion.md\\n\\n## Skills Used\\nsuperpowers:writing-plans, codex-review, memory-write\\n\\n## Environment\\ncwd: /tmp/hooktest\\nmodel:claude-opus-5[1m]\\n\\n## Data References\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:0\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:47\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:0\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:0\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:0\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:47\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:0\\n- interrupted:false stderrBytes:0\\n- … 47 older captures kept in the sandbox — query mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(source: \\\"session-events\\\").\\n\\n## Session Intent\\nimplement\\n\\n</session_guide>\\n<session_search>\\nDetailed session data is indexed in context-mode FTS5 (source: \\\"session-events\\\").\\nUse mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [...], source: \\\"session-events\\\") when you need specifics.\\nDo NOT call ctx_index() — data is already indexed.\\n</session_search>\\n</session_knowledge>\\n\\n<session_state source=\\\"compaction\\\">\\n\\n<rules>\\nFollow these decisions:\\n- use claude, that fine\\n</rules>\\n\\n<active_skills>\\nRe-invoke if relevant: superpowers:writing-plans, codex-review, memory-write\\nTo reload: call the Skill tool with the skill name.\\n</active_skills>\\n\\n<session_mode>implement</session_mode>\\n\\n</session_state>\"}}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/sessionstart.mjs\"","durationMs":772},"type":"attachment","uuid":"56461bea-5d1d-48cd-b40a-39e9641db8b7","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:27.592Z","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"56461bea-5d1d-48cd-b40a-39e9641db8b7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>","## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)","## Today\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n### Session (17:31)\n\n**Context:** Continuing the multi-task \"harness-agnostic AIOS\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) — making the skills/backup stack work on both Mac and Windows. Tasks 1–2 complete; paused, then resumed at round 10 of plan review.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Codex hit its usage limit mid-gate (reset 4:46 PM). User said \"use claude, that fine\" → reviewer switched to Claude via `--host codex`, which starts a fresh review session (resume can't cross providers).\n- The Claude reviewer found what Codex missed: **14 of 19** outward symlinks in the skills repo are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). Task 4 would have copied all of them into Eric's personal repo.\n- Reframed the inventory: **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links.**\n- Round-10 review findings (plan not yet approved): (1) serious — the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup: a refused commit is ignored but still logged as \"committed\", and the blocked link stays staged so every later backup fails silently; (2) minor — text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`; (3) minor — the Pi test user may inherit the `staff` group, stalling that boundary step.\n- User said \"pause\" (during an API ENOTFOUND error), then \"resume\".\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Third-party vendored symlinks (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay **out** of the `claude-skills` repo — same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. They were already committed and pushed as dead links on Windows; now explicit rather than silent.\n- Task 4's inversion script deleted entirely — leave the 19 third-party links alone rather than copy them in.\n- Task 2 prune executed as a **move, not delete** (reversible).\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **`.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` match directories but NOT symlinks** — the trailing slash would have silently re-added all four dead links on the next backup run.\n- Backups must be verified by **actually restoring them** — the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X → ~/.agents/skills/X → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n","<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (19.3KB). Full output saved to: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649/tool-results/hook-76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d-9-additionalContext.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n...\n</persisted-output>"],"hookName":"SessionStart","toolUseID":"SessionStart","hookEvent":"SessionStart"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"5b49ae87-58c7-4bf7-a57d-303e1cd932cc","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:32:27.599Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nSessionStart hook additional context: <EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\nYou have superpowers.\n\n**Below is the full content of your 'superpowers:using-superpowers' skill - your introduction to using skills. For all other skills, use the 'Skill' tool:**\n\n---\nname: using-superpowers\ndescription: Use when starting any conversation - establishes how to find and use skills, requiring skill invocation before ANY response including clarifying questions\n---\n\n<SUBAGENT-STOP>\nIf you were dispatched as a subagent to execute a specific task, ignore this skill.\n</SUBAGENT-STOP>\n\n<EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\nIf you think there is even a 1% chance a skill might apply to what you are doing, you ABSOLUTELY MUST invoke the skill.\n\nIF A SKILL APPLIES TO YOUR TASK, YOU DO NOT HAVE A CHOICE. YOU MUST USE IT.\n\nThis is not negotiable. You cannot rationalize your way out of this.\n</EXTREMELY-IMPORTANT>\n\n## The Rule\n\n**Invoke relevant or requested skills BEFORE any response or action** — including clarifying questions, exploring the codebase, or checking files. If it turns out wrong for the situation, you don't have to use it.\n\n**Before entering plan mode:** if you haven't already brainstormed, invoke the brainstorming skill first.\n\nThen announce \"Using [skill] to [purpose]\" and follow the skill exactly. If it has a checklist, create a todo per item.\n\n## Skill Priority\n\nWhen multiple skills apply, process skills come first — they set the approach, then implementation skills (frontend-design, etc.) carry it out. Brainstorming and systematic-debugging are Superpowers' most common process skills, but the rule holds for any of them.\n\n- \"Let's build X\" → superpowers:brainstorming first, then implementation skills.\n- \"Fix this bug\" → superpowers:systematic-debugging first, then domain skills.\n\n## Red Flags\n\nThese thoughts mean STOP—you're rationalizing:\n\n| Thought | Reality |\n|---------|---------|\n| \"This is just a simple question\" | Questions are tasks. Check for skills. |\n| \"I need more context first\" | Skill check comes BEFORE clarifying questions. |\n| \"Let me explore the codebase first\" | Skills tell you HOW to explore. Check first. |\n| \"I can check git/files quickly\" | Files lack conversation context. Check for skills. |\n| \"Let me gather information first\" | Skills tell you HOW to gather information. |\n| \"This doesn't need a formal skill\" | If a skill exists, use it. |\n| \"I remember this skill\" | Skills evolve. Read current version. |\n| \"This doesn't count as a task\" | Action = task. Check for skills. |\n| \"The skill is overkill\" | Simple things become complex. Use it. |\n| \"I'll just do this one thing first\" | Check BEFORE doing anything. |\n| \"This feels productive\" | Undisciplined action wastes time. Skills prevent this. |\n| \"I know what that means\" | Knowing the concept ≠ using the skill. Invoke it. |\n\n## Platform Adaptation\n\nIf your harness appears here, read its reference file for special instructions:\n\n- Codex: `references/codex-tools.md`\n- Pi: `references/pi-tools.md`\n- Antigravity: `references/antigravity-tools.md`\n- Hermes Agent: `references/hermes-tools.md`\n\n## User Instructions\n\nUser instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md, etc, direct requests) take precedence over skills, which in turn override default behavior. Only skip skill workflows or instructions when your human partner has explicitly told you to.\n</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>\n## User profile (global-memory/USER.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 1,375 chars. Updated when the agent learns durable user preferences. -->\n# User Profile\n\n## About\nEric Rash — Director of Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Builds athlete performance data pipelines (Football GPS, force plate). Family: wife Kristina; kids Ames, Saylor, Rowan (5, 4, 10mo as of 2026-09-01). Career target: analytics-forward player-coach near Iowa, ~$90K+ floor, ~mid-2027. Deeper context: `~/DEV/ContextVault` — read before career/personal advice.\n\n## Preferences\n- Python + R. **macOS since 2026-08-05**; Windows 11 box still ACTIVE (synced 9/9). Tool paths: see CLAUDE.md.\n- Local SQLite/DuckDB; no cloud/servers/schedulers without Baylor IT approval. OneDrive project folders — beware DuckDB locks.\n- Tangible working examples over documentation.\n\n## Working Style\n- Direct; says \"stop\" when done. Wants to be challenged, not affirmed — push on vague or rehearsed reasoning (\"sharpened, not soothed\").\n- Defers technical micro-decisions when convention exists — decide, state it, move on.\n- Builds incrementally. Nothing that breaks mid-season.\n- Authors own direction when leadership doesn't; OKR-driven change agent in a status-quo org.\n- Right question over downstream craft; Christian faith is his organizing frame.\n\n---\n\n## Working memory (global-memory/MEMORY.md)\n\n<!-- Cap: 2,500 chars. Working scratchpad; writes take effect next session. -->\n# Working Memory\n\n## Active Threads\n- **Personal site (9/2)**: `DEV/Website/scrollcraft/builds/eric-rash/`, pushed 9/9. NEXT: real-phone check; host or keep local. →Tier A personal-site-build-routes.\n- **Baseball profiles (9/2)**: `DEV/Baseball_PerformanceProfiles`, Excel-only coach. 3 .xlsx mock-data prototypes. NEXT: coach picks one + real tests.\n- **RTP CLA course (9/10)**: ingest + PDF rebuild DONE. NEXT: import .ris → Zotero; lesson 0002. →Tier A rtp-cla-course.\n- **ATH Tech framework (8/14)**: shipped. NEXT: Micah mtg (need ITS intake path); inventory refresh due 9/29. →Tier A ath-tech-framework, tech-inventory-refresh.\n- **Kinexon volleyball (8/1)**: API email→CSM, awaiting docs. Plan: sync→SQLite→dashboards; CSV interim.\n- **Status report (9/7)**: #2 drafted, UNSENT — `SecondBrain/Inbox/status-reports/2026-09-07-email.txt`. NEXT: read-check, send, rename `-email-sent.txt`, flip `sent: true`. #3 = 9/21. →Tier A supervisor-log-pipeline.\n- **Acro & Tumbling (9/8)**: owe an Excel \"pass builder\" — routines → cumulative impact load. 5-athlete pilot. Awaiting skill inventory + assignments.\n- **Open PRs (9/7)**: AgentAssist #1 (draft), CFB_Analytics_Lab #1. Deferred: FA26 camp regex.\n- **OKR/JD 2026 (7/17)**: Blake LEFT, AJ owns FB. →vault `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`.\n- Parked in Tier A: Fireflies key rotation; child dental.\n\n- **Skill audit (9/12)**: DONE. 160→61 global skills, 19.2k→6.4k tok/session. Plugins beat local copies. →Tier A skill-bloat-and-promotion\n\n## Environment Notes\n- **Portfolio dashboard** LIVE; `/project` re-renders+republishes. →Tier A command-centre, portfolio-registry-write-provenance.\n- **Terminal (9/4)**: tmux = default VS Code shell; `cc`/`cx` aliases. →vault `Knowledge/tooling/tmux-cheat-sheet.md`.\n- Hooks exist in TWO copies (`.claude/hooks/`, `.codex/hooks/`) — patch both. →Tier A hook-two-copies-trap.\n- `~/DEV` = THIN repo (`erash11/DEV`), 8 router `.md` only; `.gitignore` starts `/*` — never remove. `_ARCHIVE/` = parked. →Tier A github-consolidation.\n- Schedules/gws limits →automation-cadence. CFB capture (Mon, 8/24–1/23) →cfb-forecast-capture.\n\n## Pending Decisions\n- **DUE (was 8/31)**: OKR Q1 gate: AI integration (buy-in) + data governance (Anthony's proposal).\n- **Kitman vs MyLink**: MyLink intros (~half Kitman's cost) + buy Kitman API access. Undecided; blocks priority 3.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-harness activity (KeystoneOS unified snapshot)\n\nNot loaded here — read it only if a question needs it.\nPath: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/outputs/dashboard/unified-snapshot.md` (5670 chars, updated 2026-09-12 13:35)\n## Today\nSaturday, September 12, 2026\n\n---\n\n## Knowledge Base\n\n260 compiled articles (195 concepts, 64 connections, 0 qa). The index is NOT loaded here — read it only when a question actually needs it:\n- Full index (article, summary, sources, updated): `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/index.md`\n- Article bodies: `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/<folder>/<name>.md`\n- Search across all memory instead of reading the index: `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`\n\n---\n\n## Recent Daily Log\n\n### Session (17:31)\n\n**Context:** Continuing the multi-task \"harness-agnostic AIOS\" plan (`~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md`, pushed as `00730b6`) — making the skills/backup stack work on both Mac and Windows. Tasks 1–2 complete; paused, then resumed at round 10 of plan review.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Codex hit its usage limit mid-gate (reset 4:46 PM). User said \"use claude, that fine\" → reviewer switched to Claude via `--host codex`, which starts a fresh review session (resume can't cross providers).\n- The Claude reviewer found what Codex missed: **14 of 19** outward symlinks in the skills repo are third-party package-manager installs (including 5 `mattpocock-skills` explicitly out of scope). Task 4 would have copied all of them into Eric's personal repo.\n- Reframed the inventory: **42 skills Eric wrote, 19 third-party links.**\n- Round-10 review findings (plan not yet approved): (1) serious — the new safety hook silently breaks the daily backup: a refused commit is ignored but still logged as \"committed\", and the blocked link stays staged so every later backup fails silently; (2) minor — text still says `.gitignore` where it should say `.git/info/exclude`; (3) minor — the Pi test user may inherit the `staff` group, stalling that boundary step.\n- User said \"pause\" (during an API ENOTFOUND error), then \"resume\".\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Third-party vendored symlinks (`claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review`) stay **out** of the `claude-skills` repo — same rule as plugins. Windows needs a separate install of that package. They were already committed and pushed as dead links on Windows; now explicit rather than silent.\n- Task 4's inversion script deleted entirely — leave the 19 third-party links alone rather than copy them in.\n- Task 2 prune executed as a **move, not delete** (reversible).\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- **`.gitignore` patterns ending in `/` match directories but NOT symlinks** — the trailing slash would have silently re-added all four dead links on the next backup run.\n- Backups must be verified by **actually restoring them** — the restore returned 57/61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain (`~/.claude/skills/X → ~/.agents/skills/X → ~/.agents/vendor-review/...`) into an unbacked-up vendor folder.\n- Re-run the review gate after amending a plan — the prior approval is stale. Codex found 5 new bugs in the amendment itself.\n- `dcg` blocks `mv` with variable paths; Python with explicit containment checks is the workaround.\n- Directory counts can mismatch because `ls` omits hidden files (209 vs 208 was a hidden `.gitignore`).\n- A second reviewer with a different provider catches different classes of error — the provider switch wasn't just a fallback, it found the plan's biggest error.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Fix the three round-10 findings: make the backup report a failed commit, have the sync unstage third-party links, correct `.gitignore` → `.git/info/exclude` wording, handle the `staff`-group inheritance in the Pi boundary step.\n- Re-run the review gate, then start Tasks 3–8.\n- Windows: separately install the vendored package that provides `claudex-loop`, `claudex-route`, `codex-build`, `codex-review` (currently dead symlinks there).\n- Task 2 result to record: global skills pool pruned 306 → 208; 61 skills still resolve; ~150 third-party installs untouched.\n\n<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (19.3KB). Full output saved to: /Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649/tool-results/hook-76dc9974-bc44-4b6d-afb5-de96d817068d-9-additionalContext.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n...\n</persisted-output>\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"5b49ae87-58c7-4bf7-a57d-303e1cd932cc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# Machine\n\nMachine-specific shell, paths, and traps live in `~/.claude/machine.md` (gitignored; copy `machine.macos.md` or `machine.windows.md` to `machine.md` on a new machine). It is imported below.\n\n@machine.md\n\nBoth machines are active as of 2026-09-10: the MacBook (`~/DEV`) and the Baylor Windows laptop (`~/Desktop/DEV`). Projects sync through GitHub (`erash11`), documents through Google Drive. Every project repo carries a Session routine in its `AGENTS.md`: `git pull` + read `docs/STATUS.md` at start; update `STATUS.md`, commit, `git push` at end.\n# Workflow Expectations\n\n- When asked to **create, merge, or consolidate an artifact** (calendar, deck, doc), the expected deliverable is always a produced file. Verify what exists first, then create the artifact regardless — never conclude with \"it already exists\" instead of delivering the file.\n- When the user types `/graphify`, invoke the Skill tool with `skill: \"graphify\"` before doing anything else.\n\n# Session Startup\n\nHandled by the `keystone-unified-snapshot.js` SessionStart hook — do not read these files by hand. It injects `global-memory/USER.md`, `global-memory/MEMORY.md`, `transcripts/.errors.log` (only when non-empty), and a pointer to the KeystoneOS snapshot.\n\n- If `.errors.log` content appears, surface it once on the first response, then continue. It is normally empty; entries mean hook failures.\n- If a **\"SessionStart hook could not read a required memory file\"** block appears, say so on the first response instead of working around it.\n- **If no `## User profile` block is in context at session start, the hook did not fire.** Say so, then read `USER.md`, `MEMORY.md` and `transcripts/.errors.log` by hand.\n- What the hook injects is a frozen snapshot. Mid-session writes take effect next session. Never load more than this proactively.\n\n# Memory — routing only\n\nThe two-tier memory system (`~/.claude/global-memory/` and `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`) is owned by the **`memory-write` skill**, which holds the tier rules, character caps, slug rules, write steps, retrieval ladder, and daily-compiled-log rules.\n\nThe active project's Tier A memory (`~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/`, indexed by its `MEMORY.md`) is **auto-injected by the harness** each session. Tier A files are also indexed by memsearch, so they stay recallable across projects.\n\n- Writing a fact — \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"save this\", \"forget about\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`\n- Recalling past context — \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X\" → `Skill(\"memory-write\")`, retrieval-ladder section\n- Daily compiled logs at `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/daily/` are owned by `claude-memory-compiler`. Never hand-edit them; never read them eagerly.\n- **Slugs differ per machine** (Mac `-Users-kristinarash-DEV-<X>`, Windows `C--Users-eric-rash-Desktop-DEV-<X>`; both active as of 2026-09-10). Tier A written on one is not auto-injected on the other; memsearch still finds it. Durable project facts belong in the repo's `docs/` so both machines see them.\n\nDo not restate the memory procedure in this file. It has one home, and that home is the skill.\n\n---\n\n`AGENTS.md` is the sibling of this file for harnesses that have no Skill tool (Codex and similar). It intentionally carries the memory rules as plain prose instead of routing to a skill. **The two files are not meant to be identical — do not \"fix\" the difference by symlinking them.**"},{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/machine.md","type":"User","content":"# Shell / Environment (macOS)\n\n- zsh/bash is the native surface on this machine. PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, `/opt/homebrew/bin/pwsh`) is installed only to run the KeystoneOS `.ps1` jobs natively.\n- **The tool-path list has one home: `~/.claude/TOOLPATHS.md`.** Read that file when you need a path. Only the traps below are kept, because acting on them wrongly is expensive.\n- **Verify, don't trust any list.** `which -a <tool>` is authoritative. To read PATH honestly, scrub the environment first:\n  `env -i HOME=\"$HOME\" USER=\"$USER\" TERM=xterm SHELL=/bin/zsh /bin/zsh -lic 'echo $PATH'`\n- Traps:\n  - Python is PEP 668-managed. Use venvs or `uv`, never `pip install` globally.\n  - `uv` is a standalone install at `~/.local/bin/uv`, **not** Homebrew. Claude Code hooks call `$HOME/.local/bin/uv`; do not delete or relocate it.\n  - `timeout` is NOT installed (no GNU coreutils). Don't use it in scripts.\n  - Homebrew owns `/opt/homebrew/bin`. `/usr/local/bin` is empty and stays that way.\n  - Secrets live in the macOS Keychain, not in `~/.zshrc`.\n- DEV projects live at `~/DEV` (not `~/Desktop/DEV`). Workspace map: `~/DEV/CLAUDE.md`.\n- Primary storage drive (documents, data, deliverables): `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive`."},{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# ~/DEV — workspace map\n\nEric Rash — Applied Performance, Baylor Athletics. Sport science and\nfootball analytics, plus the agent system that runs them.\n\nThis file is a **map, not a rulebook**. Behavior rules live in\n`~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. Read only the index you need.\n\n## Areas\n\n| Area | Index | What lives there |\n|---|---|---|\n| Football | `FOOTBALL.md` | Game film, player analytics, CFB prediction. ~39k files — about two thirds of DEV. |\n| Performance | `PERFORMANCE.md` | Athlete monitoring, testing, load, risk. GPS, force plate, VALD, DEXA, S2. |\n| Research | `RESEARCH.md` | Literature, Zotero, book library, reading pipelines. |\n| Platform | `PLATFORM.md` | KeystoneOS, dashboards, CLIs, scheduled jobs. |\n| Program | `PROGRAM.md` | Staff reporting, curriculum, proposals, vetting, brand. |\n| Personal | `PERSONAL.md` | Side projects. Not Baylor work. |\n| Commands | `COMMANDS.md` | Desk reference: every report/project command Eric can run himself. |\n\n## Elsewhere on disk\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/` | Parked projects. Not a git repo, so the consolidation sweep skips it. Has its own `README.md`. |\n| `~/DEV/_INBOX/` | Loose files from the `~/DEV` root. Own repo. |\n| `~/SecondBrain/` | Obsidian vault, ~850 notes. Own `CLAUDE.md`. |\n| `~/.claude/skills/` | ~155 skills |\n| `~/go/bin/` | 8 custom CLIs built with `cli-printing-press` |\n| `~/.claude/claude-memory-compiler/knowledge/` | ~230 compiled articles |\n\n## Layout rules\n\nFull rules — grouping depth, the depth-2 discovery limit, and the gitignored-subtree\ntrap — live in project memory: `project_dev-layout-and-discovery.md`.\n\n- 23 projects carry their own `CLAUDE.md`. Read it before working in one.\n\n## Handling care\n\n- `DexaProject/` holds identifiable health data. It now lives on Google Drive at\n  `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/DexaProject`,\n  not in `~/DEV`. See `PERFORMANCE.md`.\n  Never paste its contents into chat, an artifact, or an external service.\n\n## Keeping this true\n\nWhen a file moves or a new project starts, **update the router in the same\nturn**. A stale pointer is worse than no pointer.\n\nThe counts above are approximate on purpose — they convey scale, not state, and\na `~` keeps ordinary growth from making this file wrong. Re-measure them only\nwhen one is off by enough to change a decision. Counts last checked 2026-09-05.\n\nMemory files named in these indexes (`project_*.md`, `user_*.md`) live at\n`~/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/`. They auto-load only when\ncwd is `~/DEV` itself — from inside a sub-project, open them by path.\n\nSearch everything with `memsearch search \"<query>\" --top-k 5`."},{"path":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/projects/-Users-kristinarash-DEV/memory/MEMORY.md","type":"AutoMem","content":"# Memory Index\n\nOne line per memory: title, file, and a hook that says when to open it.\nNever put memory content here — the file itself holds the facts.\n\n- [Printing Press spec traps](project_printing-press-spec-traps.md) — writing a CLI spec: pagination, envelope fields, fixtures, doc regen\n- [zsh shell traps](project_zsh-shell-traps.md) — reserved vars, word-splitting, pipeline exit codes\n- [Status report sourcing](project_status-report-sourcing.md) — which source counts as \"Shipped\", which OKR doc is live\n- [Fireflies CLI on Mac](project_fireflies-cli-broken-on-mac.md) — \"out of memory (14)\", getting transcript text, renames that don't stick\n- [gws Google Forms API](project_gws-forms-api.md) — scopes, create vs batchUpdate, duplicated items\n- [Kenny Boyd doc review](project_kenny-boyd-doc-review-preferences.md) — tone and labelling rules for review docs\n- [Terminal Fluency workspace](project_terminal-fluency-workspace.md) — resume protocol and how he learns CLI\n- [PATH shadowing and cleanup](project_path-shadowing-and-cleanup.md) — measuring PATH honestly; before deleting any binary\n- [Backup/sync system](project_backup-sync-system.md) — manifest routing, verify-by-receipt, schedule history\n- [CFB Analytics Lab](project_cfb-analytics-lab.md) — resume protocol and CFBD API traps\n- [CFB forecast capture](project_cfb-forecast-capture.md) — Big 12 weekly freeze; one wrapper command; is a gap really a missed week?\n- [Drive FUSE rsync loop](project_drive-fuse-rsync-mtime-loop.md) — rsync re-sending identical files to Google Drive\n- [SecondBrain vault location](project_secondbrain-vault-location.md) — current path and what hardcodes it\n- [Milvus lock misdiagnosis](project_milvus-lock-misdiagnosis.md) — Zotero/Milvus errors; diagnose before rebuilding\n- [Memory compiler pipeline](project_memory-compiler-pipeline.md) — dead-flush tells, backfilling a date, \"Indexed 0 chunks\"\n- [memsearch index path drift](project_memsearch-index-path-drift.md) — silent f