{"parentUuid":"ad1dd60d-a1f7-4853-b9f0-dc5fac06bbce","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GCd5Lk5oyeYDF3j1QG1BX1","type":"tool_result","content":"1546\t### Task 5: Normalize every skill to portable frontmatter\n1547\t\n1548\t**Files:**\n1549\t- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`\n1550\t- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` (frontmatter only, body untouched)\n1551\t- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md`\n1552\t\n1553\t**Interfaces:**\n1554\t- Consumes: the symlink-free canonical repo from Task 4.\n1555\t- Produces: `skill-portability-check.py`. `aios-audit` calls it as its harness-parity check. Exit `0` when every skill passes, `1` otherwise, `2` on an empty or missing inventory.\n1556\t\n1557\t**The portable frontmatter contract** (a superset — Codex ignores keys it does not know, Claude Code ignores `metadata`):\n1558\t\n1559\t```yaml\n1560\t---\n1561\tname: skill-name                       # REQUIRED. Must equal the directory name.\n1562\t                                       # TitleCase is allowed (CreateSkill, Harvest).\n1563\tdescription: >-                        # REQUIRED. <=1024 chars. Must contain BOTH:\n1564\t  <what it does, one sentence>.        #   (a) a positive clause: \"Use when ...\" with\n1565\t  Use when <literal trigger phrases>.  #       literal phrases the user would type\n1566\t  Do not use when <negative>.          #   (b) a negative clause: \"Do not use ...\" /\n1567\t                                       #       \"NOT FOR ...\" - Codex needs this to\n1568\t                                       #       avoid over-firing\n1569\tmetadata:\n1570\t  short-description: <one short line>  # optional, Codex-only, Claude ignores\n1571\targument-hint: \"[...]\"                 # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\n1572\tallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep        # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\n1573\t---\n1574\t```\n1575\t\n1576\tClaude Code triggers on literal phrases; Codex leans on the negative clause. A description with only one half fires reliably in one harness and badly in the other. **Naming is settled here (R7):** the rule is `name` equals the directory name. TitleCase is legal — `CreateSkill` and `Harvest` already ship that way and renaming them would break every cross-reference.\n1577\t\n1578\t- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing check**\n1579\t\n1580\tCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`:\n1581\t\n1582\t```python\n1583\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n1584\t\"\"\"Check every skill in the canonical repo against the portable frontmatter\n1585\tcontract. Exit 0 = all pass, 1 = failures, 2 = empty/missing inventory.\n1586\tstdlib only (PEP 668 machine). Parses the flat YAML subset we actually use.\"\"\"\n1587\timport os, re, sys, glob\n1588\t\n1589\tCANON = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.claude/skills\")\n1590\t# R8-03: every key the roster actually uses today, measured with\n1591\t#   grep '^[a-z-]*:' ~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md | sort | uniq -c\n1592\t# The contract may not outlaw keys Claude Code honours. `effort` and\n1593\t# `user-invocable` are live Claude Code keys; `repository` and `homepage`\n1594\t# are package metadata. Genuinely unknown keys are still reported.\n1595\tALLOWED = {\"name\", \"description\", \"metadata\", \"argument-hint\", \"allowed-tools\",\n1596\t           \"model\", \"disable-model-invocation\", \"license\", \"version\", \"author\",\n1597\t           \"effort\", \"user-invocable\", \"repository\", \"homepage\"}\n1598\tPOSITIVE = (\"use when\", \"use for\", \"use this skill when\", \"invoke when\")\n1599\tNEGATIVE = (\"do not use\", \"don't use\", \"not for\", \"never use\")\n1600\t\n1601\tdef frontmatter(text):\n1602\t    if not text.startswith(\"---\"):\n1603\t        return None\n1604\t    end = text.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n1605\t    return None if end == -1 else text[4:end]\n1606\t\n1607\tdef top_keys(fm):\n1608\t    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)\\s*:\", fm)]\n1609\t\n1610\tdef scalar(fm, key):\n1611\t    \"\"\"Read a flat scalar, including >- and | block styles.\"\"\"\n1612\t    m = re.search(rf\"(?ms)^{re.escape(key)}\\s*:[ \\t]*(.*?)(?=^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*\\s*:|\\Z)\", fm)\n1613\t    if not m:\n1614\t        return \"\"\n1615\t    raw = m.group(1)\n1616\t    raw = re.sub(r\"^[>|][-+]?\\s*\\n\", \"\", raw)          # strip a block indicator\n1617\t    val = \" \".join(l.strip() for l in raw.splitlines() if l.strip())\n1618\t    return val.strip().strip('\"').strip(\"'\")\n1619\t\n1620\tdef check(path):\n1621\t    name = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path))\n1622\t    problems = []\n1623\t    fm = frontmatter(open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read())\n1624\t    if fm is None:\n1625\t        return name, [\"no YAML frontmatter\"]\n1626\t    keys = top_keys(fm)\n1627\t    declared = scalar(fm, \"name\")\n1628\t    if \"name\" not in keys:\n1629\t        problems.append(\"missing 'name'\")\n1630\t    elif declared != name:\n1631\t        problems.append(f\"name '{declared}' != directory '{name}'\")\n1632\t    desc = scalar(fm, \"description\")\n1633\t    if not desc:\n1634\t        problems.append(\"missing 'description'\")\n1635\t    else:\n1636\t        low = desc.lower()\n1637\t        if len(desc) > 1024:\n1638\t            problems.append(f\"description {len(desc)} chars (>1024)\")\n1639\t        problems.extend(clause_problems(low))\n1640\t    # R2-05: reject syntax outside the supported subset rather than\n1641\t    # silently accepting it. Duplicate keys and unbalanced quotes both\n1642\t    # parse \"successfully\" with a regex reader and mean something else\n1643\t    # to a real YAML loader.\n1644\t    for k in keys:\n1645\t        if k not in ALLOWED:\n1646\t            problems.append(f\"unknown frontmatter key '{k}'\")\n1647\t    dupes = {k for k in keys if keys.count(k) > 1}\n1648\t    for k in sorted(dupes):\n1649\t        problems.append(f\"duplicate frontmatter key '{k}' - unsupported\")\n1650\t    for key in (\"name\", \"description\"):\n1651\t        raw = raw_scalar(fm, key)\n1652\t        if raw and raw[:1] in (\"'\", '\"') and not raw.endswith(raw[0]):\n1653\t            problems.append(f\"'{key}' has an unbalanced quote\")\n1654\t    return name, problems\n1655\t\n1656\tdef raw_scalar(fm, key):\n1657\t    m = re.search(rf\"(?m)^{re.escape(key)}\\s*:[ \\t]*(.*)$\", fm)\n1658\t    return m.group(1).strip() if m else \"\"\n1659\t\n1660\tdef clause_problems(low):\n1661\t    \"\"\"A positive phrase governed by a negative phrase does not count as a\n1662\t    trigger. 'do not use when auditing' must NOT satisfy the positive check.\n1663\t\n1664\t    R3-04: containment was the wrong test. 'do not use' spans [0,10) and\n1665\t    'use when' spans [7,15) - the positive is not CONTAINED, it OVERLAPS.\n1666\t    A negative span is therefore extended through any immediately following\n1667\t    'when'/'for' so it governs the whole construction, and any positive match\n1668\t    OVERLAPPING a negative span is discarded.\"\"\"\n1669\t    out = []\n1670\t\n1671\t    # Build negative spans, extended through a trailing 'when'/'for' so that\n1672\t    # 'do not use when' is one governing construction, not 'do not use' + a\n1673\t    # stray 'use when' the positive check could claim.\n1674\t    neg_spans = []\n1675\t    for n in NEGATIVE:\n1676\t        # R8-10: whole words only. A bare substring match let \"not formatted\"\n1677\t        # count as \"not for\" and \"because formatting\" as \"use for\".\n1678\t        for m in re.finditer(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(n) + r\"\\b\", low):\n1679\t            start, end = m.start(), m.end()\n1680\t            for word in (\" when\", \" for\"):\n1681\t                w_end = end + len(word)\n1682\t                if low.startswith(word, end) and (w_end == len(low) or not low[w_end].isalnum()):\n1683\t                    end = w_end\n1684\t                    break\n1685\t            neg_spans.append((start, end))\n1686\t\n1687\t    def overlaps_negative(i, j):\n1688\t        return any(i < b and a < j for a, b in neg_spans)   # interval overlap\n1689\t\n1690\t    pos_found = False\n1691\t    for p in POSITIVE:\n1692\t        for m in re.finditer(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(p) + r\"\\b\", low):\n1693\t            if not overlaps_negative(m.start(), m.end()):\n1694\t                pos_found = True\n1695\t                break\n1696\t        if pos_found:\n1697\t            break\n1698\t\n1699\t    if not pos_found:\n1700\t        out.append(\"no standalone positive clause ('Use when ...') - Claude will \"\n1701\t                   \"not trigger (a 'use when' governed by 'do not use when' does not count)\")\n1702\t    if not neg_spans:\n1703\t        out.append(\"no negative clause ('Do not use ...') - Codex over-fires\")\n1704\t    return out\n1705\t\n1706\tdef main():\n1707\t    if not os.path.isdir(CANON):\n1708\t        print(f\"FATAL canonical dir missing: {CANON}\", file=sys.stderr)\n1709\t        return 2\n1710\t    # R8-01: check only OWNED skills - real directories. A symlinked skill was\n1711\t    # installed by the skills CLI or vendored; its frontmatter belongs to a\n1712\t    # third party, and demanding our contract of it makes \"0 failing\"\n1713\t    # unreachable. Ownership is structural, so no name list is kept.\n1714\t    paths = [p for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(CANON, \"*\", \"SKILL.md\")))\n1715\t             if not os.path.islink(os.path.dirname(p))]\n1716\t    if not paths:\n1717\t        print(f\"FATAL no SKILL.md found under {CANON} - refusing to report success\",\n1718\t              file=sys.stderr)\n1719\t        return 2\n1720\t    failed = 0\n1721\t    for p in paths:\n1722\t        name, probs = check(p)\n1723\t        if probs:\n1724\t            failed += 1\n1725\t            print(f\"FAIL {name}\")\n1726\t            for x in probs:\n1727\t                print(f\"       - {x}\")\n1728\t    print(f\"\\nchecked {len(paths)} skills, {failed} failing, {len(paths)-failed} portable\")\n1729\t    return 1 if failed else 0\n1730\t\n1731\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n1732\t    sys.exit(main())\n1733\t```\n1734\t\n1735\t```bash\n1736\tchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\n1737\t```\n1738\t\n1739\t- [ ] **Step 2: Prove the checker's own failure modes first (R7)**\n1740\t\n1741\t```bash\n1742\tpython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"baseline exit=$?\"\n1743\t\n1744\t# Case 1 (R7): an empty inventory must NOT report success.\n1745\trm -rf /tmp/emptycanon && mkdir -p /tmp/emptycanon/.claude/skills\n1746\tHOME=/tmp/emptycanon python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"empty exit=$?\"\n1747\t\n1748\t# Cases 2-4 (R2-05): negative-only, duplicate key, unbalanced quote.\n1749\trm -rf /tmp/badcanon && mkdir -p /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/{negonly,dupekey,badquote,fragments}\n1750\t\n1751\tcat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/negonly/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n1752\t---\n1753\tname: negonly\n1754\tdescription: Do not use when auditing anything at all.\n1755\t---\n1756\tbody\n1757\tYAML\n1758\t\n1759\tcat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/dupekey/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n1760\t---\n1761\tname: dupekey\n1762\tdescription: Use when testing. Do not use otherwise.\n1763\tdescription: Use when testing again.\n1764\t---\n1765\tbody\n1766\tYAML\n1767\t\n1768\tcat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/badquote/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n1769\t---\n1770\tname: badquote\n1771\tdescription: \"Use when testing. Do not use otherwise.\n1772\t---\n1773\tbody\n1774\tYAML\n1775\t\n1776\t# R8-10: the clause phrases appear ONLY as fragments of other words.\n1777\tcat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/fragments/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n1778\t---\n1779\tname: fragments\n1780\tdescription: Formats output because formatting matters; output is not formatted twice.\n1781\t---\n1782\tbody\n1783\tYAML\n1784\t\n1785\tHOME=/tmp/badcanon python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"bad exit=$?\"\n1786\t```\n1787\t\n1788\tExpected:\n1789\t\n1790\t- baseline: a long FAIL list, `baseline exit=1`\n1791\t- empty: `FATAL no SKILL.md found ...`, `empty exit=2`\n1792\t- bad: **all four** named as FAIL, with `negonly` reporting *no standalone positive clause* (its only \"use when\" sits inside \"do not use when\"), `dupekey` reporting a duplicate key, `badquote` reporting an unbalanced quote, `fragments` reporting **both** clauses missing (its \"use for\" and \"not for\" exist only inside other words) — and `bad exit=1`.\n1793\t\n1794\tIf `negonly` passes, the R2-05 span-containment fix is not in. If the empty case exits 0, the R7 fix is not in. Either way, stop and fix the checker before editing a single skill — a linter that green-lights broken frontmatter is worse than none.\n1795\t\n1796\t- [ ] **Step 3: Fix frontmatter in priority order, highest-use first**\n1797\t\n1798\tDo **not** bulk-rewrite with sed. Each description is a trigger surface; a careless edit silently stops a skill firing. Edit only the frontmatter block of `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, in this order:\n1799\t\n1800\t1. Confirmed working set (3+ invocations): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `backup`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, `doctor-plus`, `startup`, `new-project`, `status-report`.\n1801\t\n1802\t   > `codex-review` was on this list and has been removed: it is a third-party symlinked skill, excluded from the checker, so editing its frontmatter would be out of scope and overwritten by the next install.\n1803\t2. Everything else Claude Code exposes.\n1804\t3. Codex-only skills last.\n1805\t\n1806\tWorked example — `backup` today has triggers but no negative clause:\n1807\t\n1808\t```yaml\n1809\tdescription: >-\n1810\t  Run the KeystoneOS daily backup on demand (git-push the memory repos, rsync\n1811\t  state to Google Drive and the CRASHBOX SSD, reindex memsearch), then report\n1812\t  what was saved and what was skipped. Use when the user says \"/backup\", \"run\n1813\t  the backup\", \"back up now\", \"did the backup run\", \"the backup failed\", or\n1814\t  when a session-start HOOK FAILURE names daily-backup. Do not use for\n1815\t  whole-stack health checks - use claude-stack-doctor instead.\n1816\t```\n1817\t\n1818\tRe-check after each batch of five:\n1819\t\n1820\t```bash\n1821\tpython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py | tail -3\n1822\t```\n1823\t\n1824\t- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm it passes**\n1825\t\n1826\t```bash\n1827\tpython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\"\n1828\t```\n1829\t\n1830\tExpected: `checked 42 skills, 0 failing, 42 portable` and `exit=0`. **42, not 61** — the checker covers owned skills only; the 19 symlinked third-party skills are skipped.\n1831\t\n1832\t- [ ] **Step 5: Prove portability in both harnesses, not just the linter**\n1833\t\n1834\tPick three edited skills. In a fresh Claude Code session, type one trigger phrase for each and confirm it fires. In a fresh Codex session (`cx`), do the same. Record all six results in the report. A linter that passes while a skill stops firing is exactly the failure this step exists to catch.\n1835\t\n1836\t- [ ] **Step 6: Write the report and commit**\n1837\t\n1838\t```bash\n1839\tcd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n1840\t{\n1841\t  echo \"# Skill portability normalization - 2026-09-12\"\n1842\t  echo\n1843\t  echo \"Contract: name (== directory name, TitleCase allowed) + description with\"\n1844\t  echo \"BOTH a positive 'Use when' clause and a negative 'Do not use' clause.\"\n1845\t  echo \"argument-hint and allowed-tools are Claude-only; metadata is Codex-only.\"\n1846\t  echo\n1847\t  echo '```'\n1848\t  python3 scripts/skill-portability-check.py || true\n1849\t  echo '```'\n1850\t  echo\n1851\t  echo \"## Live trigger test (Step 5)\"\n1852\t  echo \"| skill | Claude Code | Codex |\"\n1853\t  echo \"|---|---|---|\"\n1854\t  echo \"| FILL IN | | |\"\n1855\t} > docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\n1856\tgit add scripts/skill-portability-check.py docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\n1857\tgit commit -m \"feat: portable skill frontmatter contract + checker\n1858\t\n1859\tCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n1860\tcd ~/.claude/skills && git add -A && git commit -m \"chore: portable frontmatter across the roster\n1861\t\n1862\tCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git push\n1863\t```\n1864\t\n1865\tFill in the trigger-test table from the real runs. A report left with `FILL IN` is a failed task.\n1866\t\n1867\t- [ ] **Step 7: Resume the daily backup**\n1868\t\n1869\tPaused in Task 3 Step 0. The migration's commits are pushed, so the tree should be clean again:\n1870\t\n1871\t```bash\n1872\tgit -C ~/.claude/skills status --short\n1873\tlaunchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.dailybackup.plist\n1874\tlaunchctl list | grep dailybackup\n1875\t```\n1876\t\n1877\tExpected: `git status` prints nothing, then one `com.keystone.dailybackup` line. If `git status` is not empty, commit or discard first — reloading onto a dirty tree lets the next 09:00 run publish it.\n1878\t\n1879\t---\n1880\t"}]},"uuid":"15a6e861-103f-43cb-9cd8-f31a9ca79028","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:34:59.035Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","content":"### Task 5: Normalize every skill to portable frontmatter\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` (frontmatter only, body untouched)\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: the symlink-free canonical repo from Task 4.\n- Produces: `skill-portability-check.py`. `aios-audit` calls it as its harness-parity check. Exit `0` when every skill passes, `1` otherwise, `2` on an empty or missing inventory.\n\n**The portable frontmatter contract** (a superset — Codex ignores keys it does not know, Claude Code ignores `metadata`):\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: skill-name                       # REQUIRED. Must equal the directory name.\n                                       # TitleCase is allowed (CreateSkill, Harvest).\ndescription: >-                        # REQUIRED. <=1024 chars. Must contain BOTH:\n  <what it does, one sentence>.        #   (a) a positive clause: \"Use when ...\" with\n  Use when <literal trigger phrases>.  #       literal phrases the user would type\n  Do not use when <negative>.          #   (b) a negative clause: \"Do not use ...\" /\n                                       #       \"NOT FOR ...\" - Codex needs this to\n                                       #       avoid over-firing\nmetadata:\n  short-description: <one short line>  # optional, Codex-only, Claude ignores\nargument-hint: \"[...]\"                 # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\nallowed-tools: Read, Bash, Grep        # optional, Claude-only, Codex ignores\n---\n```\n\nClaude Code triggers on literal phrases; Codex leans on the negative clause. A description with only one half fires reliably in one harness and badly in the other. **Naming is settled here (R7):** the rule is `name` equals the directory name. TitleCase is legal — `CreateSkill` and `Harvest` already ship that way and renaming them would break every cross-reference.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the failing check**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py`:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Check every skill in the canonical repo against the portable frontmatter\ncontract. Exit 0 = all pass, 1 = failures, 2 = empty/missing inventory.\nstdlib only (PEP 668 machine). Parses the flat YAML subset we actually use.\"\"\"\nimport os, re, sys, glob\n\nCANON = os.path.expanduser(\"~/.claude/skills\")\n# R8-03: every key the roster actually uses today, measured with\n#   grep '^[a-z-]*:' ~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md | sort | uniq -c\n# The contract may not outlaw keys Claude Code honours. `effort` and\n# `user-invocable` are live Claude Code keys; `repository` and `homepage`\n# are package metadata. Genuinely unknown keys are still reported.\nALLOWED = {\"name\", \"description\", \"metadata\", \"argument-hint\", \"allowed-tools\",\n           \"model\", \"disable-model-invocation\", \"license\", \"version\", \"author\",\n           \"effort\", \"user-invocable\", \"repository\", \"homepage\"}\nPOSITIVE = (\"use when\", \"use for\", \"use this skill when\", \"invoke when\")\nNEGATIVE = (\"do not use\", \"don't use\", \"not for\", \"never use\")\n\ndef frontmatter(text):\n    if not text.startswith(\"---\"):\n        return None\n    end = text.find(\"\\n---\", 3)\n    return None if end == -1 else text[4:end]\n\ndef top_keys(fm):\n    return [m.group(1) for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^([A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*)\\s*:\", fm)]\n\ndef scalar(fm, key):\n    \"\"\"Read a flat scalar, including >- and | block styles.\"\"\"\n    m = re.search(rf\"(?ms)^{re.escape(key)}\\s*:[ \\t]*(.*?)(?=^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*\\s*:|\\Z)\", fm)\n    if not m:\n        return \"\"\n    raw = m.group(1)\n    raw = re.sub(r\"^[>|][-+]?\\s*\\n\", \"\", raw)          # strip a block indicator\n    val = \" \".join(l.strip() for l in raw.splitlines() if l.strip())\n    return val.strip().strip('\"').strip(\"'\")\n\ndef check(path):\n    name = os.path.basename(os.path.dirname(path))\n    problems = []\n    fm = frontmatter(open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"ignore\").read())\n    if fm is None:\n        return name, [\"no YAML frontmatter\"]\n    keys = top_keys(fm)\n    declared = scalar(fm, \"name\")\n    if \"name\" not in keys:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'name'\")\n    elif declared != name:\n        problems.append(f\"name '{declared}' != directory '{name}'\")\n    desc = scalar(fm, \"description\")\n    if not desc:\n        problems.append(\"missing 'description'\")\n    else:\n        low = desc.lower()\n        if len(desc) > 1024:\n            problems.append(f\"description {len(desc)} chars (>1024)\")\n        problems.extend(clause_problems(low))\n    # R2-05: reject syntax outside the supported subset rather than\n    # silently accepting it. Duplicate keys and unbalanced quotes both\n    # parse \"successfully\" with a regex reader and mean something else\n    # to a real YAML loader.\n    for k in keys:\n        if k not in ALLOWED:\n            problems.append(f\"unknown frontmatter key '{k}'\")\n    dupes = {k for k in keys if keys.count(k) > 1}\n    for k in sorted(dupes):\n        problems.append(f\"duplicate frontmatter key '{k}' - unsupported\")\n    for key in (\"name\", \"description\"):\n        raw = raw_scalar(fm, key)\n        if raw and raw[:1] in (\"'\", '\"') and not raw.endswith(raw[0]):\n            problems.append(f\"'{key}' has an unbalanced quote\")\n    return name, problems\n\ndef raw_scalar(fm, key):\n    m = re.search(rf\"(?m)^{re.escape(key)}\\s*:[ \\t]*(.*)$\", fm)\n    return m.group(1).strip() if m else \"\"\n\ndef clause_problems(low):\n    \"\"\"A positive phrase governed by a negative phrase does not count as a\n    trigger. 'do not use when auditing' must NOT satisfy the positive check.\n\n    R3-04: containment was the wrong test. 'do not use' spans [0,10) and\n    'use when' spans [7,15) - the positive is not CONTAINED, it OVERLAPS.\n    A negative span is therefore extended through any immediately following\n    'when'/'for' so it governs the whole construction, and any positive match\n    OVERLAPPING a negative span is discarded.\"\"\"\n    out = []\n\n    # Build negative spans, extended through a trailing 'when'/'for' so that\n    # 'do not use when' is one governing construction, not 'do not use' + a\n    # stray 'use when' the positive check could claim.\n    neg_spans = []\n    for n in NEGATIVE:\n        # R8-10: whole words only. A bare substring match let \"not formatted\"\n        # count as \"not for\" and \"because formatting\" as \"use for\".\n        for m in re.finditer(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(n) + r\"\\b\", low):\n            start, end = m.start(), m.end()\n            for word in (\" when\", \" for\"):\n                w_end = end + len(word)\n                if low.startswith(word, end) and (w_end == len(low) or not low[w_end].isalnum()):\n                    end = w_end\n                    break\n            neg_spans.append((start, end))\n\n    def overlaps_negative(i, j):\n        return any(i < b and a < j for a, b in neg_spans)   # interval overlap\n\n    pos_found = False\n    for p in POSITIVE:\n        for m in re.finditer(r\"\\b\" + re.escape(p) + r\"\\b\", low):\n            if not overlaps_negative(m.start(), m.end()):\n                pos_found = True\n                break\n        if pos_found:\n            break\n\n    if not pos_found:\n        out.append(\"no standalone positive clause ('Use when ...') - Claude will \"\n                   \"not trigger (a 'use when' governed by 'do not use when' does not count)\")\n    if not neg_spans:\n        out.append(\"no negative clause ('Do not use ...') - Codex over-fires\")\n    return out\n\ndef main():\n    if not os.path.isdir(CANON):\n        print(f\"FATAL canonical dir missing: {CANON}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    # R8-01: check only OWNED skills - real directories. A symlinked skill was\n    # installed by the skills CLI or vendored; its frontmatter belongs to a\n    # third party, and demanding our contract of it makes \"0 failing\"\n    # unreachable. Ownership is structural, so no name list is kept.\n    paths = [p for p in sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(CANON, \"*\", \"SKILL.md\")))\n             if not os.path.islink(os.path.dirname(p))]\n    if not paths:\n        print(f\"FATAL no SKILL.md found under {CANON} - refusing to report success\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    failed = 0\n    for p in paths:\n        name, probs = check(p)\n        if probs:\n            failed += 1\n            print(f\"FAIL {name}\")\n            for x in probs:\n                print(f\"       - {x}\")\n    print(f\"\\nchecked {len(paths)} skills, {failed} failing, {len(paths)-failed} portable\")\n    return 1 if failed else 0\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Prove the checker's own failure modes first (R7)**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"baseline exit=$?\"\n\n# Case 1 (R7): an empty inventory must NOT report success.\nrm -rf /tmp/emptycanon && mkdir -p /tmp/emptycanon/.claude/skills\nHOME=/tmp/emptycanon python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"empty exit=$?\"\n\n# Cases 2-4 (R2-05): negative-only, duplicate key, unbalanced quote.\nrm -rf /tmp/badcanon && mkdir -p /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/{negonly,dupekey,badquote,fragments}\n\ncat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/negonly/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n---\nname: negonly\ndescription: Do not use when auditing anything at all.\n---\nbody\nYAML\n\ncat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/dupekey/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n---\nname: dupekey\ndescription: Use when testing. Do not use otherwise.\ndescription: Use when testing again.\n---\nbody\nYAML\n\ncat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/badquote/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n---\nname: badquote\ndescription: \"Use when testing. Do not use otherwise.\n---\nbody\nYAML\n\n# R8-10: the clause phrases appear ONLY as fragments of other words.\ncat > /tmp/badcanon/.claude/skills/fragments/SKILL.md <<'YAML'\n---\nname: fragments\ndescription: Formats output because formatting matters; output is not formatted twice.\n---\nbody\nYAML\n\nHOME=/tmp/badcanon python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"bad exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n- baseline: a long FAIL list, `baseline exit=1`\n- empty: `FATAL no SKILL.md found ...`, `empty exit=2`\n- bad: **all four** named as FAIL, with `negonly` reporting *no standalone positive clause* (its only \"use when\" sits inside \"do not use when\"), `dupekey` reporting a duplicate key, `badquote` reporting an unbalanced quote, `fragments` reporting **both** clauses missing (its \"use for\" and \"not for\" exist only inside other words) — and `bad exit=1`.\n\nIf `negonly` passes, the R2-05 span-containment fix is not in. If the empty case exits 0, the R7 fix is not in. Either way, stop and fix the checker before editing a single skill — a linter that green-lights broken frontmatter is worse than none.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Fix frontmatter in priority order, highest-use first**\n\nDo **not** bulk-rewrite with sed. Each description is a trigger surface; a careless edit silently stops a skill firing. Edit only the frontmatter block of `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, in this order:\n\n1. Confirmed working set (3+ invocations): `closeout`, `memory-write`, `CreateSkill`, `claude-stack-doctor`, `backup`, `dev-sync`, `project`, `meeting-sync`, `graphify`, `name-meetings`, `doctor-plus`, `startup`, `new-project`, `status-report`.\n\n   > `codex-review` was on this list and has been removed: it is a third-party symlinked skill, excluded from the checker, so editing its frontmatter would be out of scope and overwritten by the next install.\n2. Everything else Claude Code exposes.\n3. Codex-only skills last.\n\nWorked example — `backup` today has triggers but no negative clause:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: >-\n  Run the KeystoneOS daily backup on demand (git-push the memory repos, rsync\n  state to Google Drive and the CRASHBOX SSD, reindex memsearch), then report\n  what was saved and what was skipped. Use when the user says \"/backup\", \"run\n  the backup\", \"back up now\", \"did the backup run\", \"the backup failed\", or\n  when a session-start HOOK FAILURE names daily-backup. Do not use for\n  whole-stack health checks - use claude-stack-doctor instead.\n```\n\nRe-check after each batch of five:\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py | tail -3\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm it passes**\n\n```bash\npython3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/skill-portability-check.py; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nExpected: `checked 42 skills, 0 failing, 42 portable` and `exit=0`. **42, not 61** — the checker covers owned skills only; the 19 symlinked third-party skills are skipped.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Prove portability in both harnesses, not just the linter**\n\nPick three edited skills. In a fresh Claude Code session, type one trigger phrase for each and confirm it fires. In a fresh Codex session (`cx`), do the same. Record all six results in the report. A linter that passes while a skill stops firing is exactly the failure this step exists to catch.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Write the report and commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n{\n  echo \"# Skill portability normalization - 2026-09-12\"\n  echo\n  echo \"Contract: name (== directory name, TitleCase allowed) + description with\"\n  echo \"BOTH a positive 'Use when' clause and a negative 'Do not use' clause.\"\n  echo \"argument-hint and allowed-tools are Claude-only; metadata is Codex-only.\"\n  echo\n  echo '```'\n  python3 scripts/skill-portability-check.py || true\n  echo '```'\n  echo\n  echo \"## Live trigger test (Step 5)\"\n  echo \"| skill | Claude Code | Codex |\"\n  echo \"|---|---|---|\"\n  echo \"| FILL IN | | |\"\n} > docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit add scripts/skill-portability-check.py docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: portable skill frontmatter contract + checker\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\ncd ~/.claude/skills && git add -A && git commit -m \"chore: portable frontmatter across the roster\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git push\n```\n\nFill in the trigger-test table from the real runs. A report left with `FILL IN` is a failed task.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Resume the daily backup**\n\nPaused in Task 3 Step 0. The migration's commits are pushed, so the tree should be clean again:\n\n```bash\ngit -C ~/.claude/skills status --short\nlaunchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.dailybackup.plist\nlaunchctl list | grep dailybackup\n```\n\nExpected: `git status` prints nothing, then one `com.keystone.dailybackup` line. If `git status` is not empty, commit or discard first — reloading onto a dirty tree lets the next 09:00 run publish it.\n\n---\n","numLines":335,"startLine":1546,"totalLines":2869}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ad1dd60d-a1f7-4853-b9f0-dc5fac06bbce","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
