{"parentUuid":"a5f32e0b-b5cf-4d5b-b5b9-926624a0e047","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>"],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_019XAGE3kWow9ZAF9VRCCpYq","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d18d4fce-76a3-4633-8d2b-7106a85c80d8","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:34:58.955Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Read hook additional context: <context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"fa86f5fa-c6c4-4d49-a008-83bbe9f5984e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019XAGE3kWow9ZAF9VRCCpYq","type":"tool_result","content":"2836\t## Review Gates (not optional)\n2837\t\n2838\tEric asked for Codex review of the plan and of any code. These are steps.\n2839\t\n2840\t- [ ] **Gate A — plan review, RE-OPENED 2026-09-12.** Round 6 APPROVED; execution then found facts the plan did not know, and rounds 7 (Codex) and 8 (Claude, after the Codex quota ran out) found defects in the resulting amendments. History of the first approval: Six rounds, same resumed Codex reviewer session each time so every new plan hash was recorded against it. Final verdict **APPROVED**, 0 findings, artifact `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r6/`. The pattern used each round, for reference when re-reviewing after edits:\n2841\t\n2842\t  ```bash\n2843\t  cd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n2844\t  python3 ~/.claude/skills/claudex-loop/scripts/runner.py review \\\n2845\t    --host claude --repo ~/DEV/KeystoneOS \\\n2846\t    --plan docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md \\\n2847\t    --resume ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-rN/claudex-*/result.json \\\n2848\t    --feedback ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-rN.md \\\n2849\t    --artifacts ~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-rN+1\n2850\t  ```\n2851\t\n2852\t  **If this plan is edited before execution, Gate A must be re-run** — an approval binds only the hash it was issued against.\n2853\t- [ ] **Gate B — after Task 5.** `codex-review` on `sync-to-codex.sh`, `sync-to-codex.ps1`, `skill-portability-check.py`, and ten rewritten frontmatter blocks. Codex is the right reviewer here specifically because it is the harness whose triggering the rewrite claims to fix.\n2854\t- [ ] **Gate C — after Task 7.** `codex-review` on `aios-metrics.py`, `aios-audit-cron.sh`, the plist, and the `aios-audit` SKILL.md body.\n2855\t- [ ] **Gate D — before declaring done.** `superpowers:verification-before-completion`. Every success claim carries a pasted command output.\n2856\t\n2857\t## Out of Scope\n2858\t\n2859\t- Rewriting plugin skills (`superpowers`, `mattpocock-skills`) — vendored, overwritten on update.\n2860\t- **All 19 third-party skills linked into `~/.claude/skills/`** — 14 `skills` CLI installs recorded in `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json` (five of them from `mattpocock-skills`), the four `vendor-review/claudex-loop` package skills, and `persistent-localhost`. Same rule as plugins: not ours to rewrite, vendor into the repo, or publish to views. **Windows consequence, stated rather than hidden:** they were already tracked as mode-120000 links that are dead on that box; untracking them means the next `dev-sync` pull deletes the dead links. Install the ones wanted on Windows separately (see `vendored-skill-install`). Nothing regresses; the breakage stops being silent.\n2861\t- **Cleaning the install ledger (R8-05).** About 30 names archived in Task 2 still have entries in `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`, so the `skills` CLI may think they are installed or try to update them. That file belongs to the CLI; Eric runs `npx skills remove <name>` for each, or accepts the stale entries. No script in this plan edits the ledger.\n2862\t- **The install procedure itself (R8-02, R9-03).** `npx skills add ... -a claude-code` keeps creating links in `~/.claude/skills/`, and the procedure is left unchanged. Two layers handle it: the sync adds every current link to the per-machine `.git/info/exclude` on its next run, and — because the sync runs only inside `dev-sync` while the backup commits daily — the **pre-commit hook installed in Task 3 Step 5b** is the actual guarantee. Cost: on a day a new install lands before a sync, the skills repo's backup commit fails visibly as `repo-commit:skills` (surfaced at session start); running the sync removes the staged link and clears it. Both halves of that sentence were false until Task 3 Step 5c (backup checks its commit) and the sync's index cleanup (R10-01). **Windows** needs the same hook installed in its own clone, since hooks are never pulled.\n2863\t- Pruning the ~150 pool entries that are third-party installs. Task 2 touches only the 98 that match yesterday's archive. The rest need a human decision via `aios-audit`.\n2864\t- Migrating all 61 hooks to Pi. Task 8 ports exactly one to measure cost.\n2865\t- **Adopting Pi.** Task 8 produces a cost number only. Adoption needs a boundary design that is its own project.\n2866\t- Deleting any skill outright. Everything moves to an archive folder or is recoverable from git.\n2867\t- **The Windows machine** (R11-01). Both machines are active; this plan executes on the Mac only. Task 3 pushes the rewritten `sync-to-codex.ps1`, and Windows `dev-sync` runs it right after its next pull. It **refuses any real run** there until `~\\.agents\\.sync-v2-ready` exists, so that pull changes nothing and `dev-sync` reports `FATAL this machine is not prepared`. Until Windows is prepared its views stay exactly as they are, and the seven skills the old twin used to copy stop being refreshed there — a visible, deliberate pause, not a silent overwrite. Preparing Windows is its own session: archive `~\\.agents\\skills` and `~\\.codex\\skills`; run Task 4 Step 2's DIVERGED/diff reconcile against that machine's pool; install the Task 3 Step 5b pre-commit hook in its clone; create the marker; then run the sync. The twin avoids native stderr under `ErrorActionPreference=Stop` so it also runs under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (R11-02), but its behaviour there is verified only when that session happens — this Mac has `pwsh` 7 only.\n2868\t- Mining the 414 JSONL sessions to reverse-engineer harness behaviour (video gap #9). Real, but its own project.\n2869\t"}]},"uuid":"fe93c3c6-78e7-4464-bc04-81357de57b87","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:34:59.041Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePat