ritten on update.\n- **All 19 third-party skills linked into `~/.claude/skills/`** — 14 `skills` CLI installs recorded in `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json` (five of them from `mattpocock-skills`), the four `vendor-review/claudex-loop` package skills, and `persistent-localhost`. Same rule as plugins: not ours to rewrite, vendor into the repo, or publish to views. **Windows consequence, stated rather than hidden:** they were already tracked as mode-120000 links that are dead on that box; untracking them means the next `dev-sync` pull deletes the dead links. Install the ones wanted on Windows separately (see `vendored-skill-install`). Nothing regresses; the breakage stops being silent.\n- **Cleaning the install ledger (R8-05).** About 30 names archived in Task 2 still have entries in `~/.agents/.skill-lock.json`, so the `skills` CLI may think they are installed or try to update them. That file belongs to the CLI; Eric runs `npx skills remove <name>` for each, or accepts the stale entries. No script in this plan edits the ledger.\n- **The install procedure itself (R8-02, R9-03).** `npx skills add ... -a claude-code` keeps creating links in `~/.claude/skills/`, and the procedure is left unchanged. Two layers handle it: the sync adds every current link to the per-machine `.git/info/exclude` on its next run, and — because the sync runs only inside `dev-sync` while the backup commits daily — the **pre-commit hook installed in Task 3 Step 5b** is the actual guarantee. Cost: on a day a new install lands before a sync, the skills repo's backup commit fails visibly as `repo-commit:skills` (surfaced at session start); running the sync removes the staged link and clears it. Both halves of that sentence were false until Task 3 Step 5c (backup checks its commit) and the sync's index cleanup (R10-01). **Windows** needs the same hook installed in its own clone, since hooks are never pulled.\n- Pruning the ~150 pool entries that are third-party installs. Task 2 touches only the 98 that match yesterday's archive. The rest need a human decision via `aios-audit`.\n- Migrating all 61 hooks to Pi. Task 8 ports exactly one to measure cost.\n- **Adopting Pi.** Task 8 produces a cost number only. Adoption needs a boundary design that is its own project.\n- Deleting any skill outright. Everything moves to an archive folder or is recoverable from git.\n- **The Windows machine** (R11-01). Both machines are active; this plan executes on the Mac only. Task 3 pushes the rewritten `sync-to-codex.ps1`, and Windows `dev-sync` runs it right after its next pull. It **refuses any real run** there until `~\\.agents\\.sync-v2-ready` exists, so that pull changes nothing and `dev-sync` reports `FATAL this machine is not prepared`. Until Windows is prepared its views stay exactly as they are, and the seven skills the old twin used to copy stop being refreshed there — a visible, deliberate pause, not a silent overwrite. Preparing Windows is its own session: archive `~\\.agents\\skills` and `~\\.codex\\skills`; run Task 4 Step 2's DIVERGED/diff reconcile against that machine's pool; install the Task 3 Step 5b pre-commit hook in its clone; create the marker; then run the sync. The twin avoids native stderr under `ErrorActionPreference=Stop` so it also runs under Windows PowerShell 5.1 (R11-02), but its behaviour there is verified only when that session happens — this Mac has `pwsh` 7 only.\n- Mining the 414 JSONL sessions to reverse-engineer harness behaviour (video gap #9). Real, but its own project.\n","numLines":34,"startLine":2836,"totalLines":2869}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fa86f5fa-c6c4-4d49-a008-83bbe9f5984e","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
