{"parentUuid":"704f23c0-2d2a-4b17-84ff-d853df620ffc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>"],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_01KxkJhWshFvJ17GrKCAGwsF","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"17ca42e4-d231-497c-9680-de61ce52a3dc","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:34:59.129Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Read hook additional context: <context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"9cbf52dc-15e0-4f13-99a6-d1648f9e43b9","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KxkJhWshFvJ17GrKCAGwsF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Harness-Agnostic AIOS Implementation Plan\n2\t\n3\t> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n4\t\n5\t**Goal:** Make every skill Eric owns load identically in Claude Code, Codex, and Pi from one versioned canonical folder, and build a recurring audit that keeps the whole AIOS from rotting.\n6\t\n7\t**Architecture:** `~/.claude/skills/` is the canonical home — it is already its own git repo (`erash11/claude-skills`) and is the only copy that versions and crosses to Windows. It holds **real directories only; no symlink is ever committed.** `~/.agents/skills/` (Pi, Cowork) and `~/.codex/skills/` (Codex) become **generated views**, rebuilt per machine by a hardened `sync-to-codex.sh` — symlinks on Mac, copies on Windows, never authored by hand. Every skill's frontmatter is normalized to a Codex-safe core so the same file fires in all three harnesses. Then one compound audit skill (`aios-audit`) orchestrates the five existing audit skills, driven by a `rot.md` decay table.\n8\t\n9\t**Tech Stack:** bash, PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, for the `.ps1` twin), python3 (stdlib only), launchd, Agent Skills standard (SKILL.md + YAML frontmatter), Claude Code, Codex CLI 0.154.0, Pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`).\n10\t\n11\t**Spec:** `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md` (gap analysis rows 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10) and `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`.\n12\t\n13\t**Review history:**\n14\t\n15\t- **Round 1** — Codex: **REVISE**, 6 high / 4 medium (R1–R10). All accepted. **The architecture above is the inverse of round 1's** — round 1 made `~/.agents/skills/` canonical, which R1/R3 showed would cause data loss.\n16\t- **Round 2** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 7 medium (R2-01 – R2-09). All accepted. Round 2 caught that the views published only 7 of 61 skills, and that the single rollback tarball would corrupt the canonical repo.\n17\t- **Round 3** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 5 medium (R3-01 – R3-07). All accepted. Round 3 caught a task-ordering deadlock and a path-traversal `rm -rf` introduced by round 2's own manifest fix.\n18\t- **Round 4** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 2 medium (R4-01 – R4-04). All accepted. Wildcard metacharacters reaching PowerShell's wildcard-aware `-Path`; archive failures suppressed before a delete; a rollback that ran a sync rejecting the layout it had just restored; copy-freshness compared by size instead of content.\n19\t- **Round 5** — Codex: **REVISE**, 0 high / 1 medium (R5-01). Accepted. Deleting a canonical skill left a dangling view link that every check missed.\n20\t- **Round 6** — Codex: **APPROVED**, 0 findings. *\"No material unresolved defects identified within the reviewed scope.\"*\n21\t- **Round 7** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 3 medium (R7-01 – R7-05), against AMEND-01 — the two-hop vendored chain found while verifying Task 1's snapshot. All accepted.\n22\t- **Round 8** — **Claude** as reviewer (the Codex usage limit was reached; Eric approved the switch): **REVISE**, 2 high / 4 medium / 5 low (R8-01 – R8-11). All accepted. R8-01 was the most consequential finding of the whole review: **all 19 outward links are third-party**, so Task 4's inversion script was deleted and ownership became structural instead of a name list. It is the first fix in eight rounds that removed code rather than adding a guard.\n23\t- **Round 9** — Claude (resumed session): **REVISE**, 2 high / 2 medium / 3 low (R9-01 – R9-07). All accepted. Both highs came from the round-8 redesign itself: a real sync ran *before* reconciliation and would have overwritten the two pool copies holding the only copy of their edits, and the generated ignore list lived in the tracked `.gitignore` shared by both machines, so each machine would have erased the other's entries and brought the committed-symlink bug back. Fixes: reconcile first; per-machine `.git/info/exclude`; a pre-commit hook as the real guarantee.\n24\t- **Round 10** — Claude (resumed): **REVISE**, 1 high / 2 low (R10-01 – R10-03). All accepted; R10-03's premise checked and found not to hold on this Mac, with its check added anyway. R10-01: the new hook's refusal was invisible — `daily-backup.ps1` discarded the commit exit code and logged \"committed\", and an already-staged link stayed staged — so the backup would have failed silently every day. Fixes: the backup checks its commit (Task 3 Step 5c); the sync removes third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, never a reset to HEAD.\n25\t- **Round 11** — Claude (resumed): **REVISE**, 0 high / 1 medium / 1 low (R11-01, R11-02). Reviewer: all three round-10 fixes hold. R11-01: Windows `dev-sync` would run the rewritten twin straight after its next pull and overwrite all 43 published entries with no snapshot or reconcile. Fix: both scripts refuse any real run until a per-machine `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` marker exists, created only after reconciling — which also turns Task 3's \"don't run dev-sync\" warning into an enforced rule on the Mac. R11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n26\t- **Round 12** — Claude (resumed): **APPROVED**, 1 low (R12-01): in the bash script a `DRY_RUN` value other than `0`/`1` skipped the first-run gate yet still ran the destructive publish loop. Fixed anyway, because on an unprepared machine it is a data-loss route; the fix changes the plan hash, so round 13 re-confirms before execution.\n27\t\n28\t**Gate A was satisfied at round 6 and is now RE-OPENED (R8-06).** Executing Task 1 found facts the plan did not know (AMEND-01, then round 8's R8-01), so the approval no longer binds this hash. Tasks 1–2 ran under the round-6 approval, and their text is unchanged since. Task 3 onward waits for a fresh APPROVED.\n29\t\n30\tFindings by round: 10 → 9 → 7 → 4 → 1 → 0. High-severity: 6 → 2 → 2 → 2 → 0 → 0. Two defects (R3-01, R3-02) were introduced by an earlier round's own fix and caught by the next — which is the argument for the gates existing.\n31\t\n32\tDisposition tables for rounds 1–3 are below; rounds 4 and 5 are recorded in `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r{4,5}.md`. Artifacts: `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r{1..6}/`.\n33\t\n34\t> **Reviewer's stated limitations, carried forward as execution risk.** The review was read-only and static: no files were edited, no tests executed, no Windows filesystem behaviour exercised, and no Pi runtime isolation verified. The host independently ran the embedded-script parse checks, the clause-detection fixtures, the `valid_entry` guard cases and the full canonical-deletion scenario — those results are real. Everything else is an approved *plan*, not proven behaviour. Gates B, C and D still apply.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## Global Constraints\n39\t\n40\t- **`~/.claude/skills/` is its own git repo** — `origin https://github.com/erash11/claude-skills.git`, currently in sync with origin. It is *gitignored by the `dotclaude` repo*, which is what the earlier handoff meant; that is not the same as unversioned. **This repo is the safety net and the Mac↔Windows bridge.**\n41\t- **Never commit a symlink into `~/.claude/skills/`.** Windows pulls this repo. A committed absolute Mac symlink arrives there as an unusable file.\n42\t- **Never point Pi at `~/DEV`.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — documented, deliberate design.\n43\t- **Never let any harness near `DexaProject`** (identifiable athlete health data, on Google Drive, not in `~/DEV`).\n44\t- **Plugin skills are out of scope.** `superpowers` (14) and `mattpocock-skills` (35) are vendored and overwritten on update. Scope = the 61 skills in `~/.claude/skills/`.\n45\t- **Hooks exist in two copies** (`~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/`). So do the sync scripts (`.sh` and `.ps1`). Any change patches both.\n46\t- Global `MEMORY.md` is at 2,489 / 2,500 chars. Consolidate before adding anything.\n47\t- `timeout` is NOT installed on this Mac (no GNU coreutils). Do not use it.\n48\t- `dcg` blocks `find ... -delete`. Use an explicit `rm` on a named path.\n49\t- Python is PEP 668-managed. `python3` with stdlib only, or `uv`. Never global `pip install`.\n50\t- **No success claim without a pasted command output.**\n51\t\n52\t### Measured baseline (2026-09-12, verified on disk this session)\n53\t\n54\t| Fact | Value |\n55\t|---|---|\n56\t| `~/.claude/skills/` SKILL.md count (`find -L`) | 61 |\n57\t| Of those, symlinks pointing **out** to `~/.agents/skills/` | 19 |\n58\t| `~/.claude/skills/` git remote | `erash11/claude-skills`, in sync with origin |\n59\t| Latest commit | `3ab4467 chore: apply global skill audit` — the 99 deletions **are committed and pushed** |\n60\t| `~/.agents/skills/` top-level entries | 306 |\n61\t| Pool entries **not** present in `~/.claude/skills/` | 248 |\n62\t| Archived yesterday to `~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/` | 99 |\n63\t| Archived skills still live in `~/.agents/skills/` | 98 |\n64\t| `~/.codex/skills/` entries | 32 (27 symlinks, 5 real dirs) |\n65\t| Real dirs in `~/.codex/skills/` diverged from Claude copy | 0 (`dev-sync`, `closeout`, `new-project`, `startup` identical; `session-handoff` is an empty orphan) |\n66\t| `~/.claude/skills/_shared/` | **load-bearing** — `config.json` + `Inference/`, referenced by 10+ skills |\n67\t| Project-level skills in `~/DEV` | 1 (`KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit`) |\n68\t| `~/.claude/rot.md` | does not exist |\n69\t\n70\t**The two lead findings:**\n71\t\n72\t1. **The pool is a stale mirror.** Yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` (and committed the cut) but never touched `~/.agents/skills/`. 98 of the 99 archived names are still there, plus 248 entries Claude Code never registered. Pi auto-discovers that folder. Installing Pi today re-exposes everything. Task 2 fixes this and must land before Task 7.\n73\t2. **Three writers already target these folders** and none of them know about the plan: `sync-to-codex.sh`, its `.ps1` twin, and `daily-backup.ps1` (which runs `git add -A` on `~/.claude/skills`). Task 3 fixes the writers *before* moving anything. Round 1 skipped this and would have destroyed canonical copies.\n74\t\n75\t### Codex frontmatter ground truth (read from `~/.codex/skills/.system/*/SKILL.md`)"}]},"uuid":"c9614be1-953a-4bca-bd7c-e0ead4365f99","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:34:59.188Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","content":"# Harness-Agnostic AIOS Implementation Plan\n\n> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.\n\n**Goal:** Make every skill Eric owns load identically in Claude Code, Codex, and Pi from one versioned canonical folder, and build a recurring audit that keeps the whole AIOS from rotting.\n\n**Architecture:** `~/.claude/skills/` is the canonical home — it is already its own git repo (`erash11/claude-skills`) and is the only copy that versions and crosses to Windows. It holds **real directories only; no symlink is ever committed.** `~/.agents/skills/` (Pi, Cowork) and `~/.codex/skills/` (Codex) become **generated views**, rebuilt per machine by a hardened `sync-to-codex.sh` — symlinks on Mac, copies on Windows, never authored by hand. Every skill's frontmatter is normalized to a Codex-safe core so the same file fires in all three harnesses. Then one compound audit skill (`aios-audit`) orchestrates the five existing audit skills, driven by a `rot.md` decay table.\n\n**Tech Stack:** bash, PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, for the `.ps1` twin), python3 (stdlib only), launchd, Agent Skills standard (SKILL.md + YAML frontmatter), Claude Code, Codex CLI 0.154.0, Pi (`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent`).\n\n**Spec:** `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-harness-over-model-mark-kashef.md` (gap analysis rows 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10) and `~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md`.\n\n**Review history:**\n\n- **Round 1** — Codex: **REVISE**, 6 high / 4 medium (R1–R10). All accepted. **The architecture above is the inverse of round 1's** — round 1 made `~/.agents/skills/` canonical, which R1/R3 showed would cause data loss.\n- **Round 2** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 7 medium (R2-01 – R2-09). All accepted. Round 2 caught that the views published only 7 of 61 skills, and that the single rollback tarball would corrupt the canonical repo.\n- **Round 3** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 5 medium (R3-01 – R3-07). All accepted. Round 3 caught a task-ordering deadlock and a path-traversal `rm -rf` introduced by round 2's own manifest fix.\n- **Round 4** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 2 medium (R4-01 – R4-04). All accepted. Wildcard metacharacters reaching PowerShell's wildcard-aware `-Path`; archive failures suppressed before a delete; a rollback that ran a sync rejecting the layout it had just restored; copy-freshness compared by size instead of content.\n- **Round 5** — Codex: **REVISE**, 0 high / 1 medium (R5-01). Accepted. Deleting a canonical skill left a dangling view link that every check missed.\n- **Round 6** — Codex: **APPROVED**, 0 findings. *\"No material unresolved defects identified within the reviewed scope.\"*\n- **Round 7** — Codex: **REVISE**, 2 high / 3 medium (R7-01 – R7-05), against AMEND-01 — the two-hop vendored chain found while verifying Task 1's snapshot. All accepted.\n- **Round 8** — **Claude** as reviewer (the Codex usage limit was reached; Eric approved the switch): **REVISE**, 2 high / 4 medium / 5 low (R8-01 – R8-11). All accepted. R8-01 was the most consequential finding of the whole review: **all 19 outward links are third-party**, so Task 4's inversion script was deleted and ownership became structural instead of a name list. It is the first fix in eight rounds that removed code rather than adding a guard.\n- **Round 9** — Claude (resumed session): **REVISE**, 2 high / 2 medium / 3 low (R9-01 – R9-07). All accepted. Both highs came from the round-8 redesign itself: a real sync ran *before* reconciliation and would have overwritten the two pool copies holding the only copy of their edits, and the generated ignore list lived in the tracked `.gitignore` shared by both machines, so each machine would have erased the other's entries and brought the committed-symlink bug back. Fixes: reconcile first; per-machine `.git/info/exclude`; a pre-commit hook as the real guarantee.\n- **Round 10** — Claude (resumed): **REVISE**, 1 high / 2 low (R10-01 – R10-03). All accepted; R10-03's premise checked and found not to hold on this Mac, with its check added anyway. R10-01: the new hook's refusal was invisible — `daily-backup.ps1` discarded the commit exit code and logged \"committed\", and an already-staged link stayed staged — so the backup would have failed silently every day. Fixes: the backup checks its commit (Task 3 Step 5c); the sync removes third-party links from the index with `git rm --cached`, never a reset to HEAD.\n- **Round 11** — Claude (resumed): **REVISE**, 0 high / 1 medium / 1 low (R11-01, R11-02). Reviewer: all three round-10 fixes hold. R11-01: Windows `dev-sync` would run the rewritten twin straight after its next pull and overwrite all 43 published entries with no snapshot or reconcile. Fix: both scripts refuse any real run until a per-machine `~/.agents/.sync-v2-ready` marker exists, created only after reconciling — which also turns Task 3's \"don't run dev-sync\" warning into an enforced rule on the Mac. R11-02: the twin's index probe no longer writes stderr, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 would treat as fatal.\n- **Round 12** — Claude (resumed): **APPROVED**, 1 low (R12-01): in the bash script a `DRY_RUN` value other than `0`/`1` skipped the first-run gate yet still ran the destructive publish loop. Fixed anyway, because on an unprepared machine it is a data-loss route; the fix changes the plan hash, so round 13 re-confirms before execution.\n\n**Gate A was satisfied at round 6 and is now RE-OPENED (R8-06).** Executing Task 1 found facts the plan did not know (AMEND-01, then round 8's R8-01), so the approval no longer binds this hash. Tasks 1–2 ran under the round-6 approval, and their text is unchanged since. Task 3 onward waits for a fresh APPROVED.\n\nFindings by round: 10 → 9 → 7 → 4 → 1 → 0. High-severity: 6 → 2 → 2 → 2 → 0 → 0. Two defects (R3-01, R3-02) were introduced by an earlier round's own fix and caught by the next — which is the argument for the gates existing.\n\nDisposition tables for rounds 1–3 are below; rounds 4 and 5 are recorded in `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/aios-dispositions-r{4,5}.md`. Artifacts: `~/.claude/jobs/c59d1788/tmp/codex-review-r{1..6}/`.\n\n> **Reviewer's stated limitations, carried forward as execution risk.** The review was read-only and static: no files were edited, no tests executed, no Windows filesystem behaviour exercised, and no Pi runtime isolation verified. The host independently ran the embedded-script parse checks, the clause-detection fixtures, the `valid_entry` guard cases and the full canonical-deletion scenario — those results are real. Everything else is an approved *plan*, not proven behaviour. Gates B, C and D still apply.\n\n---\n\n## Global Constraints\n\n- **`~/.claude/skills/` is its own git repo** — `origin https://github.com/erash11/claude-skills.git`, currently in sync with origin. It is *gitignored by the `dotclaude` repo*, which is what the earlier handoff meant; that is not the same as unversioned. **This repo is the safety net and the Mac↔Windows bridge.**\n- **Never commit a symlink into `~/.claude/skills/`.** Windows pulls this repo. A committed absolute Mac symlink arrives there as an unusable file.\n- **Never point Pi at `~/DEV`.** Pi ships no sandbox and no approval prompts — documented, deliberate design.\n- **Never let any harness near `DexaProject`** (identifiable athlete health data, on Google Drive, not in `~/DEV`).\n- **Plugin skills are out of scope.** `superpowers` (14) and `mattpocock-skills` (35) are vendored and overwritten on update. Scope = the 61 skills in `~/.claude/skills/`.\n- **Hooks exist in two copies** (`~/.claude/hooks/`, `~/.codex/hooks/`). So do the sync scripts (`.sh` and `.ps1`). Any change patches both.\n- Global `MEMORY.md` is at 2,489 / 2,500 chars. Consolidate before adding anything.\n- `timeout` is NOT installed on this Mac (no GNU coreutils). Do not use it.\n- `dcg` blocks `find ... -delete`. Use an explicit `rm` on a named path.\n- Python is PEP 668-managed. `python3` with stdlib only, or `uv`. Never global `pip install`.\n- **No success claim without a pasted command output.**\n\n### Measured baseline (2026-09-12, verified on disk this session)\n\n| Fact | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `~/.claude/skills/` SKILL.md count (`find -L`) | 61 |\n| Of those, symlinks pointing **out** to `~/.agents/skills/` | 19 |\n| `~/.claude/skills/` git remote | `erash11/claude-skills`, in sync with origin |\n| Latest commit | `3ab4467 chore: apply global skill audit` — the 99 deletions **are committed and pushed** |\n| `~/.agents/skills/` top-level entries | 306 |\n| Pool entries **not** present in `~/.claude/skills/` | 248 |\n| Archived yesterday to `~/.claude/skills-archive/2026-09-12/` | 99 |\n| Archived skills still live in `~/.agents/skills/` | 98 |\n| `~/.codex/skills/` entries | 32 (27 symlinks, 5 real dirs) |\n| Real dirs in `~/.codex/skills/` diverged from Claude copy | 0 (`dev-sync`, `closeout`, `new-project`, `startup` identical; `session-handoff` is an empty orphan) |\n| `~/.claude/skills/_shared/` | **load-bearing** — `config.json` + `Inference/`, referenced by 10+ skills |\n| Project-level skills in `~/DEV` | 1 (`KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit`) |\n| `~/.claude/rot.md` | does not exist |\n\n**The two lead findings:**\n\n1. **The pool is a stale mirror.** Yesterday's audit cut `~/.claude/skills/` (and committed the cut) but never touched `~/.agents/skills/`. 98 of the 99 archived names are still there, plus 248 entries Claude Code never registered. Pi auto-discovers that folder. Installing Pi today re-exposes everything. Task 2 fixes this and must land before Task 7.\n2. **Three writers already target these folders** and none of them know about the plan: `sync-to-codex.sh`, its `.ps1` twin, and `daily-backup.ps1` (which runs `git add -A` on `~/.claude/skills`). Task 3 fixes the writers *before* moving anything. Round 1 skipped this and would have destroyed canonical copies.\n\n### Codex frontmatter ground truth (read from `~/.codex/skills/.system/*/SKILL.md`)","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2869}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9cbf52dc-15e0-4f13-99a6-d1648f9e43b9","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
