{"parentUuid":"1a6267e5-a6b3-4624-a5d6-f85d8ceefd66","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CezM8vwMWh1e6SGfdVDNS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y79511HWFeqBEBE2ck3jE5","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/.claude/skills\ngit status --short | awk '{print $1}' | sort | uniq -c\necho \"--- files changed other than */SKILL.md ---\"\ngit status --short | grep -v '/SKILL.md$' || echo \"(none)\"\necho \"--- any diff hunk outside frontmatter? (hunk start line > 25) ---\"\ngit diff -U0 | awk '/^\\+\\+\\+ /{f=$2} /^@@/{split($3,x,\",\"); sub(/\\+/,\"\",x[1]); if (x[1]+0>25) print f, $0}' | head\ngit diff --shortstat","description":"Confirm only SKILL.md frontmatter changed"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":859,"cache_read_input_tokens":160854,"output_tokens":1238,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":170},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":859,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":32,"output_tokens":1238,"cache_read_input_tokens":160854,"cache_creation_input_tokens":859,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":859},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CezM8v2GsJh56uPw5NScN","type":"assistant","uuid":"a7deb6f9-1ee1-4f7c-bb6d-593451fcbc0c","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:39:07.425Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"a7deb6f9-1ee1-4f7c-bb6d-593451fcbc0c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y79511HWFeqBEBE2ck3jE5","type":"tool_result","content":"  33 M\n--- files changed other than */SKILL.md ---\n(none)\n--- any diff hunk outside frontmatter? (hunk start line > 25) ---\nb/html-to-pdf/SKILL.md @@ -44 +49 @@ Headless Chrome takes page size/orientation from CSS `@page`. If the source lack\n 33 files changed, 574 insertions(+), 376 deletions(-)\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/kristinarash/DEV","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b57844f1-335b-47e1-b2f9-32f1a9cf0b94","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:39:08.930Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"  33 M\n--- files changed other than */SKILL.md ---\n(none)\n--- any diff hunk outside frontmatter? (hunk start line > 25) ---\nb/html-to-pdf/SKILL.md @@ -44 +49 @@ Headless Chrome takes page size/orientation from CSS `@page`. If the source lack\n 33 files changed, 574 insertions(+), 376 deletions(-)","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/kristinarash/DEV","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a7deb6f9-1ee1-4f7c-bb6d-593451fcbc0c","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"b57844f1-335b-47e1-b2f9-32f1a9cf0b94","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass, and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff actually works — launchd agents (com.keystone.morning, agysync, dailybackup, rituals, reconcile, adjudicate), Python venvs, memsearch, memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget — and diagnose what's silently broken. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- closeout: End-of-session sign-off ritual. Sweeps loose ends, commits and pushes what needs it, verifies memory capture, writes a cross-harness handoff, and reports \"clear to sign off\" or what's still open. Use when the user says \"/closeout\", \"any loose ends?\", \"am I good to sign off?\", \"close out for the night\", \"closing out\", \"goodnight\", \"wrap up for the day\", or asks for a system health check before leaving. Do not use at the start of a session (use startup) or for a standalone automation health check (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- dev-sync: Bring this machine into line with GitHub and load back the context that came with it: locate DEV automatically, pull config and skills, inventory every project, commit and push local work, pull the other machine's work, clone what is missing, report conflicts, then read the repo's STATUS and latest handoff and report where the last session left off. Use when Eric switches machines, sits down to start work, says 'sync DEV', asks whether everything is pushed, or asks where he left off. Do not use when Eric says '/startup' or 'good morning' (use startup, which runs this sync) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- doctor-plus: Runs Claude Code's built-in /doctor health check, then audits the workspace against the 6 then-and-now context-engineering shifts Anthropic shipped with the Claude 5 models (rules to judgement, examples to interfaces, upfront to progressive disclosure, repeats to tool descriptions, CLAUDE.md memory to auto-memory, simple specs to rich references). Reports findings first, fixes only on approval. Use when the user says \"/doctor-plus\", \"doctor plus\", \"doctor-plus\", \"extended doctor\", \"then and now audit\", \"context checkup\". Do not use to check launchd jobs, the memory pipeline, or backups (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator: Generate a '5 Slides in 5 Minutes' PowerPoint deck for Baylor Athletics Applied Performance — research, narrative, pptxgenjs, .pptx build, QA. Use when the user mentions '5 slides,' '5 slides in 5 minutes,' or gives a topic they want a Performance School deck on. Also trigger for a cross-functional staff presentation, a quick sport science deck, or any short educational deck in the established series format (CMJ, GPS, VBT are prior examples). Do not use for Power BI report pages (use pbir-report-builder) or to turn an existing HTML deck into a PDF (use html-to-pdf).\n- forecast-capture: Capture and freeze the CFB Victory Model's pre-game forecasts before kickoff, then report what moved since last week. Run every Wednesday during the season. Use when the user says \"/forecast-capture\", \"capture the forecast\", \"freeze this week's predictions\", \"run the CFB capture\", \"did we capture this week\", or asks whether the weekly model snapshot has been taken. Do not use for live scores, standings, or schedules (use pp-espn).\n- game-film-timestamps: Fix wrong startTime/endTime values in football game film JSON exported from Hudl, DVSport, or similar. Fetches correct wall-clock timestamps from the ESPN API and writes corrected copies. Use when the user mentions fixing or correcting play-by-play timestamps in game film JSON, or has files with \"OFF\", \"DEF\", or \"KICKS\" in the filename and mentions timestamps or time issues. Do not use for live scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for GPS session data (use pp-catapult-openfield-connect).\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- Harvest: Distill a finished project into evergreen Knowledge/ articles in the SecondBrain vault and log the shipped artifact in the output ledger. Use when the user says \"/harvest <project>\", \"harvest this project\", \"distill what we learned from X\", declares a project finished/shipped/done, or when a session ends with a clearly shipped deliverable worth recording. Do not use for a single story or lesson (use capture-insight) or for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- improve-system: Audit the SecondBrain vault for rot — stale seed notes, conflicting claims, and duplicate notes — and propose merge/archive/update actions. Use when the user says \"/improve-system\", \"audit my notes\", \"audit the vault\", \"find stale/duplicate notes\", or \"clean up my second brain\". Do not use for mining sessions for missed learnings (use session-miner), capturing a win or lesson (use capture-insight), improving a skill (use write-a-skill), or scoring the second brain as a system (use brain-audit).\n- ingest-book: Ingest a book (PDF/EPUB full-text, Kindle highlights, or framework-only from a physical book) into the SecondBrain library as atomized, citable advisor entries. Use when the user wants to \"ingest a book\", \"add a book to my library/advisors\", \"/ingest-book\", or points at a book file for the advisor system. Do not use for articles, transcripts, or general resources (use ingest-resource).\n- ingest-resource: Ingest any article, YouTube link, transcript, PDF, or note into the SecondBrain vault — routes it to Knowledge/ (stable info) or Projects/ (active work), adds a summary block, and cross-links related notes with [[wikilinks]]. Use when the user shares a URL, file, or pasted content and says \"ingest\", \"save this\", \"add to my vault/second brain\", \"/ingest-resource\", or drops a resource with intent to keep it. Do not use for a whole book (use ingest-book) or for a plain fact to remember (use memory-write).\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync: Sync new Fireflies transcripts into the SecondBrain vault; audit vault filenames for drift against Fireflies titles. Idempotent. Use when the user says \"sync meetings\", \"pull latest meetings\", \"/meeting-sync\", \"update meeting notes\", \"any new meetings?\", or when invoked by weekly-review. Also for filename drift — \"are any meeting files misnamed?\", \"did a meeting get renamed after export?\", \"why is this meeting titled differently in Fireflies than in the vault?\", \"audit the meeting filenames\". Do not use to rename generic titles inside Fireflies (use name-meetings) or to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system at ~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/. Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". For writes, selects the correct tier and enforces deduplication and character caps. For recall, checks the injected snapshot first, then follows the memsearch retrieval ladder. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or for an article or resource to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project: Scaffold a new project in DEV the standard way: git repo, AGENTS.md with the session routine, CLAUDE.md pointer, docs/STATUS.md, .gitignore, private GitHub repo under erash11. Use when Eric starts a new project or says 'set this up as a repo'. Do not use to log an idea in the portfolio registry (use project) or to sync repos that already exist (use dev-sync).\n- pbir-report-builder: Generates Power BI report pages, visuals, and IBCS-compliant variance charts by writing PBIR JSON into PBIP project folders. Use when the user wants to create a Power BI page, add visuals, generate KPI cards, build charts, tables or dashboard layouts, or mentions 'PBIR', 'IBCS', 'variance chart', 'variance table', 'actual vs plan'. Do not use for PowerPoint decks (use five-slides-generator) or HTML dashboards outside Power BI.\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect: Every Catapult GPS metric — ACWR, velocity benchmarks, load reports — from one command, with a local SQLite mirror for offline analysis. Use when the user says `check ACWR for the squad`, `what is the player load from today's session`, `compare the last two matches`, `who is at injury risk`, `generate a weekly load report`, `sync Catapult data`, `use catapult-openfield-connect`, `run catapult CLI`. Do not use for research questions about workload or injury (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- pp-fireflies: Query Fireflies via fireflies-pp-cli — offline transcript search, cross-meeting intelligence, speaker analytics, local SQLite. Use when the user says `find stale action items from meetings`, `search my meeting transcripts for`, `who talked most in recent meetings`, `sync fireflies meetings`, `use fireflies-pp-cli`, `run fireflies`, `what did we discuss with`. Do not use for writing vault summaries (use meeting-sync).\n- pp-zotero-research-library: Your entire Zotero research library, searchable offline — version-based incremental sync into SQLite with full-text search no other Zotero tool has. Use when the user says `search my Zotero library`, `what does my library say about`, `find papers about`, `sync my Zotero library`, `what did I add to Zotero this week`, `get the citation for`, `use zotero-research-library`, `run zotero cli`. Do not use for a substantive sport-science question that needs a grounded, cited answer (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage: Generate the DEV/ project triage HTML dashboard for today or a backfill date, surfacing drift between curated and current state. Use when the user says \"project triage\", \"/project-triage\", \"run my project review\", \"dev dashboard\", \"what's open in DEV\", \"morning review\", or asks to see the open-projects HTML. Do not use to log ideas or progress in the portfolio registry (use project) or for one project's meeting brief (use meeting-prep).\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup: Start of day, or sitting down at either machine: sync this machine against GitHub, then report where the last session left off and wait. Use when Eric says '/startup', 'good morning', 'starting up', 'where did I leave off', 'what was I working on', 'picking this back up', or opens a session without naming a task. Do not use for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review: Friday close-of-week synthesis ritual. Pulls the last 7 days of meetings, interview notes, and permanent notes from the SecondBrain vault and produces a single weekly review note covering themes, project status, decisions, stale action items, essay angles, and next-week priorities. Use when user says \"/weekly-review\", \"weekly review\", \"Friday review\", \"let's do this week's review\", \"weekly synthesis\", or \"what should I work on next week?\". Do not use for the end-of-day interview (use interview-me) or the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report).","skillCount":29,"isInitial":false,"names":["claude-stack-doctor","closeout","dev-sync","doctor-plus","fireflies-intake","five-slides-generator","forecast-capture","game-film-timestamps","graphify","Harvest","html-to-pdf","improve-system","ingest-book","ingest-resource","meeting-prep","meeting-sync","memory-write","name-meetings","new-project","pbir-report-builder","pp-catapult-openfield-connect","pp-fireflies","pp-zotero-research-library","project","project-triage","sports-science-research-grounding","startup","status-report","weekly-review"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ba9e6da1-97e6-437a-8f2d-1419551bbe48","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:39:08.935Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- claude-stack-doctor: Health-check Eric's automation/memory stack in one pass, and verify the Mac-to-Windows handoff actually works — launchd agents (com.keystone.morning, agysync, dailybackup, rituals, reconcile, adjudicate), Python venvs, memsearch, memory-compiler flush pipeline, backup freshness, and context budget — and diagnose what's silently broken. Use when the user says \"/claude-stack-doctor\", \"stack doctor\", \"is my stack healthy\", \"did my scheduled jobs run\", \"check the memory pipeline\", \"why didn't the backup run\", or when closeout/morning sessions should verify automation health. Do not use to run the backup itself (use backup) or to audit context-engineering practice (use doctor-plus).\n- closeout: End-of-session sign-off ritual. Sweeps loose ends, commits and pushes what needs it, verifies memory capture, writes a cross-harness handoff, and reports \"clear to sign off\" or what's still open. Use when the user says \"/closeout\", \"any loose ends?\", \"am I good to sign off?\", \"close out for the night\", \"closing out\", \"goodnight\", \"wrap up for the day\", or asks for a system health check before leaving. Do not use at the start of a session (use startup) or for a standalone automation health check (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- dev-sync: Bring this machine into line with GitHub and load back the context that came with it: locate DEV automatically, pull config and skills, inventory every project, commit and push local work, pull the other machine's work, clone what is missing, report conflicts, then read the repo's STATUS and latest handoff and report where the last session left off. Use when Eric switches machines, sits down to start work, says 'sync DEV', asks whether everything is pushed, or asks where he left off. Do not use when Eric says '/startup' or 'good morning' (use startup, which runs this sync) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- doctor-plus: Runs Claude Code's built-in /doctor health check, then audits the workspace against the 6 then-and-now context-engineering shifts Anthropic shipped with the Claude 5 models (rules to judgement, examples to interfaces, upfront to progressive disclosure, repeats to tool descriptions, CLAUDE.md memory to auto-memory, simple specs to rich references). Reports findings first, fixes only on approval. Use when the user says \"/doctor-plus\", \"doctor plus\", \"doctor-plus\", \"extended doctor\", \"then and now audit\", \"context checkup\". Do not use to check launchd jobs, the memory pipeline, or backups (use claude-stack-doctor).\n- fireflies-intake: One-command Fireflies pipeline — sync new meeting transcripts into the SecondBrain vault, rename generically-titled meetings, then report exactly what was ingested. Use when the user says \"/fireflies-intake\", \"ingest my recent fireflies meetings\", \"sync meetings that aren't in the second brain yet\", \"pull in the new meetings\", \"meeting ingestion\", or asks whether all recent meetings have been ingested. Do not use to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- five-slides-generator: Generate a '5 Slides in 5 Minutes' PowerPoint deck for Baylor Athletics Applied Performance — research, narrative, pptxgenjs, .pptx build, QA. Use when the user mentions '5 slides,' '5 slides in 5 minutes,' or gives a topic they want a Performance School deck on. Also trigger for a cross-functional staff presentation, a quick sport science deck, or any short educational deck in the established series format (CMJ, GPS, VBT are prior examples). Do not use for Power BI report pages (use pbir-report-builder) or to turn an existing HTML deck into a PDF (use html-to-pdf).\n- forecast-capture: Capture and freeze the CFB Victory Model's pre-game forecasts before kickoff, then report what moved since last week. Run every Wednesday during the season. Use when the user says \"/forecast-capture\", \"capture the forecast\", \"freeze this week's predictions\", \"run the CFB capture\", \"did we capture this week\", or asks whether the weekly model snapshot has been taken. Do not use for live scores, standings, or schedules (use pp-espn).\n- game-film-timestamps: Fix wrong startTime/endTime values in football game film JSON exported from Hudl, DVSport, or similar. Fetches correct wall-clock timestamps from the ESPN API and writes corrected copies. Use when the user mentions fixing or correcting play-by-play timestamps in game film JSON, or has files with \"OFF\", \"DEF\", or \"KICKS\" in the filename and mentions timestamps or time issues. Do not use for live scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for GPS session data (use pp-catapult-openfield-connect).\n- graphify: Use for any question about a codebase, its architecture, file relationships, or project content — especially when graphify-out/ exists, where the question should be treated as a graphify query first. Turns any input (code, docs, papers, images, videos) into a persistent knowledge graph with god nodes, community detection, and query/path/explain tools. Do not use for a quick lookup of one known file or symbol, or for sport-science literature questions (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- Harvest: Distill a finished project into evergreen Knowledge/ articles in the SecondBrain vault and log the shipped artifact in the output ledger. Use when the user says \"/harvest <project>\", \"harvest this project\", \"distill what we learned from X\", declares a project finished/shipped/done, or when a session ends with a clearly shipped deliverable worth recording. Do not use for a single story or lesson (use capture-insight) or for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- html-to-pdf: Render any local HTML report, deck, or dashboard to a print-ready PDF via headless Chrome — replaces the manual Ctrl+P → Save as PDF ritual. Use when the user says \"export this to PDF\", \"print version\", \"PDF for the coaches\", \"make this printable\", or a coach-facing HTML deliverable needs a shareable/printable copy. Do not use to build the report or deck itself (use status-report or five-slides-generator).\n- improve-system: Audit the SecondBrain vault for rot — stale seed notes, conflicting claims, and duplicate notes — and propose merge/archive/update actions. Use when the user says \"/improve-system\", \"audit my notes\", \"audit the vault\", \"find stale/duplicate notes\", or \"clean up my second brain\". Do not use for mining sessions for missed learnings (use session-miner), capturing a win or lesson (use capture-insight), improving a skill (use write-a-skill), or scoring the second brain as a system (use brain-audit).\n- ingest-book: Ingest a book (PDF/EPUB full-text, Kindle highlights, or framework-only from a physical book) into the SecondBrain library as atomized, citable advisor entries. Use when the user wants to \"ingest a book\", \"add a book to my library/advisors\", \"/ingest-book\", or points at a book file for the advisor system. Do not use for articles, transcripts, or general resources (use ingest-resource).\n- ingest-resource: Ingest any article, YouTube link, transcript, PDF, or note into the SecondBrain vault — routes it to Knowledge/ (stable info) or Projects/ (active work), adds a summary block, and cross-links related notes with [[wikilinks]]. Use when the user shares a URL, file, or pasted content and says \"ingest\", \"save this\", \"add to my vault/second brain\", \"/ingest-resource\", or drops a resource with intent to keep it. Do not use for a whole book (use ingest-book) or for a plain fact to remember (use memory-write).\n- meeting-prep: Pre-meeting readiness brief for a project — what changed, what's open, every deliverable path. Use when the user says \"/meeting-prep <project>\", \"I have a meeting with <person> — are we in a good spot on <project>?\", \"verify we're up to speed before the meeting\", \"get me ready for this afternoon's meeting\", or names an upcoming coach/staff meeting. Do not use for the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report) or to sync meeting transcripts (use meeting-sync).\n- meeting-sync: Sync new Fireflies transcripts into the SecondBrain vault; audit vault filenames for drift against Fireflies titles. Idempotent. Use when the user says \"sync meetings\", \"pull latest meetings\", \"/meeting-sync\", \"update meeting notes\", \"any new meetings?\", or when invoked by weekly-review. Also for filename drift — \"are any meeting files misnamed?\", \"did a meeting get renamed after export?\", \"why is this meeting titled differently in Fireflies than in the vault?\", \"audit the meeting filenames\". Do not use to rename generic titles inside Fireflies (use name-meetings) or to search what was said in transcripts (use pp-fireflies).\n- memory-write: Writes, updates, removes, and recalls facts in the two-tier memory system at ~/.claude/global-memory/ and ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/memory/. Use for write requests such as \"remember this\", \"note that\", \"update memory\", \"save this\", and \"forget about\", and for recall requests such as \"did we already…?\", \"what did we decide about X?\", and \"do you remember…?\". For writes, selects the correct tier and enforces deduplication and character caps. For recall, checks the injected snapshot first, then follows the memsearch retrieval ladder. Do not use for a story or lesson worth a vault note (use capture-insight) or for an article or resource to keep (use ingest-resource).\n- name-meetings: Detects Fireflies meetings with generic titles (empty, \"Zoom Meeting\", \"Recording\", date-only, etc.), proposes structured names based on AI summary + participants, and applies approved renames via the Fireflies API. Use when user says \"/name-meetings\", \"name my meetings\", \"rename untitled meetings\", \"fix meeting titles\", \"my meetings have no names\", or when invoked by meeting-sync. Do not use to sync transcripts or audit vault filenames (use meeting-sync).\n- new-project: Scaffold a new project in DEV the standard way: git repo, AGENTS.md with the session routine, CLAUDE.md pointer, docs/STATUS.md, .gitignore, private GitHub repo under erash11. Use when Eric starts a new project or says 'set this up as a repo'. Do not use to log an idea in the portfolio registry (use project) or to sync repos that already exist (use dev-sync).\n- pbir-report-builder: Generates Power BI report pages, visuals, and IBCS-compliant variance charts by writing PBIR JSON into PBIP project folders. Use when the user wants to create a Power BI page, add visuals, generate KPI cards, build charts, tables or dashboard layouts, or mentions 'PBIR', 'IBCS', 'variance chart', 'variance table', 'actual vs plan'. Do not use for PowerPoint decks (use five-slides-generator) or HTML dashboards outside Power BI.\n- pp-catapult-openfield-connect: Every Catapult GPS metric — ACWR, velocity benchmarks, load reports — from one command, with a local SQLite mirror for offline analysis. Use when the user says `check ACWR for the squad`, `what is the player load from today's session`, `compare the last two matches`, `who is at injury risk`, `generate a weekly load report`, `sync Catapult data`, `use catapult-openfield-connect`, `run catapult CLI`. Do not use for research questions about workload or injury (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- pp-fireflies: Query Fireflies via fireflies-pp-cli — offline transcript search, cross-meeting intelligence, speaker analytics, local SQLite. Use when the user says `find stale action items from meetings`, `search my meeting transcripts for`, `who talked most in recent meetings`, `sync fireflies meetings`, `use fireflies-pp-cli`, `run fireflies`, `what did we discuss with`. Do not use for writing vault summaries (use meeting-sync).\n- pp-zotero-research-library: Your entire Zotero research library, searchable offline — version-based incremental sync into SQLite with full-text search no other Zotero tool has. Use when the user says `search my Zotero library`, `what does my library say about`, `find papers about`, `sync my Zotero library`, `what did I add to Zotero this week`, `get the citation for`, `use zotero-research-library`, `run zotero cli`. Do not use for a substantive sport-science question that needs a grounded, cited answer (use sports-science-research-grounding).\n- project: Manage the portfolio project registry (ideas, updates, ranking, promotion, dev stages) and render/publish the dashboard. Use when the user says \"/project\", \"new idea:\", \"log progress on\", \"project update\", \"add to my project inbox\", \"show my projects\", \"re-rank my projects\", \"set the stage on\", \"mark <project> as spec'd/planned/shipped\". Do not use for the DEV drift dashboard (use project-triage) or to scaffold a new repo (use new-project).\n- project-triage: Generate the DEV/ project triage HTML dashboard for today or a backfill date, surfacing drift between curated and current state. Use when the user says \"project triage\", \"/project-triage\", \"run my project review\", \"dev dashboard\", \"what's open in DEV\", \"morning review\", or asks to see the open-projects HTML. Do not use to log ideas or progress in the portfolio registry (use project) or for one project's meeting brief (use meeting-prep).\n- sports-science-research-grounding: Use when the user asks a substantive question about sport science, return to play or sport, rehabilitation, strength and conditioning, workload, recovery, testing, athlete health, injury, performance, or wants a research-backed answer, and for Zotero full-library batch processing. Searches the user's Zotero library first, clearly labels external peer-reviewed fallback sources, and applies tier-aware model routing during bounded batch work. Do not use for sports scores or schedules (use pp-espn) or for a simple Zotero search or sync (use pp-zotero-research-library).\n- startup: Start of day, or sitting down at either machine: sync this machine against GitHub, then report where the last session left off and wait. Use when Eric says '/startup', 'good morning', 'starting up', 'where did I leave off', 'what was I working on', 'picking this back up', or opens a session without naming a task. Do not use for the end-of-session sign-off (use closeout).\n- status-report: Build the every-two-weeks Applied Performance status report for Kenny Boyd and Chris Ruf — paste-ready email, one-page PDF, internal record. Use when the user says \"/status-report\", \"build my status report\", \"time for the Chris report\", \"biweekly update\", \"status update for Chris and Kenny\", \"what do I send Chris this week\", or when a scheduled report date arrives. Do not use for a single meeting brief (use meeting-prep) or the end-of-day supervisor log interview (use interview-me).\n- weekly-review: Friday close-of-week synthesis ritual. Pulls the last 7 days of meetings, interview notes, and permanent notes from the SecondBrain vault and produces a single weekly review note covering themes, project status, decisions, stale action items, essay angles, and next-week priorities. Use when user says \"/weekly-review\", \"weekly review\", \"Friday review\", \"let's do this week's review\", \"weekly synthesis\", or \"what should I work on next week?\". Do not use for the end-of-day interview (use interview-me) or the biweekly supervisor report (use status-report).\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
