{"parentUuid":"e6797677-76ae-48cc-8583-cba42e72c1a5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CezMuUiGqKpqSzkdebV81","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015wmpkDLLsQQ2gi6F9epThp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md","limit":30},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2405,"cache_read_input_tokens":253684,"output_tokens":1095,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":455},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2405,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":32,"output_tokens":1095,"cache_read_input_tokens":253684,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2405,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2405},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CezMuTWb2kZa2J1GKMASM","type":"assistant","uuid":"9bd5fc31-22a3-48bf-9328-88a56753fa7d","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:49:07.700Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"9bd5fc31-22a3-48bf-9328-88a56753fa7d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015wmpkDLLsQQ2gi6F9epThp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: os-audit\n3\tdescription: Use when someone asks to run an OS audit, check their AIOS for stale or outdated data, verify routing points at things that actually exist, find duplicate or bloated folders, clean up or organize their project, check for context failure modes (poisoning, bloat, confusion, clash), or says \"os audit\", \"is my setup stale\", \"run a data audit\", \"my project root is a mess\", or \"my agent keeps missing things that are there\". Read-only drift, freshness, and organization audit of the current project.\n4\targument-hint: \"[optional: a subfolder to scope the audit to]\"\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t# OS Audit — is your AIOS still true?\n8\t\n9\tYour operating manual, indexes, and wikis are claims about what exists and what's current. This audit checks every claim against reality. Structure problems are loud; freshness problems are silent. When people say \"my agent keeps forgetting things,\" it's usually the agent faithfully reading a frozen index.\n10\t\n11\t**Read-only.** Never fix, move, rename, or delete anything during the audit. The only write is the report file at the end. Fixes happen after the user approves them.\n12\t\n13\t**Works on any Claude Code project.** Look for patterns and intent, not exact paths. The operating manual might be CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, or a README with a routing table. An index might be `_index.md`, `INDEX.md`, or a catalog section. A wiki might be one folder or ten. Detect by role, not by name. Never assume a specific person's folder layout.\n14\t\n15\t## Today's context\n16\t\n17\t- Date: use today's real date; all freshness math depends on it\n18\t- Scope: the current project root, or the subfolder passed as `$ARGUMENTS`\n19\t\n20\t## The lens: four failure modes, two context types\n21\t\n22\tEvery finding this audit produces is a risk of one of the four ways context breaks (field vocabulary via LangChain/Drew Breunig; \"bloat\" names the cause):\n23\t\n24\t- **Poisoning** — false information sits where the agent reads it, and the model treats everything in context as true. A stale number stated as current, an index claiming \"recently active\" about a month-old list, an unlabeled snapshot of data that lives live elsewhere.\n25\t- **Bloat** — too much piles in and the model loses the thread. Oversized always-loaded files, scratch inside the knowledge tree, one mega-store where segmented stores should be.\n26\t- **Confusion** — something the agent needs is missing, or something off-topic is present. Unmapped folders, data pulled but never ingested, off-domain notes in the knowledge layer.\n27\t- **Clash** — two pieces of context contradict, usually old versus new. Duplicate folders, the same fact living in two stores at two ages, rules added piecewise with no declared winner.\n28\t\n29\tAnchor: poisoning is false, bloat is too much, confusion is wrong-or-missing, clash is contradictory. **Tag every finding in the report with the failure mode it feeds.** A finding that feeds none of the four is probably cosmetic; say so and rank it last.\n30\t"}]},"uuid":"db760e8c-4fbc-45c1-a8ae-6185e268231f","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:49:08.225Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/.claude/skills/os-audit/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: os-audit\ndescription: Use when someone asks to run an OS audit, check their AIOS for stale or outdated data, verify routing points at things that actually exist, find duplicate or bloated folders, clean up or organize their project, check for context failure modes (poisoning, bloat, confusion, clash), or says \"os audit\", \"is my setup stale\", \"run a data audit\", \"my project root is a mess\", or \"my agent keeps missing things that are there\". Read-only drift, freshness, and organization audit of the current project.\nargument-hint: \"[optional: a subfolder to scope the audit to]\"\n---\n\n# OS Audit — is your AIOS still true?\n\nYour operating manual, indexes, and wikis are claims about what exists and what's current. This audit checks every claim against reality. Structure problems are loud; freshness problems are silent. When people say \"my agent keeps forgetting things,\" it's usually the agent faithfully reading a frozen index.\n\n**Read-only.** Never fix, move, rename, or delete anything during the audit. The only write is the report file at the end. Fixes happen after the user approves them.\n\n**Works on any Claude Code project.** Look for patterns and intent, not exact paths. The operating manual might be CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, or a README with a routing table. An index might be `_index.md`, `INDEX.md`, or a catalog section. A wiki might be one folder or ten. Detect by role, not by name. Never assume a specific person's folder layout.\n\n## Today's context\n\n- Date: use today's real date; all freshness math depends on it\n- Scope: the current project root, or the subfolder passed as `$ARGUMENTS`\n\n## The lens: four failure modes, two context types\n\nEvery finding this audit produces is a risk of one of the four ways context breaks (field vocabulary via LangChain/Drew Breunig; \"bloat\" names the cause):\n\n- **Poisoning** — false information sits where the agent reads it, and the model treats everything in context as true. A stale number stated as current, an index claiming \"recently active\" about a month-old list, an unlabeled snapshot of data that lives live elsewhere.\n- **Bloat** — too much piles in and the model loses the thread. Oversized always-loaded files, scratch inside the knowledge tree, one mega-store where segmented stores should be.\n- **Confusion** — something the agent needs is missing, or something off-topic is present. Unmapped folders, data pulled but never ingested, off-domain notes in the knowledge layer.\n- **Clash** — two pieces of context contradict, usually old versus new. Duplicate folders, the same fact living in two stores at two ages, rules added piecewise with no declared winner.\n\nAnchor: poisoning is false, bloat is too much, confusion is wrong-or-missing, clash is contradictory. **Tag every finding in the report with the failure mode it feeds.** A finding that feeds none of the four is probably cosmetic; say so and rank it last.\n","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":167}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9bd5fc31-22a3-48bf-9328-88a56753fa7d","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"db760e8c-4fbc-45c1-a8ae-6185e268231f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CezMuUiGqKpqSzkdebV81","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Fe4Rpiw3WXiLZTZCkBe38A","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","offset":2341,"limit":495},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2405,"cache_read_input_tokens":253684,"output_tokens":1095,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":455},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2405,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":32,"output_tokens":1095,"cache_read_input_tokens":253684,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2405,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2405},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CezMuTWb2kZa2J1GKMASM","type":"assistant","uuid":"e0c47fcf-f3d7-4669-a865-c266f4173e26","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:49:08.830Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
