{"parentUuid":"858b4e6b-46e5-40c7-9afc-6a580814cdbc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_additional_context","content":["<context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>"],"hookName":"PreToolUse:Read","toolUseID":"toolu_01Fe4Rpiw3WXiLZTZCkBe38A","hookEvent":"PreToolUse"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"09310469-5aa7-4941-809b-ed3710e97b79","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:49:09.021Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nPreToolUse:Read hook additional context: <context_guidance>\n  <tip>\n    Reading to Edit the file? Read is correct — Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against.\n    Reading to analyze, summarize, or extract from the file? Use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code) — the bytes stay in the sandbox and only what your code prints enters your conversation.\n  </tip>\n</context_guidance>\n</system-reminder>"}],"session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
{"parentUuid":"e0c47fcf-f3d7-4669-a865-c266f4173e26","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fe4Rpiw3WXiLZTZCkBe38A","type":"tool_result","content":"2341\t### Task 7: Put the metrics on a monthly clock\n2342\t\n2343\t**Files:**\n2344\t- Create: `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`\n2345\t- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`\n2346\t- Modify: `~/.claude/rot.md` (cadence note)\n2347\t\n2348\t**Interfaces:**\n2349\t- Consumes: `aios-metrics.py` from Task 6.\n2350\t- Produces: a monthly trend row in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`. Terminal deliverable.\n2351\t\n2352\t**Design choice, tightened after R9.** The job runs the **metrics script only**, appends a trend row, and writes a nudge. It does **not** commit, does **not** push, and does **not** run the agentic audit. Reasons, all from R9: committing from the shared working tree publishes whatever the user had staged; a `git push` failure is easy to mask; and the 09:00 backup may run up to 240 minutes, so start-time spacing is not a race guard. The human commits the trend row during the audit. The job's only job is to make the audit impossible to forget.\n2353\t\n2354\t- [ ] **Step 1: Write the cron wrapper**\n2355\t\n2356\tCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`:\n2357\t\n2358\t```bash\n2359\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n2360\t# Monthly: append AIOS metrics to the trend table and nudge for a real audit.\n2361\t# Deliberately does NOT commit, push, or run the agentic audit (see R9):\n2362\t#  - committing from the shared tree would publish the user's staged files\n2363\t#  - the agentic audit proposes deletions and needs a human reading them\n2364\tset -uo pipefail\n2365\t\n2366\tREPO=\"$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS\"\n2367\tTREND=\"$REPO/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\n2368\tLOG=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\"\n2369\tLOCK=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\"\n2370\tmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$LOG\")\" \"$(dirname \"$TREND\")\" \"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts\"\n2371\t\n2372\texec >>\"$LOG\" 2>&1\n2373\techo \"=== $(date -u +%FT%TZ) aios-audit-cron ===\"\n2374\t\n2375\t# Single-instance lock. mkdir is atomic; no flock on stock macOS.\n2376\t# R2-06: a kill or reboot must not wedge every future run. The lock records\n2377\t# its owner PID; a lock whose owner is gone, or that is older than a day, is\n2378\t# stale and gets reclaimed.\n2379\tacquire_lock() {\n2380\t  if mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then\n2381\t    echo $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"\n2382\t    return 0\n2383\t  fi\n2384\t  local owner=\"\"\n2385\t  [ -f \"$LOCK/pid\" ] && owner=\"$(cat \"$LOCK/pid\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n2386\t  if [ -n \"$owner\" ] && kill -0 \"$owner\" 2>/dev/null; then\n2387\t    echo \"ABORT live run (pid $owner) holds $LOCK\"\n2388\t    return 1\n2389\t  fi\n2390\t  # Owner is dead, or the lock has no pid file at all.\n2391\t  local age_days\n2392\t  age_days=\"$(find \"$LOCK\" -maxdepth 0 -mtime +1 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\n2393\t  if [ -z \"$owner\" ] && [ \"$age_days\" = \"0\" ]; then\n2394\t    echo \"ABORT lock with no owner, younger than a day - waiting one cycle\"\n2395\t    return 1\n2396\t  fi\n2397\t  echo \"RECLAIM stale lock (owner '${owner:-none}' not running)\"\n2398\t  rm -rf \"$LOCK\"\n2399\t  if mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then\n2400\t    echo $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"\n2401\t    return 0\n2402\t  fi\n2403\t  echo \"ABORT could not reclaim $LOCK\"\n2404\t  return 1\n2405\t}\n2406\t\n2407\tif ! acquire_lock; then\n2408\t  exit 0\n2409\tfi\n2410\ttrap 'rm -rf \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null' EXIT\n2411\t\n2412\tif ! M=\"$(python3 \"$REPO/scripts/aios-metrics.py\" 2>&1)\"; then\n2413\t  echo \"FAIL metrics script errored:\"\n2414\t  echo \"$M\"\n2415\t  exit 1\n2416\tfi\n2417\techo \"$M\"\n2418\t\n2419\tget() {\n2420\t  local v\n2421\t  v=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$M\" | grep \"^$1=\" | cut -d= -f2-)\"\n2422\t  if [ -z \"$v\" ]; then echo \"MISSING\"; else echo \"$v\"; fi\n2423\t}\n2424\t\n2425\tif [ ! -f \"$TREND\" ]; then\n2426\t  {\n2427\t    echo \"# AIOS trend\"\n2428\t    echo\n2429\t    echo \"One row per monthly run. Written by scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh.\"\n2430\t    echo \"The script does not commit; the human commits during the audit.\"\n2431\t    echo\n2432\t    echo \"| date | owned | third-party | pool | codex | project | never used | roster tok | portability fails | tracked links |\"\n2433\t    echo \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"\n2434\t  } > \"$TREND\"\n2435\tfi\n2436\t\n2437\t# R7-04: these keys MUST match aios-metrics.py exactly. A renamed metric here\n2438\t# produces MISSING, which aborts the run before any row or nudge is written -\n2439\t# i.e. one rename silently kills the monthly job.\n2440\tROW=\"$(printf '| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |' \\\n2441\t  \"$(get date)\" \"$(get canonical_skills)\" \"$(get third_party_links_in_canonical)\" \\\n2442\t  \"$(get pool_skills)\" \"$(get codex_skills)\" \\\n2443\t  \"$(get project_skills)\" \"$(get never_used)\" \"$(get roster_tokens_est)\" \\\n2444\t  \"$(get portability_failures)\" \"$(get tracked_symlinks_in_canonical)\")\"\n2445\t\n2446\tif printf '%s' \"$ROW\" | grep -q 'MISSING'; then\n2447\t  echo \"FAIL a metrics key did not resolve; row not written:\"\n2448\t  echo \"$ROW\"\n2449\t  exit 1\n2450\tfi\n2451\t\n2452\t# R2-06: check every required write. Without errexit, a full disk or a\n2453\t# read-only path would fail silently and the job would still report success.\n2454\tif ! printf '%s\\n' \"$ROW\" >> \"$TREND\"; then\n2455\t  echo \"FAIL could not append the trend row to $TREND\"\n2456\t  exit 1\n2457\tfi\n2458\tif ! grep -qF \"$ROW\" \"$TREND\"; then\n2459\t  echo \"FAIL trend row not readable back from $TREND\"\n2460\t  exit 1\n2461\tfi\n2462\techo \"wrote: $ROW\"\n2463\t\n2464\tNUDGE=\"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\"\n2465\tNUDGE_TEXT=\"[$(date +%F)] aios-audit due - run \\\"audit my AI OS\\\" in ~/DEV/KeystoneOS (uncommitted trend row waiting)\"\n2466\tif ! printf '%s\\n' \"$NUDGE_TEXT\" >> \"$NUDGE\"; then\n2467\t  echo \"FAIL could not write the nudge to $NUDGE\"\n2468\t  exit 1\n2469\tfi\n2470\t\n2471\techo \"=== done ok ===\"\n2472\texit 0\n2473\t```\n2474\t\n2475\t```bash\n2476\tchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh\n2477\t```\n2478\t\n2479\t- [ ] **Step 2: Run it by hand and verify the trend row**\n2480\t\n2481\t```bash\n2482\t~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\n2483\tcat ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n2484\ttail -25 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\n2485\tgit -C ~/DEV/KeystoneOS status --short\n2486\t```\n2487\t\n2488\tExpected: `exit=0`; a header plus one row with **no `MISSING` cells**; the log ends `=== done ok ===`; `git status` shows `aios-trend.md` as **modified but uncommitted** — that is correct, the human commits it.\n2489\t\n2490\t**A `MISSING` cell means a `get` key does not match a metrics key.** Confirm the two agree before loading launchd — this is how a rename silently kills the job (R7-04):\n2491\t\n2492\t```bash\n2493\tdiff <(python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py | cut -d= -f1 | sort) \\\n2494\t     <(grep -o '\\$(get [a-z_]*)' ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh \\\n2495\t       | sed 's/\\$(get //;s/)//' | sort -u) \\\n2496\t  | grep '^>' && echo \"ABOVE: keys the wrapper wants that metrics does not emit\" \\\n2497\t  || echo \"every wrapper key is emitted by the metrics script\"\n2498\t```\n2499\t\n2500\tExpected: `every wrapper key is emitted by the metrics script`.\n2501\t\n2502\t- [ ] **Step 3: Prove the failure paths (R9's masked-failure point)**\n2503\t\n2504\t```bash\n2505\t# A bad metrics script must fail the job, not write a row.\n2506\tmv ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py /tmp/aios-metrics.bak\n2507\t~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 1)\"\n2508\tmv /tmp/aios-metrics.bak ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n2509\t\n2510\t# A LIVE lock must make the job stand down.\n2511\tL=~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\n2512\trm -rf \"$L\"; mkdir -p \"$L\"; echo $$ > \"$L/pid\"     # this shell is alive\n2513\t~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 0, ABORT live run)\"\n2514\trows_before=$(grep -c '^| 20' ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md)\n2515\t\n2516\t# A STALE lock must be RECLAIMED, not wedge the job forever (R2-06).\n2517\trm -rf \"$L\"; mkdir -p \"$L\"; echo 999999 > \"$L/pid\"  # a pid that cannot exist\n2518\t~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 0, RECLAIM then run)\"\n2519\trows_after=$(grep -c '^| 20' ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md)\n2520\techo \"rows before=$rows_before after=$rows_after (expect after = before + 1)\"\n2521\trm -rf \"$L\"\n2522\ttail -20 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\n2523\t```\n2524\t\n2525\tExpected:\n2526\t\n2527\t- missing metrics script: `exit=1`, log says `FAIL metrics script errored`, **no** row added\n2528\t- live lock: `exit=0`, log says `ABORT live run (pid ...)`, **no** row added\n2529\t- stale lock: `exit=0`, log says `RECLAIM stale lock`, **and one row IS added** — `rows_after = rows_before + 1`\n2530\t\n2531\tThat last line is the whole point of the fix. Round 2 caught that the earlier test removed the lock by hand, which proved nothing about recovery. If a stale lock does not get reclaimed, one crashed run silences the job permanently — fix before loading launchd.\n2532\t\n2533\t- [ ] **Step 4: Write the launchd plist**\n2534\t\n2535\tCreate `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`. Day 1 at 14:00 — well clear of the 09:00 backup's possible 240-minute window, and the lock covers the rest:\n2536\t\n2537\t```xml\n2538\t<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n2539\t<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\"\n2540\t  \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n2541\t<plist version=\"1.0\">\n2542\t<dict>\n2543\t  <key>Label</key><string>com.keystone.aiosaudit</string>\n2544\t  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n2545\t  <array>\n2546\t    <string>/bin/bash</string>\n2547\t    <string>-lc</string>\n2548\t    <string>$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh</string>\n2549\t  </array>\n2550\t  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n2551\t  <dict>\n2552\t    <key>Day</key><integer>1</integer>\n2553\t    <key>Hour</key><integer>14</integer>\n2554\t    <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>\n2555\t  </dict>\n2556\t  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>\n2557\t  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.out</string>\n2558\t  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err</string>\n2559\t</dict>\n2560\t</plist>\n2561\t```\n2562\t\n2563\t- [ ] **Step 5: Load it and verify launchd accepted it**\n2564\t\n2565\t```bash\n2566\tlaunchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist 2>/dev/null\n2567\tlaunchctl load  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist\n2568\tlaunchctl list | grep aiosaudit\n2569\t```\n2570\t\n2571\tExpected: one line ending `com.keystone.aiosaudit`. A non-zero middle column means the last run failed — read `/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err`.\n2572\t\n2573\t- [ ] **Step 6: Force a run through launchd, not just bash**\n2574\t\n2575\t```bash\n2576\tlaunchctl start com.keystone.aiosaudit\n2577\tcat /tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err\n2578\ttail -5 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n2579\t```\n2580\t\n2581\tExpected: `.err` empty, a second row appended. launchd has a different PATH than an interactive shell — this step catches that. If `python3` is not found, put an absolute interpreter in `aios-metrics.py`'s shebang.\n2582\t\n2583\t- [ ] **Step 7: De-duplicate test rows, note the cadence, commit**\n2584\t\n2585\t```bash\n2586\tcd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n2587\tpython3 - <<'EOF'\n2588\tp = \"docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\n2589\tlines = open(p).read().splitlines()\n2590\tseen, out = set(), []\n2591\tfor l in lines:\n2592\t    if l.startswith(\"| 20\"):\n2593\t        if l in seen:\n2594\t            continue\n2595\t        seen.add(l)\n2596\t    out.append(l)\n2597\topen(p, \"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\")\n2598\tEOF\n2599\tcat docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n2600\tcat >> ~/.claude/rot.md <<'EOF'\n2601\t\n2602\t## Cadence\n2603\t\n2604\tMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 14:00.\n2605\tIt writes a trend row and a nudge. It does not commit and does not push.\n2606\tThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.\n2607\tEOF\n2608\tcp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md\n2609\tgit add scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh docs/audits/aios-trend.md docs/rot.md\n2610\tgit commit -m \"feat: monthly AIOS metrics on launchd, no unattended commits\n2611\t\n2612\tCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n2613\t```\n2614\t\n2615\t---\n2616\t\n2617\t### Task 8: Pi trial under a real boundary\n2618\t\n2619\t**Files:**\n2620\t- Create: macOS standard user `piuser` (or an equivalent enforced boundary)\n2621\t- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md`\n2622\t- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`\n2623\t- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`\n2624\t\n2625\t**Interfaces:**\n2626\t- Consumes: a copy of the pruned pool (Task 2) and the portable frontmatter (Task 5).\n2627\t- Produces: a measured migration cost. Nothing consumes it; it is a decision input.\n2628\t\n2629\t**Why the boundary is mandatory (R5).** Round 1 ran Pi with `cd ~/pi-trial` and called that a sandbox. It is not — `cd` restricts nothing, and the Pi research note says so explicitly. Pi ships no approval prompts by design. A regex denylist inside the agent is defence-in-depth, **not** a boundary: `cat ../DEV/file`, a shell-expanded path, or any tool the guard does not cover walks straight past it. The only enforced boundary available without new infrastructure is **OS file permissions**, so Pi runs as a different user that cannot read Eric's home.\n2630\t\n2631\t**Scope, narrowed.** The deliverable is a **cost number**, not a working Pi setup. **ADOPT is not an available verdict in this plan** — adopting Pi for real work requires a boundary design that is its own project. Verdicts here are `COST-MEASURED` or `BLOCKED`.\n2632\t\n2633\t**Stop conditions.** If the user cannot be created, or Pi will not install, or the guard does not block after two attempts, stop and write up what failed. Do not iterate past that.\n2634\t\n2635\t- [ ] **Step 1: Create the boundary user**\n2636\t\n2637\tThis needs admin rights and is the one step Eric may prefer to run himself:\n2638\t\n2639\t```bash\n2640\tsudo sysadminctl -addUser piuser -fullName \"Pi Trial\" -password - -home /Users/piuser\n2641\tsudo dscl . -read /Users/piuser NFSHomeDirectory\n2642\t```\n2643\t\n2644\tThen prove the boundary actually holds:\n2645\t\n2646\t```bash\n2647\t# R9-07: sysadminctl gives piuser the primary group `staff` (20), and a macOS\n2648\t# home is normally user:staff with group r-x. \"Not world-readable\" is therefore\n2649\t# the wrong test - piuser would read ~ through the GROUP bits. Default chosen\n2650\t# here: give piuser its own primary group. (Tightening ~ to drwx------ is the\n2651\t# alternative; that is Eric's call - record which was used in the report.)\n2652\tdscl . -list /Groups PrimaryGroupID | awk '$2==4242'   # must print nothing (GID unused)\n2653\tsudo dscl . -create /Groups/pitrial\n2654\tsudo dscl . -create /Groups/pitrial PrimaryGroupID 4242\n2655\tsudo dscl . -create /Users/piuser PrimaryGroupID 4242\n2656\tid piuser                        # must NOT list staff(20)\n2657\tdseditgroup -o checkmember -m piuser staff   # must say piuser is NOT a member (R10-03: also catches nested membership)\n2658\tdscl . -read /Groups/staff NestedGroups 2>&1  # checked 2026-09-12 on this Mac: \"No such key\" - staff nests nothing here\n2659\tls -ld ~                         # record owner, group and the group bits\n2660\tsudo -u piuser ls ~/DEV          2>&1 | tail -2\n2661\tsudo -u piuser ls ~/SecondBrain  2>&1 | tail -2\n2662\t```\n2663\t\n2664\tExpected: both `ls` attempts print **Permission denied**. If either succeeds, the boundary does not exist — STOP. Do not fall back to \"just be careful.\"\n2665\t\n2666\t- [ ] **Step 2: Install Pi as that user**\n2667\t\n2668\t`npm install -g` would target `/opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules`, which is `drwxr-xr-x kristinarash:admin` — `piuser` cannot write there and the install fails before any measurement happens (R2-08). Install into a prefix `piuser` owns, and call that executable by absolute path:\n2669\t\n2670\t```bash\n2671\tsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/pi-trial /Users/piuser/.npm-global\n2672\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'npm config set prefix /Users/piuser/.npm-global'\n2673\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent' 2>&1 | tail -5\n2674\tPI=/Users/piuser/.npm-global/bin/pi\n2675\tsudo -u piuser \"$PI\" --version\n2676\t```\n2677\t\n2678\tExpected: an install summary and a version string. `--ignore-scripts` is deliberate — no postinstall scripts run. Everything lands under `/Users/piuser/.npm-global`, so teardown is one `rm -rf` and Eric's Homebrew tree is never touched.\n2679\t\n2680\t> Use the absolute `$PI` path in every later step. `piuser`'s login PATH does not include the new prefix, and `which pi` would silently find nothing or, worse, Eric's copy.\n2681\t\n2682\t- [ ] **Step 3: Give it a copy of the pruned pool, not the real one**\n2683\t\n2684\t```bash\n2685\tsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/.agents\n2686\tsudo rsync -a --copy-links ~/.agents/skills/ /Users/piuser/.agents/skills/\n2687\tsudo chown -R piuser:staff /Users/piuser/.agents\n2688\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'ls /Users/piuser/.agents/skills | wc -l'\n2689\t```\n2690\t\n2691\tExpected: the post-Task-4 `pool_skills` value. A **copy**, so a Pi misstep cannot corrupt the real pool.\n2692\t\n2693\t- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm what Pi discovers**\n2694\t\n2695\t```bash\n2696\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cat > /Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md <<EOF\n2697\t# pi-trial\n2698\t\n2699\tThrowaway sandbox for evaluating the Pi harness. Nothing here is real work.\n2700\tThis user has no read access to the operator home directory by design.\n2701\tEOF\n2702\tcd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && /Users/piuser/.npm-global/bin/pi --print \"List every skill you can see. Print only the count and the first ten names.\" 2>&1 | tail -20'\n2703\t```\n2704\t\n2705\tExpected: the `pool_skills` value `aios-metrics.py` reported after Task 4 Step 6 — **not** 306. **If it reads ~306, Task 2 did not take — stop and re-run it.** This step is the proof that pruning the pool was the right fix.\n2706\t\n2707\t> Round 1 had a probe here that asked an unguarded Pi to `ls ~/DEV`. It is deleted. Pointing an unrestrained agent at real directories to demonstrate that it is unrestrained is not a test worth running. The OS boundary in Step 1 already establishes the same fact safely.\n2708\t\n2709\t- [ ] **Step 5: Port `dcg` to a Pi extension, using the documented contract**\n2710\t\n2711\tRead the existing guard first:\n2712\t\n2713\t```bash\n2714\tls ~/.claude/hooks/ | grep -i -E 'dcg|guard'\n2715\tgrep -n 'pi.on\\|ExtensionAPI\\|event.input' ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md\n2716\t```\n2717\t\n2718\tRound 1 used `export default { tool_call }` and `ev.args`. The research note documents an **exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`**, registration via `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, and a payload at `event.input` (R4). Write `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts` to the documented shape. If the note and the installed Pi version disagree, **the installed version wins** — record the discrepancy in the report.\n2719\t\n2720\t```typescript\n2721\t// Minimal port of the dcg guard. Proves the hook -> extension mapping.\n2722\t// Defence in depth only: the real boundary is the piuser file permissions.\n2723\t//\n2724\t// R2-07: match the protected NAME anywhere a path separator or string edge\n2725\t// precedes it, so /Users/piuser/DEV, ~/DEV, ./DEV and a bare DEV/ all hit.\n2726\t// The earlier expression required a whitespace/quote boundary and therefore\n2727\t// missed the very probe used to test it.\n2728\tconst DENY: RegExp[] = [\n2729\t  /\\brm\\s+-rf\\s+\\/(?!tmp)/,\n2730\t  /DexaProject/i,\n2731\t  /GoogleDrive-/,\n2732\t  /(^|[\\s\"'=:/])DEV(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,\n2733\t  /(^|[\\s\"'=:/])SecondBrain(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,\n2734\t];\n2735\t\n2736\texport default function guard(pi: any) {\n2737\t  pi.on(\"tool_call\", (event: any) => {\n2738\t    const blob = JSON.stringify(event?.input ?? {});   // R4: input, not args\n2739\t    console.log(`[guard] saw tool_call: ${blob.slice(0, 200)}`);\n2740\t    for (const re of DENY) {\n2741\t      if (re.test(blob)) {\n2742\t        return { block: true, reason: `guard: blocked by ${re}` };\n2743\t      }\n2744\t    }\n2745\t    return { block: false };\n2746\t  });\n2747\t}\n2748\t```\n2749\t\n2750\tThe `console.log` is deliberate: Step 6 must distinguish \"the guard blocked it\" from \"the handler never ran.\" Print mode may require explicitly trusting a project extension; if Pi asks, grant it for this project only and note the step in the report.\n2751\t\n2752\t- [ ] **Step 6: Prove the guard blocks — on a fixture that really exists**\n2753\t\n2754\tCreate a disposable fixture first, so a block cannot be confused with a missing-directory error (R2-07):\n2755\t\n2756\t```bash\n2757\tsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV\n2758\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'echo canary > /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV/file.txt'\n2759\tsudo -u piuser ls /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV        # proves it exists and is readable\n2760\t```\n2761\t\n2762\tThen run the three probes:\n2763\t\n2764\t```bash\n2765\tPI=/Users/piuser/.npm-global/bin/pi\n2766\t# A: must be BLOCKED by the guard, not by a missing path.\n2767\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc \"cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && $PI --print 'Run: ls /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV' 2>&1 | tail -12\"\n2768\t# B: must SUCCEED - a guard that blocks everything is not a guard.\n2769\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc \"cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && $PI --print 'Run: echo hello' 2>&1 | tail -6\"\n2770\t# C: must SUCCEED - a sibling path that is not protected.\n2771\tsudo -u piuser bash -lc \"cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && $PI --print 'Run: ls /Users/piuser/fixture' 2>&1 | tail -6\"\n2772\t```\n2773\t\n2774\tExpected:\n2775\t\n2776\t- **A** — `[guard] saw tool_call:` appears (the handler ran) **and** the result carries `guard: blocked by`. The fixture exists and is readable, so a \"no such directory\" message would mean the guard did **not** fire.\n2777\t- **B** — prints `hello`, no block.\n2778\t- **C** — lists `DEV`, no block. This is the false-positive check: the guard must key on the path segment, not on any string containing those letters.\n2779\t\n2780\tIf A is not blocked, or the `[guard]` line never appears, after two attempts at the extension path or event name, stop per the stop conditions and record which of the two failed — \"handler never registered\" and \"regex did not match\" are different costs to report.\n2781\t\n2782\t- [ ] **Step 7: Write the cost measurement**\n2783\t\n2784\t```bash\n2785\tcd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\n2786\tcat > docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md <<'EOF'\n2787\t# Pi trial - 2026-09-12\n2788\t\n2789\tBoundary: separate macOS user `piuser`, no read access to the operator home.\n2790\t`cd` into a directory is NOT a boundary and was not treated as one (R5).\n2791\t\n2792\t| Question | Answer |\n2793\t|---|---|\n2794\t| pi version | FILL IN |\n2795\t| piuser blocked from ~/DEV and ~/SecondBrain? | FILL IN (expect: yes, Permission denied) |\n2796\t| skills auto-discovered | FILL IN (expect the post-Task-4 `pool_skills` value, not 306) |\n2797\t| skills needing edits to fire in Pi | FILL IN |\n2798\t| extension contract matched the research note? | FILL IN (factory + pi.on + event.input) |\n2799\t| minutes to port the dcg guard | FILL IN |\n2800\t| hooks still to port | FILL IN of 61 |\n2801\t| verdict | COST-MEASURED / BLOCKED |\n2802\t\n2803\t## What the guard proved\n2804\t\n2805\tFILL IN: paste the blocked and the allowed output from Step 6.\n2806\t\n2807\t## What a real adoption would still need\n2808\t\n2809\tNot in scope here. At minimum: a boundary design that is not a second user\n2810\taccount, an approval-prompt layer, and a port of the remaining hooks.\n2811\tEOF\n2812\t```\n2813\t\n2814\tFill in every `FILL IN` from the real runs. A report left with placeholders is a failed task. Then:\n2815\t\n2816\t```bash\n2817\tcd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md && git commit -m \"docs: Pi harness trial under an OS boundary + dcg guard port cost\n2818\t\n2819\tCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n2820\t```\n2821\t\n2822\t- [ ] **Step 8: Tear down**\n2823\t\n2824\t```bash\n2825\tsudo rm -rf /Users/piuser/pi-trial /Users/piuser/.agents \\\n2826\t            /Users/piuser/.npm-global /Users/piuser/fixture\n2827\tls /Users/piuser\n2828\t# Keep or remove the account - Eric's call. To remove:\n2829\t# sudo sysadminctl -deleteUser piuser\n2830\t```\n2831\t\n2832\tEverything installed lives under `/Users/piuser/.npm-global`, so removing that directory is the whole uninstall — no `npm uninstall -g` needed and nothing outside that user is touched. The account may stay for future trials; the skill copy, the fixture and the Pi install should not linger.\n2833\t\n2834\t---\n2835\t"}]},"uuid":"43e12206-d7f1-468b-8f7f-619b4d66c419","timestamp":"2026-09-12T22:49:09.195Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-harness-agnostic-aios.md","content":"### Task 7: Put the metrics on a monthly clock\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`\n- Modify: `~/.claude/rot.md` (cadence note)\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: `aios-metrics.py` from Task 6.\n- Produces: a monthly trend row in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md`. Terminal deliverable.\n\n**Design choice, tightened after R9.** The job runs the **metrics script only**, appends a trend row, and writes a nudge. It does **not** commit, does **not** push, and does **not** run the agentic audit. Reasons, all from R9: committing from the shared working tree publishes whatever the user had staged; a `git push` failure is easy to mask; and the 09:00 backup may run up to 240 minutes, so start-time spacing is not a race guard. The human commits the trend row during the audit. The job's only job is to make the audit impossible to forget.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Write the cron wrapper**\n\nCreate `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Monthly: append AIOS metrics to the trend table and nudge for a real audit.\n# Deliberately does NOT commit, push, or run the agentic audit (see R9):\n#  - committing from the shared tree would publish the user's staged files\n#  - the agentic audit proposes deletions and needs a human reading them\nset -uo pipefail\n\nREPO=\"$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS\"\nTREND=\"$REPO/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nLOG=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\"\nLOCK=\"$HOME/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\"\nmkdir -p \"$(dirname \"$LOG\")\" \"$(dirname \"$TREND\")\" \"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts\"\n\nexec >>\"$LOG\" 2>&1\necho \"=== $(date -u +%FT%TZ) aios-audit-cron ===\"\n\n# Single-instance lock. mkdir is atomic; no flock on stock macOS.\n# R2-06: a kill or reboot must not wedge every future run. The lock records\n# its owner PID; a lock whose owner is gone, or that is older than a day, is\n# stale and gets reclaimed.\nacquire_lock() {\n  if mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then\n    echo $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n  local owner=\"\"\n  [ -f \"$LOCK/pid\" ] && owner=\"$(cat \"$LOCK/pid\" 2>/dev/null)\"\n  if [ -n \"$owner\" ] && kill -0 \"$owner\" 2>/dev/null; then\n    echo \"ABORT live run (pid $owner) holds $LOCK\"\n    return 1\n  fi\n  # Owner is dead, or the lock has no pid file at all.\n  local age_days\n  age_days=\"$(find \"$LOCK\" -maxdepth 0 -mtime +1 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\n  if [ -z \"$owner\" ] && [ \"$age_days\" = \"0\" ]; then\n    echo \"ABORT lock with no owner, younger than a day - waiting one cycle\"\n    return 1\n  fi\n  echo \"RECLAIM stale lock (owner '${owner:-none}' not running)\"\n  rm -rf \"$LOCK\"\n  if mkdir \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null; then\n    echo $$ > \"$LOCK/pid\"\n    return 0\n  fi\n  echo \"ABORT could not reclaim $LOCK\"\n  return 1\n}\n\nif ! acquire_lock; then\n  exit 0\nfi\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$LOCK\" 2>/dev/null' EXIT\n\nif ! M=\"$(python3 \"$REPO/scripts/aios-metrics.py\" 2>&1)\"; then\n  echo \"FAIL metrics script errored:\"\n  echo \"$M\"\n  exit 1\nfi\necho \"$M\"\n\nget() {\n  local v\n  v=\"$(printf '%s\\n' \"$M\" | grep \"^$1=\" | cut -d= -f2-)\"\n  if [ -z \"$v\" ]; then echo \"MISSING\"; else echo \"$v\"; fi\n}\n\nif [ ! -f \"$TREND\" ]; then\n  {\n    echo \"# AIOS trend\"\n    echo\n    echo \"One row per monthly run. Written by scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh.\"\n    echo \"The script does not commit; the human commits during the audit.\"\n    echo\n    echo \"| date | owned | third-party | pool | codex | project | never used | roster tok | portability fails | tracked links |\"\n    echo \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\"\n  } > \"$TREND\"\nfi\n\n# R7-04: these keys MUST match aios-metrics.py exactly. A renamed metric here\n# produces MISSING, which aborts the run before any row or nudge is written -\n# i.e. one rename silently kills the monthly job.\nROW=\"$(printf '| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |' \\\n  \"$(get date)\" \"$(get canonical_skills)\" \"$(get third_party_links_in_canonical)\" \\\n  \"$(get pool_skills)\" \"$(get codex_skills)\" \\\n  \"$(get project_skills)\" \"$(get never_used)\" \"$(get roster_tokens_est)\" \\\n  \"$(get portability_failures)\" \"$(get tracked_symlinks_in_canonical)\")\"\n\nif printf '%s' \"$ROW\" | grep -q 'MISSING'; then\n  echo \"FAIL a metrics key did not resolve; row not written:\"\n  echo \"$ROW\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n\n# R2-06: check every required write. Without errexit, a full disk or a\n# read-only path would fail silently and the job would still report success.\nif ! printf '%s\\n' \"$ROW\" >> \"$TREND\"; then\n  echo \"FAIL could not append the trend row to $TREND\"\n  exit 1\nfi\nif ! grep -qF \"$ROW\" \"$TREND\"; then\n  echo \"FAIL trend row not readable back from $TREND\"\n  exit 1\nfi\necho \"wrote: $ROW\"\n\nNUDGE=\"$HOME/.claude/global-memory/transcripts/.errors.log\"\nNUDGE_TEXT=\"[$(date +%F)] aios-audit due - run \\\"audit my AI OS\\\" in ~/DEV/KeystoneOS (uncommitted trend row waiting)\"\nif ! printf '%s\\n' \"$NUDGE_TEXT\" >> \"$NUDGE\"; then\n  echo \"FAIL could not write the nudge to $NUDGE\"\n  exit 1\nfi\n\necho \"=== done ok ===\"\nexit 0\n```\n\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Run it by hand and verify the trend row**\n\n```bash\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$?\"\ncat ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\ntail -25 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\ngit -C ~/DEV/KeystoneOS status --short\n```\n\nExpected: `exit=0`; a header plus one row with **no `MISSING` cells**; the log ends `=== done ok ===`; `git status` shows `aios-trend.md` as **modified but uncommitted** — that is correct, the human commits it.\n\n**A `MISSING` cell means a `get` key does not match a metrics key.** Confirm the two agree before loading launchd — this is how a rename silently kills the job (R7-04):\n\n```bash\ndiff <(python3 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py | cut -d= -f1 | sort) \\\n     <(grep -o '\\$(get [a-z_]*)' ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh \\\n       | sed 's/\\$(get //;s/)//' | sort -u) \\\n  | grep '^>' && echo \"ABOVE: keys the wrapper wants that metrics does not emit\" \\\n  || echo \"every wrapper key is emitted by the metrics script\"\n```\n\nExpected: `every wrapper key is emitted by the metrics script`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Prove the failure paths (R9's masked-failure point)**\n\n```bash\n# A bad metrics script must fail the job, not write a row.\nmv ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py /tmp/aios-metrics.bak\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 1)\"\nmv /tmp/aios-metrics.bak ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-metrics.py\n\n# A LIVE lock must make the job stand down.\nL=~/.claude/scripts/.aios-audit.lock\nrm -rf \"$L\"; mkdir -p \"$L\"; echo $$ > \"$L/pid\"     # this shell is alive\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 0, ABORT live run)\"\nrows_before=$(grep -c '^| 20' ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md)\n\n# A STALE lock must be RECLAIMED, not wedge the job forever (R2-06).\nrm -rf \"$L\"; mkdir -p \"$L\"; echo 999999 > \"$L/pid\"  # a pid that cannot exist\n~/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh; echo \"exit=$? (expect 0, RECLAIM then run)\"\nrows_after=$(grep -c '^| 20' ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md)\necho \"rows before=$rows_before after=$rows_after (expect after = before + 1)\"\nrm -rf \"$L\"\ntail -20 ~/.claude/scripts/logs/aios-audit.log\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n- missing metrics script: `exit=1`, log says `FAIL metrics script errored`, **no** row added\n- live lock: `exit=0`, log says `ABORT live run (pid ...)`, **no** row added\n- stale lock: `exit=0`, log says `RECLAIM stale lock`, **and one row IS added** — `rows_after = rows_before + 1`\n\nThat last line is the whole point of the fix. Round 2 caught that the earlier test removed the lock by hand, which proved nothing about recovery. If a stale lock does not get reclaimed, one crashed run silences the job permanently — fix before loading launchd.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Write the launchd plist**\n\nCreate `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist`. Day 1 at 14:00 — well clear of the 09:00 backup's possible 240-minute window, and the lock covers the rest:\n\n```xml\n<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC \"-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN\"\n  \"http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd\">\n<plist version=\"1.0\">\n<dict>\n  <key>Label</key><string>com.keystone.aiosaudit</string>\n  <key>ProgramArguments</key>\n  <array>\n    <string>/bin/bash</string>\n    <string>-lc</string>\n    <string>$HOME/DEV/KeystoneOS/scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh</string>\n  </array>\n  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>\n  <dict>\n    <key>Day</key><integer>1</integer>\n    <key>Hour</key><integer>14</integer>\n    <key>Minute</key><integer>0</integer>\n  </dict>\n  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>\n  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.out</string>\n  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err</string>\n</dict>\n</plist>\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Load it and verify launchd accepted it**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist 2>/dev/null\nlaunchctl load  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.keystone.aiosaudit.plist\nlaunchctl list | grep aiosaudit\n```\n\nExpected: one line ending `com.keystone.aiosaudit`. A non-zero middle column means the last run failed — read `/tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err`.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Force a run through launchd, not just bash**\n\n```bash\nlaunchctl start com.keystone.aiosaudit\ncat /tmp/com.keystone.aiosaudit.err\ntail -5 ~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/aios-trend.md\n```\n\nExpected: `.err` empty, a second row appended. launchd has a different PATH than an interactive shell — this step catches that. If `python3` is not found, put an absolute interpreter in `aios-metrics.py`'s shebang.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: De-duplicate test rows, note the cadence, commit**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\npython3 - <<'EOF'\np = \"docs/audits/aios-trend.md\"\nlines = open(p).read().splitlines()\nseen, out = set(), []\nfor l in lines:\n    if l.startswith(\"| 20\"):\n        if l in seen:\n            continue\n        seen.add(l)\n    out.append(l)\nopen(p, \"w\").write(\"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\")\nEOF\ncat docs/audits/aios-trend.md\ncat >> ~/.claude/rot.md <<'EOF'\n\n## Cadence\n\nMonthly metrics: launchd `com.keystone.aiosaudit`, day 1 at 14:00.\nIt writes a trend row and a nudge. It does not commit and does not push.\nThe agentic audit is run by hand: \"audit my AI OS\" in `~/DEV/KeystoneOS`.\nEOF\ncp ~/.claude/rot.md docs/rot.md\ngit add scripts/aios-audit-cron.sh docs/audits/aios-trend.md docs/rot.md\ngit commit -m \"feat: monthly AIOS metrics on launchd, no unattended commits\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n### Task 8: Pi trial under a real boundary\n\n**Files:**\n- Create: macOS standard user `piuser` (or an equivalent enforced boundary)\n- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md`\n- Create: `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts`\n- Create: `~/DEV/KeystoneOS/docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md`\n\n**Interfaces:**\n- Consumes: a copy of the pruned pool (Task 2) and the portable frontmatter (Task 5).\n- Produces: a measured migration cost. Nothing consumes it; it is a decision input.\n\n**Why the boundary is mandatory (R5).** Round 1 ran Pi with `cd ~/pi-trial` and called that a sandbox. It is not — `cd` restricts nothing, and the Pi research note says so explicitly. Pi ships no approval prompts by design. A regex denylist inside the agent is defence-in-depth, **not** a boundary: `cat ../DEV/file`, a shell-expanded path, or any tool the guard does not cover walks straight past it. The only enforced boundary available without new infrastructure is **OS file permissions**, so Pi runs as a different user that cannot read Eric's home.\n\n**Scope, narrowed.** The deliverable is a **cost number**, not a working Pi setup. **ADOPT is not an available verdict in this plan** — adopting Pi for real work requires a boundary design that is its own project. Verdicts here are `COST-MEASURED` or `BLOCKED`.\n\n**Stop conditions.** If the user cannot be created, or Pi will not install, or the guard does not block after two attempts, stop and write up what failed. Do not iterate past that.\n\n- [ ] **Step 1: Create the boundary user**\n\nThis needs admin rights and is the one step Eric may prefer to run himself:\n\n```bash\nsudo sysadminctl -addUser piuser -fullName \"Pi Trial\" -password - -home /Users/piuser\nsudo dscl . -read /Users/piuser NFSHomeDirectory\n```\n\nThen prove the boundary actually holds:\n\n```bash\n# R9-07: sysadminctl gives piuser the primary group `staff` (20), and a macOS\n# home is normally user:staff with group r-x. \"Not world-readable\" is therefore\n# the wrong test - piuser would read ~ through the GROUP bits. Default chosen\n# here: give piuser its own primary group. (Tightening ~ to drwx------ is the\n# alternative; that is Eric's call - record which was used in the report.)\ndscl . -list /Groups PrimaryGroupID | awk '$2==4242'   # must print nothing (GID unused)\nsudo dscl . -create /Groups/pitrial\nsudo dscl . -create /Groups/pitrial PrimaryGroupID 4242\nsudo dscl . -create /Users/piuser PrimaryGroupID 4242\nid piuser                        # must NOT list staff(20)\ndseditgroup -o checkmember -m piuser staff   # must say piuser is NOT a member (R10-03: also catches nested membership)\ndscl . -read /Groups/staff NestedGroups 2>&1  # checked 2026-09-12 on this Mac: \"No such key\" - staff nests nothing here\nls -ld ~                         # record owner, group and the group bits\nsudo -u piuser ls ~/DEV          2>&1 | tail -2\nsudo -u piuser ls ~/SecondBrain  2>&1 | tail -2\n```\n\nExpected: both `ls` attempts print **Permission denied**. If either succeeds, the boundary does not exist — STOP. Do not fall back to \"just be careful.\"\n\n- [ ] **Step 2: Install Pi as that user**\n\n`npm install -g` would target `/opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules`, which is `drwxr-xr-x kristinarash:admin` — `piuser` cannot write there and the install fails before any measurement happens (R2-08). Install into a prefix `piuser` owns, and call that executable by absolute path:\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/pi-trial /Users/piuser/.npm-global\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'npm config set prefix /Users/piuser/.npm-global'\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'npm install -g --ignore-scripts @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent' 2>&1 | tail -5\nPI=/Users/piuser/.npm-global/bin/pi\nsudo -u piuser \"$PI\" --version\n```\n\nExpected: an install summary and a version string. `--ignore-scripts` is deliberate — no postinstall scripts run. Everything lands under `/Users/piuser/.npm-global`, so teardown is one `rm -rf` and Eric's Homebrew tree is never touched.\n\n> Use the absolute `$PI` path in every later step. `piuser`'s login PATH does not include the new prefix, and `which pi` would silently find nothing or, worse, Eric's copy.\n\n- [ ] **Step 3: Give it a copy of the pruned pool, not the real one**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/.agents\nsudo rsync -a --copy-links ~/.agents/skills/ /Users/piuser/.agents/skills/\nsudo chown -R piuser:staff /Users/piuser/.agents\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'ls /Users/piuser/.agents/skills | wc -l'\n```\n\nExpected: the post-Task-4 `pool_skills` value. A **copy**, so a Pi misstep cannot corrupt the real pool.\n\n- [ ] **Step 4: Confirm what Pi discovers**\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'cat > /Users/piuser/pi-trial/AGENTS.md <<EOF\n# pi-trial\n\nThrowaway sandbox for evaluating the Pi harness. Nothing here is real work.\nThis user has no read access to the operator home directory by design.\nEOF\ncd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && /Users/piuser/.npm-global/bin/pi --print \"List every skill you can see. Print only the count and the first ten names.\" 2>&1 | tail -20'\n```\n\nExpected: the `pool_skills` value `aios-metrics.py` reported after Task 4 Step 6 — **not** 306. **If it reads ~306, Task 2 did not take — stop and re-run it.** This step is the proof that pruning the pool was the right fix.\n\n> Round 1 had a probe here that asked an unguarded Pi to `ls ~/DEV`. It is deleted. Pointing an unrestrained agent at real directories to demonstrate that it is unrestrained is not a test worth running. The OS boundary in Step 1 already establishes the same fact safely.\n\n- [ ] **Step 5: Port `dcg` to a Pi extension, using the documented contract**\n\nRead the existing guard first:\n\n```bash\nls ~/.claude/hooks/ | grep -i -E 'dcg|guard'\ngrep -n 'pi.on\\|ExtensionAPI\\|event.input' ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/2026-09-12-pi-harness-research.md\n```\n\nRound 1 used `export default { tool_call }` and `ev.args`. The research note documents an **exported factory receiving `ExtensionAPI`**, registration via `pi.on('tool_call', …)`, and a payload at `event.input` (R4). Write `/Users/piuser/pi-trial/.pi/extensions/guard.ts` to the documented shape. If the note and the installed Pi version disagree, **the installed version wins** — record the discrepancy in the report.\n\n```typescript\n// Minimal port of the dcg guard. Proves the hook -> extension mapping.\n// Defence in depth only: the real boundary is the piuser file permissions.\n//\n// R2-07: match the protected NAME anywhere a path separator or string edge\n// precedes it, so /Users/piuser/DEV, ~/DEV, ./DEV and a bare DEV/ all hit.\n// The earlier expression required a whitespace/quote boundary and therefore\n// missed the very probe used to test it.\nconst DENY: RegExp[] = [\n  /\\brm\\s+-rf\\s+\\/(?!tmp)/,\n  /DexaProject/i,\n  /GoogleDrive-/,\n  /(^|[\\s\"'=:/])DEV(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,\n  /(^|[\\s\"'=:/])SecondBrain(\\/|$|[\\s\"'])/,\n];\n\nexport default function guard(pi: any) {\n  pi.on(\"tool_call\", (event: any) => {\n    const blob = JSON.stringify(event?.input ?? {});   // R4: input, not args\n    console.log(`[guard] saw tool_call: ${blob.slice(0, 200)}`);\n    for (const re of DENY) {\n      if (re.test(blob)) {\n        return { block: true, reason: `guard: blocked by ${re}` };\n      }\n    }\n    return { block: false };\n  });\n}\n```\n\nThe `console.log` is deliberate: Step 6 must distinguish \"the guard blocked it\" from \"the handler never ran.\" Print mode may require explicitly trusting a project extension; if Pi asks, grant it for this project only and note the step in the report.\n\n- [ ] **Step 6: Prove the guard blocks — on a fixture that really exists**\n\nCreate a disposable fixture first, so a block cannot be confused with a missing-directory error (R2-07):\n\n```bash\nsudo -u piuser mkdir -p /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc 'echo canary > /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV/file.txt'\nsudo -u piuser ls /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV        # proves it exists and is readable\n```\n\nThen run the three probes:\n\n```bash\nPI=/Users/piuser/.npm-global/bin/pi\n# A: must be BLOCKED by the guard, not by a missing path.\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc \"cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && $PI --print 'Run: ls /Users/piuser/fixture/DEV' 2>&1 | tail -12\"\n# B: must SUCCEED - a guard that blocks everything is not a guard.\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc \"cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && $PI --print 'Run: echo hello' 2>&1 | tail -6\"\n# C: must SUCCEED - a sibling path that is not protected.\nsudo -u piuser bash -lc \"cd /Users/piuser/pi-trial && $PI --print 'Run: ls /Users/piuser/fixture' 2>&1 | tail -6\"\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n- **A** — `[guard] saw tool_call:` appears (the handler ran) **and** the result carries `guard: blocked by`. The fixture exists and is readable, so a \"no such directory\" message would mean the guard did **not** fire.\n- **B** — prints `hello`, no block.\n- **C** — lists `DEV`, no block. This is the false-positive check: the guard must key on the path segment, not on any string containing those letters.\n\nIf A is not blocked, or the `[guard]` line never appears, after two attempts at the extension path or event name, stop per the stop conditions and record which of the two failed — \"handler never registered\" and \"regex did not match\" are different costs to report.\n\n- [ ] **Step 7: Write the cost measurement**\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS\ncat > docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md <<'EOF'\n# Pi trial - 2026-09-12\n\nBoundary: separate macOS user `piuser`, no read access to the operator home.\n`cd` into a directory is NOT a boundary and was not treated as one (R5).\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| pi version | FILL IN |\n| piuser blocked from ~/DEV and ~/SecondBrain? | FILL IN (expect: yes, Permission denied) |\n| skills auto-discovered | FILL IN (expect the post-Task-4 `pool_skills` value, not 306) |\n| skills needing edits to fire in Pi | FILL IN |\n| extension contract matched the research note? | FILL IN (factory + pi.on + event.input) |\n| minutes to port the dcg guard | FILL IN |\n| hooks still to port | FILL IN of 61 |\n| verdict | COST-MEASURED / BLOCKED |\n\n## What the guard proved\n\nFILL IN: paste the blocked and the allowed output from Step 6.\n\n## What a real adoption would still need\n\nNot in scope here. At minimum: a boundary design that is not a second user\naccount, an approval-prompt layer, and a port of the remaining hooks.\nEOF\n```\n\nFill in every `FILL IN` from the real runs. A report left with placeholders is a failed task. Then:\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/KeystoneOS && git add docs/audits/2026-09-12-pi-trial.md && git commit -m \"docs: Pi harness trial under an OS boundary + dcg guard port cost\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\"\n```\n\n- [ ] **Step 8: Tear down**\n\n```bash\nsudo rm -rf /Users/piuser/pi-trial /Users/piuser/.agents \\\n            /Users/piuser/.npm-global /Users/piuser/fixture\nls /Users/piuser\n# Keep or remove the account - Eric's call. To remove:\n# sudo sysadminctl -deleteUser piuser\n```\n\nEverything installed lives under `/Users/piuser/.npm-global`, so removing that directory is the whole uninstall — no `npm uninstall -g` needed and nothing outside that user is touched. The account may stay for future trials; the skill copy, the fixture and the Pi install should not linger.\n\n---\n","numLines":495,"startLine":2341,"totalLines":2869}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e0c47fcf-f3d7-4669-a865-c266f4173e26","session_id":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/KeystoneOS","sessionId":"c59d1788-5ebe-4b20-81a1-9007c16d8649","version":"2.1.269","gitBranch":"main","slug":"replicated-greeting-planet"}
