{"parentUuid":"e5782348-e429-47bb-a787-8993a70301de","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6232cf53-7ac0-4333-99ca-e5a272e4bab3","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DQ8VSJWdXCe2GgqQqWBt6a","type":"tool_result","content":"}\nstatus: completed | VERDICT: REVISE\nsummary: The checker improvements address several earlier cases, but YAML validation still accepts invalid inputs and the seven acknowledged roster-parity failures remain unresolved.\n\n[B2-01] medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:39\n  evidence: plain_scalar_problems exempts quoted values entirely, while check only compares their opening and ending quote characters. A description such as \"Use when testing. Do not use otherwise. \\q\" passes these checks despite containing an invalid YAML escape. Similarly, flow collections are skipped rather than rejected. The checker can therefore report a skill portable when its frontmatter cannot load.\n  fix: Implement strict validation of the supported scalar subset, including quoted-string escapes and block structure, and explicitly reject unsupported collection/tag syntax. Preserve the stdlib constraint by rejecting syntax the checker cannot validate.\n\n[B2-02] medium docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md:65\n  evidence: The report explicitly leaves seven owned skills disabled in Claude Code while publishing them to the other views. The live settings and sync enumeration support that discrepancy: publication includes every owned SKILL.md directory without consulting overrides. Detecting the discrepancy does not fulfill Task 5's zero-failure acceptance criterion or the stated identical-loading goal.\n  fix: Resolve the enabled-roster decision and apply it consistently across harnesses, then update acceptance evidence. Until that decision is made, retain this as an unresolved Gate B finding.\n\n[B2-03] medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:159\n  evidence: claude_overrides catches unreadable or malformed settings and returns a partial or empty mapping; tracked_spellings likewise returns an empty mapping when git inspection fails. main treats both as successful absence of restrictions. Once existing roster failures are resolved, an inaccessible settings file or failed git probe can produce '0 failing' even though required parity or spelling evidence was never inspected. The planned metrics caller captures stderr and extracts that success count.\n  fix: Propagate required configuration and git-inspection failures as a nonzero verification result, distinguish unavailable evidence from an empty configuration, and have the metrics caller honor the checker exit code.\n\nlimitations: ['Read-only static review; no edits, tests, harness invocations, or delegation performed.', 'Reported host verification was read as evidence, not independently reproduced.', 'scripts/aios-metrics.py is not present; its consumer behavior was reviewed from the plan.', 'The PowerShell twin, Windows filesystem behavior, and Pi runtime were not independently inspected in this pass.']\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/kristinarash/DEV","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ab651e09-2ae5-484f-8c6c-f17fc9cc7e26","timestamp":"2026-09-12T23:00:45.203Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"}\nstatus: completed | VERDICT: REVISE\nsummary: The checker improvements address several earlier cases, but YAML validation still accepts invalid inputs and the seven acknowledged roster-parity failures remain unresolved.\n\n[B2-01] medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:39\n  evidence: plain_scalar_problems exempts quoted values entirely, while check only compares their opening and ending quote characters. A description such as \"Use when testing. Do not use otherwise. \\q\" passes these checks despite containing an invalid YAML escape. Similarly, flow collections are skipped rather than rejected. The checker can therefore report a skill portable when its frontmatter cannot load.\n  fix: Implement strict validation of the supported scalar subset, including quoted-string escapes and block structure, and explicitly reject unsupported collection/tag syntax. Preserve the stdlib constraint by rejecting syntax the checker cannot validate.\n\n[B2-02] medium docs/audits/2026-09-12-portability.md:65\n  evidence: The report explicitly leaves seven owned skills disabled in Claude Code while publishing them to the other views. The live settings and sync enumeration support that discrepancy: publication includes every owned SKILL.md directory without consulting overrides. Detecting the discrepancy does not fulfill Task 5's zero-failure acceptance criterion or the stated identical-loading goal.\n  fix: Resolve the enabled-roster decision and apply it consistently across harnesses, then update acceptance evidence. Until that decision is made, retain this as an unresolved Gate B finding.\n\n[B2-03] medium scripts/skill-portability-check.py:159\n  evidence: claude_overrides catches unreadable or malformed settings and returns a partial or empty mapping; tracked_spellings likewise returns an empty mapping when git inspection fails. main treats both as successful absence of restrictions. Once existing roster failures are resolved, an inaccessible settings file or failed git probe can produce '0 failing' even though required parity or spelling evidence was never inspected. The planned metrics caller captures stderr and extracts that success count.\n  fix: Propagate required configuration and git-inspection failures as a nonzero verification result, distinguish unavailable evidence from an empty configuration, and have the metrics caller honor the checker exit code.\n\nlimitations: ['Read-only static review; no edits, tests, harness invocations, or delegation performed.', 'Reported host verification was read as evidence, not independently reproduced.', 'scripts/aios-metrics.py is not present; its consumer behavior was reviewed from the plan.', 'The PowerShell twin, Windows filesystem behavior, and Pi runtime were not independently inspected 